window.nttNotebookSearchIndex = [{"title": "Next GPU rental queue", "summary": "Read the completed Ada batch and prepare the primary RTX 5090 session.", "group": "Plans", "url": "next-gpu-rental.html", "text": "Next GPU rental queue Both NTT versions passed the November 19 native Ada checks and compiled for sm_120. The verified batch includes 48 measurement jobs, 40 sanitizer checks and a CUDA timeline. RTX 5090 acceptance remains open. Read full notes Bundle the next RTX 5090 session ¶ Status: the expanded batch passed on November 19 on a server reporting RTX 4080 SUPER / compute 8.9. Both schedules also compiled for sm_120. The results and CUDA timeline were copied to the M1 Pro and verified. RTX 5090 hardware acceptance remains open. The queue below can be reused for that device after the next group of changes is ready. The paired Ada analysis is complete on the M1 Pro. The November 21 conversion benchmark adds both ModUp input formats, independent ModDown, their resident composition and two host-boundary controls. The November 20 resident ModUp/ModDown composition is a later source change. Its extended resident_cuda_exact fixture and --smoke cases must pass on the next GPU run. The earlier native result covers standalone conversion and the earlier resident NTT chain. See the conversion guide . The collector runs two versions: the new group-twiddle schedule and the older, or legacy, schedule. It checks that both use the same inputs and GPU, and saves each version’s results separately. The resident benchmark checks each result against an exact CPU product. The deferred host driver exercises the protocol and labels its output as simulation. The native collector rejects that output. An independent CPU calculation matches the selected OpenFHE version’s ApproxModUp and ApproxModDown operations. These change the set of primes used to represent a polynomial. Tests use N=65,536, one to five active Q primes and three auxiliary P primes. The reusable converter now also has a broader all-dimension CPU comparison and prepared CUDA correctness probes. See conversion preparation . One rental, ordered GPU work ¶ Order GPU-only item Evidence to bring home 1 Preflight the actual RTX 5090, toolkit, driver, and profiler tools Device identity, commit, build options and tool versions 2 Verify the new default CUDA path and resident chain Native exactness against pinned OpenFHE, lifecycle/failure checks, and the NTT/resident smoke scopes under all four sanitizers 3 Verify the legacy control build before timing Native exactness and appropriate sanitizer smoke, with separate binary hashes and logs 4 Check Shoup and approximate basis conversion Native integer and pinned OpenFHE comparisons, plus all four sanitizer tools 5 Compare legacy versus group twiddles on matched inputs Raw paired resident forward/inverse samples first; public-call timings reported separately 6 Measure the resident operation chain and capture a selected profile End-to-end chain versus an intentionally host-roundtripped chain; device timeline and hardware counters if permitted Use a small first timing block: N=4,096 and 65,536; K=3 and 40 towers; 30- and 60-bit primes. These choices give eight workloads per build. Use the same thread layout, modular arithmetic, random-input seed, warmup count and measurement count for both versions. Record GPU clock speeds. If temperature changes affect the results, repeat with the versions run in the opposite order. Expand to 50/55-bit primes and the remaining dimensions only after the required block is saved. The small timing block does not replace the full native exactness matrix. Commands to prepare before provisioning ¶ The compiler-only CI matrix builds pinned OpenFHE and both schedules for sm_89 and sm_120, including the new native test executables, with no driver. For a local dry run that also links pinned OpenFHE, use a Linux host with the toolkit installed: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode offline --compare-ntt \\ --architectures 120 --cuda-root /path/to/cuda \\ --openfhe-prefix /path/to/openfhe \\ --build-dir build/ntt-ab-offline --output-dir bench-results-ntt-ab-offline On the rented GPU, confirm that the selected device is the RTX 5090 (compute capability 12.0) and that the driver and toolkit work before timing: nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,name,compute_cap,driver_version --format=csv nvcc --version compute-sanitizer --version nsys --version ncu --version In a separate terminal, record clock and temperature readings while the collector runs. Stop the clock log when the collector finishes. nvidia-smi --query-gpu=timestamp,index,temperature.gpu,clocks.sm,clocks.mem,power.draw \\ --format=csv -l 2 > bench-results-ntt-ab-5090-clocks.csv From a clean checkout of the verified revision, use fresh directories for the native eight-shape sweep: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode gpu --compare-ntt --benchmark \\ --architectures 120 --cuda-root /path/to/cuda \\ --openfhe-prefix /path/to/openfhe \\ --build-dir build/ntt-ab-5090 --output-dir bench-results-ntt-ab-5090 \\ --benchmark-dimensions 4096 65536 --benchmark-towers 3 40 \\ --benchmark-prime-bits 30 60 --benchmark-scope gpu-conversion \\ --benchmark-warmups 2 --benchmark-samples 10 This selection records 32 measurement jobs per build, including eight conversion jobs. Every conversion sample is checked against pinned OpenFHE. Fixed NTT session setup is excluded. Per-call conversion parameter construction and upload, allocation and host orchestration are included. The final checks for resident operations download outside their timers. The two host controls include their boundary transfers. The conversion selection also runs its benchmark at N=4,096/K=40/60-bit, one warmup and two samples, under all four sanitizer tools. Those instrumented times belong to correctness logs. The paired timing sweep starts afterward. Both child runs include the new arithmetic and conversion probes. A conversion failure is retained with its test output and prevents timing that build. Both child runs must pass their exactness and sanitizer gates before their own timings. comparison.json only reports matched runs when the two manifests agree on the environment and the actual workload records match (including the roots and seed). The two sets of raw records stay separate. The collector finishes the default schedule before starting the legacy one. Temperature or clock changes between runs could therefore affect the comparison, even with matching inputs. Use --ntt-schedule to collect separately controlled repeats or a separate profiler capture. The combined command takes fresh directories and does not resume or profile. One failed child does not erase the other child's results. Capture the resident timeline and export evidence ¶ After the default branch of the paired run passes, reuse its build and N=65,536/K=3/60-bit vector. Run the timeline and hardware-counter captures independently so a counter permission failure cannot cost the timeline. Use fresh output names if you repeat a capture. records=bench-results-ntt-ab-5090 binary=build/ntt-ab-5090/group_twiddle_ct/ntt_resident_bench vector=\"$records/group_twiddle_ct/benchmark-vector-n65536-k3-b60.txt\" timeout 180 nsys profile --trace=cuda --sample=none --cpuctxsw=none \\ --output \"$records/resident-timeline\" \"$binary\" \"$vector\" 1 2 \\ > \"$records/resident-nsys.log\" 2>&1 || echo \"$?\" > \"$records/resident-nsys.exit\" timeout 180 ncu --set basic --launch-count 8 \\ --export \"$records/resident-counters\" \"$binary\" \"$vector\" 1 2 \\ > \"$records/resident-ncu.log\" 2>&1 || echo \"$?\" > \"$records/resident-ncu.exit\" Profile timings stay separate from benchmark samples. On the Linux host, move the clock log into the record directory, then archive it: mv \"$records-clocks.csv\" \"$records/clocks.csv\" tar -czf \"$records.tar.gz\" \"$records\" sha256sum \"$records.tar.gz\" > \"$records.tar.gz.sha256\" Copy both files to the M1 Pro and verify before ending the rental: shasum -a 256 -c bench-results-ntt-ab-5090.tar.gz.sha256 tar -tzf bench-results-ntt-ab-5090.tar.gz > /dev/null When to rent, and when to stop ¶ Before renting, confirm that the code compiles for sm_120, the RTX 5090 architecture, without requiring a GPU driver. Set a rental time budget and check that the provider allows the profiling tools and counters you need. Run correctness before timing. If a required gate fails, save the failure evidence and skip timing that build. If counters are unavailable, record that limitation separately and continue independent checks or timings. End the session after the planned work (or budget cutoff) once raw logs, measurements and profiles are copied locally and their hashes verified. Review results offline, then choose which NTT path to carry into GPU base conversion. See collector details and the integration roadmap . Next: Offline test tooling →"}, {"title": "Project & setup", "summary": "Build the CPU version first and learn which GPU results have been checked.", "group": "Start here", "url": "project.html", "text": "Project & setup You can build and test this project without a GPU. Start with the CPU commands and the eight-value debugging example. The November 19 Ada run passed the resident chain and new arithmetic/conversion checks. The primary RTX 5090 test remains open. Read full notes ntt-gpu ¶ This project builds exact GPU polynomial operations for CKKS, an encryption scheme that supports computation on encrypted data. Start with the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT), which makes polynomial multiplication easier by evaluating at special modular points. Next add base conversion, which changes the set of primes representing each coefficient, and Hybrid key switching, which uses these operations to change a ciphertext's key representation. Finally connect the GPU implementation to the OpenFHE cryptography library. What works: the CPU reference and tested CUDA baseline agree with OpenFHE. A temporary Ada GPU run passed its selected native checks. The newer resident-chain prototype passes host tests and compiles for CUDA; it has not run on a GPU. Next: validate the current source on RTX 5090. RTX 4090 remains the planned control. The main workload has 65,536 coefficients. Build & test ¶ Requires CMake, a C++20 compiler, and Python 3. CUDA is optional and detected automatically. cmake -S . -B build -DNTT_ENABLE_CUDA=OFF cmake --build build -j ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure These commands build the program and run its CPU tests. The tests check the arithmetic, transform, assignment of work, buffer lifetimes and saved reports. A separate, slower calculation checks small examples independently. Other tests use the full N=65536 size. To follow one butterfly, use the N=8 debugging example . CUDA builds register additional tests labelled gpu . Run those on actual hardware; a passing CPU simulation or CUDA compilation cannot validate device barriers, driver failures or GPU speed. The preparation guide lists the current native checks. Exact OpenFHE compatibility ¶ The optional compatibility test uses this exact OpenFHE 1.6.1 revision: b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f : bash scripts/openfhe_setup.sh The optional CPU suite compares complete arrays with real OpenFHE operations: Check Read more Forward and independent inverse through NativePoly::SwitchFormat Representation, roots and tower packing Hybrid digit extension, key multiplication and reduction Exact operation graph Buffer metadata and resident-chain host behavior Backend contract and prototype For the Hybrid tests, enable OpenFHE's public-key encryption module (PKE), select 64-bit native integers, and turn reduced-noise mode OFF. CKKS decryption returns approximate numbers, so a plausible decrypted answer could hide an array mismatch. These tests require the same values, positions and roots. Design decisions and remaining implementation work appear in the architecture decision record (ADR) and backlog . To reuse an installation, configure with -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON and set CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH to its install prefix. OpenFHE is not required for the standalone build. First session on a rented GPU ¶ Finish the offline preparation first. On the GPU machine, use the chosen source revision and the installation directory of the required OpenFHE version: bash scripts/autodl_setup.sh The helper records the machine and software versions, builds the code, and checks the answers. It also runs sanitizer tools, which detect memory and threading errors, before collecting 280 GPU measurement jobs. The jobs cover N=4096–65536, 30/50/55/60-bit primes and 1–40 RNS towers. A tower is one copy of the polynomial modulo one prime. Public-call and device-event timings stay separate. Use NTT_REPO_DIR , NTT_CUDA_ROOT and NTT_OPENFHE_PREFIX for machine-specific paths. --resume --output-dir <record> reruns checks and reuses only matching, hash-verified measurements. --profile requests Nsight captures. The helper does not pull source, install dependencies or change drivers. Failed stages return nonzero; retain the complete record directory. See the collector guide for timing scopes and options. Collect the matching 140-job CPU baseline locally without a CUDA installation: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode cpu \\ --openfhe-prefix build/openfhe-compat/install \\ --benchmark --output-dir bench-results-local-cpu Use the same problem sizes and repetition counts in CPU and GPU sessions. The reports still identify each machine and build separately. Direct GPU collector commands default to the combined 420-job selection; choose --benchmark-scope gpu for GPU-only work. Changing modes or scopes requires a new record directory. The default build targets sm_89 and, with CUDA 12.8+, sm_120 . For one known GPU, use -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=native (CMake 3.24+). Default CUDA uses one OpenFHE-style group twiddle per butterfly, with the same two-pass thread mapping. Configure a separate build with -DNTT_CUDA_LEGACY_BUTTERFLY=ON for the old two-multiply baseline. GPU JSONL records identify the choice as group_twiddle_ct or legacy_dif . The November 19 paired run measured both schedules on a server reporting RTX 4080 SUPER / compute 8.9. Design ¶ The resident operation-chain prototype adds polynomial handles whose inputs cannot be changed, one ordered GPU work queue (a stream), and temporary CPU buffers owned by the session. A limited operation log retains resources needed by unfinished work. Its CPU driver and CUDA adapter pass exact OpenFHE comparisons. The November 19 native Ada batch also passed the selected sanitizer checks. Production OpenFHE integration remains planned. The November 20 resident basis-conversion addition composes ModUp and ModDown without intermediate polynomial downloads. Its CPU/OpenFHE and failure checks pass, and both CUDA schedules compile. That new composition still needs its own native GPU and sanitizer run. Both backends use the same negacyclic transform convention: psi is a primitive 2N-th root modulo q; psi^N = -1 forward: A_j = sum_i a_i * psi^((2j+1)*i) mod q input: natural coefficient order; output: bit-reversed evaluation order The CUDA schedule views each tower as a 256×256 array. Pass 1 gathers columns, whose elements are 256 positions apart; pass 2 loads rows of adjacent elements. Each pass handles eight stages using memory shared by a thread block. One forward transform launches two kernels and reads/writes the full array twice. Column accesses do not combine efficiently into memory transactions, so counting array bytes alone does not measure traffic to GPU memory. Stage bodies in transform_stage.hpp are compiled for both CPU simulation and CUDA. The current __uint128_t remainder arithmetic is a correctness baseline. Shoup/Barrett reduction, coalesced layouts, and register staging are planned optimizations, subject to exact checks and profiling. Use the work-distribution explorer to inspect blocks, threads, pairs, and twiddle exponents. Roadmap ¶ Deliverable State CPU reference, oracle, schedule simulation Complete CUDA kernels and automatic GPU test registration Native Ada diagnostic passed; RTX 5090 validation pending Named-device baseline and tuning Initial Ada sample collected; full profiling/tuning pending Base conversion and Hybrid key switching Planned OpenFHE integration with CPU fallback Planned The v0.1 target is 2027-01-31; v0.2 key-switch/automorphism work targets 2027-04-30. See the tracked integration plan for working-day gates and the benchmark plan for measurements. Performance claims require named hardware and matching timing boundaries. Docs ¶ Start with NTT and butterflies , then CPU to CUDA . The implementation survey and GPU comparison explain the design choices. python3 -m venv .venv .venv/bin/python -m pip install -r docs/requirements.txt .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory build/docs-site Open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ . See writing and publishing for editing, journals, and GitHub Pages export. Provenance ¶ Independent clean-room implementation. No code, data, or artifacts from past or present employers. Development history is recorded in git. License ¶ TBD before accepting outside contributions. Next: Run & debug →"}, {"title": "NTT & butterflies", "summary": "Learn how repeated two-value operations compute a polynomial transform.", "group": "Learn", "url": "butterfly-explained.html", "text": "NTT & butterflies An NTT evaluates a polynomial at special points using remainders modulo a prime. A butterfly shares one multiplication between two outputs. Follow the small examples first, then learn why high-degree terms wrap back with a minus sign in the project’s 65,536-coefficient polynomials. Open interactive lesson ntt-gpu · a beginner's field guide NTT and butterflies. The NTT changes how a polynomial is represented so multiplication becomes easier. Small operations called butterflies compute this change efficiently. no prerequisites examples use mod 5 and mod 17 interactive diagrams × w a b a + w·b a − w·b the only shape this whole page needs The idea in four steps Read this first. The chapters below explain why each step is valid. 1 · Start A polynomial is stored as a list of coefficients. 2 · Transform The NTT evaluates that list at special modular points. 3 · Reuse A butterfly computes one shared sum-and-difference pair. 4 · Repeat N/2 butterflies across log₂N stages finish the transform. Shortest path: data → NTT → sum to butterfly → butterfly → network → negacyclic . The other chapters are optional detail. Contents 01 Input: coefficients 02 Special points 03 What the NTT does 04 Cost & savings 05 Sum → butterflies 05+ Derivation and twiddle factors 06 One butterfly 07 Full N = 8 network 09 Negacyclic NTT 10 Code mapping 01 Start with coefficients The input is a list of N numbers called coefficients. Position 0 is the constant term, position 1 multiplies x, position 2 multiplies x², and so on. Together they describe a polynomial: 3 slot 0 7 slot 1 1 slot 2 9 slot 3 4 slot 4 0 slot 5 5 slot 6 2 slot 7 For this project, N is 65,536. We need to multiply two such lists efficiently: multiply two lists of length N, get back a list of length N The NTT (Number Theoretic Transform) is how that multiplication gets done fast. (How the product stays length N — the fold rule — is chapter 9's story.) 02 Choose special modular points Modulo 17 means keep the remainder after division by 17: for example, 20 becomes 3 and −1 becomes 16. Start at 1, multiply by 2, and reduce after each step. After eight different values, the sequence returns to 1: The wheel — the eight powers of 2, mod 17 · start at 1, keep multiplying by 2 1 2 4 8 16 15 13 9 ω⁰ ω¹ ω² ω³ ω⁴ = −1 ω⁵ ω⁶ ω⁷ dashed diameters: opposite points sum to 17 = q — each is the other's negative (property iii, halfway = minus) A root of unity satisfies \\(ω^N=1\\). It is primitive when no smaller positive power is 1. Here the eight powers of 2 modulo 17 give eight distinct evaluation points. Modulo 17, 2, 8, 9 and 15 are all primitive eighth roots. They visit the same points in different orders; choosing the same root matters when comparing arrays. Optional: why these points make the NTT fast i. Closed: multiply = move Powers never leave the wheel: \\(ω^{a}\\,ω^{b} = ω^{a+b}\\) . Multiplying two powers adds their exponents. This lets us generate every evaluation point by repeatedly multiplying by one root. ii. Distinct: nothing is lost A primitive root visits N different points. For a polynomial of degree less than N, those N values determine all its coefficients, so the transform can be reversed. This cyclic example needs N to divide q−1, written N | q−1 ( ntt.hpp:61 ). iii. Halfway = minus \\(ω^{N/2} = -1\\) : opposite points are negatives (above: 1↔16, 2↔15, 4↔13, 8↔9 — each pair sums to 17). Why: the ± that makes output pairs — the butterfly's two wings (chapter 6). iv. Squaring = a half wheel Taking every second point gives N/2 distinct points before returning to 1. Their root is ω². This makes each smaller problem another NTT, so the split in chapter 5 can be repeated. Why not just any N distinct numbers? Distinct points make polynomial evaluation reversible. Roots of unity also let smaller transforms share work, which gives the butterfly algorithm. 03 What the NTT does A list like \\((2, 1)\\) represents 2+x. Substituting a number t for x is called evaluation. To evaluate a product, evaluate each polynomial at t and multiply the two answers: \\[(A\\cdot B)(t) = A(t)\\times B(t)\\] At one point, this needs just one multiplication of evaluated values. The identity holds for every t. Both roads below use \\(t = 2\\) (mod 5); change any coefficient and they still agree: Interactive · two roads, one answer (mod 5) A slot 0 − 2 + A slot 1 · x − 1 + B slot 0 − 3 + B slot 1 · x − 2 + ROAD 1 · EXPAND BY HAND ROAD 2 · PLUG IN t = 2 ✓ the two roads agree At a single point, multiplying the evaluated values avoids expanding all coefficient products. The NTT repeats this idea at a structured set of points. N values determine the product after reduction modulo the chosen degree-N ring polynomial. This example leads to the cyclic rule; chapter 9 introduces the negacyclic rule used by CKKS. the definition · the whole transform in one line \\[A_j = a_0+a_1\\omega^j+a_2\\omega^{2j}+a_3\\omega^{3j}+\\cdots \\pmod{q}\\] Evaluating the list at all N wheel points is the NTT . Output j is the list plugged in at wheel point ωʲ — and because the points are distinct (wheel property ii), the transform inverts: evaluate → multiply pointwise → invert replaces a list multiplication with N plain multiplies. The rest of this page makes that round trip cheap. Why \"NTT\" and not \"FFT\"? The Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) usually operates on floating-point complex values, which can introduce rounding error. This Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) uses exact integers modulo a prime. A cyclic length-N transform needs N to divide q−1; our negacyclic form needs 2N to divide q−1. 04 Direct evaluation vs butterflies: work saved The equation demands N outputs, each a sum of N products — computed one by one, that's \\(N \\times N\\) multiplications per transform: Interactive · feel the N² pain list length N = 65,536 2 8 … 2 18 Radix-2 uses \\(N/2\\) butterflies in each of \\(\\log_2 N\\) stages. At N=65536, direct evaluation forms 4,294,967,296 products ; the network uses 524,288 butterflies . The ratio is 8192:1 in these different counting units. Interactive · direct N² vs butterflies (N/2)·log₂N — log–log plot direct — each output on its own (N² products) butterflies — shared brackets ((N/2)·log₂N units) hover the plot — at N = 65,536 the direct line stands 8,192× above the butterfly line This compares arithmetic counts, not measured speed. A twiddle is a precomputed modular multiplier. A cyclic butterfly and the current negacyclic CUDA butterfly each use one twiddle multiplication; the older DIF schedule uses two. Doubling N multiplies the direct count by 4, and the butterfly count by \\(2(\\log_2N+1)/\\log_2N\\). 05 Turn the NTT sum into butterflies Start from the ordinary NTT definition. For output index k, direct evaluation forms one N-term sum: \\[ A_k = \\sum_{r=0}^{N-1} a_r\\,\\omega^{rk}, \\qquad 0\\leq k<N \\] Split the exact sum into even and odd input indices. Write \\(r = 2m\\) for even indices and \\(r = 2m+1\\) for odd indices: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\textcolor{#14655A}{E_k} &= \\sum_{m=0}^{N/2-1} a_{2m}\\,(\\omega^2)^{mk} \\\\ \\textcolor{#C2410C}{O_k} &= \\sum_{m=0}^{N/2-1} a_{2m+1}\\,(\\omega^2)^{mk} \\\\[0.5em] A_k &= \\textcolor{#14655A}{E_k} + \\omega^k\\,\\textcolor{#C2410C}{O_k} \\end{aligned} \\] Now look at the output halfway around the wheel. Since \\(ω^{N/2} = -1\\) , its odd half changes sign while its even half does not: \\[ \\begin{aligned} A_{k+N/2} &= \\textcolor{#14655A}{E_k} - \\omega^k\\,\\textcolor{#C2410C}{O_k} \\\\ t &= \\omega^k O_k \\\\[0.5em] (A_k,\\ A_{k+N/2}) &= (E_k+t,\\ E_k-t) \\end{aligned} \\] That final pair is one butterfly. The two outputs reuse the same \\(E[k]\\) , the same \\(O[k]\\) , and—most importantly—the same multiplication \\(t = ω^k\\,O[k]\\) . 1 · DIRECT Compute N independent sums, each with N terms. \\(\\text{cost: } O(N^2)\\) 2 · SPLIT Separate every sum into its even-index and odd-index terms. \\(A=E+\\omega^k O\\) 3 · SHARE Pair outputs k and k+N/2. They share everything except a sign. \\(E+t,\\ E-t\\) 4 · RECURSE E and O are smaller NTTs using the root ω². Split them again. \\(\\log_2 N\\text{ stages}\\) Why the optimization keeps working \\(ω^{2}\\) is an N/2-th root of unity, so E and O have exactly the same shape as the original problem, only half as large. Repeating the split reaches two-value problems. Each level contains N/2 butterflies, and there are log₂N levels. What exactly happens in each stage? This is cyclic DIT , short for decimation in time: split by input indices, then merge smaller results. First reverse the binary digits of each input index to arrange the coefficients; then merge short transforms into longer ones. Write \\(X_i^{(s)}\\) for the value in array slot \\(i\\) before stage \\(s\\), counting stages from zero. The superscript is a stage label, not a power. 1. Pick the group size \\[d=2^s,\\qquad M=2d=2^{s+1}.\\] Read the equation: \\(N\\) is the full array length and stays fixed. \\(M\\) is the width of one group in this stage. \\(d\\) is both the width of either half and the distance between the two input slots. The stage number runs from \\(0\\) to \\(\\log_2N-1\\); at the last stage \\(M=N\\). Two completed \\(d\\)-point results sit side by side. The butterfly stage merges them into one \\(M\\)-point result. At \\(s=0\\), a single coefficient counts as a completed 1-point result; the group sizes then grow as 2, 4, 8, …, \\(N\\). 2. Read the same offset from both halves \\[ \\begin{aligned} U &= gM+j,\\\\ V &= U+d. \\end{aligned} \\] Address comments: \\(gM\\) skips \\(g\\) complete groups. Adding \\(j\\) selects a slot in the first half; adding \\(d\\) reaches its partner at the same offset in the second half. The bounds \\(0\\le g<N/M\\) and \\(0\\le j<d\\) keep both addresses inside the array. With \\(N/M\\) groups and \\(d\\) pairs per group, each stage has exactly \\(N/2\\) disjoint butterflies. 3. Multiply once, then write both outputs \\[ \\begin{aligned} w_{s,j} &= \\omega_N^{\\,j(N/M)},\\\\ t &= w_{s,j}X_V^{(s)},\\\\ X_U^{(s+1)} &= X_U^{(s)}+t,\\\\ X_V^{(s+1)} &= X_U^{(s)}-t. \\end{aligned} \\] Select the weight: \\(w_{s,j}\\) depends on the stage and the offset, not the group number. The subscript \\(N\\) in \\(\\omega_N\\) names the root's order; it is not an exponent. Compute the shared product: \\(t\\) is the old value at slot \\(V\\) times that weight. It is computed once and reused by both writes. Write the two results: slot \\(U\\) gets the old upper value plus \\(t\\); slot \\(V\\) gets the same old upper value minus \\(t\\). Save it before overwriting \\(U\\). Reduce both results modulo \\(q\\). The superscript changes from \\((s)\\) to \\((s+1)\\) after the writes. It labels the new array state, not a different address or a power. Worked example: one pair at N = 8 Choose stage \\(s=1\\), group \\(g=1\\), and offset \\(j=1\\). Then \\(M=4\\) and \\(d=2\\). The second group starts at slot 4, so read \\(U=4+1=5\\) and \\(V=5+2=7\\). The exponent is \\(j(N/M)=1(8/4)=2\\). With \\(q=17\\) and \\(\\omega_8=2\\) (of order 8), the twiddle is \\(2^2=4\\). Reduce both outputs modulo 17: \\[ \\begin{aligned} (X_5^{(1)},X_7^{(1)})&=(6,3),\\\\ t&=4\\cdot3=12,\\\\ X_5^{(2)}&=6+12\\equiv1,\\\\ X_7^{(2)}&=6-12\\equiv11. \\end{aligned} \\] Here 6 and 3 are example values after stage 0 , not original coefficients. The twiddle derivation below explains why the exponent is 2, not 1. The production CUDA butterfly also multiplies once, but it implements a negacyclic transform with decreasing distances and a group-indexed table. Do not use this lesson's cyclic twiddle formula in that kernel. The handwritten derivation below expands N=4, recurses into the second split, and derives the twiddle exponent for every stage. Try its four-point arithmetic here first: Interactive · both levels, real numbers — mod 17, ω = 4 (ω² = 16 ≡ −1) a₀ − 3 + a₁ · x − 1 + a₂ · x² − 5 + a₃ · x³ − 2 + Change any coefficient: level 1 feeds level 2, four butterflies total — and the result is checked against the direct definition. The cyclic butterfly reuses one multiply, one add and one subtract. The notes below expand the cyclic derivation; the later negacyclic example illustrates the CPU reference's separate folded factors. 05+ Recursive decomposition and twiddle factors Optional derivation: follow the even/odd split through a second recursion, an N=4 network and the stage twiddle formula. Then compare cyclic DIT with the repository's negacyclic DIF. Notation In the ordinary NTT portion of the notes, φ denotes a primitive N-th root of unity ; here we write ω N , with ω N N/2 = −1. This repository uses ψ, a primitive 2N-th root of unity , with ψ N = −1 and ω N = ψ². The ordinary NTT requires N to divide q−1 and works modulo x N −1; the negacyclic form requires 2N to divide q−1 and works modulo x N +1. All arithmetic below is modulo a prime q, and N is a power of two. 1. Split one N-point NTT into two half-length NTTs Start with the definition: i indexes the input coefficients and j indexes the outputs. Split i into 2r and 2r+1: \\[ \\begin{aligned} b_j&=\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}a_i\\omega_N^{ij}\\\\ &=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r}\\omega_N^{2rj} +\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r+1}\\omega_N^{(2r+1)j}\\\\ &=\\underbrace{\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r}(\\omega_N^2)^{rj}}_{A_j} +\\omega_N^j\\underbrace{\\sum_{r=0}^{N/2-1}a_{2r+1}(\\omega_N^2)^{rj}}_{B_j}. \\end{aligned} \\] A is the N/2-point NTT of the even-index coefficients; B is the N/2-point NTT of the odd-index coefficients. Both use the root ω N ² = ω N/2 . Both are periodic in j with period N/2, so only 0 ≤ j < N/2. \\[ \\begin{aligned} A_{j+N/2}&=A_j,\\qquad B_{j+N/2}=B_j,\\\\ \\omega_N^{j+N/2}&=-\\omega_N^j,\\\\ b_j&=A_j+\\omega_N^jB_j,\\\\ b_{j+N/2}&=A_j-\\omega_N^jB_j. \\end{aligned} \\] This produces one butterfly pair: compute t = ω N j B j once, then output A j +t and A j −t . The output indices differ by N/2 and share one multiplication. 2. Recurse: where do ω² and ω⁴ come from? Split A's input sequence again. Its even and odd positions correspond to a 4r and a 4r+2 in the original array: \\[ \\begin{aligned} A_j&=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r}\\omega_N^{4rj} +\\omega_N^{2j}\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r+2}\\omega_N^{4rj}\\\\ &=C_j+\\omega_N^{2j}D_j,\\\\ C_j&=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r}(\\omega_N^4)^{rj},\\qquad D_j=\\sum_{r=0}^{N/4-1}a_{4r+2}(\\omega_N^4)^{rj},\\\\ A_{j+N/4}&=C_j-\\omega_N^{2j}D_j,\\qquad 0\\le j<N/4. \\end{aligned} \\] The minus sign follows from (ω N ²) N/4 = −1. Likewise, B splits into a 4r+1 and a 4r+3 . Each recursion halves the length and squares the root: ω N → ω N ² → ω N ⁴ → … , down to length 2. Bottom-up combination visits these lengths in the opposite order. 3. N=4: expand the sums and draw the network Let ω = ω 4 , so ω²=−1 and ω³=−ω. Expand all four outputs and regroup the shared intermediate values: \\[ \\begin{aligned} b_0&=a_0+a_1+a_2+a_3=(a_0+a_2)+(a_1+a_3),\\\\ b_1&=a_0+a_1\\omega+a_2\\omega^2+a_3\\omega^3=(a_0-a_2)+\\omega(a_1-a_3),\\\\ b_2&=a_0+a_1\\omega^2+a_2\\omega^4+a_3\\omega^6=(a_0+a_2)-(a_1+a_3),\\\\ b_3&=a_0+a_1\\omega^3+a_2\\omega^6+a_3\\omega^9=(a_0-a_2)-\\omega(a_1-a_3). \\end{aligned} \\] The first level computes P=a₀+a₂, Q=a₀−a₂, R=a₁+a₃ and S=a₁−a₃. The second combines P and R into b₀ and b₂, and Q and S into b₁ and b₃. Only the latter pair needs a multiplication by ω. Four-point NTT: two levels and four butterflies Inputs a0 and a2 produce P and Q; a1 and a3 produce R and S. P and R produce b0 and b2; Q and omega times S produce b1 and b3. Outputs are drawn in the order b0, b2, b1, b3. a₀ a₁ a₂ a₃ P = a₀+a₂ R = a₁+a₃ Q = a₀−a₂ S = a₁−a₃ b₀ = P+R b₂ = P−R b₁ = Q+ωS b₃ = Q−ωS Level 1: factor 1 Level 2: factor 1 or ω + − + − × ω Each pair shares its intermediate values; four butterflies complete the transform. Scroll horizontally on small screens. The drawing groups outputs as b₀, b₂, b₁, b₃ to show shared intermediates; the mathematical output index is still j. The drawing's vertical order does not prescribe memory order. Standard iterative DIT usually starts with bit-reversed inputs and produces natural-order outputs. The CPU reference’s forward DIF takes natural-order inputs and produces bit-reversed evaluations. See the N=8 network and the D7 representation contract for the exact ordering. Compare OpenFHE’s format-to-NTT dispatch in the actual implementation. 4. Derive the stage twiddle from its length Choose the root for an \\(M\\)-point transform \\(\\omega_N\\) has order \\(N\\): its powers return to 1 after exactly \\(N\\) steps. An \\(M\\)-point merge needs a root with order \\(M\\), so take a stride of \\(N/M\\) in that same power table. \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\omega_M &= \\omega_N^{N/M},\\\\ (\\omega_M)^M &= \\omega_N^N=1. \\end{aligned} \\] For any positive \\(k<M\\), \\(k(N/M)\\) is strictly between 0 and \\(N\\), so \\((\\omega_M)^k\\ne1\\). Thus the smaller root has exactly order \\(M\\), not just a power that happens to equal 1. Example: with \\(N=8\\) and \\(M=4\\), the stride is 2. Instead of using all eight powers of \\(\\omega_8\\), the four-point transform uses exponents \\(0,2,4,6\\). The next step reaches exponent 8 and returns to 1. Take \\(j\\) steps in the smaller root's powers The odd-index half of the merge is weighted by \\(\\omega_M^j\\), as in the even/odd sum above. Its partner output at \\(j+d\\) uses the opposite sign because \\(\\omega_M^d=-1\\). That is why one product feeds both the sum and the difference. \\[ \\begin{aligned} w_{s,j} &= \\omega_M^j\\\\ &= \\omega_N^{\\,j(N/M)}\\\\ &= \\omega_N^{\\,jN/2^{s+1}}. \\end{aligned} \\] In the worked pair above , \\(N=8\\), \\(M=4\\), and \\(j=1\\): the stride is 2, so the factor is \\(\\omega_8^2\\). At \\(j=0\\), it is always 1. Changing \\(g\\) selects a different block of array slots but leaves the exponent unchanged in this cyclic DIT schedule . Read the equal signs: the first line chooses the \\(j\\)-th local power; the second expresses it in the global table by multiplying the step count \\(j\\) by the stride \\(N/M\\); the third replaces \\(M\\) with \\(2^{s+1}\\). These are three ways to name one weight, not three multiplications. Only the first half's offsets \\(0\\le j<M/2\\) need butterflies: each one also writes its partner at \\(j+M/2\\) within the group. N s M ω N exponent 8 0 2 j=0: exponent 0, factor 1 8 1 4 2j: 0, 2 8 2 8 j: 0, 1, 2, 3 65536 0 2 32768j, with j restricted to 0 65536 1 4 16384j: 0, 16384 65536 15 65536 j: 0…32767 Explore the twiddle exponent by stage and offset Convention Notes: cyclic DIT CPU reference: negacyclic DIF Length N 4 (teaching example) 8 16 65536 (project target) stage s (zero-based) Local offset j Group g ω N exponent E ψ exponent for repository w upper / lower / Pair distance d 5. Read the sequence 8; 4,12; 2,10,6,14 For N=16 and four-bit reversal, these entries are rev₄(1)…rev₄(15). For example, reversing 0001 gives 1000=8; reversing 0011 gives 1100=12. Table index k rev₄(k) 1 8 2, 3 4, 12 4…7 2, 10, 6, 14 8…15 1, 9, 5, 13, 3, 11, 7, 15 If a root table is defined as T[k]=r rev₄(k) , the table above lists its exponents. These numbers alone do not define the order of r, the transform direction, or the butterfly schedule. They are not a universal cyclic DIT twiddle list . In particular, the legacy DIF build uses natural powers; default CUDA uses the OpenFHE group-twiddle indexing. See D7's root and cache contract . 6. Connect the derivation to the forward negacyclic loop The CPU reference and legacy CUDA build use this forward DIF schedule. Stage t starts at zero, and distance d=N/2 t+1 decreases. For group length M=2d and local offset J: \\[ \\begin{aligned} E&=\\frac{N}{2d}J,\\qquad w=\\omega_N^E=\\psi^{(N/d)J},\\qquad c=\\psi^d,\\\\ (u,v)&\\longmapsto\\bigl(u+cv,\\;(u-cv)w\\bigr)\\pmod q. \\end{aligned} \\] The change-of-root rule still raises the global root to N/M, but stages run in the opposite direction and include c to fold the negacyclic twist. At N=65536 the first forward stage has d=32768 and w=ω N J =ψ 2J . In the final stage d=1, J must be 0 and w=1. DIT stage 0 is not the first stage of the repository's forward DIF. The inverse uses reciprocal w and c, reverses the stage order, and finally multiplies by N⁻¹. For the exact coefficient indices, bit-reversed output order, and CUDA groups, see the forward and inverse work distribution . 06 Meet the butterfly A butterfly combines two input values into two outputs using a multiplier, an addition and a subtraction. Its drawing resembles a butterfly. DIT means decimation in time: split input indices. DIF means decimation in frequency: split output indices. Their cyclic forms place the multiplier on different sides: × w + − a b a + b·w a − b·w 1 2 DIT — ① multiply the input , ② add / subtract · what chapters 5–7 derive + − × w a b a + b (a − b)·w 1 2 DIF — ① add / subtract, ② multiply the difference output · the same unit, run the other way The gold ①② marks the order of operations. DIT multiplies an input before adding and subtracting. DIF multiplies the difference afterward. For the negacyclic transform, the CPU reference also multiplies the lower input by ψ^d; chapter 10 shows that extra factor. 07 Connect butterflies into stages Each of the eight wires carries one value from left to right. Every X combines a pair. Press play to run the stages modulo 17 with ω=2, or click a butterfly to inspect its arithmetic. The distance between partners doubles from 1 to 2 to 4 as smaller transforms are merged. Interactive · the N = 8 butterfly network (mod 17) ▶ play all stages next stage ⏭ reset stage 0 / 3 — values not yet transformed Hover or click a butterfly (the X crossings) to see exactly what it computes. wire (a value flowing right) butterfly value chip (appears as stages complete) Why are the inputs scrambled? The leftmost coefficients are arranged by bit-reversed index : reverse the three binary digits of each index to get 0,4,2,6,1,5,3,7. This initial rearrangement lets the cyclic DIT network produce outputs in ordinary order. The CPU reference instead starts with ordinary coefficients and produces bit-reversed evaluations. Its inverse expects that order directly, so no separate array rearrangement is needed between forward, pointwise multiplication and inverse ( bitrev.hpp documents the permutation itself). And the negacyclic variant? This network is the plain (\"cyclic\") transform. The negacyclic variant below uses the same pair network with a folded ψ factor on the lower input of each butterfly ( ntt.hpp:87 ). 09 CKKS uses the negacyclic form A product of two N-coefficient polynomials can have more than N coefficients. To keep fixed-size arrays, reduce it using a ring rule: \\[(2+x)(3+2x)=6+7x+2x^2\\] The two rules used here are: \\[ \\begin{aligned} x^N &= +1 && \\text{cyclic: wrap with a plus} \\\\ x^N &= -1 && \\text{negacyclic: wrap with a minus} \\end{aligned} \\] The cyclic fold is precisely the world of chapters 3–7: every ω-wheel point satisfies x^N = ω^N = 1. The negacyclic fold is the one this repo implements. With \\(x^{2} = -1\\) (N = 2, mod 5), the overflow folds back like this: \\[ 6+7x+2x^2 \\;\\longmapsto\\; (6-2)+7x \\;\\equiv\\; 4+2x \\pmod{5} \\] All coefficients are also reduced modulo the prime q. The ring rule controls where high-degree terms go; reduction modulo q controls their values. Which points respect the minus fold? Those with \\(x^N = -1\\) — the roots of \\(x^N + 1\\) . They are the odd steps of a doubled wheel: a number ψ with \\(ψ^N = -1\\) closes after 2N points, and \\((ψ^{\\mathrm{odd}})^N = (-1)^{\\mathrm{odd}} = -1\\) . Mod 17, ψ = 6, and \\(ψ^{2} = ω\\) — its even steps are chapter 2's wheel: The doubled wheel · ψ = 6, a 16-th root of unity mod 17 (ψ⁸ = −1, ψ² = ω) even steps = the ω wheel: 1 ψ⁰ 2 ψ² 4 ψ⁴ 8 ψ⁶ 16 ψ⁸ 15 ψ¹⁰ 13 ψ¹² 9 ψ¹⁴ odd steps = negacyclic: 6 ψ¹ 12 ψ³ 7 ψ⁵ 14 ψ⁷ 11 ψ⁹ 5 ψ¹¹ 10 ψ¹³ 3 ψ¹⁵ spot-check one highlighted point: 6⁸ = (6²)⁴ = 2⁴ = 16 ≡ −1 (mod 17) ✓ · chapter 3's t = 2 mod 5 was exactly an odd step of the small doubled wheel there (2² ≡ −1, 2⁴ ≡ 1) The key substitution: pre-twist, then use the ordinary NTT Let \\(ω = ψ^{2}\\) and pre-twist coefficient r as \\(b[r] = a[r]\\,ψ^r\\) . An ordinary cyclic NTT of b then gives \\(\\sum_{r=0}^{N-1} b_r\\omega^{rk} = \\sum_{r=0}^{N-1} a_r\\psi^{(2k+1)r}\\) . In other words, the twist moves evaluation from the even powers of ψ to exactly the odd powers—the roots of x^N+1. The inverse runs the cyclic inverse and multiplies coefficient r by \\(ψ^{-r}\\) . Derive the factors from the first split Choose a primitive 2N-th root ψ in the current prime field, so ψᴺ=−1, and set ω=ψ². For power-of-two N, checking ψᴺ=−1 proves that ψ has order 2N. The prime must satisfy \\(2N\\mid(q-1)\\). OpenFHE compatibility additionally requires the exact same ψ as its tower parameters (see RootOfUnity ); another primitive root changes the output order. \\[ F_k=\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}a_i\\psi^{(2k+1)i} =\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}(a_i\\psi^i)\\omega^{ki}. \\] This derivation follows the CPU reference and optional legacy CUDA build. The default CUDA path now uses an OpenFHE-style one-multiply butterfly . Both compute the same final evaluations, but their intermediate values and twiddle tables differ. Now split input into a low half and a high half, d=N/2. This is the DIF split; the even/odd split above was DIT. The high-half factor contains a sign that depends only on k: \\[ \\begin{aligned} F_k&=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\\left[a_j+a_{j+d}\\psi^{(2k+1)d}\\right]\\psi^{(2k+1)j},\\\\ \\psi^{(2k+1)d}&=\\psi^d(\\psi^N)^k=(-1)^k\\psi^d. \\end{aligned} \\] Separate the even output indices k=2ℓ and odd indices k=2ℓ+1. Both smaller transforms use root ψ², but the odd branch needs an extra ψ²ʲ: \\[ \\begin{aligned} F_{2\\ell}&=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\\underbrace{(a_j+\\psi^d a_{j+d})}_{x_j}(\\psi^2)^{(2\\ell+1)j},\\\\ F_{2\\ell+1}&=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}\\underbrace{(a_j-\\psi^d a_{j+d})\\psi^{2j}}_{y_j}(\\psi^2)^{(2\\ell+1)j}. \\end{aligned} \\] The first split gives c=ψᴺᐟ² and w=ψ²ʲ. To derive all later folded factors without guessing a root for each branch, compare with the ordinary cyclic DIF network applied to the pre-twisted coefficients. At a current block of length 2d, let its cyclic values and folded values be related by \\(b_j=\\psi^j a_j\\), where j is local to that block. The cyclic butterfly reads \\(b_{j+d}=\\psi^{j+d}a_{j+d}\\): \\[ \\begin{aligned} b'_j&=b_j+b_{j+d}=\\psi^j(a_j+\\psi^d a_{j+d}),\\\\ b'_{j+d}&=(b_j-b_{j+d})\\omega_N^{jN/(2d)} =\\psi^j(a_j-\\psi^d a_{j+d})\\omega_N^{jN/(2d)}. \\end{aligned} \\] Both child blocks now have length d and local index j. Removing their common local twist ψʲ leaves the folded outputs \\(x_j=a_j+\\psi^d a_{j+d}\\) and \\(y_j=(a_j-\\psi^d a_{j+d})\\omega_N^{jN/(2d)}\\). The same relation holds for the next split with d halved. Initially it is exactly the pre-twist; at length one j=0, so the remaining twist is 1. This proves every stage and the final result with no extra twist pass. What stays the same The radix-2 network, paired indices, number of stages, and N/2 butterflies per stage do not change. \\[ \\begin{aligned} u' &= u+v \\\\ v' &= (u-v)\\,w \\end{aligned} \\] What negacyclic changes The ψ twist changes the factors carried by the butterfly. This repo folds it into the same pass instead of storing a separate twist table. \\[ \\begin{aligned} u' &= u+cv \\\\ v' &= (u-cv)\\,w \\end{aligned} \\] For the repo's forward DIF loop, a stage pairs entries distance \\(d\\) apart. Its folded factors are: \\[ \\begin{aligned} c_d &= \\psi^d \\\\ w_{d,j} &= \\psi^{\\frac{N}{d}j} = \\omega^{\\frac{N}{2d}j} \\\\[0.5em] \\mathrm{upper}' &= \\mathrm{upper}+c_d\\,\\mathrm{lower} \\\\ \\mathrm{lower}' &= (\\mathrm{upper}-c_d\\,\\mathrm{lower})\\,w_{d,j} \\end{aligned} \\] The group number does not select the twiddle . At a fixed stage, every group repeats the same sequence: j starts at 0, so \\(w = 1\\) , then advances by \\(ψ^{N/d}\\) for each butterfly offset. Negacyclic therefore changes the factors, not the network's pairing pattern. The inverse uses reciprocal ψ factors, reverses the stage direction, and applies the final \\(N^{-1}\\) scale. 10 Map the picture to code Here is the actual loop from this repo's CPU reference ( include/ntt/ntt.hpp , the forward DIF sweep — twist folded in as ladder constants, natural values in, bit-reversed values out). Read the comments with chapter 7 open next to it — every line is a part of the picture: for ( uint64_t stage_pair_distance = n_ / 2 ; stage_pair_distance >= 1 ; stage_pair_distance >>= 1 ) { const uint64_t ladder_twiddle = pow_mod (psi_, stage_pair_distance, q_); // c_d = ψ^d const uint64_t twiddle_step = pow_mod (psi_, n_ / stage_pair_distance, q_); // ψ^(N/d) for ( uint64_t group_start_index = 0 ; group_start_index < n_; group_start_index += 2 * stage_pair_distance) { uint64_t twiddle_power = 1 ; // j = 0 for ( uint64_t pair_offset = 0 ; pair_offset < stage_pair_distance; ++pair_offset) { const uint64_t upper_index = group_start_index + pair_offset; const uint64_t lower_index = upper_index + stage_pair_distance; const uint64_t upper_value = values[upper_index]; const uint64_t scaled_lower_value = mul_mod (ladder_twiddle, values[lower_index], q_); values[upper_index] = add_mod (upper_value, scaled_lower_value, q_); // upper + c_d·lower values[lower_index] = mul_mod ( sub_mod (upper_value, scaled_lower_value, q_), twiddle_power, q_); // (upper − c_d·lower)·w_d,j twiddle_power = mul_mod (twiddle_power, twiddle_step, q_); } } } The loops visit stages, groups and pairs. Each pair uses the weighted sum/difference above. CUDA assigns independent pairs to threads and groups stages in shared memory; N=65536 forward uses two launches. Recap The NTT evaluates a polynomial at modular roots of unity. Reusing even/odd subtransforms reduces N² direct products to (N/2)·log₂N butterflies. At N=65,536 that is 524,288 butterflies. CKKS uses odd powers of a primitive 2N-th root, giving multiplication modulo x^N+1. ntt-gpu · docs/butterfly-explained.html mod-5 & mod-17 examples · local KaTeX · view source freely Nov 2026 Next: From CPU to CUDA →"}, {"title": "From CPU to CUDA", "summary": "Learn how GPU threads cooperate to run the NTT.", "group": "Learn", "url": "gpu-ntt-explained.html", "text": "From CPU to CUDA A thread computes assigned pairs. A block is a team of threads that reuses a loaded group in shared memory across several stages. At N=65536, columns handle far-apart pairs and rows handle nearby pairs. Threads wait at barriers between stages; kernel order separates the passes. Open interactive lesson ntt-gpu · a beginner's field guide to the GPU port From CPU to CUDA. A GPU thread follows instructions for one assigned pair at a time. Threads work in teams called blocks. Each block keeps a small part of the array in shared memory while it runs several NTT stages. target: RTX 5090 ring dimension: 65,536 256 threads per block every cook, same step, own ingredient The GPU plan in four steps These four steps summarize the design. The chapters below define the GPU terms and connect them to the code. VRAM means the GPU’s main memory; a tile is one small group of array values. 1 · SPLIT Give each independent butterfly pair to a GPU thread. 2 · LOAD A block gathers one 256-value group into shared memory. 3 · COMPUTE Run several stages locally, with a barrier between stages. 4 · REGROUP Use a far-pair pass and a near-pair pass to cover the full NTT. Current status: correctness-first baseline. Results must match the OpenFHE-compatible CPU reference exactly. In the column pass, neighboring threads read widely separated addresses. The GPU cannot combine those requests efficiently (coalescing), so a small count of array reads and writes can still require many memory transactions. Contents 01 CPU vs GPU 02 GPU vocabulary 03 Parallel butterflies 04 Two-pass design 05 Shared-memory tile 06 Code mapping 07 Synchronization 08 Memory cost 09 Current limits 01 CPU and GPU favor different work A CPU is good at a few complicated tasks. A GPU is good at many copies of the same small task. A GPU schedules groups of 32 threads called warps . Threads in a warp normally execute the same instruction on different data; different warps can make progress independently. If threads in one warp take different branches, the GPU may execute those paths separately. This is called branch divergence and can waste work. At each NTT stage, disjoint pairs use the same butterfly formula. Assigning these pairs to threads exposes parallel work without changing the result. 02 Six GPU terms These six terms connect the GPU model to this repository. RNS means Residue Number System: store a polynomial under several primes, with one array called a limb or tower for each prime. An SM (streaming multiprocessor) is a GPU processor that schedules thread blocks. Occupancy measures how many warps can remain active on an SM relative to its limit. Word Plain meaning In this repo thread One execution instance. It computes assigned pairs; it does not permanently own a coefficient. we use T = 256 per block block A team of threads sharing memory and block barriers. one block owns one tile grid All blocks in a launch. The x and y coordinates identify independent work. x = tile index, y = RNS limb shared memory Fast storage shared by the threads of one block. 256 values = 2 KB per limb global memory Device-wide memory holding coefficients, moduli, and twiddle tables. the whole polynomial, all limbs kernel A function launched across a grid of threads. six kernels in ntt_kernels.cu A barrier ( __syncthreads() ) makes threads wait until the whole block reaches the same point. The diagrams use a kitchen analogy: a cook is a thread, a station is a block, and its counter is shared memory. how the pieces nest · one picture HOST your Mac / the CLI starts the kernel launch GPU — one chip GLOBAL MEMORY (VRAM) — the warehouse · shared by every block BLOCK (x=0, y=1) its counter (shared) BLOCK (x=1, y=1) its counter (shared) BLOCK (x=2, y=1) its counter (shared) every block loads its tile from VRAM, computes on its counter, writes back — blocks never see each other's counters thread (a cook) → inside a block (a station, with its private counter) → blocks tile the grid (x = tile, y = limb) → all inside one GPU around one warehouse Optional: RTX 4090 and RTX 5090 capacity estimates the picture, counted · for both rentable cards RTX 4090 RTX 5090 station groups (SMs) 128 170 cooks in flight (resident threads) 196,608 261,120 our 256-cook stations, resident at once 768 1,020 max cooks per block / per station 1,024 / 1,536 1,024 / 1,536 counter (shared) per block · per station 48 KB · ~100 KB 48 KB · ~100 KB warehouse (VRAM) 24 GB GDDR6X 32 GB GDDR7 pipe into the chip — VRAM bandwidth ≈ 1.0 TB/s ≈ 1.8 TB/s traffic inside the chip — shared, aggregate ≈ 40 TB/s ≈ 50 TB/s The table estimates that shared memory inside the chip can move data much faster than external VRAM. This motivates reusing a loaded group across stages. The shared-memory figures are rough estimates from per-processor capacity, processor count and boost clock; they are not measurements of these kernels. // the first rows are one multiplication chain — the table explains itself: 4090: 128 SMs × 1,536 threads/SM = 196,608 in flight ÷ 256 (our T) = 768 of our blocks resident 5090: 170 SMs × 1,536 = 261,120 ÷ 256 = 1,020 These are thread-limit ceilings, not measured occupancy. Registers, shared memory, and block limits can reduce residency. At 40 limbs each pass launches 10,240 blocks; the scheduler runs them as resources become available. At N = 65,536 with 3 limbs, each pass launches 256 × 3 = 768 blocks . How many stay resident at once depends on threads, registers, shared memory, and the device's block limits; measure it instead of assuming one wave. 03 One stage has independent pairs A stage contains N/2 disjoint butterfly pairs. At N=16, eight pairs can run independently; the next stage must wait for their results. The example follows all four N=16 stages. The first two fit inside strided columns; the last two fit inside contiguous rows. Earlier 8-point pairing animation one stage at N = 8 · click a stage stage d = 4 stage d = 2 stage d = 1 pick 0,2,4,6 → 2 stages Every position belongs to exactly one pair in this stage. These four butterflies read and write separate values, so they can run in any order. The fourth button demonstrates a small version of pass 1 from chapter 4. Butterflies in one stage write disjoint positions, so their execution order does not affect the result. Synchronize before the next stage reads those positions. A distance-d butterfly stays inside a group of 2d values. This lets one block keep several stages local, provided every dependency stays in its group. CPU simulation checks the shared index and arithmetic logic. Native CUDA tests must separately check device compilation, barriers and runtime behavior. 04 Split the NTT into two passes A full N=65536 tower needs 512 KiB, which exceeds the shared-memory capacity available to one block. Split it into smaller groups: Why three towers need 1.5 MiB · compare one tower with block memory 1 value (one coefficient, one limb) = 64 bits = 8 bytes one tower = 2¹⁶ values × 8 bytes = 524,288 B = 512 KiB 3 towers = 3 × 512 KiB = 1,536 KiB = 1.5 MiB 40 towers = 40 × 512 KiB = 20 MiB 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes; 1 MiB = 1,024 KiB A limb, or tower, stores the polynomial modulo one prime. More towers mean more data and more transforms, but each tower uses the same independent NTT algorithm. The grid’s y coordinate chooses the tower; it is separate from the column index inside a tower. Forty towers are an example workload, not a requirement of all ciphertexts. The two-pass design follows from four choices: ① How the CPU does it — and where it hurts The CPU reference visits stages and pairs serially. At N=65536, each tower needs 32,768 butterflies per stage and 524,288 in total. The GPU assigns independent pairs to threads; actual speed depends on arithmetic, memory and scheduling costs. ② First GPU idea: every pair on a cook, everything from VRAM A simple GPU port could launch one kernel per stage and load/store the whole array each time. That repeats global-memory traffic across all 16 stages. Shared-memory staging reduces those repeated loads and stores. ③ The hardware rule that shapes the fix — and the one choice we get to make Shared memory belongs to one block. Several stages can run there if all their dependencies fit inside that block's data. This implementation chooses 256 values per group: counter budget: 48 KB ÷ 8 bytes per value = up to 6,144 values per group choose: 256 values (= 2 KB of the 48) — and 65,536 = 256 × 256 A power-of-two group contains complete butterfly groups. At N=65536, 256 × 256 gives a symmetric row/column split. Each block requests 2 KiB; registers and hardware limits also determine how many blocks can run together. one block's counter budget: 48 KB 2 KB spare — 46 KB of the allowance unused one SM station — neighbors bring their own tiles: 2 KB group block A 2 KB group block B 2 KB group block C ④ That number is the pair distance d View the array as a 256×256 grid. Distances of 256 or more preserve the column index, so columns own the far stages. Distances 128 or less stay within a row, so rows own the near stages. These two sets cover all 16 stages. Stated cleanly, the four steps compress into one rule — the one structural idea of this port: the two-pass split · groups of 256, twice PASS 1 — the strided groups (d = 32,768 … 256) → 256 groups {r, r+256, …} each gather to a counter, run 8 stages, scatter PASS 2 — the contiguous tiles (d = 128 … 1) → 256 tiles of 256 each gather, run 8 stages, scatter Both passes preserve the CPU reference’s paired positions and final result. The default CUDA butterfly has different intermediate values. Pass 1 handles far pairs; pass 2 handles near pairs. This is four logical value movements, but pass 1 currently uses strided, uncoalesced addresses, so the hardware may issue many more memory transactions. 05 Keep a group in shared memory A block copies its assigned group from device-wide global memory into shared memory. All its threads can then access that group during several stages. After the final stage, the block copies the group back: one block, one group · the kernel's whole life VRAM (global) the polynomial BLOCK — 256 cooks x = group id · y = limb 0..2 the block's COUNTER — 2 KB 256 values · 1 fetch in + 1 write out, total 1 2 4 3 8 stages · d = 128…1 · barrier each ① 256 cooks gather 256 values (1 each) · ② roll call — values land on the counter · ③ eight butterfly stages, barrier between · ④ carry the results back Naive code reads and writes VRAM at every stage. The two-pass design loads and stores each group once per pass. That reduces logical traffic, but pass 1 still needs a coalesced transpose or Four-Step layout to make those accesses efficient. A 256-value group uses 2 KiB of shared memory per block. An SM is one of the GPU processors that schedules blocks; it may hold several blocks at once. Actual occupancy on the RTX 5090 must be measured after register use and launch limits are known. The same trick, taken twice The same staging pattern is used twice: once for strided columns and once for contiguous rows. one forward at N = 65,536 · two passes, four truck trips PASS 1 strided groups {r, r+256, …} × 256 groups × 3 limbs 8 stages d = 32,768…256 8 stages on its counter 8 stages on its counter VRAM — the warehouse · the mix: pass 1 scatters strided, pass 2 gathers contiguous 8 stages d = 128…1 8 stages on its counter 8 stages on its counter PASS 2 contiguous tiles 256q … 256q+255 × 256 tiles × 3 limbs 1 2 3 4 per value: ① fetch + ② write-back + ③ fetch + ④ write-back = 4 warehouse trips — versus 32 for 16 separate stages Forward runs columns then rows. Inverse runs rows then columns and finishes with normalization. At N=65536 this means two forward launches and three inverse launches. 06 Map the design to code For the current arithmetic and a worked CUDA table lookup, see Why these inputs and twiddles? . The shared stage helper assigns disjoint pairs to threads. Here is the forward arithmetic with names shortened. A window maps local position u to global position origin + stride × u. for (p = threadIdx.x; p < window_length / 2; p += blockDim.x) { u = p + (p / local_distance) * local_distance; v = u + local_distance; d = local_distance * stride; group = (origin + stride * u) / (2 * d); zeta = table[N / (2 * d) + group]; a = shared[u]; t = mul_mod(shared[v], zeta, q); shared[u] = add_mod(a, t, q); shared[v] = sub_mod(a, t, q); } Second, a column-pass block gathers its strided group, calls the shared stage helper and scatters results. The current kernel keeps this mapping in forward_strided_group_kernel : __global__ void forward_strided_group_kernel (uint64_t* a, const uint64_t* pp, const uint64_t* q, uint64_t n, uint32_t sub) { const uint64_t limb_q = q[blockIdx.y]; const uint64_t* table = pp + (uint64_t)blockIdx.y * (n + 1); extern __shared__ uint64_t smem[]; // the counter const uint64_t r = blockIdx.x; // x = which strided group uint64_t* al = a + ( uint64_t )blockIdx.y * n; // y = which limb for (uint32_t t = threadIdx.x; t < sub; t += blockDim.x) // ① gather: value r+256t, smem[t] = al[r + ( uint64_t (t) << 8 )]; // one per cook __syncthreads(); // ② roll call for (uint64_t delta = sub / 2 ; delta >= 1 ; delta >>= 1 ) { // ③ the 8 far stages, transform_stage<false, 256>(smem, sub, delta, n, table, limb_q, threadIdx.x, blockDim.x, r); __syncthreads(); // barrier each } for (uint32_t t = threadIdx.x; t < sub; t += blockDim.x) // ④ scatter back strided al[r + ( uint64_t (t) << 8 )] = smem[t]; } The pass-2 kernel ( forward_contiguous_tile_kernel ) is the same shape with a contiguous gather — it is this kernel with r + (t<<8) replaced by base + t , running the near stages with transform_stage<false, 1> and the tile’s global starting index. Third, the launch grid identifies independent groups and prime towers. RNS stores one polynomial modulo several primes; each prime's array is called a limb or tower. The grid's y coordinate selects that tower: // one forward = TWO launches (grid = x × limbs): forward_strided_group_kernel<<< dim3 ( 256 , num_limbs), T, sub * sizeof (uint64_t)>>>(buf, d_pp, d_q, n, sub); forward_contiguous_tile_kernel<<< dim3 (n/ 256 , num_limbs), T, 256 * sizeof (uint64_t)>>>(buf, d_pp, d_q, n, 256 ); // pass 1: block (x=r, y=k) = strided group r of limb k — owns {r, r+256, …} // pass 2: block (x=q, y=k) = contiguous tile q of limb k — owns 256q … 256q+255 Three limbs produce 768 blocks per pass. The y grid coordinate batches limbs in one launch; hardware resource limits determine how many blocks run at once. There are three synchronization boundaries: ⇄ Between the two passes: the mix is pure data movement Pass 2 reads pass 1's results. Their same-stream kernel order ensures pass 1 completes first. The scatter/gather changes which block owns each group; it adds no arithmetic transform. ⇄ Between stages inside a pass: the barrier, because partners swap Within a pass, a stage can read values written by other threads in the previous stage. Every thread reaches __syncthreads() before the next stage starts. ∅ Between blocks: silence — and that is the design Within one pass, blocks have disjoint groups and no cross-block dependencies. Each block can finish its eight stages independently. With three towers, a pass launches 768 blocks; they need not run simultaneously. 07 Synchronize between stages Two barrier rules matter: the roll call rule · why barriers sit between stages the barrier — nobody passes alone cook A — done early cook B — working cook C — nearly there cook D — the slowest waiting… still working all cross together — when D arrives time → a block is exactly as fast as its slowest thread — so keep everyone's work the same shape Rule 1: the barrier is all-or-nothing Every thread in a block must reach the same __syncthreads() . A barrier in a branch taken by only some threads is invalid and can hang the kernel. Here barriers sit outside the pair loop. Rule 2: idle cooks still attend roll call A small N=64 tile has only 32 butterfly pairs but still launches 256 threads. Threads without a pair skip the arithmetic loop, then reach the barrier normally. Small fixtures check this boundary. 08 Memory costs and timing boundaries Registers, shared memory, global memory and host/device transfers have different costs. This diagram illustrates their relative scale; it is not a measurement of these kernels. cost to fetch one value · relative, order-of-magnitude Log-scale bars — each gridline is ×10. Exact cycles vary by GPU generation; the gaps are what matter. Keep loop variables in registers, stage each group in shared memory and use global memory between passes. The host-facing multiplication uploads two inputs and downloads the final result, rather than transferring after every NTT stage. The fused chain is two forwards, pointwise multiplication and one inverse. Its intermediate arrays remain on the GPU. 09 Current baseline and next optimizations The CPU simulator checks shared stage logic, while native tests check actual CUDA results and execution. The current modular multiplication first forms the full product in a wider integer type, then takes its remainder modulo q. Its device instruction cost needs profiling; it is not one native 128-bit divide instruction. The temporary Ada results establish the tested baseline. Candidate improvements include division-free Shoup/Barrett arithmetic, coalesced loads and more register work. Test and measure each change separately. RTX 5090 validation remains pending. The newer resident runtime also needs its own native lifetime, failure and sanitizer checks. Recap A thread computes pairs; a block owns a group. Shared memory reuses that group across stages. Columns handle far pairs, rows handle near pairs, and barriers preserve dependencies. Next, use the N=65536 explorer to trace a specific block and thread. Start here if you haven't: Butterflies all the way down — why the NTT is butterflies at all. Next: 65536: work distribution →"}, {"title": "65536: work distribution", "summary": "See which array positions each block and thread reads and writes.", "group": "Learn", "url": "ntt-65536-work-distribution.html", "text": "65536: work distribution Picture one prime tower as 256 rows by 256 columns. Columns run the first eight forward stages; rows run the last eight. Choose a stage, block and thread to inspect its pair. Inverse runs the passes in reverse order and finishes with a modular scale factor. Open interactive lesson CUDA execution · a worked example How 65,536 coefficients map to GPU threads. Follow one polynomial through the forward and inverse NTT. A block is a team of GPU threads with shared memory. First see which values each block loads, then inspect the pairs assigned to one thread. Polynomial → Pass → Block → Thread The whole job The two passes Resources & memory access Forward proof Inverse proof · D3 Row kernels & coverage · D4 Explore a thread Synchronization & RNS 01 Start with the data N is the number of coefficients in one polynomial, here 65,536. A tower stores those N coefficients reduced modulo one prime q , at slots 0 through N−1. Each stored residue uses 8 bytes, so one tower needs 512 KiB. Other towers use different primes, but the same array length. 65536 coefficients per modulus 16 stages · 32768 butterflies each 524,288 butterflies · two kernel launches Imagine the same array as 256 rows × 256 columns . This is an index view; the stored array stays flat. global_index = row × 256 + column Rows and columns both start at 0. Multiplying the row number by 256 skips complete rows; adding the column number selects a slot inside the row. These are element indices, not byte offsets. 02 Divide the work by distance Pass 1 · far stages One block owns one column 32768 → 16384 → 8192 → 4096 → 2048 → 1024 → 512 → 256 Moving by any of these distances leaves the column number unchanged. Each block gathers its column’s 256 values, which are spaced 256 positions apart. It runs eight stages in shared memory, then writes the values back to their original addresses. 256 blocks per modulus. Every block launches 256 threads: 256 load/store values; 128 compute butterflies at each stage. 256 values · 2 KiB shared memory / block Pass 2 · near stages One block owns one row 128 → 64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1 These butterflies stay inside an aligned 256-value row. Each block loads its contiguous row into shared memory, executes eight stages, and writes it back. 256 blocks per modulus. All 256 threads load/store values; 128 compute butterflies at each stage. 256 values · 2 KiB shared memory / block A twiddle is the precomputed modular multiplier used by a butterfly. Both passes use twiddles from the same 65536-point transform . A barrier is a point where every thread in a block must wait for the others. Threads 128–255 still load, store and reach every barrier, even when they have no butterfly. 03 Resources & memory access The two launches use different views of the same flat array: 256 columns, then 256 rows. Each block owns 256 values and runs eight stages locally. Forward schedule per modulus · all blocks launch 256 threads Task / resource N = 65536 Polynomial storage 512 KiB Stages / total butterflies 16 / 524,288 Pass 1 blocks / values per block 256 / 256 Pass 1 shared memory per block 2 KiB Pass 1 loading / butterfly threads 0–255 / 0–127 Pass 1 global distances 32768 down to 256 · 8 stages Pass 2 blocks / values per block 256 / 256 Pass 2 shared memory per block 2 KiB Pass 2 loading / butterfly threads 0–255 / 0–127 Pass 2 global distances 128 down to 1 · 8 stages Read the memory access map Same threads, different global addresses Compare the two forward passes using the first four loading threads. The top boxes show global array indices; the bottom boxes show shared-memory slots. t0 means thread 0. Pass 1 · strided column loads 256 values apart · 2,048 bytes global[r + 256 × t] → shared[t] Strided global reads become adjacent shared-memory writes For the first column, threads 0, 1, 2 and 3 load global indices 0, 256, 512 and 768 into shared slots 0, 1, 2 and 3. Ellipses indicate skipped values; gaps are not drawn to scale. Global memory (VRAM) 0 256 512 768 … … … t0 t1 t2 t3 0 1 2 3 Shared slots · 8 bytes each Pass 2 · contiguous row loads 1 value apart · 8 bytes global[256 × b + t] → shared[t] Adjacent global reads stay adjacent in shared memory For the first row, threads 0, 1, 2 and 3 load consecutive global indices 0, 1, 2 and 3 into shared slots 0, 1, 2 and 3. The touching boxes show contiguous values. Global memory (VRAM) 0 1 2 3 t0 t1 t2 t3 0 1 2 3 Shared slots · 8 bytes each Load once → 8 stages in shared memory → Store to the same global addresses The diagram uses the first column (r=0) and first row (b=0). Each block loads 256 values; only four are shown. Gaps are schematic. Column reads are far apart in global memory. Row reads are adjacent, so the GPU can combine them into fewer memory transactions. When storing results, follow the arrows in reverse. In pass 1, adjacent loading threads access global addresses 2048 bytes apart (256 × 8 bytes), but write adjacent 8-byte entries in shared memory. The scatter reverses this mapping. In pass 2, adjacent threads read and write adjacent 8-byte global entries. Each block loads once, reuses shared memory across all its stages, and stores once. Two reads and two writes of the value array give a logical minimum of 4 × N × 8 bytes per forward transform: 2 MiB at N=65536, excluding twiddle/modulus reads and host transfers. This is requested array traffic, not measured DRAM traffic . Strided accesses, caches, registers, and occupancy need real-GPU profiling; 128 active butterfly threads out of 256 is not a hardware occupancy measurement. Source map: CUDA kernels and launchers define the grid and gather/scatter; the shared stage helper maps threads to pairs and applies the twiddle. Each stage touches every position once. 04 · D2 Why these inputs and twiddles? The default CUDA path now uses the OpenFHE-style one-multiply butterfly . The column and row passes still assign the same pairs to threads. What changed is how each pair gets its twiddle. 1. Read two slots, then write two results U is the first slot of the pair in the full tower array. d is the distance in slots at this stage, so its partner is V = U + d . The old values at U and V are a and b , respectively. \\[\\begin{aligned} t&=\\zeta b,\\\\ x&=a+t,\\\\ y&=a-t. \\end{aligned}\\] ζ (zeta) is the group's twiddle multiplier. Compute the temporary product t once, then write x at U and y at V. Both writes use the same old a. All value arithmetic is modulo q; the slot indices are ordinary integers. 2. Turn a slot index into a twiddle One butterfly group contains two halves of d slots each, so the group width is 2d . The two halves are paired offset by offset. \\[\\begin{aligned} m&=N/(2d),\\\\ g&=\\lfloor U/(2d)\\rfloor,\\\\ k&=m+g. \\end{aligned}\\] m counts the groups at this stage. N/d would count half-groups; divide by 2 to count whole groups. g is the zero-based group containing U: the floor brackets round down, so every slot in the same width-2d group gives the same g. k is the table index; this stage uses entries m through 2m−1. \\[ e=\\operatorname{rev}_{\\log_2 N}(k),\\qquad \\zeta=T[k]=\\psi^e. \\] T is the forward twiddle table. ψ (psi) is the tower's primitive 2N-th root modulo q: its powers first return to 1 at exponent 2N. e is an exponent, not a slot or table index. The notation \\(\\operatorname{rev}_{\\log_2 N}\\) means reverse exactly \\(\\log_2 N\\) binary digits, including leading zeros (16 digits here). This reverses the bits of k at table setup, not the coefficient array during the butterfly. Example: with N=65,536, d=256 and U=2,563, the group width is 512, m=128 and g=5 because 2,563 is between slots 2,560 and 3,071. Thus k=133, whose 16-bit reversal is 41,216. The pair is (2,563, 2,819), and it uses \\(T[133]=\\psi^{41216}\\). Why one twiddle is enough: view each group as a polynomial split into a low half \\(A(X)\\) and a high half \\(X^d B(X)\\). Its two child groups correspond to \\(X^d=\\zeta\\) and \\(X^d=-\\zeta\\). Substituting gives \\(A+\\zeta B\\) and \\(A-\\zeta B\\): each pair shares the same product \\(\\zeta b\\). At the first split, \\(\\zeta=\\psi^{N/2}\\), so \\(\\zeta^2=-1\\), as required by \\(X^N+1\\). The bit-reversed table stores the factor for each group in advance. There is no separate input twist pass and no multiply on the difference output. This follows the OpenFHE butterfly and group selection , not its CPU reduction routine. Find the pair in a column r is the column number. δ (delta) is the pair distance in shared-memory slots, \\(d/256\\), because each adjacent entry in a column is 256 slots apart in the tower array. p is the pair number in that column, equal to the active thread's index here. Dividing p by δ and rounding down gives g; the remainder j is the pair offset within that local group. \\[\\begin{aligned} u_{\\mathrm{local}}&=2\\delta g+j,\\\\ U&=256u_{\\mathrm{local}}+r,\\\\ V&=U+d. \\end{aligned}\\] The first line finds the upper shared-memory slot. The second maps it back to a full-array slot. Expanding gives \\(U=2dg+(256j+r)\\); the last term is smaller than d, so the column's local group number is also the global group number. Changing the column changes the addresses, not the twiddle for that group. N=65536 · column 3, thread 5 d U ↔ V m+g ψ exponent 32768 1283 ↔ 34051 1 32768 256 2563 ↔ 2819 133 41216 At the first stage, every pair uses ψ³²⁷⁶⁸, including column 3/thread 0. The legacy DIF derivation is kept separately for reference; it uses different intermediate values. 05 · D3 Undo the forward butterfly The inverse visits the same pairs in reverse stage order. It selects the reciprocal from the inverse table at the same index m+g. For forward outputs x=a+ζb and y=a−ζb, the inverse butterfly produces: \\[\\begin{aligned} x+y&=2a,\\\\ (x-y)\\zeta^{-1}&=2b. \\end{aligned}\\] Adding x and y cancels the two terms involving b. Subtracting gives twice ζb; multiplying by ζ⁻¹ , the modular reciprocal of ζ, cancels ζ because their product is 1 modulo q. Each of the \\(\\log_2 N\\) stages contributes a factor of two, for a total factor of N. The final kernel multiplies by N⁻¹ , the modular reciprocal of N for this tower, to cancel it. Forward runs columns then rows; inverse runs rows then columns then scaling. The inverse consumes the forward transform's bit-reversed evaluation order directly. The schedule simulator shares the CUDA stage helpers and checks forward arrays, independent inverse arrays and products against the CPU reference. With OpenFHE enabled, it also compares forward and independent inverse arrays directly with OpenFHE. These are host checks, not GPU measurements. 06 · D4 Why two passes equal the full transform The column proof handles far stages. The remaining kernels— forward_contiguous_tile_kernel and inverse_contiguous_tile_kernel —assign one contiguous tile to each block. For our target sizes, tile length L is 256; for smaller transforms it is min(256,N) . 1. Map a row thread to the CPU pair R is the row block number, L is its tile length, and p is the pair index ( threadIdx.x for active threads here). The rows are contiguous, so the local pair distance is the same d as in the full tower. \\[\\begin{aligned} g_{\\rm local}&=\\lfloor p/d\\rfloor,\\\\ j&=p\\bmod d. \\end{aligned}\\] The quotient selects the group inside this tile; the remainder selects the pair inside that group. Here “mod d” finds an index remainder, not a residue modulo the tower prime q. \\[\\begin{aligned} U&=LR+2d\\,g_{\\rm local}+j,\\\\ V&=U+d,\\\\ g&=LR/(2d)+g_{\\rm local}. \\end{aligned}\\] LR is the start slot of the tile; \\(2d\\,g_{\\rm local}\\) skips complete groups within it. Because 2d divides L, \\(LR/(2d)\\) is the number of complete groups before the tile. Adding the local group gives the global g used in \\(T[m+g]\\). For example, row 3 starts at 768; at d=128 it starts at global group 3, not group 0. A row is not an independent 256-point NTT. 2. Trace row 3, thread 5 Row 3, thread 5 at N=65536 d Global group U ↔ V Table index 128 3 773 ↔ 901 259 8 48 773 ↔ 781 4144 1 389 778 ↔ 779 33157 At N=65536, row 255/thread 127/distance 128 reaches the final coefficient: U=65407, V=65535 . The group is 255 and the table index is 511. Inverse uses that index in the reciprocal table. 3. Account for every stage exactly once The forward distances are the powers of two from N/2 down to 1, measured in full-array slots. For N=65,536 they split at 256: \\[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Far: }&d=32768,16384,\\ldots,256,\\\\\\text{Near: }&d=128,64,\\ldots,1.\\end{aligned}\\] Forward runs far distances descending, then near distances descending. Inverse reverses both passes and scales by the inverse of 65536. For other supported N≥512, far distances begin at N/2. There is no missing distance between 128 and 256: no power of two lies between them. Neither set overlaps the other. The far count is log₂N − 8 ; the near count is 8. Each stage has exactly N/2 disjoint butterflies, so the full count is (N/2) × log₂N per direction. Scaling is not an additional butterfly stage. N=65536 · forward and inverse share these stage counts N Far + near stages Total butterflies / direction 65536 8 + 8 = 16 524,288 At N≤256, the far pass is skipped and one tile covers all log₂N stages. The coverage check also exercises N=2, 64, 256 and 512, including the tile clamp and first column-pass boundary. 4. Why block-local stage fusion is safe During all far stages, every dependency stays inside the same column; during all near stages, every dependency stays inside the same tile. A block may therefore finish all its stages before another block finishes its first: those blocks do not consume each other's intermediate values within that pass. This reorders independent operations without changing the CPU result. Loading thread tid copies values[L*R + tid] into shared[tid] . Here tid is an index, not the butterfly product t. All threads reach the load barrier and every stage barrier. Threads 0…127 compute butterflies at L=256, while all 256 threads participate in loading, storing, and synchronization. The block stores its tile once after the last stage. Between passes, scatter must finish before the next gather. Same-stream kernel order provides that boundary. Gather/scatter changes block ownership; it does not transpose the stored array or perform another transform. 5. Executable evidence tile_mapping_check checks the shared pair geometry and the legacy twiddle lookup with tagged inputs. device_schedule_check exercises the default group-twiddle arithmetic on CPU using the same helper as CUDA. The test also checks the order of stages and confirms that each stage visits every required pair at N=65536. Small-size tests cover the points where the schedule changes. Other tests check real roots and the inverse’s final scale. These tests execute on CPU: they do not test GPU compilation, thread races, barriers or speed. cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure 07 Follow one thread An active thread computes one butterfly per stage and writes both results to shared memory. Its pair changes with the stage. Loading a value and computing a butterfly are separate assignments; a thread does not permanently own one coefficient. Keep block 3 and thread 5. Try distances 32768, 128, then 1. Try thread 200 to see the difference between load/store work and butterfly work. Polynomial size · one RNS limb N = 65536 Transform direction Forward Inverse Pass 1 · column blocks · butterfly distance Pass 2 · row blocks · butterfly distance Block index Thread index Global array positions · 256 rows × 256 columns Block-owned positions Upper input Lower input The matrix shows positions, not coefficient values . Contents change during the transform; the index layout stays the same. 08 What keeps the work ordered? Inside a block All 256 threads reach __syncthreads() after loading and after each stage—even threads without a butterfly. This barrier synchronizes only that block. Between the passes A CUDA stream is an ordered work queue. Both kernels use the same stream, so pass 2 starts after pass 1 finishes. Blocks inside one pass are independent. The GPU’s processors (SMs) run them as resources become available, rather than necessarily running every block at once. For CKKS with multiple RNS primes, repeat this arrangement per limb. Storage is limb-major: values[limb × N + index] . The zero-based limb skips whole towers; index is the slot inside that tower. blockIdx.y selects the limb, and blockIdx.x selects the column or row for the current pass. The mental model: a block owns a data subset; a thread computes a butterfly within it at each stage. Next, follow the constructor, buffers, and host/device dataflow (D5) . Next: CUDA buffers & dataflow →"}, {"title": "CUDA buffers & dataflow", "summary": "Follow polynomial values from CPU arrays to GPU buffers and back.", "group": "Learn", "url": "cuda-buffer-dataflow.html", "text": "CUDA buffers & dataflow A tower stores one polynomial’s remainders under one prime. Its 65,536 eight-byte values use 512 KiB; three towers use 1.5 MiB. One combined multiplication call uploads two inputs and downloads one result. Separate calls copy intermediate arrays between CPU and GPU. Read full notes Where the CUDA wrapper stores and moves data ¶ CudaNttTransform accepts arrays in CPU memory, called host vectors. Each call copies its inputs to GPU memory, runs GPU functions called kernels, and copies the result back before returning. This makes the interface synchronous. Its multiply() combines several operations in one call, keeping their intermediate arrays on the GPU. Separate calls copy those arrays back and forth. This page maps the interface and implementation . It describes the baseline wrapper, not the newer resident-handle API . Use the work-distribution explorer for the kernel schedule. The Ada report records native baseline evidence; later runtime changes need their own verification. The big picture: N=65536, three towers ¶ One CudaNttTransform fixes the polynomial length N and the ordered pairs \\((q_k,\\psi_k)\\) : a prime modulus and an NTT root for each tower. The caller supplies these parameters and packed host vectors . One instance can then process different polynomials with that same layout. What are the three towers? ¶ Write a polynomial as \\(a(x)=a_0+a_1x+\\cdots+a_{N-1}x^{N-1}\\) . In the Residue Number System (RNS), each coefficient modulo \\(Q=q_0q_1q_2\\) is represented by three remainders: \\[ a_i\\quad\\longrightarrow\\quad \\begin{pmatrix} a_i\\bmod q_0\\\\ a_i\\bmod q_1\\\\ a_i\\bmod q_2 \\end{pmatrix}. \\] A tower collects all N remainders for one prime. Three towers therefore represent one polynomial under three primes , not three ciphertexts or three unrelated messages. Each prime fits in a machine word even when their product Q does not. The NTT runs independently in each tower, using that tower's q and ψ. In a CKKS pipeline, a ciphertext commonly contains polynomial components such as c₀ and c₁; each component has its own towers. These buffers hold polynomial residues, not the user's decoded complex slots. This standalone wrapper takes the packed values from its caller; it does not encrypt data or unpack an OpenFHE ciphertext automatically. See the actual numbers in a small example ¶ For illustration only, take N=4 , primes 17, 41, 73 , and \\(a(x)=1-2x+20x^2+100x^3\\) . Reduce each coefficient separately. On small screens, scroll wide tables horizontally. \\(a_i\\) Mod 17 Mod 41 Mod 73 1 1 1 1 −2 15 39 71 20 3 20 20 100 15 18 27 For example, −2 becomes q−2 in each tower, while \\(100\\bmod41=18\\) . The wrapper receives only these nonnegative, canonical residues. Casting −2 to an unsigned integer is not the same operation as reducing it modulo q. Read down each prime column in the table, then concatenate the columns. The input is flattened tower by tower , not coefficient by coefficient: std::vector<uint64_t> values{ 1, 15, 3, 15, // tower 0: all four coefficients modulo 17 1, 39, 20, 18, // tower 1: all four coefficients modulo 41 1, 71, 20, 27 // tower 2: all four coefficients modulo 73 }; This array has \\(3\\times4=12\\) words, or \\(12\\times8=96\\) bytes. The example's roots can be [2, 3, 10] , respectively; each satisfies \\(\\psi_k^4=-1\\pmod{q_k}\\) . These small parameters illustrate storage, not the N=65536 workload. Actual primes and roots come from the chosen context; see the OpenFHE representation contract . In the first tower, forward NTT changes [1, 15, 3, 15] into [10, 16, 3, 9] . The first array contains coefficient residues; the second contains evaluations in bit-reversed order. Both still occupy four 8-byte words. The evaluation and inverse formulas explain what those new values mean. Scale the same layout to N=65536 ¶ Now use N=65536 and K=3 . Here K counts prime towers in one polynomial. For tower k and position i: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{word index}&=kN+i,\\\\ \\text{byte offset}&=8(kN+i). \\end{aligned} \\] Tower Word range Byte offset 0 (q₀) 0…65535 0 1 (q₁) 65536…131071 524288 2 (q₂) 131072…196607 1048576 In coefficient format, values[N + 5] is coefficient 5 modulo q₁. After forward NTT, the same location holds evaluation position 5 of that tower in bit-reversed order. The values change meaning, but the array still has K×N entries. Inverse returns coefficient format. Ring multiplication also returns N coefficients per tower because the result is reduced modulo \\(x^N+1\\) . Every entry uses uint64_t : 64 bits ÷ 8 bits per byte = 8 bytes . A 50- or 60-bit modulus does not shrink this implementation's storage word. Therefore: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{One tower}&=65536\\times8\\\\ &=524288\\ \\text{bytes}=512\\ \\text{KiB},\\\\[0.4em] \\text{One polynomial}&=3\\times65536\\times8\\\\ &=1572864\\ \\text{bytes}=1.5\\ \\text{MiB}. \\end{aligned} \\] KiB means 1024 bytes; MiB means \\(1024^2=1048576\\) bytes. The polynomial occupies one contiguous buffer containing all three towers. A host vector and its GPU copy have the same numeric payload, but live in separate memory. What else is stored besides the polynomial? ¶ The wrapper allocates two operand buffers , A and B, for two different polynomial inputs. Each buffer contains all three towers and is 1.5 MiB. A forward-only call uses A; the instance still owns both allocations. During fused multiplication, A and B hold inputs, then evaluations; the pointwise product and final inverse result overwrite A. Four more arrays contain parameters and precomputed constants: Forward twiddles: powers of each tower's ψ, used by the butterflies. The default build stores the first N entries in bit-reversed exponent order. Inverse twiddles: corresponding powers of ψ-inverse for the reverse transform. Moduli: one q per tower, so every arithmetic operation reduces by the correct prime. This is [q₀, q₁, q₂] , not the composite product Q. Inverse lengths: one \\(N^{-1}\\bmod q_k\\) per tower for final normalization. These are modular inverses, not floating-point 1/N values. These constants depend on N, primes, roots and the selected transform schedule, not on the input coefficients. The table builder and constructor show their actual generation and uploads. There is no separate GPU root array; the roots are already encoded in the twiddle tables. For the small N=4 example, the moduli array is [17, 41, 73] and the inverse lengths are [13, 31, 55] , since each value times 4 is 1 modulo its prime. The first tower, with q=17 and ψ=2, has: forward table: [1, 4, 2, 8, 16] inverse table: [1, 13, 9, 15, 16] The first four forward exponents are 0, 2, 1, 3 . The last entry is \\(\\psi^4\\bmod17=16\\) . This shows why these arrays contain constants , not another copy of the coefficient data. Derive the six-buffer total ¶ The current table allocation retains N+1 words per tower in both build variants: N indexed entries plus the endpoint \\(\\psi^N\\) (or its inverse-root counterpart). That extra entry is a table convention, not an extra coefficient. Each complete twiddle array uses: \\[ \\begin{aligned} 3\\times(65536+1)&=196611\\ \\text{words},\\\\ 196611\\times8&=1572888\\ \\text{bytes}. \\end{aligned} \\] Buffer Count Bytes each Operands A/B 2 1,572,864 Twiddle tables 2 1,572,888 Moduli 1 24 Inverse lengths 1 24 Each small array has three words: \\(3\\times8=24\\) bytes. Add those six allocations: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{Operands}&=2\\times1572864=3145728\\ \\mathrm B,\\\\ \\text{Twiddles}&=2\\times1572888=3145776\\ \\mathrm B,\\\\ \\text{Small constants}&=24+24=48\\ \\mathrm B,\\\\ \\text{Total}&=6291552\\ \\mathrm B. \\end{aligned} \\] That is 6 MiB plus 96 bytes , not the size of one polynomial. At setup, only the four constant arrays are uploaded: \\(3145776+48=3145824\\) bytes. A and B receive coefficient/evaluation data later, when an operation uploads its host inputs. The sizes below count requested GPU buffer payloads; host objects, CUDA context/allocator overhead and per-block shared memory are separate. ① Construct once Allocate six GPU buffers: 6,291,552 B total. Upload four constant arrays: 3,145,824 B, four copies. ② Reuse for each call Host inputs → GPU A/B → kernels → host result. A and B are each 1.5 MiB. Constants stay on the GPU. ③ Destroy Free all six allocations after successful construction. CPU vectors and GPU allocations have separate ownership. Constants and scratch allocations persist across calls. Operand contents are scratch state and are overwritten by the next call. Only the internal chain inside multiply() remains device-resident between its arithmetic operations. Calling public forward() , pointwise_multiply() , and inverse() separately does not preserve a transfer-free pipeline. Constructor: what happens, in order ¶ Create the uniquely owned Impl ; pointers start null. Validate the supported size, prime/root pairs and checked buffer extents before CUDA calls. Build CPU references, bit-reversed forward and reciprocal twiddle tables, and N-inverse for each tower. Set tile length min(256,N) and column length N/256 . Capture the current device, allocate six persistent buffers and upload four constant arrays. Record launch geometry: 256 threads per block, max(1,N/256) row blocks per tower and ceil(K*N/256) elementwise blocks. The raw CPU NTT constructor assumes prime q. The CUDA wrapper adds prime/range, root and extent checks in cuda_validation.hpp . Temporary host power tables are released after setup; the moduli and CPU references remain. Persistent allocation and ownership map ¶ To shorten the formulas, use S for one operand’s bytes, T for one twiddle table’s bytes, and R for one small constant array’s bytes. At N=65536/K=3, S=1,572,864 B, T=1,572,888 B and R=24 B. In general, \\(S=8KN,\\ T=8K(N+1),\\ R=8K\\) bytes. Every stored element is uint64_t . Device allocation Elements / bytes Layout and role Writer / reader device_operand_a 196,608 / 1,572,864 B [limb][coefficient or evaluation position] ; working input/output Public input copy and kernels write; result copy reads device_operand_b 196,608 / 1,572,864 B Second operand; separate from A Input copy and its forward transform write; pointwise kernel reads device_twiddle_powers 196,611 / 1,572,888 B [limb][table index] , \\(\\psi^{\\operatorname{rev}(i)}\\) Constructor uploads; forward kernels read device_inverse_twiddle_powers 196,611 / 1,572,888 B [limb][table index] , \\((\\psi^{-1})^{\\operatorname{rev}(i)}\\) Constructor uploads; inverse kernels read device_moduli 3 / 24 B q for each limb Constructor uploads; arithmetic kernels read device_inverse_transform_lengths 3 / 24 B N-inverse modulo each q Constructor uploads; final scale kernel reads CudaNttTransform keeps its implementation in a unique_ptr<Impl> , which ensures one owner. Inside Impl , SetupResources<CudaSetupRuntime> tracks the six GPU allocations and releases them. The named pointers refer to those privately owned buffers; callers cannot keep direct buffer views. The transform object cannot be copied or moved. Use an instance from only one caller at a time, because its methods overwrite the same A/B buffers without locks or separate scratch space. On destruction or constructor failure, the allocation owner releases acquired pointers in reverse order while selecting/restoring their owning device. Preflight rejection has no allocations to free. A cleanup trace records failures, but the nonthrowing destructor cannot report them to its caller. Failed device selection skips the free. Concurrent use or destruction is not supported. Exact byte counts (requested allocations, not measured device usage) ¶ \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{Operands}&=2S=3{,}145{,}728\\ \\mathrm{B},\\\\ \\text{Constants}&=2T+2R=3{,}145{,}824\\ \\mathrm{B},\\\\ \\text{All six}&=2S+2T+2R=6{,}291{,}552\\ \\mathrm{B}. \\end{aligned} \\] For three limbs , the source formulas give: N One operand S Both operands Constants / constructor upload All six allocations 65536 1,572,864 B (1.5 MiB) 3,145,728 B 3,145,824 B 6,291,552 B The extra 96 bytes comprise 48 bytes for the two power tables' final entries and 48 bytes for the moduli and inverse lengths. CUDA context/allocator overhead, registers, per-block shared memory, host vectors, and CPU-reference objects are not included. These are formulas, not a VRAM measurement or a memory-capacity benchmark. Public API contracts ¶ For operands, flat index k*N+i belongs to limb k. Power-table index k*(N+1)+e selects table position e of that limb. For e<N, its exponent is bit-reversed in the default build and e in the legacy build; position N holds the retained endpoint. Transform kernels select limbs with blockIdx.y . Pointwise and scale kernels instead use a flat grid over K*N entries and recover the limb by integer division by N. Public call Input → output representation Host-visible effect Copies / launches at N≥512 forward(values) Natural coefficients → bit-reversed evaluations Mutates host vector H2D A, column + row, D2H A: 2 copies, 2 launches inverse(values) Bit-reversed evaluations → natural coefficients Mutates host vector H2D A, row + column + scale, D2H A: 2 copies, 3 launches pointwise_multiply(left,right) Matching bit-reversed evaluations → same order Replaces left; right is a const input H2D A+B, pointwise, D2H A: 3 copies, 1 launch multiply(left,right) Natural coefficients → natural negacyclic product Left is passed by value; returns its result buffer H2D A+B, 2 forwards + pointwise + inverse, D2H A: 3 copies, 8 launches Each input must contain exactly K×N values. Values in tower k must be from 0 to q[k]−1. Before uploading, the methods check the length and every value, then select the GPU that owns the buffers. They do not record whether an array currently contains coefficients or evaluations. The caller must choose the correct operation and supply matching primes and roots. An array made under a different prime set can pass these checks if its length and values happen to fit; the wrapper does not track where it came from. n() and limbs() return scalar geometry without a device copy. q(k) and ref(k) use bounds-checked host vectors; ref(k) is a borrowed const reference valid only while the transform object exists. These getters do not expose the GPU allocation or transfer data. Follow a complete multiplication ¶ HOST · input left: 196,608 residues right: 196,608 residues ↓ H2D · 2 copies · 3 MiB total GPU · reusable A and B A · 1.5 MiB Coefficients ↓ column stages · 256 blocks/tower ↓ row stages · 256 blocks/tower Bit-reversed evaluations B · 1.5 MiB Coefficients ↓ column stages · 256 blocks/tower ↓ row stages · 256 blocks/tower Bit-reversed evaluations ↓ A × B, write A · 1 kernel · 768 blocks A · product evaluations ↓ row DIT → column DIT → inverse scale 3 kernels; result: 196,608 coefficient residues Constants stay here: forward powers 1,572,888 B + inverse powers 1,572,888 B + moduli 24 B + inverse lengths 24 B. ↓ D2H · 1 copy · 1.5 MiB HOST · returned left 196,608 residues · 1.5 MiB · natural coefficient order In the diagram, H2D means CPU-to-GPU and D2H means GPU-to-CPU. A block is a group of threads that can share a small memory area. For this three-tower example, each transform launch has a 256×3 grid, 256 threads per block, and 2 KiB shared memory per block. The pointwise and scale grids each have 768 blocks. A complete multiply uses eight launches and three operand copies, totaling 4,718,592 B (4.5 MiB) . The four constant buffers are read throughout this chain; they are not re-uploaded per operation. After the call, B is temporary working storage. A later call cannot assume it still contains a reusable input. Every multiply() uploads both inputs again. Passing an lvalue as left creates a host copy; passing a moved vector can transfer its host allocation into the by-value parameter instead. Each public method uses cudaMemcpy , not cudaMemcpyAsync , and returns the downloaded host result. Launches specify no explicit stream; there is no public stream/event parameter, asynchronous completion handle, or device-resident buffer API. cudaGetLastError() follows launches; copies are also checked by CHECK_CUDA . There is no automatic CPU fallback on a CUDA error. Transfer boundary: fused versus separate calls ¶ Excluding construction, each forward/inverse transfers 2S bytes and pointwise or fused multiply transfers 3S bytes. For a fair comparison of two inputs : Route for two input polynomials Copies Requested transfer payload Kernel launches Four public calls: forward A, forward B, pointwise, inverse 9 14,155,776 B (13.5 MiB) 8 One multiply(A,B) call 3 4,718,592 B (4.5 MiB) 8 Both paths perform eight kernels at the target sizes. Fusion removes six operand-sized transfer copies (9S→3S), not six kernels. At N=65536/K=3 this is 14,155,776 B versus 4,718,592 B of requested H2D+D2H payload. It is not a measured latency speedup. Host vector copies, allocator costs and PCIe/cache effects are separate. At N≤256 forward uses one kernel, inverse two and multiply five; the host-copy counts are unchanged. The benchmark wraps public calls in wall-clock timing, so its GPU columns include operand transfers. Construction/table uploads occur outside that per-call region; see run_bench in the implementation. It is not kernel-only timing. Open ownership and integration issues ¶ Constructor cleanup: the original raw-Impl ownership gap is repaired with unique_ptr . Partial device state now reaches the cleanup destructor during unwinding. Shared ownership logic now has host failure tests and a prepared native pre-call injection probe; neither establishes real driver OOM, DMA or context-error safety. Native execution of the new probe remains required; host rejection tests do not prove CUDA cleanup under a failed runtime/context. The wrapper captures its owning device and guards operations/destruction. Native wrong-device, restore-failure and cleanup-error tests are still open. No stream/event lifetime contract, buffer view, representation/level tag, evaluation-key cache, or OpenFHE Q/P base-switch integration exists in this interface. Reusable allocations are not the final integration API. The wrapper compiles and links for sm_89/sm_120 with stock CUDA 13.0. The older Ada baseline passed its selected native checks; current source and failed-context behavior still require the recorded native acceptance gates. D9's backend proposal , accepted by D10's ADR , specifies these ownership/API decisions; the production device API and baseline RAII changes still require explicit native failure/lifetime tests. The D6 arithmetic review examines arithmetic bounds, parameter assumptions, and adversarial exactness. Verification and limits ¶ Source-contract tests check that this map matches current allocation sizes, frees, public copy directions, operation ordering and host-only interface shape. They also pin the N=65536 arithmetic above. These tests read the source text; they do not execute CUDA. They can catch a mismatch between the document and code, but cannot prove that cleanup works after a real GPU failure. Existing CTests verify CPU arithmetic and shared stage helpers. No new claim of actual GPU support/performance is made by completing D5. Next: Resident operation chain →"}, {"title": "Arithmetic bounds & tests", "summary": "Learn why stored values and temporary products need different integer widths.", "group": "Learn", "url": "modular-arithmetic-contract.html", "text": "Arithmetic bounds & tests Canonical residues are integers from 0 to q−1. Each butterfly reduces its results back into that range. A 60-bit value fits in a 64-bit word, but two such values can produce a 120-bit product. Independent calculations check that the code preserves it. Read full notes Keep every modular operation exact ¶ For a prime q, a canonical residue is an integer from 0 to q−1. For example, −1 is stored as 16 modulo 17. Every butterfly in this implementation starts and ends with canonical values. The main target is N=65536 coefficients with 50–60-bit primes. Each stored value uses uint64_t , an unsigned 64-bit integer. One 60-bit value fits, but the product of two can need 120 bits. This page explains the temporary widths, input assumptions and independent checks. Read the buffer map for where arrays live. Sources: mod_arith.hpp , ntt.hpp and dev_core.hpp . 1. What callers must supply ¶ The notation \\([0,q)\\) includes 0 and excludes q: it means integers 0, 1, …, q−1. These small arithmetic functions run inside the main loops, so they assume their inputs are in range instead of checking every call. Operation Required input Output add_mod(a,b,q) Canonical a,b; \\(0<q<2^{63}\\) Canonical \\((a+b)\\bmod q\\) sub_mod(a,b,q) Canonical a,b; \\(0<q<2^{63}\\) Canonical \\((a-b)\\bmod q\\) mul_mod(a,b,q) Canonical a,b; \\(0<q<2^{64}\\) Canonical \\(ab\\bmod q\\) pow_mod(a,e,q) Any uint64_t a,e; q>0 Canonical \\(a^e\\bmod q\\) ; q=1 returns 0 inv_mod(x,q) Prime q≥2; x not divisible by q Canonical inverse via \\(x^{q-2}\\) Add/sub retain their documented conservative bound; this review does not expand the public contract. Multiplication's wider bound does not make a full transform with q≥2^63 part of the supported contract. For a transform, also require a power-of-two N≥2, prime q with \\(2N\\mid(q-1)\\) , and the same primitive 2N-th root ψ as the comparison implementation. At the target N, \\(2N=131072\\) and q>N. Forward consumes canonical coefficients in natural order; inverse consumes canonical evaluations in bit-reversed order. The code stores ordinary residues. It does not use Montgomery encoding (an alternative arithmetic representation) or lazy reduction (temporarily allowing values above q−1). The raw CPU NegacyclicNtt constructor checks size, divisibility and ψᴺ=q−1, but assumes primality and the documented width. Its array methods check length, not residue bounds. The CUDA wrapper and resident runtime add their own validation; do not assume the raw hot arithmetic functions reject invalid inputs. 2. Why intermediates fit ¶ For canonical a,b: Addition: \\(0\\le a+b\\le 2q-2\\) . One subtraction is sufficient. The code forms the sum in 128 bits; after subtraction it is below q and fits uint64_t. Subtraction: if a≥b, use a-b. Otherwise \\(0\\le a+(q-b)<q\\) ; the selected branch therefore cannot overflow uint64_t. Do not compute unsigned a-b first and assume wraparound implements reduction modulo q. Multiplication: \\(0\\le ab\\le(q-1)^2<2^{128}\\) . The cast happens before multiplication, preserving the full product; remainder produces a value below q. Multiplying in uint64_t first would lose the high half. At q<2^60, the product is below 2^120 and a sum below 2^61. Storage in uint64_t is safe, but a uint64_t product is still insufficient. Exponentiation reduces a before its first multiply. Each multiplication preserves canonical range. A uint64_t exponent takes at most 64 loop iterations. Even e=0 computes 1 % q , so q=0 is never legal. These are bounds for the current exact-remainder code. Barrett, Shoup, Montgomery, 32-bit limbs, or lazy reduction will need their own proofs, including quotient error, carries, temporary width, and final correction. 3. The invariant through every butterfly ¶ There are two things to preserve: what each value means and how it is stored . A stage must compute the correct partial polynomial evaluations, and every stored word must remain in \\([0,q)\\) . The negacyclic merge equations explain both. All equations in this section use arithmetic modulo q. Split a negacyclic polynomial into two smaller problems ¶ Start with \\(a(x)\\) modulo \\(x^N+1\\) . Set \\(d=N/2\\) and split its coefficient array into low and high halves: \\[ \\begin{aligned} a(x)&=U(x)+x^dV(x),\\\\ U(x)&=\\sum_{i=0}^{d-1}a_i x^i,\\\\ V(x)&=\\sum_{i=0}^{d-1}a_{i+d}x^i. \\end{aligned} \\] Because \\(\\psi^N=-1\\) , choose \\(\\alpha=\\psi^d\\) , so \\(\\alpha^2=-1\\) . The original modulus factors as \\[ x^N+1=(x^d-\\alpha)(x^d+\\alpha). \\] Inside the first smaller ring, \\(x^d=\\alpha\\) ; inside the second, \\(x^d=-\\alpha\\) . Substitution gives the negacyclic merge : \\[ \\begin{aligned} a(x)\\bmod(x^d-\\alpha)&=U(x)+\\alpha V(x),\\\\ a(x)\\bmod(x^d+\\alpha)&=U(x)-\\alpha V(x). \\end{aligned} \\] For each coefficient pair \\(u=a_i\\) , \\(v=a_{i+d}\\) , the two output words are therefore \\(u+\\alpha v\\) and \\(u-\\alpha v\\) . One multiplication is shared by the plus and minus branches: this is a butterfly. The minus sign comes from the two factors of the negacyclic modulus, not from an extra array operation. Repeat the same merge through all 16 stages ¶ At a later stage, a block of 2d coefficients represents a remainder modulo \\(x^{2d}-\\beta\\) . Choose the appropriate \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha^2=\\beta\\) . The same identity applies: \\[ x^{2d}-\\beta=(x^d-\\alpha)(x^d+\\alpha). \\] The block splits into its two child remainders \\(U+\\alpha V\\) and \\(U-\\alpha V\\) . This is the meaning invariant : each block is still the original polynomial reduced modulo its current factor. At d=1, each factor is linear, \\(x-z\\) , and its remainder is exactly \\(a(z)\\) . For the default shared CPU/CUDA stage helper , let \\(m=N/(2d)\\) be the number of groups, \\(g\\in[0,m)\\) the group index, and \\(\\operatorname{rev}_L\\) reverse L bits, where \\(L=\\log_2N\\) . Its group twiddle is \\[ \\alpha_{m,g}=\\psi^{\\operatorname{rev}_L(m+g)}\\bmod q. \\] One twiddle applies to every coefficient pair in that group. As d runs from N/2 down to 1, these choices select the successive factors above. At the end, array position k holds \\[ a\\!\\left(\\psi^{2\\operatorname{rev}_L(k)+1}\\right)\\bmod q. \\] Thus the transform produces evaluations at the roots of \\(x^N+1\\) , in bit-reversed order. It keeps N words throughout; the intermediate words are partial remainders, not final evaluations yet. See the exact evaluation formulas for the direct sum that these butterflies compute. Keep the stored values canonical, and reverse the merge ¶ The default forward butterfly implements those child remainders with canonical operations: \\[ \\begin{aligned} t&=\\operatorname{mul\\_mod}(\\alpha,v,q),\\\\ p&=\\operatorname{add\\_mod}(u,t,q),\\\\ r&=\\operatorname{sub\\_mod}(u,t,q). \\end{aligned} \\] The inputs u, v and table entry α are in \\([0,q)\\) . Multiplication reduces the full-width product before storing t; addition and subtraction reduce before storing p and r. Consequently each output is again in \\([0,q)\\) , even though an unreduced sum could reach 2q−2 or a difference could be negative. This proves the range invariant by induction over all 16 stages. To reconstruct the parent, add and subtract its two child remainders: \\[ \\begin{aligned} p+r&\\equiv2u\\pmod q,\\\\ (p-r)\\alpha^{-1}&\\equiv2v\\pmod q. \\end{aligned} \\] The inverse helper forms a canonical difference before multiplying: \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\delta&=\\operatorname{sub\\_mod}(p,r,q),\\\\ \\widetilde u&=\\operatorname{add\\_mod}(p,r,q),\\\\ \\widetilde v&=\\operatorname{mul\\_mod}(\\delta,\\alpha^{-1},q). \\end{aligned} \\] Here \\(\\widetilde u\\) and \\(\\widetilde v\\) represent 2u and 2v modulo q. The helper postpones division by 2: every reversed stage contributes a factor 2, so 16 stages contribute \\(2^{16}=N\\) . One final multiplication by \\(N^{-1}\\bmod q\\) recovers the original coefficients. All these stored intermediates are canonical too. Pointwise multiplication preserves the same range; its evaluation products correspond to polynomial multiplication modulo \\(x^N+1\\) . A complete four-word example ¶ For illustration, use N=4, q=17 and ψ=2. Start with canonical coefficients [1, 15, 3, 15] , representing \\(1-2x+3x^2-2x^3\\) modulo 17. Forward, default group-twiddle schedule: d=2, alpha=4: [1, 15, 3, 15] -> [13, 7, 6, 6] d=1, alphas=2, 8: [13, 7, 6, 6] -> [10, 16, 3, 9] Inverse, without the final scale yet: d=1, inverses=9,15: [10, 16, 3, 9] -> [9, 14, 12, 12] d=2, inverse=13: [9, 14, 12, 12] -> [4, 9, 12, 9] multiply by 4^-1=13 modulo 17: -> [1, 15, 3, 15] For example, the first forward pair is \\(t=4\\times3\\bmod17=12\\) , \\(p=(1+12)\\bmod17=13\\) , \\(r=(1-12)\\bmod17=6\\) . Every displayed word stays between 0 and 16. The final values evaluate the polynomial at [2, 15, 8, 9] , the odd powers of ψ in bit-reversed order. N=4 only makes the arithmetic visible; the project workload remains N=65536. How the CPU reference folds the same negacyclic twist into its butterflies The CPU reference and optional legacy CUDA build use a different intermediate layout. Write \\(b_i=\\psi^i a_i\\) and \\(\\omega=\\psi^2\\) . The negacyclic evaluation sum becomes a cyclic sum: \\[ a(\\psi^{2j+1})=\\sum_i a_i\\psi^{(2j+1)i} =\\sum_i b_i\\omega^{ji}. \\] The reference carries the factors \\(\\psi^i\\) implicitly rather than storing a separate twisted array. For a pair at local offset i and distance d, write the implicit inputs as \\(\\psi^iu\\) and \\(\\psi^{i+d}v\\) . With \\(c=\\psi^d\\) and \\(w=\\psi^{iN/d}\\) , a cyclic DIF split factors as \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\psi^iu+\\psi^{i+d}v&=\\psi^i(u+cv),\\\\ (\\psi^iu-\\psi^{i+d}v)w&=\\psi^i(u-cv)w. \\end{aligned} \\] After leaving the common \\(\\psi^i\\) implicit, the stored pair is \\(p=(u+cv)\\bmod q\\) , \\(r=(u-cv)w\\bmod q\\) . The reverse butterfly computes \\(t=rw^{-1}\\bmod q\\) , then \\((p+t)\\bmod q\\) and \\((p-t)c^{-1}\\bmod q\\) , giving 2u and 2v. It also postpones normalization until the final N-inverse multiply. For the example above, the reference's first-stage array is [13, 7, 6, 7] , while the default helper's is [13, 7, 6, 6] . Their final evaluations are identical. Do not require intermediate arrays from these two schedules to match. Each schedule preserves its own partial-transform meaning and the same canonical range. There is no stage where values are intentionally in \\([0,2q)\\) or \\([0,4q)\\) . The upstream native forward NTT uses lazy intermediate arithmetic. Feeding lazy OpenFHE/internal buffers directly into this interface without an explicit representation contract would violate its assumptions. What arithmetic work is actually counted ¶ N=65536 has \\(32768\\times16=524288\\) butterflies per direction and per tower. The CPU reference uses three modular multiplications per butterfly: two for data and one to advance the twiddle. The optional legacy device schedule uses two, reading precomputed twiddles instead. The default group-twiddle helper uses one, as in the merge equations above. Thus their loop bodies perform 1,572,864, 1,048,576 and 524,288 multiplications respectively; inverse adds 65,536 normalization multiplies per tower in each schedule. Setup powers/table generation are additional work. The old arithmetic-header comment counted the CPU loop incorrectly; D6 fixes that comment without changing the algorithms. __uint128_t remainder is the correctness baseline. The CUDA compiler may translate this remainder expression into many instructions. Inspect the generated GPU code and profile it; one source expression does not imply one hardware division instruction. 4. Executable adversarial coverage ¶ Two required checks, called gates, run in the ordinary CPU CTest suite. They use difficult boundary inputs as well as normal examples: Gate Inputs and comparison What it establishes arithmetic_bigint_check 14,956 compiled results versus Python arbitrary-precision addition, subtraction, multiplication, power and modular inverse Exact host primitive answers, not a copy of the same C++ reduction expression arithmetic_transform_check N=65536 at generated 50-, 55-, 60-bit primes; six patterns in both coefficient/evaluation roundtrips Canonical forward/inverse results and two-sided roundtrip identity Same transform gate \\(x^{N-1}\\cdot x\\) must produce q-1 at index 0 and zero elsewhere Closed-form negacyclic wrap sign, not merely roundtrip consistency Primitive inputs include 0, 1, 2, q/2, q-2, q-1, values around 2^32, and 256 seeded random pairs per modulus. Moduli include small, even, composite, 50/55/60-bit boundaries, just below 2^63, and full-width uint64_t boundaries. The latter use mul/pow only, respecting the add/sub contract. Exponents include 0, 1, 2, 63, 64 and UINT64_MAX; pow also receives an unreduced base. Inverse checks small and generated 50/55/60-bit primes against Python's modular-inverse algorithm, and confirms the host exception for zero/multiples of q. Parameter generation is reused; this is not an independent primality certification test. The six transform inputs are all zero, all q−1, alternating zero/q−1, one nonzero final coefficient, increasing values, and repeatable pseudorandom values. A forward/inverse round trip may hide matching mistakes in the two directions. Independent evaluation checks are also needed to establish the required order and OpenFHE compatibility. cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build -j 8 ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure ctest --test-dir build -R 'arithmetic_' -V Sources: C++ probe and full-size tests , independent primitive oracle . 5. Exactness ladder: do not substitute one gate for another ¶ Existing gate Scope Limit unit_tests Small arithmetic and NTT product against schoolbook Schoolbook shares arithmetic primitives oracle_crosscheck N=1024 forward arrays against direct Python NTT sum, plus CRT and identity Not a full-size GPU test Column/inverse/tile mapping checks Exhaustive target-size index/twiddle probes of shared CPU/CUDA helpers CPU execution; no CUDA barriers/races tested device_schedule_check Exact simulator/reference arrays, including N=65536 fixtures Current fixtures use 46-bit primes openfhe_exact_compat (opt-in) N=65536 NativePoly forward/inverse arrays, including independent evaluation input; OpenFHE selects parameters via FirstPrime(50) and LastPrime(59) Pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1 CPU comparison, not a CUDA/OpenFHE pipeline Native CUDA oracle/reference gates (when CUDA is available) Actual kernel outputs versus reference/oracle Run on named hardware; see the dated Ada report for exercised cases The OpenFHE gate includes zero, all q-1, ramp, random coefficients and random evaluation arrays. Same q and root are essential: another valid root may give a different evaluation permutation. The D7 contract freezes the representation and tower boundary. CKKS approximate decryption is not a replacement for these exact per-index comparisons. 6. Deferred adversarial checks and unsafe inputs ¶ D6's review is complete; these are explicit later implementation gates: P2/P3, real GPU: repeat the 50/55/60-bit patterns through actual kernels, compare each forward/inverse/pointwise result and canonical range, then run compute-sanitizer race/memory checks and test launch failures. CPU success does not establish device reduction correctness or timing for a new variant. API hardening: D9/D10 define the contracts. Later offline preparation adds baseline rejection of noncanonical residues, invalid roots, composite moduli, unsafe dimensions and vector lengths, with host negative tests. Runtime format/provenance tags, device failure paths and the production resident API remain later gates; current APIs do not reject every possible misuse. Parameter helpers: find_primes currently assumes safe shifts and a nonzero, nonoverflowing 2N . Invalid bit counts can cause undefined shifts; find_psi assumes a compatible prime q>3 and has no attempt limit. These helpers are not hardened untrusted-input parsers. Raw primitive misuse: q=0 causes invalid remainder/division operations; it is not a test input to execute. Composite q violates Fermat inverse's premise even if x is invertible: for example q=15,x=2 returns 2, not 8. Host zero-input inverse throws; the device branch returns 0 and is not a supported inverse/error-reporting API. Device kernels currently never invert. Future reduction optimization: exercise maximum correction counts, carry/borrow transitions, high-word products and every accepted lazy range against the independent bigint gate before allowing an optimized dispatch. These gaps do not block the D6 source-review deliverable. They do block claims of a hardened production API or GPU/OpenFHE integration. The D8 Hybrid graph and D9 buffer/stream/cache contract are complete. D10's ADR closes P1's design gate; native hardening and implementation are tracked in the backlog . Next: OpenFHE representation →"}, {"title": "OpenFHE representation", "summary": "Match the values, prime parameters and array order used by OpenFHE.", "group": "Learn", "url": "openfhe-representation-contract.html", "text": "OpenFHE representation OpenFHE keeps one array per prime, called a tower. Preserve each tower’s prime, root and position when copying its numbers. Forward transforms coefficients into evaluations in bit-reversed index order. Changing the format label alone does not transform the values. Read full notes Match OpenFHE's polynomial representation ¶ A GPU result must match both the values and their positions in OpenFHE’s arrays. This page specifies how to store and interpret those numbers for OpenFHE 1.6.1 with 64-bit native integers and N=65536 coefficients. The pinned commit is b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f . A NativePoly stores one polynomial modulo one prime. A DCRTPoly stores several such arrays, called towers, with different primes. Format says whether an array contains coefficients or NTT evaluations. Preserve each tower's prime, root, format and position when moving it to the GPU. The executable checks use the real OpenFHE core library on CPU. Read the Hybrid graph after this contract. OpenFHE's public aliases resolve to PolyImpl and DCRTPolyImpl in the source. Linked excerpts are © 2022 OpenFHE, under BSD 2-Clause . 1. The boundary in one table ¶ Property Required contract Ring \\(\\mathbb Z_q[x]/(x^N+1)\\) , \\(N=65536\\) ; cyclotomic order \\(m=2N=131072\\) Coefficient index i Coefficient of \\(x^i\\) , in natural order Evaluation memory index k Evaluation at \\(\\psi^{2\\operatorname{rev}_{16}(k)+1}\\) , with 16-bit index reversal Tower modulus q The exact modulus in that tower's OpenFHE parameters Root ψ The exact primitive 2N-th root in those same parameters Residue representation Unsigned canonical values in \\([0,q)\\) ; no external twist or Montgomery conversion Forward Natural coefficients → bit-reversed evaluations, in place Inverse Bit-reversed evaluations → natural coefficients; N-inverse normalization included GPU buffer order values[limb*N + index] , preserving the supplied tower list In the table, a ring means that coefficients are reduced modulo q and powers are reduced using x^N=−1. A canonical residue is the stored remainder from 0 to q−1. A primitive 2N-th root is a modular number whose powers first return to 1 after 2N steps. The evaluation positions here are not the complex-number slots seen by a CKKS user . Encoding and decoding between user values and polynomial coefficients are separate operations, outside this page’s scope. 2. What each format means ¶ OpenFHE's Format enum names COEFFICIENT and EVALUATION . The label alone does not express index ordering. For the power-of-two ring, NativePoly::SwitchFormat calls ForwardTransformToBitReverseInPlace or InverseTransformFromBitReverseInPlace . The NTT's evaluation array is therefore bit-reversed even though its public format is simply EVALUATION . Read the format-to-transform dispatch OpenFHE · poly-impl.h:467–473 if (m_format != Format::COEFFICIENT) { m_format = Format::COEFFICIENT; ChineseRemainderTransformFTT<VecType>().InverseTransformFromBitReverseInPlace(ru, co, &(*m_values)); return; } m_format = Format::EVALUATION; ChineseRemainderTransformFTT<VecType>().ForwardTransformToBitReverseInPlace(ru, co, &(*m_values)); The two branches call different transform entry points; changing the format is an operation on the array. Our reference and CUDA entry points do not store an OpenFHE format flag. The caller must know the current domain before invoking forward() or inverse() . Calling forward() twice transforms the numbers twice; it does not simply ensure that the array has an evaluation label. No explicit input twist or output permutation is needed at this boundary. The negacyclic twist is already folded into the butterfly schedule. Ordinary bit reversal changes an index , not byte order or the bits of a residue. 3. Exact evaluation and inverse formulas ¶ Work with one tower : one polynomial modulo one prime q. The input array \\([a_0,a_1,\\ldots,a_{N-1}]\\) stores the coefficients of \\(a(x)=a_0+a_1x+\\cdots+a_{N-1}x^{N-1}\\) . Forward describes this same polynomial by its values at N special points; inverse recovers its coefficients. Repeat the operation independently for other towers. Read the symbols ¶ Symbol Meaning \\(N\\) Power-of-two transform length: the number of coefficients and evaluation points; 65536 in the project \\(q\\) This tower's prime modulus; stored values lie in \\([0,q)\\) \\(\\psi\\) A primitive 2N-th root: \\(\\psi^{2N}=1\\) and \\(\\psi^N=-1\\pmod q\\) \\(i,\\ a_i\\) Coefficient index and coefficient of \\(x^i\\) , with \\(0\\le i<N\\) \\(k,\\ y_k\\) Memory index and stored evaluation value, with \\(0\\le k<N\\) \\(j\\) Natural evaluation-point index; it can differ from memory index k \\(\\operatorname{rev}_b(k)\\) Reverse exactly b binary digits of k, where \\(b=\\log_2N\\) ; keep leading zeros \\(r_k\\) The evaluation point assigned to memory position k \\(N^{-1}\\) The modular inverse of N: \\(N\\cdot N^{-1}\\equiv1\\pmod q\\) Choose the point stored at position k ¶ For N=65536, an index has 16 bits. First find \\(j=\\operatorname{rev}_{16}(k)\\) , then choose the odd root power: \\[ r_k=\\psi^{2j+1}=\\psi^{2\\operatorname{rev}_{16}(k)+1}\\pmod q. \\] Why odd powers? This project multiplies modulo \\(x^N+1\\) , so the evaluation points must satisfy \\(r_k^N=-1\\) . Odd powers have that property because \\((\\psi^{2j+1})^N=(-1)^{2j+1}=-1\\) . Why bit reversal? OpenFHE's NTT stores these evaluations in bit-reversed point order. It rearranges the addresses of the results, not their numeric bits. The first memory positions therefore mean: Memory k Natural point index j = rev₁₆(k) Root exponent 2j+1 0 0 1 1 32768 65537 2 16384 32769 3 49152 98305 4 8192 16385 In particular, \\(r_0=\\psi\\) , while \\(r_1=\\psi^{N+1}=\\psi^N\\psi=-\\psi\\pmod q\\) . Thus memory positions 0 and 1 contain \\(a(\\psi)\\) and \\(a(-\\psi)\\) . A negative value is stored as its canonical residue; for example, −2 is 15 modulo 17. This ordering concerns NTT evaluations, not decoded CKKS slots. Forward: plug the point into the polynomial ¶ \\[ \\begin{aligned} y_k&=\\mathrm{forward}(a)[k]\\\\ &=a(r_k)\\\\ &=\\sum_{i=0}^{N-1}a_i r_k^i\\pmod q. \\end{aligned} \\] For one fixed k , the sum means \\(a_0+a_1r_k+a_2r_k^2+\\cdots+a_{N-1}r_k^{N-1}\\) . Multiply each coefficient by the corresponding power, add the terms, and reduce modulo q. That gives one output value yₖ. Repeating for all k gives the complete evaluation array. The equation defines the required answer. Real implementations use butterflies to share work rather than calculate N separate N-term sums directly. Inverse: collect contributions back into each coefficient ¶ \\[ \\begin{aligned} a_i&=\\mathrm{inverse}(y)[i]\\\\ &=N^{-1}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}y_k r_k^{-i}\\pmod q. \\end{aligned} \\] Now hold coefficient index i fixed and sum over all evaluation positions k . Every yₖ contributes with weight \\(r_k^{-i}\\) . Add those contributions and multiply by N-inverse to obtain aᵢ. Two details matter: \\(r_k^{-i}=(r_k^{-1})^i\\) . A negative exponent means a modular inverse, not a negative array index or an ordinary floating-point reciprocal. N-inverse removes the factor N accumulated by the inverse sum. It is an integer modulo q, not decimal division by N. N is invertible because the supported prime satisfies \\(2N\\mid(q-1)\\) . Keep each yₖ paired with its original rₖ. The inverse consumes the forward's bit-reversed evaluation order directly and returns coefficients in natural order; do not sort the values or add another bit-reversal pass. A complete four-point example ¶ Use N=4, q=17, ψ=2 , so \\(\\psi^4=16\\equiv-1\\pmod{17}\\) . This is a small arithmetic example; the project workload remains N=65536. The index now has two bits , so use rev₂ instead of rev₁₆. Start with \\(a(x)=1+2x+3x^2+4x^3\\) , stored as [1, 2, 3, 4] . Here k is the memory position, j is the natural point index, and the two-bit labels make the reversal visible: \\(k\\) (bits) \\(j\\) (bits) \\(r_k\\) \\(y_k\\) 0 ( 00 ) 0 ( 00 ) 2 15 1 ( 01 ) 2 ( 10 ) 15 11 2 ( 10 ) 1 ( 01 ) 8 13 3 ( 11 ) 3 ( 11 ) 9 16 For k=0, substitute r₀=2: \\[ \\begin{aligned} y_0&=1+2\\cdot2+3\\cdot2^2+4\\cdot2^3\\\\ &=49\\equiv15\\pmod{17}. \\end{aligned} \\] The stored forward array is [15, 11, 13, 16] . Evaluating in natural j order would give [15, 13, 11, 16] : the same point values at different addresses. Exact OpenFHE compatibility requires the stored order. For the inverse, \\(4^{-1}=13\\pmod{17}\\) , since \\(4\\cdot13=52\\equiv1\\) . The inverse points are [9, 8, 15, 2] ; for example, \\(2\\cdot9\\equiv1\\pmod{17}\\) . For i=0, every weight is 1. The evaluations sum to 55, which is 4 modulo 17; scaling gives \\(a_0=13\\cdot4\\bmod17=1\\) . For i=1, call the weighted sum S₁: \\[ \\begin{aligned} S_1&=15\\cdot9+11\\cdot8\\\\ &\\quad+13\\cdot15+16\\cdot2\\\\ &=450\\equiv8\\pmod{17},\\\\ a_1&=13S_1\\bmod17=2. \\end{aligned} \\] All four coefficient calculations are: Coefficient i Weighted sum, reduced mod 17 Multiply by 13, mod 17 0 4 1 1 8 2 2 12 3 3 16 4 The inverse returns [1, 2, 3, 4] : the original coefficients. Why does the inverse recover exactly the original coefficients? Let h be a second coefficient index while i stays fixed. Substitute the forward definition into the inverse and collect the contribution from aₕ: \\[ \\mathrm{inverse}(y)[i] =\\sum_{h=0}^{N-1}a_h \\left(N^{-1}\\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}r_k^{h-i}\\right)\\pmod q. \\] The inner sum is N when h=i, because every term is 1. Otherwise it is zero. To see why, write \\(\\omega=\\psi^2\\) , a primitive N-th root. The points rₖ visit all \\(\\psi\\omega^j\\) , only in a permuted order, so: \\[ \\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}r_k^{h-i} =\\psi^{h-i}\\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}(\\omega^{h-i})^j. \\] For h≠i, set \\(z=\\omega^{h-i}\\) . Then z≠1 but \\(z^N=1\\) . Multiplying the geometric sum by 1−z cancels its middle terms: \\[ (1-z)(1+z+\\cdots+z^{N-1})=1-z^N=0\\pmod q. \\] Because q is prime and 1−z is nonzero, it has a modular inverse, so the sum itself must be zero. For h=i, every term is 1 and the sum is N. The final N-inverse therefore keeps aᵢ and cancels every other coefficient's contribution. Bit reversal does not change this sum, because it visits each point exactly once. Simple probes that catch ordering and scaling errors ¶ Returning to N=65536, the test suite uses polynomials with easy expected answers. For example, evaluating a(x)=x returns the points themselves, which reveals their stored order. Setting just one evaluation value to 1 isolates one term of the inverse formula. Probe Expected array \\(a(x)=1\\) Every evaluation is 1 \\(a(x)=x\\) Slot k is \\(r_k\\) \\(a(x)=x^{17}\\) Slot k is \\(r_k^{17}\\) \\(a(x)=x^{N-1}\\) Slot k is \\(r_k^{N-1}\\) Evaluation array with 1 only at slot s Coefficient i is \\(N^{-1}r_s^{-i}\\) These formulas give expected answers without running our forward transform. They can expose ordering or scaling mistakes even when a matching mistake in the inverse makes a round trip recover the input. The D7 gate checks all N outputs, not just the first few slots. See the upstream inverse normalization loop for the scaling implementation, and our CPU reference for the matching transform convention. 4. Changing format versus changing its label ¶ The shared SetFormat implementation calls SwitchFormat only when the requested format differs from the current one. Read SetFormat’s conditional conversion OpenFHE · ilelement.h:474–478 inline void SetFormat(const Format format, uint32_t thread_limit = 0) { if (this->GetFormat() != format) { this->SwitchFormat(thread_limit); } } The condition is why a same-format request preserves values. Compare the label-only DCRTPoly::OverrideFormat before importing GPU arrays. API Effect SetFormat(current_format) No conversion; values remain unchanged SetFormat(other_format) Runs the forward or inverse transform SwitchFormat() Always toggles format and transforms values NativePoly::SetValues(values, format) Assigns the values and label; does not transform them NativePoly::OverrideFormat(format) Changes only the label DCRTPoly::OverrideFormat(format) Changes only the outer label; does not retag or transform its NativePoly children The last two methods are not substitutes for conversion. A DCRT object with outer EVALUATION and coefficient-format children is inconsistent. Keep the outer and child formats aligned when importing GPU results. To import an already-transformed array, create an evaluation-format NativePoly for each tower and assign its values with the evaluation label. Inverting it again during import would undo the GPU computation. D7 tests this import path using a separate DCRT object. 5. Roots, prime widths, and the transform cache ¶ A valid primitive root is not necessarily the same root. Replacing ψ by \\(\\psi^3\\) still gives a primitive 2N-th root but changes the evaluation addresses. Do not regenerate roots using this repository's independent parameter finder when comparing or importing an OpenFHE context. Read each tower's root. The pinned RootOfUnity implementation chooses the smallest primitive root by enumerating primitive powers. The D7 fixtures use this function with LastPrime(bits, 2N) for exact 50-, 55-, and 60-bit primes. The test independently enumerates odd root powers and checks the minimum. FirstPrime(bits, m) searches above \\(2^{\\mathrm{bits}}\\) ; its argument is not a guarantee of a prime with exactly that many bits. LastPrime searches below that bound and checks the resulting bit length. This is why the D7 matrix uses LastPrime. Cache constraint: the pinned FTT table cache looks up by modulus and checks table length; it does not compare the requested root on a cache hit. Use a consistent root for each modulus/ring-size pair. The gate tests the normal OpenFHE-generated root choice. Arbitrary custom-root contexts sharing a modulus are not covered by this result. Do not call a global cache reset inside an integration adapter to work around this constraint. Read the FTT cache-hit condition OpenFHE · transformnat-impl.h:720–726 const uint32_t ringDim = (cycloOrder >> 1); { std::shared_lock<std::shared_mutex> rlk(TablesMutex()); auto it = m_tablesByModulus.find(modulus); if (it != m_tablesByModulus.end() && it->second->rootReverse.GetLength() == ringDim) return it->second; } The hit checks the modulus and table length. No root comparison appears in this condition, which motivates retaining exact imported roots. Default CUDA uses the same bit-reversed group indexing for root and inverse-root powers, but owns its tables and uses the repository's modular reduction. The legacy build uses natural powers \\([\\psi^0,\\ldots,\\psi^N]\\) . Do not pass OpenFHE's internal tables or preconditioned multiply constants to CUDA. Share the exact parameters and polynomial arrays; construct backend-specific tables separately. 6. DCRT tower order and flat GPU packing ¶ DCRTPoly owns a vector of NativePoly objects. Each tower has its own modulus, root, format, and value allocation. The ILDCRTParams constructor retains the provided modulus/root order and forms the composite modulus Q as their product. Multiplying all tower primes gives the composite modulus Q, which may need more than 64 bits. Each CUDA modulus entry must hold one tower’s prime q. Putting a shortened 64-bit version of Q there would describe a different calculation. For N=65536, each row below is copied from its own NativePoly allocation: OpenFHE tower Parameters Flat GPU element range Byte offset 0 \\((q_0,\\psi_0)\\) 0…65535 0 1 \\((q_1,\\psi_1)\\) 65536…131071 524288 2 \\((q_2,\\psi_2)\\) 131072…196607 1048576 \\[ \\mathrm{address}(\\ell,i)=65536\\ell+i,\\qquad Q=q_0q_1q_2. \\] Read the active tower list from the object, not a hard-coded sorted list or an assumed CKKS level. The D7 test deliberately uses bit widths in the order 55, 50, 60 so sorting or swapping towers cannot silently pass. Packing coefficient data requires \\(a_i\\bmod q_\\ell\\) at each address. For an integer coefficient -1, this is \\(q_\\ell-1\\) in every tower. The test starts from a BigInteger polynomial containing \\(Q-1\\) and \\(2^{64}+17\\) , imports it as a DCRTPoly, and checks all residues and coefficient-domain CRT reconstruction. No interpolation is needed to run the per-tower NTT itself. CRTInterpolate() requires coefficient format and returns a BigInteger Poly with the placeholder root 1. That root is not a valid primitive 2N-th root; do not use the interpolated object's placeholder to configure an NTT backend. OpenFHE allocates each tower separately. Copy its numbers tower by tower into the flat GPU input array. Treating the whole DCRT object as a uint64_t* would read object storage rather than the intended packed numbers; the C++ integer objects need not have the same memory layout as GPU scalars. The D5 dataflow map describes the current host-vector API and the ownership/stream design subsequently specified in D9 and accepted by D10 . 7. Pointwise products and active levels ¶ If both arrays use the same q, ψ, format and position order, multiply their corresponding evaluation values and then run the inverse. The result is the polynomial product reduced using x^N=−1, called the negacyclic product. D7 multiplies a full three-tower DCRT polynomial by x and checks the independent coefficient rule: \\[ \\mathrm{out}_i= \\begin{cases} -\\mathrm{in}_{N-1}\\pmod q,&i=0,\\\\ \\mathrm{in}_{i-1},&1\\le i<N. \\end{cases} \\] Both the wrap term and large-input coefficient residues are checked. DropLastElement() removes the last tower and its parameter. It retains the remaining ordered prefix without scaling or transforming values. It is not CKKS rescaling, ModUp, or ModDown. D7 checks this prefix behavior, including that dropping a copied object's last tower does not change the original's parameter list. The D8 graph records the actual Hybrid basis and reduction operations. 8. Adapter checklist and remaining scope ¶ Before handing an object to the backend: Check N and cyclotomic order, then obtain the actual ordered active towers. Retain each tower's q and ψ; validate the supported modulus/root contract. Establish the domain and ordering, with consistent outer/child formats. Pack canonical values at limb*N+index , without a twist or permutation. Run the explicitly requested operation. Inverse includes normalization. Import results with matching parameters and truthful format labels. This freezes the power-of-two NTT and DCRT array boundary. It does not freeze Hybrid digit order, Q/P concatenation, rounding, key layout, device ownership, stream/event lifetime, or CPU fallback. The D8 graph now specifies Hybrid digit order, Q/P layout, and the pinned CKKS reduction sequence. D9's device ownership/API design is now accepted by D10's ADR , closing P1's architecture gate. Native runtime implementation and its exactness/failure tests remain later work. 9. Run the checks ¶ After installing the pinned OpenFHE release with scripts/openfhe_setup.sh : cmake -S . -B build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON \\ -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" cmake --build build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build -j ctest --test-dir build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -R 'openfhe_exact_compat|openfhe_representation_contract' --output-on-failure The original gate retains random and boundary coefficient cases plus an independent inverse input. The new representation gate adds full-size monomial and inverse-basis formulas, format API behavior, root selection, three-tower packing/import, BigInteger coefficients, pointwise multiplication, and tower dropping. It links only OpenFHE core. See the daily entry for observed build and publication results. Pinned source references ¶ All links below identify the exact OpenFHE commit inspected for this contract. The linked entry points lead to the statements used above. Format enum and SetFormat . NativePoly SwitchFormat and SetValues . FTT dispatch and cached tables . RootOfUnity , FirstPrime , and LastPrime . DCRT format conversion and tower parameters . DCRT interpolation and dropping a tower . Next: Hybrid operation graph →"}, {"title": "Hybrid operation graph", "summary": "Follow the arrays and source code in OpenFHE Hybrid key switching.", "group": "Learn", "url": "openfhe-hybrid-operation-graph.html", "text": "Hybrid operation graph Hybrid key switching groups prime towers into digits, adds auxiliary prime towers, multiplies by key arrays, and reduces two accumulated outputs. The exact order of conversion and reduction affects the arrays. CPU comparisons check this sequence; a GPU key-switch implementation is still planned. Read full notes How OpenFHE Hybrid key switching works ¶ Key switching changes a ciphertext’s key representation without decrypting its data. Hybrid key switching groups its prime towers into chunks called digits. For each chunk it computes residues under additional primes, multiplies by evaluation-key arrays, and adds the products into two outputs. It then reduces those outputs back to the original active prime set. This page follows OpenFHE 1.6.1 , commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f , at N=65536 with NativeInteger 64. Read the representation guide first: a tower is one prime-modulus array, and its format determines whether it contains coefficients or NTT evaluations. A basis is an ordered prime list. Q is the active ciphertext basis; P contains extra primes used during switching. QP means the Q towers followed by the P towers; in formulas it also denotes the product of those primes. The CPU tests compare real OpenFHE PKE arrays with an independent unreduced-CRT formula. They establish this graph, not a GPU key-switch implementation. Source excerpts are © 2022 OpenFHE, under BSD 2-Clause . 1. Read the complete path ¶ The ordinary path starts at KeySwitchCore(c,key) . Its output is two contributions in the active Q basis, ordered B first, A second . The KeySwitchInPlace wrapper decides how to combine them with existing components. Read the core’s actual call chain OpenFHE · keyswitch-hybrid.cpp:295–298 std::vector<DCRTPoly> KeySwitchHYBRID::KeySwitchCore(const DCRTPoly& a, const EvalKey<DCRTPoly> evalKey) const { return EvalFastKeySwitchCore(EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore(a, evalKey->GetCryptoParameters()), evalKey, a.GetParams()); } Precomputation produces the digit arrays. The fast core multiplies them by key arrays, adds the products (multiply-accumulate, or MAC), and reduces the two results to the active Q basis. Split the active Q towers Contiguous groups of up to α towers form the digits. Keep their original evaluation arrays. Convert each digit to coefficients Inverse NTT only the towers in that digit. Extend to its complement Approximate CRT produces coefficients in every other active Q tower and every P tower. Return the complement to evaluations Forward NTT those new towers, then stitch them around the saved original digit towers. Multiply and accumulate in QlP All digits contribute to two evaluation arrays, using the B and A key vectors. Reduce each accumulated result Inverse NTT its P towers, approximate-switch P to active Q, forward NTT the correction, subtract, and multiply by the inverse of P. Update the ciphertext Add the first contribution to c0; add or replace c1 according to the caller. Notice which arrays actually change format. During extension, OpenFHE keeps the digit’s original evaluation towers. During ModDown, it keeps the Q evaluation towers. It does not inverse-transform and forward-transform every Q_lP tower at each step. 2. Names, levels, and tower order ¶ Let M be the full context's Q-tower count, L the current ciphertext's active Q-tower count, K the P-tower count, and D the configured large-digit count. The active basis is an ordered prefix of Q: \\[ Q_\\ell=\\prod_{i=0}^{L-1}q_i,\\qquad P=\\prod_{j=0}^{K-1}p_j,\\qquad \\alpha=\\left\\lceil\\frac{M}{D}\\right\\rceil,\\qquad \\beta_\\ell=\\left\\lceil\\frac{L}{\\alpha}\\right\\rceil. \\] The source also caps the active digit count at the configured partition count. For valid active prefixes this does not alter the formula above. α belongs to the full context; do not recompute it from L. The last active digit may be shorter than α, including when only part of the first digit remains. See PrecomputeCRTTables (full-context digit width) and EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore (active digit count). Object Tower order Representation Input component q₀ … qₗ₋₁ Evaluation, bit-reversed within each tower Digit d's source q at indices dα … min((d+1)α,L)−1 Evaluation, then coefficient Digit d's complement Other active Q towers in order, then p₀ … pₖ₋₁ Coefficient, then evaluation Assembled digit Active Q prefix, then all P Evaluation Stored key pair for digit d Full Q, then all P Evaluation Each MAC output Active Q prefix, then all P Evaluation Each ModDown output Active Q prefix Evaluation Here L is a tower count , not the ciphertext's public GetLevel() value. Derive it from the actual element parameters; the D9 proposal keeps these metadata consistent. Every tower retains its exact OpenFHE modulus and root. 3. Why approximate CRT must keep the unreduced sum ¶ For a digit's actual source basis \\(B=\\prod_i b_i\\) , define \\(\\widehat B_i=B/b_i\\) . At coefficient index r, the implementation computes: \\[ t_i=[x_i\\widehat B_i^{-1}]_{b_i},\\qquad S=\\sum_i t_i\\widehat B_i,\\qquad \\widetilde x_m=[S]_m. \\] The Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) combines residues under different primes. Here brackets mean “take the remainder from 0 to modulus−1.” The x_i are coefficient residues in the source towers. Each destination prime m belongs to the complement: the other active Q primes and all P primes. There is no reduction of S modulo B before reducing it modulo m. In general: \\[ S=x+\\kappa B,\\qquad 0\\le x<B,\\qquad [S]_m\\ne[x]_m. \\] ApproxSwitchCRTBasis implements this sum using the precomputed hat inverses, hat residues, and modular multiplication/accumulation. Our test independently forms the full unreduced sum with arbitrary-precision BigInteger arithmetic; it does not read those conversion tables to compute its expected answer. Apply this formula to coefficients, so inverse-transform the source digit first. Then forward-transform each newly created destination tower with its own root. Copy the original digit evaluation towers into the remaining positions. Evaluation positions under different roots are not the same coefficient; applying this CRT formula to them directly would give a different result. Read the digit’s coefficient → complement → evaluation sequence OpenFHE · keyswitch-hybrid.cpp:346–353 partsCt.SetFormat(Format::COEFFICIENT, threads); auto partsCtCompl = partsCt.ApproxSwitchCRTBasis(cryptoParams->GetParamsPartQ(part), cryptoParams->GetParamsComplPartQ(sizeQl - 1, part), cryptoParams->GetPartQlHatInvModq(part, sizePartQl - 1), cryptoParams->GetPartQlHatInvModqPrecon(part, sizePartQl - 1), cryptoParams->GetPartQlHatModp(sizeQl - 1, part), cryptoParams->GetmodComplPartqBarrettMu(sizeQl - 1, part)); partsCtCompl.SetFormat(Format::EVALUATION, threads); The first format conversion is on the digit; the second is only on its newly constructed complement. This contract uses WITH_REDUCED_NOISE=OFF . The centered-conversion branch selected by that build option is different and is outside this fixture matrix. 4. A reduced level changes key addresses ¶ A key keeps all M Q towers followed by K P towers. A ciphertext digit contains only L Q towers followed by those same K P towers. For active-array index i: \\[ \\operatorname{keyIndex}(i)= \\begin{cases} i,&i<L,\\\\ i+(M-L),&i\\ge L. \\end{cases} \\] For the fixture M=5, α=3, K=3 , reducing to L=4 gives: Array Tower positions Active digit q₀, q₁, q₂, q₃, p₀, p₁, p₂ Full key indices used 0, 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7 Full key index skipped 4, the dropped q₄ Index 4 in the active digit is p₀, while index 4 in the key is q₄. Using the same flat index for both silently reads the wrong key tower when unchecked multiplication is used. See EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt for the exact i + delta selection. The diagnostic key gives every digit, tower, and evaluation position a distinct deterministic value to expose this. Read reduced-level key addressing and B/A accumulation OpenFHE · keyswitch-hybrid.cpp:409–418 for (uint32_t i = 0; i < sizeQlP; ++i) { const auto idx = (i >= sizeQl) ? i + delta : i; auto& r0 = result[0].GetAllElements()[i]; auto& r1 = result[1].GetAllElements()[i]; for (uint32_t j = 0; j < limit; ++j) { const auto& cji = (*digits)[j].GetElementAtIndex(i); r0.MultAccEqNoCheck(cji, bv[j].GetElementAtIndex(idx)); r1.MultAccEqNoCheck(cji, av[j].GetElementAtIndex(idx)); } } Here delta is M−L. The first result uses B and the second uses A, with the same corrected key index. Explore the active level Choose the number of remaining Q towers in the executable fixture. 5 Q towers 4 Q towers 3 Q towers 2 Q towers 1 Q tower 5. Two MACs, then two ModDowns ¶ For digit evaluation array d and key arrays A and B, each active tower m and bit-reversed evaluation coordinate k compute: \\[ Z_{0,m,k}=\\left[\\sum_d D_{d,m,k}B_{d,\\operatorname{keyIndex}(m),k}\\right]_m, \\] \\[ Z_{1,m,k}=\\left[\\sum_d D_{d,m,k}A_{d,\\operatorname{keyIndex}(m),k}\\right]_m. \\] EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt returns these two QlP arrays. The multiplication is pointwise; there is no NTT inside the MAC loop. Each product and sum is reduced modulo its tower prime, so stored values remain between 0 and that prime minus one. The evaluation key already supplies the gadget scaling: in private-to-private generation, its old-secret term is P times the old secret on that digit's Q towers, and zero on its other towers. Do not add an extra global twist or a guessed CRT/gadget multiplier to the digits. The KeySwitchGenInternal private-to-private branch shows the PModq[i] * soi term only within that digit’s Q interval. For each accumulated array Z, extract and inverse-transform its P towers: \\[ u_j=\\operatorname{INTT}_{p_j}(Z_{p_j}),\\qquad \\widehat P_j=P/p_j, \\] \\[ R_i=\\left[\\sum_j [u_j\\widehat P_j^{-1}]_{p_j}\\widehat P_j\\right]_{q_i}, \\] \\[ Y_{q_i}=\\left[(Z_{q_i}-\\operatorname{NTT}_{q_i}(R_i)) [P^{-1}]_{q_i}\\right]_{q_i}. \\] Read these equations in order: inverse-transform the P towers to get u, convert those coefficient residues to a correction R under Q, then transform R, subtract it from Z, and multiply by the modular inverse of P. This is the exact order in the selected CKKS source. Its noise scale is 1, so it passes t=0 and skips factors used by the separate BGV encryption scheme. These steps use integer modular arithmetic, with no floating-point rounding. Calling this “division by P” does not authorize replacing its approximate representative with a canonical or centered CRT representative, or with a generic rounding rule. Follow ApproxModDown for P-tower INTT and basis conversion. Its final Q loop is: Read the final Q correction and P-inverse scale OpenFHE · dcrtpoly-impl.h:1047–1053 for (uint32_t i = 0; i < sizeQ; ++i) { // Multiply everything by t mod Q (BGVrns only) if (t > 0) partP.m_vectors[i] *= t; partP.m_vectors[i].SetFormat(Format::EVALUATION); ans.m_vectors[i] = (m_vectors[i] - partP.m_vectors[i]) * PInvModq[i]; } For this CKKS path t=0, so the BGV-only multiplication is skipped. The correction is forward-transformed before subtraction and multiplication by P-inverse. Accumulate before ModDown. Moving ModDown inside the digit sum changes carry/correction terms. A simple scalar illustration with q=97 and P=17 is \\(\\operatorname{Down}(16)=0\\) , \\(\\operatorname{Down}(1)=0\\) , but \\(\\operatorname{Down}(17)=1\\) . This is a modular-arithmetic example, not a small polynomial fixture. The full-size test also explicitly checks that the wrong per-digit reduction order produces different arrays. 6. Which wrapper calls which graph? ¶ Entry point Result and caller obligation EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore β active digit arrays in QlP; reusable for hoisted operations with compatible parameters EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt Two QlP MAC arrays; reduction is deferred EvalFastKeySwitchCore Extended MAC followed by ModDown of both arrays KeySwitchCore Precompute followed by the fast core; two Q contributions KeySwitchInPlace with two components Add B contribution to c₀; replace c₁ with the A contribution KeySwitchInPlace with three components Add B to c₀ and A to c₁; discard c₂ RelinearizeInPlace Switch each component from index 2 using the corresponding evaluation key, accumulate into c₀/c₁, then resize to two For the in-place wrapper: \\[ c'_0=c_0+Y_0,\\qquad c'_1=\\begin{cases}Y_1,&\\text{two-component input},\\\\c_1+Y_1,&\\text{three-component input}.\\end{cases} \\] D8 executes the two- and three-component wrapper cases. The source trace also records the general relinearization loop; higher ciphertext degrees and their full key families remain a later integration test. Delayed QP operations are distinct from digit extension ¶ KeySwitchExt(ciphertext,addFirst) multiplies the retained Q evaluations by P and appends zero P towers . It does not approximate-lift the original ciphertext into P. If addFirst=false , its entire first component is zero. KeySwitchDown reduces both accumulated QP components; KeySwitchDownFirstElement reduces only the first. The test checks both addFirst settings and delayed addition of a QP MAC result before reduction. The pinned CKKS EvalFastRotationExt has another ordering requirement: it computes the extended MAC, optionally adds P times c₀ on the Q towers, then applies the automorphism to both QP components . Reduction is deferred. The automorphism map and full rotation API are source-traced here; their exact end-to-end arrays remain P6/P7 work. Do not move the permutation across the selected rotation key without a separate equivalence proof. 7. Full-size fixture and evidence ¶ The deterministic fixture uses FIXEDMANUAL CKKS with multiplicative depth 4, first modulus size 60, scaling modulus size 50, two large digits, and the fixed ring dimension. It produces five Q towers and three P towers . Tower Modulus Primitive root q₀ 1152921504606584833 18043022392882 q₁ 1125899902124033 15995550378 q₂ 1125899911168001 21670117260 q₃ 1125899903827969 938640682 q₄ 1125899908022273 6511788946 p₀ 1152921504598720513 800790938143 p₁ 1152921504597016577 17749908910371 p₂ 1152921504595968001 11469071954203 The configuration requests 50 scaling bits, but q₂ and q₄ are 51-bit primes just above the power-of-two boundary; q₁ and q₃ are 50-bit. All P towers and q₀ are 60-bit. Use the exact table rather than inferring widths from the knob. These exact parameters are asserted in the executable. Pattern generation and diagnostic key generation use fixed integer formulas in that source file. The diagnostic key uses predictable patterns to make wrong addresses easy to detect. It is not a cryptographic evaluation key . A separate pair of actual generated secret keys supplies a real key-switching key; its fixed arrays are shared by all comparisons in that run. Random key generation is not compared across separate runs. Cases Coverage L=5,4,3,1 × four input patterns Full level, partial last digit, exact digit boundary, partial first digit Zero, all q−1, monomial x¹⁷, deterministic dense residues Zero path, bounds, closed-form coefficient structure, tower-distinct data Actual generated key at L=5 and L=4 Same exact graph with real cryptographic key arrays Intermediate states Digit INTT, complement CRT and NTT, digit assembly, both MACs, P INTT, correction CRT and NTT, final scale Composition and wrappers Fast/full core equality, input immutability, two/three-component update, delayed QP path Negative alternatives Canonical-lift substitution, missing P-key offset, per-digit ModDown Observed on Linux x86_64 with GCC 14.2, Release, NativeInteger 64, and OpenMP off: 18 cases and 117,571,584 residue comparisons passed . All 15 CTests in the OpenFHE-enabled build passed. The deterministic reduced-level fixture found 524,288 residues that distinguish the wrong per-digit reduction order. Every comparison checks all 65536 entries of every involved tower, plus modulus/root order and outer/child format. To observe an intermediate array, the test calls the corresponding public OpenFHE operation separately. It does not modify OpenFHE to inspect hidden state. A further comparison checks that the complete core produces the same result as the independent step-by-step calculation. Run the opt-in gate after installing pinned OpenFHE : cmake -S . -B build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON \\ -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" cmake --build build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build -j ctest --test-dir build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -R 'openfhe_' --output-on-failure The Hybrid gate links OpenFHE PKE in addition to core. The standalone build still has no OpenFHE dependency. The setup script already builds all modules. 8. Work counts and the next boundary ¶ For one core call, counting transforms per tower rather than per launch, derived from digit precomputation and the two ApproxModDown calls : \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\text{Digit INTTs}&=L,\\\\ \\text{Complement NTTs}&=\\beta_\\ell(L+K)-L,\\\\ \\text{ModDown INTTs}&=2K,\\\\ \\text{ModDown NTTs}&=2L. \\end{aligned} \\] At M=L=5, K=3, α=3, β=2, this is 11 inverse and 21 forward NTTs . At L=4 it is 10 inverse and 18 forward. These are logical counts from the source graph, not GPU launch counts or measured performance. With 8-byte residues and N=65536, the assembled digits request \\(8N\\beta_\\ell(L+K)\\) bytes and the two QP accumulators request \\(16N(L+K)\\) bytes. Each is 8 MiB at full fixture level. Keys, temporary coefficient buffers, parameters, allocator overhead, and simultaneous lifetime choices are additional. D9 specifies the proposed lifetimes ; an implementation and allocation measurements are still needed before claiming a peak-memory bound. D8 freezes this source graph and the exercised array boundary. D10's ADR accepts it together with the D9 device/buffer/stream/cache design , closing P1's architecture gate while leaving runtime implementation and native validation open. P4/P6 must broaden base-conversion and cryptographic fixtures across the production parameter/level/key matrix. This is not a GPU implementation, performance result, or complete CKKS encryption/decryption integration gate. Pinned source references ¶ Hybrid key generation and ordinary/delayed core . ApproxSwitchCRTBasis and ApproxModDown . Full-context partition construction . Relinearization and ciphertext component accumulation . CKKS extended fast rotation ordering . Next: Device backend contract →"}, {"title": "Device backend contract", "summary": "Specify what a stored GPU polynomial needs besides its memory address.", "group": "Learn", "url": "device-backend-contract.html", "text": "Device backend contract A handle must identify the primes, array format, owner and unfinished work. A pointer and length cannot describe those facts. This page proposes the full backend design. A separate resident prototype implements part of it; the cache and OpenFHE adapter remain planned. Read full notes What a GPU polynomial handle must know ¶ A pointer and a length cannot tell us which primes an array uses, whether it contains coefficients or evaluations, or whether its producer has finished. A GPU calculation may still be running after the CPU submits it. This design therefore keeps the array’s description with its owner and progress record. A layout describes what the numbers mean and their order. A completion lets later code find out whether the queued work finished successfully. This page records design milestone D9 for N=65536. D7, D8 and D9 are work items in the roadmap, not calendar dates. A backend is the part of the program that performs operations on a selected device. D7 defines the arrays, D8 defines Hybrid operations, and the ADR accepts the design. The separate resident prototype implements a bounded subset; the full backend, cache and OpenFHE adapter remain proposed. 1. What D9 delivers ¶ Artifact State after D9 Immutable parameter snapshot, layouts, views, cache identities and dependency checks Implemented as a portable host metadata prototype Backend, immutable polynomial, stream and completion interfaces Compile-checked abstract proposal; no runtime factory OpenFHE metadata bridge Checked against actual D8 Hybrid outputs and evaluation-key parameters CUDA allocations, streams, events, asynchronous copies and cache eviction Proposed lifecycle; not implemented by these headers OpenFHE adapter and device dispatch Proposed first seam; implementation follows acceptance and native GPU gates The portable rules live in backend_contract.hpp , the abstract interfaces in backend_api.hpp , and their tests in backend_contract_tests.cpp . ntt::proposal marks these as design interfaces, with no complete runtime factory. They add no CUDA or OpenFHE dependency to the standalone build. The existing wrapper has separate ownership/input fixes. The resident prototype , added November 16, has host checks and compiled CUDA transport, with native verification pending. Neither promotes this full proposal to a production backend. 2. Carry the complete polynomial identity ¶ The Parameters object makes its own copy of the following values. They cannot change after construction, even if the caller changes the originals: ring dimension N; full, ordered Q and P lists, each containing the exact modulus and primitive 2N-th root; full-context digit width α, which must not be recomputed after dropping Q towers. Its factory rejects invalid dimensions, composite or duplicate moduli, invalid roots, and invalid digit geometry. For these power-of-two rings, the root test is ψᴺ = −1 mod q; together with prime q and q ≡ 1 mod 2N, it establishes order 2N. The semantic bound is q < 2⁶³. The initial device shape is narrower: N=65536 and canonical 50–60-bit primes. A valid shape still needs an available backend and an operation verified on the selected GPU. Alternative primitive roots can be mathematically valid metadata. The first OpenFHE adapter must import the exact existing roots and respect D7's shared FTT-cache constraint; support for conflicting custom-root contexts is not implied. Q is the ciphertext’s prime list and P is an auxiliary prime list used in key switching. M counts all Q primes; L counts those still active; K counts P primes. A basis is an ordered selection from those lists. PolynomialLayout records that selection with TowerRef {Q or P, index} . It also records whether the array contains coefficients or evaluations (its domain), how they are ordered, and how residues are encoded. Dropping a level reduces L but retains the full original parameter description. Upstream ILDCRTParams constructs towers in the supplied modulus/root order; GetAllElements() exposes their individual polynomials. Field Initial supported contract Coefficient domain Natural coefficient order Evaluation domain OpenFHE-compatible bit-reversed slot order Residues Canonical uint64_t , 0 ≤ x < q for each tower Packing Tower-major; each selected tower has exactly N consecutive residues Device identity Device ordinal plus backend-session identity Allocation identity Registry ID plus generation; never an OpenFHE object address Montgomery and lazy residues are explicit enum values but rejected by this prototype. They cannot enter the canonical kernels under a different label. The host-value validator checks the exact element count and every residue. An adapter must verify the outer DCRT format and each tower's format, dimension, modulus and root before constructing this layout; relabeling data is not conversion. Forward requires coefficient input and returns evaluation layout. Inverse requires evaluation input and returns coefficient layout, including the existing N-inverse normalization. Pointwise multiplication requires identical evaluation layouts. Parameter equality compares complete values, not hash equality alone. 3. Make views explicit and bounded ¶ The planned runtime keeps its own registry of allocated buffers. A public view identifies a buffer by ID and generation number, then selects its first tower, tower count and active Q count. The generation prevents an old handle from referring to a reused ID. Validation checks that the buffer is still alive, belongs to the right device/session, and is large enough for the selected whole towers. Size calculations reject integer overflow. \\[ \\operatorname{offset}(t,i)=8(tN+i),\\qquad \\operatorname{bytes}(T)=8NT. \\] At N=65536, one tower is 524,288 bytes, or 0.5 MiB . A whole-tower subview refers to part of the same buffer without copying it. It cannot modify the input, and it keeps both the buffer and the record of when its data become ready alive. Arbitrary coefficient strides and partial-tower views are deferred. A reduced QP basis is a particularly important boundary: Basis Logical order Indices in the full five-Q/three-P key Full QP, L=5 q₀ q₁ q₂ q₃ q₄ p₀ p₁ p₂ 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 Active QP, L=4 q₀ q₁ q₂ q₃ p₀ p₁ p₂ 0 1 2 3 5 6 7 Shortening the full allocation to seven towers produces q₀…q₄,p₀,p₁, which is wrong. The validator rejects that attempted L=4 view. The upstream EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt uses the full-key offset for exactly this case. Materialize active QP in a fresh contiguous allocation using the explicit tower map. A Q prefix alone can use a zero-copy prefix view; the full key's P suffix can also be viewed without copying. For initial Hybrid execution, gathering the active key into a packed immutable snapshot is the simple proposed option; strided key access requires a separately accepted kernel contract. The proposal returns fresh output allocations for forward, inverse and pointwise operations. Inputs are immutable, and output aliasing is rejected. Existing kernels may still mutate a private output or private scratch buffer. An initial implementation may therefore need an extra device-to-device copy; that cost must appear in benchmarks. Public in-place operations wait for a separate alias and concurrency design. 4. Submit work with an explicit completion ¶ Backend proposes create_stream , upload , forward , inverse , pointwise , subview , download , and close . Submit carries an owned stream lease and owned dependency handles. PendingPolynomial couples an immutable result handle with its completion. This is the intended usage, not a runnable backend: Submit queue{backend.create_stream(), {}}; auto resident = backend.upload(coefficient_layout, host_values, queue); auto transformed = backend.forward(resident.value, queue); auto copy = backend.download(transformed.value, queue); auto evaluation_values = copy->wait_values(); The upload must validate and copy the borrowed host span into owned staging before returning. Download owns its host destination and exposes values only after successful completion. The same stream orders this example; a consumer on another stream must explicitly depend on the producer's completion. Producer state Consumer rule Pending, same stream FIFO stream order establishes the dependency Pending, different stream Queue a wait for the exact producer event and generation Succeeded Consumption is allowed after actual event success is established Failed Reject the consumer and propagate the producer error Different device or backend session Reject; peer transfer is not part of this API Every submitted operation receives a monotonically increasing ID in its backend session. Only earlier submitted completions can be dependencies; callers cannot manufacture future events or introduce cyclic waits. Each completion owns one immutable event instance. Re-recording an event behind an existing completion handle is forbidden. The host prototype checks metadata supplied as trusted registry records. The runtime must resolve every handle through its private registry, refresh event status, and issue actual cudaStreamWaitEvent calls. Passing the prototype a fabricated record does not create a CUDA event or prove synchronization. 5. Keep resources alive through completion ¶ A lease is a reference that keeps a resource alive. A pin is a lease held by unfinished work. Each submitted operation pins its input and output buffers, work queue, precomputed tables, keys, temporary CPU transfer buffers and GPU scratch space. The backend retains these pins even when the caller drops the returned completion. Discarding a handle does not cancel queued GPU work. Temporary memory can be reused only after every operation that might access it has finished, or after the runtime proves that the device has stopped accessing it. This proved stopped state is called quiescence. The proposed lifecycle is: Validate all metadata, capability and dependency handles before submission. Reserve output/scratch and cache leases under the appropriate locks. Submit work and record a completion event; retain all pins in an operation record. On success, publish readiness and release operation pins. On failure, mark the result failed and quarantine affected resources until the stream/device is drained or torn down safely. A failed event is not evidence that the device has stopped using memory. The can_retire prototype permits retirement only with zero external leases, zero in-flight pins, and either successful readiness or proven quiescence. Runtime pin counts are registry facts, not guesses from shared_ptr::use_count . A budget exhausted by pinned entries produces backpressure or a reported allocation failure; it never permits eviction of a live entry. The backend state should use unique_ptr<Impl> , move-only allocation owners, and a device guard on every runtime call. Constructors must unwind partial allocation safely. close() stops submission, drains work, reports errors and invalidates further use; destructors are nonthrowing. Outstanding leases retain backend state until safe release. An unrecoverable context failure needs a quarantine/teardown policy rather than immediate pool reuse. D5 identified a raw- Impl* constructor-failure leak in the existing wrapper. This proposal specifies the replacement discipline. Subsequent offline preparation fixes baseline ownership with unique_ptr and device guards; native allocation-failure injection and teardown tests remain in the accepted implementation backlog. 6. Cache semantic identities, then pin leases ¶ A cache saves tables or keys so later operations can reuse them. Each cache belongs to one session and device. A hash can quickly find a likely match, but reuse still requires comparing every field that affects the result. A pointer, N alone, or an OpenFHE key tag does not identify all those fields. Cache Required identity NTT plans/tables Device/session, N, exact ordered q/ψ pairs, encoding, table convention, arithmetic revision Digit conversion plans Full parameter context including α, L, digit index, ordered source/complement bases, conversion-rule revision Evaluation-key material Device/session, full context, immutable material ID/generation, key tag, key kind and index, packing revision The upstream GetTables is the concrete cache behavior reviewed here. The NTT plan bundles forward/inverse powers and normalization constants. Its current table convention is natural ψ powers including the N endpoint. It cannot reuse an OpenFHE bit-reversed FTT table as if the two layouts were equal. A forward/inverse plan may share identity because both directions are included; the operand's domain still controls which operation is valid. For digit d, the conversion-plan validator derives its contiguous Q partition using the full α, then verifies the exact ordered active-Q/P complement. Its rule revision includes D8's uncentered, unreduced approximate CRT sum , implemented by upstream ApproxSwitchCRTBasis . A canonical-CRT plan is a different operation. ModDown needs a distinct plan kind for its P→active-Q conversion and P-inverse scale (see ApproxModDown ); the prototype's ConversionPlanKey covers digit extension only. The proposed cache copies the evaluation key’s A/B arrays into snapshots that cannot be changed. These evaluation-key arrays support encrypted operations; the secret key itself is not uploaded. The actual OpenFHE input arrays come from GetAVector() and GetBVector() ; GetKeyTag() returns a tag, not a digest of those arrays. A material ID/generation is assigned to that snapshot. Mutating the original OpenFHE key requires a new snapshot and identity. A rotation index or relinearization component is part of the identity; equal tags do not establish equal key data. Packing validates digit count, full QP order and every residue. If a requested item is missing, the planned cache creates one pending entry under a lock. Other requests share that entry instead of uploading it again. Consumers on another stream wait for its upload event. Each reader keeps the entry alive until its own work finishes. A least-recently-used (LRU) policy may remove old entries only when no caller or GPU work still uses them. The proposal contains equality/validation rules and retirement checks, not an implemented cache, uploader, hash table or LRU policy. The abstract API exposes cache_stats() , set_cache_budget(bytes) , and trim_cache() . Statistics count allocated cache bytes including pending uploads, with pinned bytes counted once per entry; operand/scratch pools are separate. Budget is reserved before allocation. Lowering the budget evicts eligible entries first and fails if live pins prevent the requested bound. Trimming returns only bytes actually freed. ADR 0001 selects the initial budget and admission policy; their runtime implementation is pending. Hoisting means computing a polynomial’s digit decomposition once and reusing it for several operations, such as rotations. The resulting digit arrays are explicit read-only results rather than hidden global cache entries. Their provenance includes the complete parameter snapshot, source allocation ID/generation, complete active-Q view, domain/order and rule revision. The prototype rejects stale or incompatible provenance. Reuse across rotation keys is permitted only when that input/context is unchanged and each key matches the same Q/P/α context; the digits do not depend on a particular key's A/B data. A private registry must also validate that the referenced source exists and has the claimed layout. 7. Budget the Hybrid lifetimes ¶ D8 defines the operation order; D9 specifies how long each buffer must survive. Keep a digit’s coefficient scratch until conversion to its other primes finishes. Keep each evaluation digit until every multiply-accumulate (MAC) with key arrays B/A has finished. The running QP sums must survive until ModDown or a later QP operation completes. Keep P scratch and Q corrections through the final transforms and subtraction. Every reader retains the keys and tables it needs. For the full D8 fixture M=L=5, K=3, α=3, β=2 at N=65536: Storage Logical size Lifetime Input Q polynomial 2.5 MiB Upload through last decomposition reader Two assembled QP digits 8 MiB Hoist result through last MAC reader Two QP accumulators 8 MiB MAC through last ModDown/delayed reader Two final Q outputs 5 MiB Returned immutable outputs Full two-digit A/B key 16 MiB Cached snapshot plus in-flight reader pins The sizes follow 8NT bytes per T-tower polynomial. They are logical payloads, not a measured or proven peak allocation bound. Streaming digits can release scratch earlier; preserving a hoist for several rotations keeps all digits alive. Allocator alignment, twiddle tables, host staging, concurrent streams, conversion scratch and out-of-place copies add memory. D10 accepts explicit budgets and fail-fast admission in ADR 0001; P2/P3 must measure actual live bytes. 8. Integrate OpenFHE through a transactional adapter ¶ The proposed first seam is an explicit opt-in DCRT format-conversion adapter with SetFormat -equivalent behavior. It does not replace OpenFHE's global DCRTPoly::SwitchFormat or patch every NativePoly call. This small boundary tests exact tower packing, transform ordering, ownership and failure handling before Hybrid GPU integration adds conversion and key-material lifetimes. The host operation is synchronous even if its internal backend is asynchronous: Snapshot the DCRT context and validate all tower metadata, labels and values. If the requested format already matches, return without conversion. During preflight, select the device route or an explicitly permitted CPU route. Pack each tower using ConvertToInt() into canonical tower-major storage. Do not reinterpret OpenFHE native integer objects as a flat uint64_t* . Upload, transform, download and wait. Import into a private candidate object, set each tower's actual format and the DCRT format, and verify metadata. Compare upstream NativePoly::SetValues and the label-only DCRTPoly::OverrideFormat ; neither should accidentally transform an already-computed result. Replace the caller's object only after the entire candidate succeeds. The caller must serialize access to the same host object during the operation. The candidate preserves strong exception safety: a failed upload, launch, completion, download or import leaves the original host object unchanged. This is a proposed guarantee; it requires adapter and fault-injection tests. Situation RequireDevice AllowCpuBeforeSubmit Invalid metadata, residues or format labels Error Error Already in the requested format, valid input No-op No-op Available, supported and verified device operation Device Device Device unavailable, valid unsupported shape, or unverified operation Error CPU before submission Any failure after submission Propagate error Propagate error CPU fallback uses a private host candidate too. No automatic CPU retry occurs after device submission. A caller may explicitly retry the unchanged original after handling the backend error. An unverified CUDA operation cannot advertise production support merely because a kernel compiles; a diagnostic harness can run the native exactness gates to establish that capability. 9. Verification and D10 acceptance ¶ backend_contract_check runs in the normal CPU build. It checks valid and invalid roots/parameters, immutable snapshots, domain transitions, canonical host values, byte overflow, reduced-QP mistakes, view ranges, stale generations, aliasing, stream dependencies, failed-resource retirement, cache identities, hoist provenance, and preflight-only fallback. The opt-in openfhe_hybrid_graph additionally imports real pinned OpenFHE parameters at L=5,4,3,1 and checks the proposed active QP layout and key indices against actual Hybrid outputs and full-context key towers. It retains D8's 18 cases and exact residue-array comparisons. .venv/bin/cmake --build build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build -j2 .venv/bin/ctest --test-dir build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build --output-on-failure -j2 D10's accepted ADR records these decisions: the immutable, out-of-place initial API and its device-to-device copy cost; the DCRT format adapter as the first seam and its preflight-only fallback; private-registry provenance, resource pins and error quarantine; cache upload coordination, memory budget and backpressure; the ordered implementation backlog: RAII/failure cleanup, native exactness, resident NTT backend, adapter tests, then conversion/Hybrid integration. Required runtime tests include partial-constructor failure, wrong device, concurrent cache misses, missing event waits, completion abandonment, cache pressure with live readers, asynchronous failure and drain, and host rollback. None is replaced by metadata simulation. Real CUDA execution, complete Hybrid GPU support, CKKS application integration and performance remain later gates. Next: Resident operation chain →"}, {"title": "Resident operation chain", "summary": "Keep intermediate arrays on the GPU while composing polynomial operations.", "group": "Learn", "url": "resident-operation-chain.html", "text": "Resident operation chain Upload two polynomials, transform both, multiply matching evaluations, inverse-transform, then download only the final result. The prototype preserves inputs and retains queued buffers. Native OpenFHE and sanitizer checks passed on compute 8.9 on November 19. Read full notes Keep the NTT chain on the device ¶ Upload two inputs, compose their operations on the GPU and download the final result: upload(a, b) → forward(a), forward(b) → pointwise multiply → inverse → download A session is one batch of work with its own resources. It owns a GPU stream (an ordered work queue) and returns handles, which let callers refer to stored polynomials. Operations leave their inputs unchanged. Queued work keeps input buffers alive even if the caller discards a handle. An upload first copies the caller’s array into a temporary CPU buffer owned by the session. Status: CPU tests cover delayed work, ownership and failure handling. On November 19, native OpenFHE and sanitizer checks passed on a server reporting RTX 4080 SUPER / compute 8.9. Both schedules also compiled for sm_120. The daily record contains the measurement and export results. B06 preparation continues with production ownership requirements, driver/context failure handling and RTX 5090 acceptance. November 20 preparation adds resident approximate basis conversion and ModUp/ModDown composition. Its new host checks and CUDA compilation are recorded separately in the conversion guide . This new path still requires native execution after the November 19 source. What is implemented ¶ Part Concrete implementation Public prototype resident.hpp : sessions, immutable polynomial handles, completions and owned host results Runtime and registry resident.cpp : private allocation provenance, dependencies, memory reservations, operation journal and cleanup Transport boundary resident_driver.hpp : device buffers/events and a single-stream command interface CUDA adapter resident_adapter.inc , included by ntt_kernels.cu , reuses the existing kernels Deferred host transport resident_host_driver.hpp : queues CPU work for ownership/failure tests; never represents GPU execution Each session fixes N and the exact ordered list of primes and roots. This list is its basis. Uploaded arrays must identify whether they contain coefficients in ordinary order or evaluations in the required bit-reversed order. Each uint64_t stores a remainder from 0 to q−1, called a canonical residue. A subview selects whole prime towers from an existing buffer; it keeps that buffer and its completion information alive. Transforms allocate new outputs instead of overwriting their inputs. Use one host thread per session, including handle access and destruction. The API is separate from the abstract proposed Backend . It supplies no production OpenFHE adapter, cache, CPU fallback, cross-device operation or multi-stream execution. For conversion, an explicit-layout upload(layout, values) can select a contiguous whole-tower subset of the session's basis. Coefficients use natural order; an explicitly declared evaluation layout uses bit-reversed order. The runtime validates parameter equality, tower order, length and every uploaded residue before queuing the copy. Compose a product ¶ On a real GPU, after creating the validated coefficient layout from the D9 contract : #include \"ntt/resident.hpp\" auto session = ntt::resident::make_cuda_session(coefficient_layout, 0); auto da = session.upload(a); auto db = session.upload(b); auto fa = session.forward(da); auto fb = session.forward(db); auto product = session.pointwise(fa, fb); auto coefficients = session.inverse(product); auto output = session.download(coefficients); auto values = output.wait_values(); session.close(); values is a read-only view of memory owned by output . Keep output alive while reading that view. wait_values() waits until the download finishes and checks that every value is in the allowed range. Matching the values against OpenFHE is a separate test. session.multiply(a, b) composes the same chain and validates both external arrays before the first command is submitted. For an independent inverse input, upload_evaluation(values) explicitly declares a bit-reversed evaluation array. It does not transform or reorder it. Every input must use the session’s complete prime list in the same order, with each value in range. subview() can then select adjacent whole towers. It cannot remove towers from the middle: for example, an array with active Q_l towers followed by P must be packed explicitly if unused Q towers lie between them. What moves, and when ¶ For the complete product, the runtime records seven operations and six fresh operand/result allocations: Transfer or operation Count Host-to-device input uploads 2 Device-to-device copies for immutable outputs 4 Device-to-host final download 1 NTT/pointwise kernel launches at N=65536 8 Twiddle tables, moduli and inverse lengths are uploaded once during session setup in four additional constant-array copies. These are separate from the input/output counts. Each forward or inverse first copies its input to fresh device storage; pointwise multiplication likewise preserves both inputs. Every CUDA copy, kernel and completion event in the adapter uses one explicit nonblocking stream. The existing launcher helpers accept that stream while their old callers keep their default behavior. There are no intermediate host-array downloads or per-operation host scans of resident coefficients. The prototype uses legacy cudaMalloc / cudaFree and owned pageable host staging. These can introduce synchronization or staging costs. No copy/compute overlap, allocation pool, cache reuse or performance improvement is claimed. Measure those costs on named hardware before optimizing them. Ownership and failure rules ¶ A private registry issues handles and validates their allocation/session IDs, layout and view range. Session IDs are unique and allocation IDs are never reused within a session. Copying a descriptor cannot create a trusted handle. Wrong sessions, domains, extents and noncanonical uploads fail before enqueue. Before submitting an operation, the runtime retains its input, output and temporary buffers, along with any earlier work it depends on. These retained references are called pins. Discarding a public handle cannot free memory that queued work still uses. When a section of the queue finishes, the runtime releases its pins. It keeps a record of a failed submission even when the caller never received a completion handle. State Required behavior Preflight or admission refusal Submit no command for that operation; report the error Pending work Retain allocation, staging, event and dependency leases Successful completion Release that prefix's in-flight pins; external handles keep their own storage Submission, query, wait or output-validation failure Reject new work, retain the failure and attempt to drain Cannot prove queued work has stopped Keep the driver and complete resource journal alive; do not free queued storage Later successful drain Reclaim pins; explicit close still reports the original failure Completion::query() reports a failed outcome when a transport error is found; wait() propagates the saved error. close() stops submission, drains and invalidates polynomial handles. An already completed owned host result can outlive the session. Destruction is nonthrowing; call close() explicitly to observe errors. If work cannot be proved stopped, resources are deliberately retained until a later successful close or process exit. This is conservative failure handling, not a finished recovery service. Final deallocation errors are best-effort in destructors; prior recorded cleanup errors are checked during explicit close. Actual failed-context behavior still needs native verification. Bounded resources ¶ Defaults are 256 MiB device storage , including fixed CUDA tables, 128 MiB owned host staging , and 1,024 operation records per session. The CUDA setup also checks its temporary table staging against the host limit and releases it after setup synchronization. Payload reservations exist before native allocation and follow the resource's actual lifetime. After releasing buffers, the operation log keeps a small record of each completed operation. Completed work still counts toward the operation limit. Start a new session for another batch. These are prototype session limits, not a device-wide cache or a replacement for B07 admission/cache work . Observed checks ¶ 450 host control checks cover independent negacyclic convolution, immutable inputs, owned uploads, explicit/implicit dependencies, nested views, foreign/closed handles, budgets, every synchronous transport step, failures after enqueue, asynchronous errors, abandoned outputs and quarantine recovery. The conversion additions check every transport failure point, every failure after an enqueue, parameter-table budgets and retention through a failed drain. The same control tests pass AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and leak detection on Linux. The original NTT/product fixture matches pinned OpenFHE on 4,456,448 residues at N=65536 with an unsorted 55/50/60-bit basis. Cases cover zero, bounds, monomials and dense values, forward, independent inverse, roundtrip, product, input immutability and tower views. The extended conversion fixture checks both ModUp input formats, an independently generated ModDown input, composed operations, unchanged inputs and nonzero-offset tower views. It covers N=64/4096/65536, five active Q levels, mixed 30/50/55/60-bit primes, four coefficient patterns and an additional 40-Q/three-P dense case. Stock CUDA 13 compiles the adapter and the prepared native test for sm_89 and sm_120 . Five invalid CUDA-linked factory inputs reject without requiring a driver. These checks execute no device arithmetic. The exact fixture uses real OpenFHE NativePoly format conversion and multiplication. Its transform path reaches NativePoly::SwitchFormat through the format API. Root/modulus selection comes from the pinned OpenFHE implementation; complete arrays are compared, not approximate CKKS decryptions. The CUDA variant passed the earlier NTT/product fixture on reported compute 8.9 on November 19. The new conversion cases compile in both CUDA schedules for sm_89/sm_120; executing those cases on a GPU is still pending. Reproduce and verify next ¶ With the pinned OpenFHE install available, on the CPU workspace: cmake -S . -B build/native-cpu -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\ -DNTT_ENABLE_CUDA=OFF -DNTT_REQUIRE_CUDA=OFF \\ -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON \\ -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" cmake --build build/native-cpu -j2 ctest --test-dir build/native-cpu -R '^resident_' --output-on-failure The session collector now requires resident_runtime_host , resident_openfhe_host , the CUDA-linked preflight, and the GPU-labelled resident_cuda_exact in the applicable modes. On a rental, it also runs a dense resident smoke case with each of the four Compute Sanitizer tools. Skipping sanitizers remains an explicit limited run. On the next RTX 5090 rental, run the current GPU answer and error-handling tests, the new resident CUDA test, and the memory/threading sanitizer tools. Then use a profiler to measure where execution time goes. D11/P2 remains open for primary-device acceptance, driver/context failure handling and hardware-counter profiling. Next: Implementation backlog →"}, {"title": "Run & debug", "summary": "Pause a small CPU transform and inspect each pair of values.", "group": "Learn", "url": "vscode-debugging.html", "text": "Run & debug The eight-coefficient example prints its input, evaluations and recovered coefficients. It runs without CUDA hardware. Use the terminal commands on a supported CPU platform. The supplied VS Code debugger configuration targets macOS. Read full notes Run and debug the NTT in VS Code ¶ Use the N=8 CPU example to follow an eight-coefficient polynomial. The forward transform computes its values at eight special points; the inverse recovers the coefficients. No CUDA hardware is needed. The terminal commands work on supported CPU platforms; the supplied VS Code debugger setup targets macOS. One-time setup ¶ Open the repository folder in VS Code: code . If VS Code asks, install the recommended extensions: - CMake Tools - C/C++ - LLDB DAP Wait for CMake Tools to finish configuring build/debug . The workspace uses Xcode's lldb-dap , the program that lets VS Code control the debugger. Install the Xcode command-line tools before using this configuration. Run the small example ¶ From a terminal: cmake -S . -B build -DNTT_ENABLE_CUDA=OFF cmake --build build --target ntt_debug_example ./build/ntt_debug_example Open the command palette with Cmd+Shift+P , choose Tasks: Run Task , then choose Run: NTT debug example . The expected output is: natural-order coefficients: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 bit-reversed evaluation values: 5 0 13 8 9 11 5 8 recovered coefficients: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 roundtrip passed Debug it step by step ¶ Open examples/step_by_step.cpp . Click left of the transform.forward(values) line to create a breakpoint. Open Run and Debug with Cmd+Shift+D . Select Debug: NTT example (step by step) . Press F5 . The program first stops at main because stopOnEntry is on. Press F5 again to reach the breakpoint. Press F11 to step into NegacyclicNtt::forward in include/ntt/ntt.hpp . Use F10 to advance one source line at a time. Start by watching values (the array), upper_index and lower_index (the two positions being combined), and twiddle_power (their modular multiplier). Then inspect stage_pair_distance , group_start_index and pair_offset to understand how the loops choose that pair. For N=8 , the forward transform has three stages with pair distances 4 , 2 , and 1 . Expand values after each butterfly to see the array change. The code overwrites the same array rather than creating a new one. Other shared commands ¶ Cmd+Shift+B : configure and build the debug example. Test: All CPU checks : build and run the complete CPU test suite. Debug: CPU unit tests : generate the N=1024 fixture and debug the tests. The debugger deliberately targets the CPU reference. CUDA kernel debugging will be added on the RTX machine using cuda-gdb or Nsight after the native CUDA build is available. CodeLLDB crash on current macOS/Xcode ¶ Recorded workaround for CodeLLDB 1.11.4 The recorded CodeLLDB 1.11.4 / LLDB 19.1.7 setup crashed with SIGBUS on AppleClang symbols. Xcode lldb-dap stopped correctly at main . If an old lldb configuration appears, run Developer: Reload Window and select the lldb-dap configuration. Next: NTT & butterflies →"}, {"title": "Prepare basis conversion", "summary": "Understand approximate CRT and compose basis conversion with resident NTT operations.", "group": "Learn", "url": "basis-conversion-preparation.html", "text": "Prepare basis conversion Preserve OpenFHE's unreduced CRT sum and source-tower order when constructing conversion plans. Standalone probes passed the November 19 Ada run. The newer resident ModUp/ModDown chain passes CPU tests and CUDA compilation; its GPU run is pending. Read full notes Prepare basis conversion before the next GPU rental ¶ We can prepare conversion arithmetic and reference answers on the M1 Pro. The November 19 run checked the CUDA implementation on compute 8.9. The RTX 5090 hardware check remains in the next combined session. What the converter computes ¶ A polynomial has N coefficients. For each source prime q_i, a tower holds N residues. Towers occupy consecutive sections of the array. A conversion changes the primes under which these coefficients are represented. Let Q be the product of all active source primes, and let p_j be one target prime. The coefficient at position k is converted with \\[ \\begin{aligned} \\widehat Q_i &= Q/q_i, \\\\ u_i(k) &= a_i(k)\\widehat Q_i^{-1}\\bmod q_i. \\end{aligned} \\] \\[ \\begin{aligned} b_j(k) &= \\Bigl(\\sum_i u_i(k) \\\\ &\\qquad\\cdot(\\widehat Q_i\\bmod p_j)\\Bigr)\\bmod p_j. \\end{aligned} \\] Here a_i(k) is the input residue under q_i. The inverse of the product of the other source primes scales that residue. Multiplying by the reduced product and summing gives the target residue b_j(k). The sum follows OpenFHE's approximate CRT convention. Reducing the complete sum modulo Q first can change b_j(k). Independent tests include inputs that detect this change. The plan snapshots the source and target primes in their given order. Shortening the active source prefix or a Hybrid digit requires a new plan. Its constants depend on that complete source product. Shoup multiplication ¶ The source scaling uses a precomputed constant w. Store \\[ \\begin{aligned} w' &= \\left\\lfloor\\frac{w2^{64}}q\\right\\rfloor, \\\\ h &= \\left\\lfloor\\frac{xw'}{2^{64}}\\right\\rfloor, \\\\ r &= xw-hq. \\end{aligned} \\] x is the input residue, w is the inverse above, and q is its source prime. For canonical x and w with q below 2^63, h underestimates the exact quotient by at most one. Therefore r lies between zero and 2q, and one conditional subtraction produces a residue below q. The host computes the high product with a 128-bit integer. CUDA uses __umul64hi . The per-coefficient Shoup operation uses multiplication and subtraction. The general target-product operation currently retains the reference 128-bit remainder calculation. Performance selection follows native measurements. Local checks and the rental batch ¶ The independent Python oracle computes the unreduced sums with arbitrary-size integers. It covers 30/50/55/60/63-bit primes, source counts through 40, both directions, boundary values, and rejected input shapes or parameters. The OpenFHE comparison covers N=4,096 through 65,536, 30/50/55/60-bit primes, and source counts 1, 3 and 40. It checks every coefficient in both directions using zero, q−1, monomial and deterministic dense inputs. Source towers are deliberately unsorted. The Hybrid fixtures also compare the reusable converter against their independent CRT answers, including shortened digits and the P-to-Q correction used by ModDown. ctest --test-dir build/openfhe-compat/ntt-build \\ -R 'basis|arithmetic_bigint|openfhe_hybrid' --output-on-failure The prepared native tests are cuda_shoup_exact and cuda_basis_exact . The collector requires both and runs their sanitizer checks before timing. The CUDA probe assigns one target coefficient to each thread and loops over the source primes. Its OpenFHE fixture supplies exact expected arrays. On November 19, both schedules compiled for sm_120, and the native probes passed on a server reporting RTX 4080 SUPER / compute 8.9. The conversion test compared 107,675,648 residues against pinned OpenFHE in each build. All four sanitizer tools passed the selected scopes. The RTX 5090 session remains the primary-device check. Native validation of the new resident ModUp/ModDown composition, GPU Hybrid key switching, and tuned arithmetic remain later deliverables. Keep ModUp and ModDown in the resident chain ¶ The resident prototype now connects the existing NTT and basis-conversion operations without downloading their intermediate polynomial arrays. A session uses one active Q prefix followed by all its P towers. Create a separate session when that active prefix changes. switch_basis(input, target_layout) takes coefficients and computes the unreduced approximate CRT sum under a disjoint destination basis. Both bases must be contiguous selections from the session's parameter tables. mod_up(input) accepts Q coefficients or Q evaluations. It preserves the original Q values in evaluation format, computes the P coefficients from Q coefficients, transforms P, and packs Q followed by P. Its output is always in evaluation format, matching OpenFHE. mod_down(input) accepts QP evaluations. It inverse-transforms only P, converts P coefficients to the Q correction, transforms that correction, subtracts it from the original Q evaluations, and multiplies by P-inverse. The output contains Q evaluations. The public API , queue implementation , and shared arithmetic helpers show the actual implementation. Compare OpenFHE's pinned ApproxModUp and ApproxModDown . This path follows CKKS with reduced-noise mode OFF; it does not implement the BGV correction factors or a complete Hybrid key switch. After importing and validating the exact Q/P parameters, the call sequence is: // qp_layout describes active Q followed by P, in coefficient format. auto session = ntt::resident::make_cuda_session(qp_layout); auto q_layout = qp_layout; q_layout.towers = ntt::proposal::q_prefix(*q_layout.parameters, q_layout.active_q); auto q = session.upload(q_layout, coefficient_values); auto qp = session.mod_up(q); auto reduced = session.mod_down(qp); auto result = session.download(reduced); auto evaluation_values = result.wait_values(); session.close(); Keep result alive while reading evaluation_values . ModDown changes scale using the modular inverse of P. Compare the composed result with OpenFHE's corresponding operations; do not assume it recovers the original input. Temporary tables, memory and errors ¶ Inputs remain unchanged, and each result has a new allocation. Each basis switch builds and uploads a small parameter table; ModDown's final correction also uploads its moduli and P-inverse values. These copies contain parameters, not intermediate polynomial arrays. Stats::parameter_uploads counts them separately from polynomial uploads/downloads and device copies. An upload → ModUp → ModDown chain has one polynomial upload, three parameter uploads and one final polynomial download, excluding the session's fixed setup tables. The runtime reserves device memory for those parameter tables and CPU memory for their staging. These budgets count the requested table/staging payloads; temporary CPU plan objects and allocator overhead are separate. Queued operations retain the tables and staging through completion. If submission fails and the stream cannot be proved stopped, the existing quarantine keeps these resources alive too. Per-operation budgets apply; a composed call can fail after earlier operations were queued. The original input remains unchanged, and there is no automatic CPU retry. This prototype builds tables per operation. Reusable conversion-plan caches, allocation pools and performance selection remain later work. The new composition needs its own native CUDA and sanitizer run; the November 19 standalone conversion results do not validate later code. What has been checked locally ¶ All 29 CPU/OpenFHE CTests pass after adding the conversion chain. The resident control suite passes 450 checks, including injected failures and parameter-table memory limits, and passes AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and leak detection. The shared fixture checks both ModUp input formats and an independent ModDown input against the actual OpenFHE public operations. Cases include all five active prefixes of a five-Q/three-P context and a 40-Q/three-P dense case, preserving the given prime order. Both default and legacy CUDA builds compile the extended fixture for sm_89 and sm_120 with the stock CUDA 13 compiler. Driver-free constructor/factory preflight tests pass. No execution or timing of the new conversion kernels is claimed here. The next native resident_cuda_exact run and its sanitizer smoke will execute those prepared cases on the selected GPU. Next: Next GPU rental queue →"}, {"title": "GPU NTT implementations", "summary": "Learn what other GPU NTT implementations do and which ideas to test.", "group": "Research", "url": "gpu-ntt-survey.html", "text": "GPU NTT implementations Candidate improvements include faster modular products, nearby memory accesses, more values per thread and fewer CPU–GPU copies. The papers and source code suggest experiments. Only matching tests and measurements can show whether those ideas improve this project. Read full notes GPU NTT implementations ¶ Date: 2026-11-05 Project target: CKKS, OpenFHE 1.6.1, Hybrid RNS key switching, NVIDIA RTX 5090 ( sm_120 ), ring dimension 65536 Historical source snapshot: 363f972 ; see the current GPU report for later measurements. Evidence policy: implementation facts below come only from author papers, official repositories/source code, or NVIDIA documentation. Recommendations and inferences are explicitly labelled. Executive conclusions ¶ The aim is to produce exactly the same arrays as OpenFHE, then make that work faster. Three costs are worth measuring: reducing wide integer products modulo a prime, reading values far apart in memory, and repeatedly copying arrays between CPU and GPU. A profiler must establish which cost dominates. Arithmetic: test Shoup multiplication for fixed NTT multipliers, called twiddles. Test Barrett or Montgomery reduction for products of two changing values. These methods replace expensive remainder operations with bounded integer calculations. Layout: test a transpose/Four-Step schedule that places neighboring threads’ data together. The GPU can combine these accesses (coalescing). Thread work: let each thread keep four or eight values in its registers, and test whether this reduces waits between threads. Integration: keep buffers and keys on the GPU through Hybrid key switching: extend from Q primes to Q plus auxiliary P primes, compute, then return to Q. Evidence: use FIDESlib as the closest CKKS/OpenFHE comparison; measure complete operations as well as individual NTTs. These are recommendations from the sources below, not measured speedups for this repository. 1. Current implementation ¶ 1.1 Sourced facts from this repository ¶ This subsection describes the historical source snapshot named above. The current default butterfly has since changed; see the butterfly invariant . The historical CUDA code computes products modulo x^N+1. It folds the root factors, or twist, into its butterflies rather than making a separate pass. Its schedule is: Forward: DIF column pass followed by a DIF contiguous-tile pass. Inverse: DIT contiguous-tile pass followed by a DIT column pass and a separate scale kernel. Fixed split width and block size of 256. Limb-major storage [limb][coefficient] ; blockIdx.y selects the limb. One full table of powers of psi and psi^-1 , each [limb][N+1] . Two launches per forward transform for N > 256 ; three for inverse. Host upload and download on every public forward , inverse , and pointwise_multiply call; multiply at least keeps its two forward transforms, pointwise product, and inverse together on device. Device multiplication is currently (__uint128_t(a) * b) % q . CPU simulation shares the exact stage bodies with CUDA, and CUDA tests compare exact arrays to a CPU reference and Python big-integer oracle. Primary code: ntt_kernels.cu , dev_core.hpp , mod_arith.hpp , and cuda_ntt.hpp . 1.2 Strengths worth retaining ¶ Recommendation/inference: keep these properties through optimization: The exact mathematical contract: natural-order input, bit-reversed evaluation output, exact inverse, and exact equality to the CPU oracle. The shared simulator/device stage definitions where practical. Optimized arithmetic can sit behind a second policy while the reference policy remains unchanged. Limb-major RNS storage as the baseline. It maps naturally to one modulus per independent transform, pointwise CKKS arithmetic, and OpenFHE's DCRT-tower model . Precomputation at context construction rather than in a hot operation. Named-hardware benchmark provenance and automatic correctness gates. 1.3 Immediate limitations ¶ Observed source behavior Recommended experiment mul_mod uses 128-bit remainder Division-free Shoup and Barrett policies Column threads load a[r + 256*t] : 2,048-byte spacing Coalesced transpose/Four-Step; measure sectors per requested byte Full power table and a barrier after every radix-2 stage Packed twiddles, Shoup quotients, radix-4/8 register work Fixed 256-thread split Tune launch shape for N=65536 and real tower/batch counts Host vectors and synchronous copies Add device views, stream ownership, and reusable scratch Keep the simple reference implementation as a way to check answers. Reading and writing an array only a few times does not prove that the GPU uses memory efficiently: widely spaced requests may fetch many unused bytes. 2. Libraries and techniques ¶ Reference Technique to evaluate FIDESlib Exact OpenFHE interoperability, device state, transform-boundary fusion GPU-NTT / HEonGPU Merge versus Four-Step schedules, batches, streams, memory pools CKKS-GPU-CORE Eight values per thread and padded shared memory PhantomFHE Radix-8, lazy Shoup ranges, key-switch fusion TensorFHE Operation batching; tensor arithmetic is a separate research path ICICLE Explicit ordering, device domains, and asynchronous APIs cuFHE / cuHE Resident keys and stream-aware operations in a different FHE scheme Theodosian RTX 5090 cache/write-bandwidth limits and fusion tradeoffs Read the implementation evidence and source links 2.1 FIDESlib: the closest match to the project target ¶ FIDESlib is a server-side GPU library for CKKS that can exchange data with OpenFHE. The cited release states support for OpenFHE 1.6.1, Hybrid key switching, CUDA 12/13 and GPU architectures sm_70 through sm_120. It says its GPU arrays are compared bit for bit with a patched OpenFHE CPU reference. These claims make it a close reference for our encryption scheme, library version and target GPU. See the official README and its bit-compatibility design record . Its forward transform uses Cooley–Tukey (CT) butterflies; inverse uses Gentleman–Sande (GS) butterflies. The code loads groups into shared memory, coordinates nearby threads within a warp, and reads/writes 16 bytes at a time. Templates select Shoup or Barrett modular arithmetic. Other template choices combine an NTT with the next operation, such as rescaling, plaintext multiplication, ModDown or key-product accumulation. This can avoid storing and reloading an intermediate array. See NTT_1D.cu , INTT_1D.cu , NTTfusions.cuh , and ModMult.cuh . Its Hybrid pipeline has separate kernels for digit decomposition, extending the prime basis (ModUp), and reducing it (ModDown). It assigns prime towers to devices and tracks dependencies with CUDA streams and completion events. The scheduling code can record repeated work as a CUDA Graph. The high-level polynomial object records whether auxiliary primes are present and offers combined key-switch operations. See Conv.cu , LimbPartition.cu , and RNSPoly.cpp . For this project: Use FIDESlib to study exact OpenFHE interoperability and transform-boundary fusion. Preserve QP towers and compare the complete Hybrid operation, not only NTT latency. Inference: FIDESlib is both a design reference and a competitor baseline. Before designing a new OpenFHE patch layer, evaluate whether this project's intended contribution is (a) independent kernels integrated directly into stock OpenFHE, (b) an alternative backend with a stable ABI, or (c) improvements that can be contributed to FIDESlib/OpenFHE. Duplicating its entire compatibility layer without a differentiator is high effort. 2.2 GPU-NTT / HEonGPU: Merge NTT and Four-Step NTT ¶ Özcan and Savaş compare an iterative “Merge NTT” with a Four-Step NTT. Their homomorphic-encryption tests include lengths 2^12 through 2^16; proving workloads use larger lengths. They choose kernel counts and block shapes to make neighboring memory accesses combine efficiently. Their results distinguish time for one transform from transforms completed per second. The paper reports cases where iterative Merge has higher throughput despite Four-Step’s more local accesses. See the author paper, Two Algorithms for Fast GPU Implementation of NTT , especially its design and evaluation sections. The cited GPU-NTT source provides Barrett arithmetic for 32- and 64-bit integers, with modulus limits of 30 and 61 bits respectively. Transform lengths are specialized at compile time, and configuration tables choose kernels for each size. The library supports prime towers, batches and caller-selected CUDA streams. It uses different execution paths for smaller rings and larger transforms. See the official repository , ntt_launch_impl.cuh , and modular_arith.cuh . HEonGPU uses GPU-NTT inside a GPU-resident BFV/CKKS library. Its paper describes a GPU memory pool, pinned host pool, and multi-stream execution in addition to the NTT itself. See HEonGPU and the official repository . For this project: Compare Merge and Four-Step schedules with matched modulus, order and batching. Tune N=65536 across tower counts rather than copying another GPU's launch table. 2.3 CKKS-GPU-CORE: memory-centric CKKS and 8-point-per-thread NTT ¶ Jung et al. keep up to eight NTT values in each thread’s private registers. Threads exchange values through shared memory between their small local transforms. Extra padding changes shared-memory addresses to reduce bank conflicts, where accesses compete for the same memory bank. For basis conversion, threads cover output primes and coefficients, add input-tower products, and then apply Barrett reduction. See Over 100x Faster Bootstrapping in Fully Homomorphic Encryption through Memory-Centric Optimization with GPUs and the official CKKS-GPU-CORE repository . The source implements two NTT phases, register arrays of eight coefficients per thread, Shoup butterflies, padded shared memory, batched ModUp, and fused ModDown/NTT epilogues. See NttImple.cu and Context.cu . For this project: Test eight register-held values per thread, padded shared memory and conversion/NTT fusion as separate measured changes. 2.4 PhantomFHE: radix-8 NWT, lazy Shoup butterflies, and scheme-level fusion ¶ Phantom supports the BFV, BGV and CKKS encryption schemes on CUDA. Small transforms run in one block. Larger transforms use two phases, eight values per thread and padded shared-memory exchanges. Its CT forward butterflies use Shoup multiplication and temporarily allow residues in [0,2q) or [0,4q). This is lazy reduction: postponing some reductions rather than keeping every intermediate below q. Its GS inverse also postpones final reduction and scaling. See the official repository , ntt_1d.cu , fntt_2d.cu , and butterfly.cuh . The Phantom paper describes intra-kernel fusion that reuses register temporaries and inter-kernel fusion across operations such as NWT plus scaling and ModDown plus correction. Its key-switch path connects ModUp, evaluation-key inner product, and ModDown on a supplied stream. See Phantom: A CUDA-Accelerated Word-Wise Homomorphic Encryption Library and eval_key_switch.cu . For this project: Study radix-8 staging and stream-aware key switching. Prove each lazy-residue range before removing reductions; a different butterfly convention is not automatically array-compatible. 2.5 TensorFHE: tensor-core NTT and operation-level batching ¶ TensorFHE breaks a 32-bit NTT into matrix multiplications for Tensor Cores. Its aim is high throughput across many CKKS operations, rather than minimum time for one operation. It changes the layout from (batch, limb, N) to (limb, batch, N), placing the same prime tower from different operations together in memory. Independent matrix products use separate streams. See the author paper, TensorFHE , especially Sections IV-C and IV-D. For this project: Measure operation batching and limb/batch layouts for throughput. Tensor arithmetic is a separate experiment with different width and exactness requirements. Inference/caution: do not make tensor-core NTT the first implementation path. TensorFHE's arithmetic decomposition and throughput objective differ from OpenFHE's usual 50–60-bit RNS towers and this project's immediate exact-compatibility goal. Establish a fast integer-core Shoup/Barrett backend first; treat tensor cores as a separate experimental backend. 2.6 ICICLE: useful API ideas, not a direct CKKS backend ¶ ICICLE's official NTT API exposes stream, batch size, row/column batch layout, input/output ordering, device-resident flags, async execution, reusable per-device domains, fast-twiddle mode, and an explicit mixed-radix selection. See the official NTT API . For this project: Borrow the API ideas: explicit order, batches, device state and readiness. Verify transform semantics and licensing before treating ICICLE as a CKKS backend. Inference/caution: ICICLE is primarily a proof-system field library, not an OpenFHE CKKS/Hybrid implementation. Its API concepts transfer; its field choices and transform semantics must not be assumed compatible. Its CUDA backend also has source-availability/licensing conditions that should be checked before any reuse. 2.7 cuFHE/cuHE: historically relevant but not the integration model ¶ cuFHE implements TFHE gates and credits cuHE's GPU NTT; its API supports CUDA streams and keeps a public bootstrapping key initialized on the GPU. See the official cuFHE repository and official cuHE repository . For this project: Keep reusable keys resident and expose stream dependencies. TFHE gate bootstrapping is a different workload from CKKS Hybrid switching. Inference/caution: TFHE gate bootstrapping has different polynomial sizes, dataflow, and key-switch structure from CKKS Hybrid RNS. cuFHE is not a suitable algorithmic baseline for the OpenFHE integration; it is mainly historical evidence for GPU-resident keys and stream-level concurrency. 2.8 Theodosian: RTX 5090 changes the optimization priority ¶ Theodosian profiles CKKS on RTX 5090. It reports that its optimized NTT and basis-conversion kernels are limited by how fast data move through the L2 cache. Its NTT uses two kernels and can generate twiddles during execution, trading extra arithmetic for fewer table reads. Small tower counts leave GPU capacity unused, so it batches work while the data still fit effectively in L2. For Hybrid key switching, it reuses multiple polynomials in cache and schedules complementary work. CUDA Graphs reduce repeated launch setup for fixed operation sequences. See Theodosian . The paper reports that batching can hurt after the footprint exceeds the 5090's effective L2 capacity, and that key switching benefits more from cache-aware multi-polynomial execution and graph scheduling than from additional local kernel fusion once the baseline is already heavily fused. For this project: Profile the actual RTX 5090's L2 traffic and working-set size. Test twiddle generation, cache-aware batching and graphs only after measuring their tradeoffs. 3. Algorithms and arithmetic ¶ 3.1 CT/GS versus Stockham and Four-Step ¶ Sourced observation: Phantom and FIDESlib use CT forward plus GS inverse; GPU-NTT compares iterative Merge and Four-Step schedules; ICICLE exposes mixed-radix and ordering choices. Recommendation: keep CT/DIF forward and GS/DIT inverse as the first optimized path because the current repository already has the equivalent ordering contract and no bit-reversal pass. Add a Four-Step/transpose prototype only as a benchmarked alternative for the strided column pass. A Stockham autosort path may offer regular accesses and natural ordering, but it normally ping-pongs global buffers; for the target sizes, its extra full-array traffic must beat the cost of the current uncoalesced gather before adoption. 3.2 Mixed radix and register staging ¶ Sourced observation: CKKS-GPU-CORE and Phantom use up to eight values per thread and radix-8 local transforms; GPU-NTT notes higher-radix butterflies as a further optimization. Recommendation: implement radix-4 first, then radix-8. Radix describes how many values a local transform combines at once. Registers are private storage for one thread; shared memory lets threads in a block exchange values. Test this sequence: Load 4/8 values per thread using coalesced vectorized transactions. Execute the local stages in registers. Store into padded shared memory for the permutation between local groups. Use warp shuffles where partner lanes remain within a warp. Store once at the end of the phase. This schedule can reduce multiplier-table reads, block-wide waits (barriers), and shared-memory accesses compared with assigning only one butterfly to each thread per stage. Measure register use and speed to check the benefit. 3.3 Modular multiplication ¶ Sourced observation: GPU-NTT uses Barrett with explicit modulus-bit bounds; Phantom and CKKS-GPU-CORE use Shoup values for twiddles and lazy butterflies; FIDESlib provides Shoup and Barrett paths. Recommendation: use three explicit primitives rather than one universal mul_mod : mul_const_shoup(x, w, w_shoup, q) for twiddles, N^-1 , basis constants, and evaluation-key constants when precomputation is useful. mul_var_barrett(a, b, q, mu) or a benchmarked Montgomery alternative for pointwise variable products. reduce_wide(acc, q, mu) for basis-conversion/key-switch accumulators with a proved accumulator bound. Retain the current __uint128_t % q only as host/device test oracle. Test each optimized primitive exhaustively around 0 , q-1 , lazy bounds, and the maximum supported OpenFHE prime size. 3.4 Twiddle representation ¶ Recommendation/inference: replace [psi^0, ..., psi^N] as the only device representation with: stage/phase-major twiddles in the order consumed by warps; a parallel Shoup quotient table; optional compact “seed” twiddles for on-the-fly expansion in the phase with O(N) table traffic; a metadata record containing transform convention, direction, modulus, ordering, and supported lazy range. The CPU reference can continue to build a simple power table; a packing step should produce the device format and be independently cross-checked. 4. RNS layout and Hybrid state ¶ 4.1 Baseline layout ¶ Recommendation: keep coefficient data limb-major: poly[component][digit-or-batch][limb][coefficient] Here adjacent coefficients are adjacent in memory. A limb is one prime tower. Neighboring threads can then load nearby coefficients for the same prime, and all threads in a block can use the same modulus constants. Views should describe the spacing between dimensions so batches can be interpreted without copying the arrays. 4.2 Extended-basis state ¶ Recommendation: make these states explicit and non-interchangeable: Q, coefficient Q, evaluation QP, coefficient QP, evaluation Q denotes the ciphertext primes; P denotes extra primes used during key switching. “Coefficient” and “evaluation” identify what the stored numbers mean. A buffer view must record the active Q and P counts, current level, array order, device and queued-work dependencies. Otherwise the adapter could drop needed towers or transform values that are already transformed. 4.3 Target Hybrid pipeline ¶ Recommendation/inference: design the first end-to-end kernel graph around OpenFHE's actual Hybrid formulation, approximately: input c2 in Q/evaluation -> digit partition / inverse NTT as required -> Q-digit to QP basis extension -> forward NTT of extended digits -> evaluation-key multiply-accumulate into two QP outputs -> approximate ModDown QP -> Q -> add/fold c0 as required by the exact OpenFHE operation Read the selected OpenFHE 1.6.1 source to determine exactly where transforms and rounding occur, then test those boundaries. Two formulas can describe the same modular result yet select different intermediate integers. Exact array compatibility requires OpenFHE’s choices at each checked step. 4.4 Fusion candidates ¶ High-value, low-ambiguity experiments: INTT final scaling + decomposition pre-scale. Basis-extension output + first forward-NTT load/prologue. Forward-NTT final store + evaluation-key dot product when register and occupancy costs permit. INTT or NTT boundary + ModDown correction. Pointwise accumulation across Hybrid digits, deferring reduction only under a proved bound. Combining operations in one kernel (fusion) can save memory traffic. It can also use more registers or shared memory, leaving room for fewer active threads, or make cache reuse worse. Theodosian reports that more fusion is not always faster on RTX 5090. Measure each combined version. 5. Streams and memory ¶ NVIDIA documents that asynchronous host/device copies require pinned host memory and can overlap with kernels in other streams. It also documents global-to-shared asynchronous copies and pipelines, and states that CUDA Graphs reduce repeated launch setup cost for fixed workflows. See the CUDA Best Practices Guide , asynchronous copy documentation , and CUDA Graphs documentation . Recommended API split: NttContext : immutable moduli, roots, packed twiddles, reduction constants, launch table, and allocator handle. DeviceRnsView : pointer(s), dimensions/strides, basis/domain/order metadata, device, and readiness event. enqueue_forward/inverse(view, stream) : no allocation, no synchronization, no host copy. convenience host methods: pinned staging plus explicit or documented synchronization. reusable scratch arena sized for maximum supported level/digit count. captured CUDA graphs for fixed ring dimension, level, digit count, and operation sequence. Recommendation/inference: try cuda::memcpy_async /pipeline only after the coalesced phase layout is fixed. The current column gather has no useful sequential “next tile” per block to hide without first changing data ownership; async copy cannot repair uncoalesced addresses. 6. Correctness and measurement ¶ 6.1 Correctness ladder ¶ Check primitives, butterflies, full transforms and polynomial products before composing basis conversion and Hybrid operations. Compare complete arrays with the same parameters and keys. The arithmetic guide explains the independent oracles; the benchmark plan defines the full matrix and required record. 6.2 Required performance views ¶ Separate setup, resident-device events, transfer-inclusive calls and complete operations. Report latency and throughput across ring sizes, widths and tower counts. Retain raw samples, named hardware, peak memory and profiler counters; the collector guide explains the implemented scopes. A kernel-only time excludes CPU–GPU copies and other call costs. Compare it only with a measurement that excludes the same work. 6.3 Baselines ¶ This repository's current pre-Barrett kernel, labelled clearly as a correctness baseline. Optimized kernel with identical transfers. GPU-NTT or another standalone GPU NTT with matched modulus/order/batch where license and semantics permit. FIDESlib 2.2/OpenFHE 1.6.1 for NTT microbenchmarks and, more importantly, full Hybrid key switch/rotation. Stock OpenFHE 1.6.1 CPU, with build flags and thread count recorded. Do not compare one implementation's kernel-only time with another's transfer-inclusive time, nor one-limb latency with an entire RNS polynomial. 7. Priorities ¶ Priority Deliverable P0 Real-GPU exact checks, arithmetic policies, device-buffer/stream contract P1 Shoup/Barrett kernels, register staging, coalesced layout, measured dispatch P2 Explicit Q/QP state, exact basis conversion, resident keys, safe fusion P3 Hoisting, operation batching, graph replay, whole-inference profiling The integration plan tracks completion; the benchmark plan defines the workload and acceptance record. The priorities here do not mark a roadmap gate complete. 8. Licensing and provenance ¶ This section is informational, not legal advice. FIDESlib: MIT license in its official repository. Its OpenFHE interoperability currently depends on patches, which should be reviewed separately for upstream/license provenance. GPU-NTT: Apache-2.0. HEonGPU: check the repository's current license before copying code; concepts described by the paper can be independently implemented. PhantomFHE: GPLv3. Copying its source into a differently licensed project can impose copyleft obligations. Use the paper and behavior as design evidence unless the project intentionally adopts compatible licensing. CKKS-GPU-CORE: CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 in the repository. The non-commercial/share-alike terms make direct code reuse unsuitable for many projects. Independently implement paper-level ideas and preserve clean-room provenance. ICICLE CUDA backend: source availability and the backend license have changed across releases. Verify the exact version's terms before any reuse. cuFHE/cuHE: their official repositories state their licenses; verify individual files before reuse. Recommendation: continue the repository's clean-room policy. Cite papers and inspect behavior/interfaces, but write original kernels. If code is adapted from MIT/Apache sources, preserve required notices and record the exact file/commit. Do not copy GPL or CC BY-NC-SA kernel code unless the project's license decision explicitly permits it. 9. Next implementation steps ¶ Follow the current roadmap and backlog , rather than treating this November 5 survey as the live tracker: Validate the current source and resident chain on RTX 5090; collect profiling. Test Shoup/Barrett arithmetic and coalesced layouts independently against the reference path. Compare radix-4/8 variants under the same timing boundary. Select measured configurations for real tower/batch counts. Extend resident buffers into exact base conversion and Hybrid key switching, with FIDESlib as a relevant complete-operation comparison. Keep explanations tied to the selected implementation. Four logical array movements do not prove coalesced traffic, and more fusion or larger batches can hurt when they increase register pressure or exceed cache capacity. Primary sources ¶ Papers, repositories, and NVIDIA documentation Özcan and Savaş, Two Algorithms for Fast GPU Implementation of NTT ; GPU-NTT source . Özcan and Savaş, HEonGPU ; HEonGPU source . Yang et al., Phantom ; PhantomFHE source . Jung et al., Over 100x Faster Bootstrapping through Memory-Centric Optimization with GPUs ; CKKS-GPU-CORE source . Fan et al., TensorFHE . Agulló-Domingo et al., FIDESlib source and documentation . Choi et al., Theodosian . Ingonyama, ICICLE NTT API . Vernam Lab, cuFHE and cuHE . NVIDIA, CUDA C++ Programming Guide , CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide , and Nsight Compute documentation . Next: Benchmarks & tuning →"}, {"title": "Choosing the GPU", "summary": "Choose a GPU by measuring this integer workload and its memory needs.", "group": "Research", "url": "gpu-selection-survey.html", "text": "Choosing the GPU RTX 5090 is the development target; RTX 4090 is the planned comparison device. Consider larger cards when measured memory use or speed justifies them. Published bandwidth and Tensor Core scores do not predict exact modular arithmetic speed. Compare complete operations with matching inputs. Read full notes Choosing a GPU for CKKS ¶ This hardware assessment was recorded on November 6, 2026. RTX 5090 is the development choice; choosing production hardware still requires the tests below. The working set is all the arrays, keys and temporary buffers that must fit in GPU memory at the same time. The listed specifications and old prices provide context; they are not live quotes or measured program speeds. Decision ¶ Role GPU Reason Primary development RTX 5090 32 GB, 1.792 TB/s bandwidth, Blackwell sm_120 Compatibility control RTX 4090 Ada sm_89 ; useful when owned or rented More capacity / enterprise support RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell 96 GB ECC; evaluate when the working set exceeds 32 GB Later production comparison H200 / B200 Larger HBM and bandwidth; benchmark the complete workload Use the 5090 for development. Select a production GPU only after measuring resident memory and end-to-end Hybrid key switching. Measure integer arithmetic and memory ¶ These GPU functions compute exact remainders using 32- or 64-bit unsigned integers ( u32 / u64 ). RNS stores each polynomial under several primes, with one array per prime. Tensor TOPS measures trillions of operations per second for supported Tensor Core arithmetic; it does not measure these modular operations. NVIDIA's Tensor Core type table does not list arbitrary u32 / u64 modular operations. Relevant primitives include mul.hi , mul.lo , and mul.wide and __umul64hi . The native instruction table does not publish a directly comparable 64-bit multiply throughput. Measure Shoup/Barrett instruction chains rather than deriving an “INT64 TOPS” ranking. Official specification comparison ¶ Read the table as hardware specifications, not a ranking of NTT speed. Bandwidth is the maximum published memory-transfer rate. L2 is the GPU’s shared cache; CC means compute capability, which selects a CUDA architecture. INT32 TOPS is peak ordinary 32-bit integer throughput, not modular-product throughput. ECC is memory error correction. A dash means the cited source did not publish a comparable number. GPU Memory Peak bandwidth L2 CC INT32 TOPS Power RTX 5090 32 GB GDDR7 1.792 TB/s 96 MB 12.0 104.8 575 W RTX 4090 24 GB GDDR6X 1.008 TB/s 72 MB 8.9 41.3 450 W RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell 96 GB ECC GDDR7 1.792 TB/s 128 MB 12.0 126 600 W L40S 48 GB ECC GDDR6 864 GB/s Not stated 8.9 — 350 W A100 80 GB SXM 80 GB HBM2e 2.039 TB/s 40 MB 8.0 19.5 400 W standard H100 SXM 80 GB HBM3 3.35 TB/s 50 MB 9.0 30 Up to 700 W H200 SXM 141 GB HBM3e 4.8 TB/s 50 MB 9.0 — Up to 700 W B200 SXM 180 GB HBM3e Up to 8 TB/s 126 MB 10.0 — Up to 1,000 W CC 12.0/10.0 compilation requires CUDA 12.8+. RTX 5090 memory correction is not the same enterprise reliability/support offering as RTX PRO or data-center ECC. L40S L2 is not stated on its product page; the full AD102/L40 figure must not be silently substituted. Primary sources for the table ¶ Open NVIDIA specification sources and qualifications NVIDIA's CUDA GPU compute-capability list maps RTX 5090 and RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell to CC 12.0, B200 to CC 10.0, H100/H200 to CC 9.0, RTX 4090/L40S to CC 8.9, and A100 to CC 8.0. NVIDIA's CUDA architecture/toolkit matrix lists first toolkit support and ongoing support; CUDA 12.8 added sm_100 , sm_101 , and sm_120 compiler support. The RTX Blackwell architecture whitepaper provides the RTX 5090's 170 SMs, 21,760 CUDA cores, 32 GB GDDR7, 1.792 TB/s, 96 MB L2, 575 W, 104.8 INT32 TOPS, and built-in GDDR7 single-bit correction. It also provides RTX 4090 comparison figures. The RTX PRO Blackwell architecture whitepaper provides 96 GB ECC GDDR7, 1.792 TB/s, 128 MB L2, 600 W, and 126 INT32 TOPS. The shorter product datasheet confirms the main board specifications. The official L40S product page specifies 48 GB GDDR6 with ECC, 864 GB/s, 18,176 CUDA cores, and 350 W. The Ada architecture whitepaper documents the 96 MB L2 full-AD102/L40 configuration; the current L40S page does not separately state L2. The A100 product page specifies 80 GB HBM2e, 2,039 GB/s for SXM, and 400 W. NVIDIA's Ampere architecture analysis documents 40 MB L2 and SECDED protection of HBM, L2, L1, and register files. The H100 datasheet specifies 80 GB, 3.35 TB/s, and up to 700 W. NVIDIA's Hopper architecture analysis documents 50 MB L2 and its architecture comparison. The H200 datasheet specifies 141 GB HBM3e, 4.8 TB/s, and up to 700 W. NVIDIA's HGX component table specifies H100/H200/B200 capacity and bandwidth and a configurable limit up to 1 kW per GPU. The Blackwell tuning guide documents B200/GB200's 180 GB memory support and 126 MB L2. NVIDIA's GPU memory-error management guide documents HBM ECC correction and error-management behavior for Ampere, Hopper, and Blackwell data-center families. Cost and availability snapshot ¶ Prices in the original assessment were regional, time-sensitive reference points. Compare the complete workstation or VM cost and measured work per hour. Read the historical pricing evidence Prices are volatile, regional, and not directly comparable between a bare card and a complete cloud VM. The following are official reference points, not a purchasing quote: NVIDIA lists the RTX 5090 from $1,999 . NVIDIA launched the RTX 4090 from $1,599 , but its current official product page no longer provides a meaningful live purchase price. A used or already-owned 4090 can still be economical; a new project should not assume launch-price availability. NVIDIA Marketplace listed a partner RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition at $14,079.99 when this report was checked. That is about seven times the RTX 5090 list price for three times the VRAM, professional ECC positioning, MIG, enterprise support, and about 20% more published non-Tensor INT32 peak—not seven times the kernel performance. See the official NVIDIA Marketplace listing . Google Cloud lists an RTX PRO 6000 96 GB virtual-workstation GPU at $1.09565 per GPU-hour in the first displayed region, before the VM, storage, network, and any licensing charges. The page states that price and availability vary by region. See Google Cloud GPU pricing . AWS Capacity Blocks list examples of about $5.97 per H100-hour and $14.21–$14.80 per B200-hour , sold as eight-GPU blocks in the shown regions. These are suitable for bounded cross-GPU experiments, not evidence that either card is cost-effective for daily development. See AWS Capacity Blocks pricing . Inference: local RTX 5090 ownership is the best development economics when the card remains well utilized. A cloud RTX PRO 6000 is an attractive way to validate the 96 GB path without buying a workstation card. H200/B200 rentals should be delayed until optimized kernels and a fixed benchmark harness exist, otherwise expensive time will mostly measure unfinished code. Interpretation for this project ¶ Comparison Published difference What still needs measurement 5090 versus 4090 1.78× bandwidth, 33% more VRAM/L2, about 2.54× INT32 peak Actual NTT and key-switch speedup PRO 6000 versus 5090 Same bandwidth, 3× VRAM, 33% more L2, about 20% more INT32 peak Benefit from capacity, reliability, and batching H200/B200 versus RTX Larger HBM and higher bandwidth Modular arithmetic, cache behavior, and end-to-end cost At N=65536, a 40-tower polynomial uses 65536 × 40 × 8 = 20 MiB . This is only one polynomial. Evaluation keys, temporary arrays under both Q and auxiliary P primes, precomputed NTT multipliers (twiddles), and other ciphertexts all need memory too. Measure the peak with all required keys loaded before choosing the GPU’s capacity. Recommended acquisition strategy ¶ For dated provider rates, billing rules and the first paid session, read the GPU rental market survey , checked November 13, 2026. Keep those rental observations separate from this hardware assessment's historical purchase prices. Develop and tune on RTX 5090; check workstation power and cooling. Retain or rent RTX 4090 for cross-architecture correctness and timing. Evaluate RTX PRO 6000 if measured residency exceeds 28 GB or enterprise reliability/support is required. Rent H200/B200 after the optimized kernels and fixed harness exist. Mandatory cross-GPU benchmark gate ¶ Run the same commit, parameters, and timing boundaries. Record toolkit, clocks, power policy, median, p95, and peak memory. Layer Required cases Arithmetic u32/u64 add/subtract, high/low products, Shoup, Barrett, reference remainder; registers and spills Memory / NTT Coalesced copy, strided gather, L2/DRAM cases, forward/inverse at N=65536, realistic tower counts Hybrid ModUp, ModDown, decomposition, key MAC, relinearization, rotation; one request and batches Correctness Exact pinned-OpenFHE arrays at every intermediate boundary: NTT and Hybrid core See the benchmark plan for counters and result records. Keep kernel-only and transfer-inclusive timing separate. Selection rule ¶ Keep RTX 5090 as the primary choice if all required data fit in its memory and the complete operation takes no more than 15% longer than on the fastest tested card. If memory capacity is the only problem, compare RTX PRO 6000 with the rental and deployment cost of H200. Use profiling to learn whether memory access or modular multiplication causes the main delay before changing the arithmetic or buying different hardware. Decision to revisit ¶ Keep RTX 5090 as the development target. Revisit production hardware after measuring the complete workload, memory footprint and cost. Tensor throughput alone cannot rank exact modular arithmetic. Next: GPU rental market →"}, {"title": "GPU rental market", "summary": "Compare dated rental prices and the total cost of a useful session.", "group": "Research", "url": "gpu-rental-market-survey.html", "text": "GPU rental market The November 13 survey compares RunPod RTX 5090 rates with a saved Vast.ai offer search. Recheck availability and the final quote before renting. Check compiler tools and profiling permissions. Include setup and storage costs, and download all results before ending the rental. Read full notes GPU rental market: where to run this project ¶ Checked November 13, 2026 · single-GPU CUDA development · N=65536 Start with a short RTX 5090 rental billed as you use it (on-demand). Compare RunPod Community Cloud with verified Vast.ai offers. Choose a machine that supports our compiler and performance-measurement tools, then compare the cost of the whole session. Keep an RTX 4090 as a compatibility control. H100/H200/B200 rentals become useful when a measured workload justifies their capacity or performance. This survey uses provider pages and a saved marketplace search. No machine was booked or tested. The observed prices do not reserve a GPU. Check the actual configuration and final total before renting. What we are renting for ¶ The first primary-device session verifies the RTX 5090 environment and runs the current native CUDA checks. The offline preparation is complete; the D11 hardware gate remains open. Learning, documentation, host testing and most build preparation can continue on a CPU. Requirement Project choice Primary GPU One full RTX 5090, 32 GB, sm_120 Control GPU One full RTX 4090, 24 GB, sm_89 Workload Exact u64 modular arithmetic and N=65536 transforms; later OpenFHE Hybrid operations Environment Linux x86_64, SSH, C++20 compiler, compatible driver and CUDA development toolkit Profiling Compute Sanitizer; Nsight Compute performance-counter access when profiling is scheduled Practical build allowance About 8 vCPUs, 16–32 GB host RAM and 40–80 GB disk as a starting recommendation The CPU/RAM/disk allowance is our planning estimate, not a measured minimum. More host resources can save paid compilation time. A runtime-only CUDA image may lack nvcc ; choose a development image or install the toolkit. RTX 5090 compilation needs CUDA 12.8 or newer, and the driver must support the toolkit actually used: see NVIDIA's architecture/toolkit matrix . Our offline build used CUDA 13.0; that does not establish a rental's compatibility. Use the hardware assessment for GPU specifications. Floating-point scores, image-generation speed and language-model token rates do not predict the speed of our exact integer calculations. MIG divides some GPUs into smaller instances; such a slice is not a full GPU. Record regional model variants separately too. How the market is divided ¶ Rental model What matters for us Host marketplace, such as Vast.ai Variable price, location, CPU allocation, driver and host reliability; inspect each offer GPU containers, such as RunPod Pods and AutoDL containers Convenient environments; host-controlled driver and profiling permissions still matter GPU virtual machines, such as TensorDock and Jarvislabs VM More operating-system control; inspect passthrough, resources and counter permissions Data-center GPU clouds, such as Lambda Useful for later HBM/capacity comparisons; select the actual instance size Serverless endpoints and inference APIs A different product; exclude their rates from an interactive CUDA development comparison Choose on-demand for the first debugging session so a provider does not interrupt the work to reclaim discounted capacity. Spot rentals may be useful later for repeatable jobs that save results and can restart. Monthly reservations make little sense until GPU use is regular. Renting first also lets us measure usage before buying a workstation. Published price snapshot ¶ All dollar prices below are USD per hour . RunPod values are the public Pods rate card with its hourly toggle selected. TensorDock's figure is an advertised starting price. Jarvislabs values use the one-GPU selector. These are different bundles, so compare CPU/RAM, disk and final billing before choosing. Provider / product GPU Published rate Evidence and qualification RunPod Community Cloud RTX 5090 · 32 GB $0.69 Pods pricing , Community tab; 9 vCPUs / 35 GB host RAM shown RunPod Secure Cloud RTX 5090 · 32 GB $1.39 Same page, Secure tab; compare the available region and deployment total RunPod Community Cloud RTX 4090 · 24 GB $0.34 Same page, Community tab; 6 vCPUs / 41 GB host RAM shown RunPod Secure Cloud RTX 4090 · 24 GB $0.89 Same page, Secure tab TensorDock on-demand VM RTX 4090 · 24 GB From $0.37 Model page ; CPU/RAM allocation affects the price; current full VM quote unverified Jarvislabs on-demand RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell · 96 GB $2.19 Pricing , 1 GPU; 28 vCPUs / 160 GB host RAM shown Jarvislabs on-demand H100 SXM · 80 GB $3.49 Same page, 1 GPU; later data-center comparison candidate The RunPod page says it was updated November 12. Both cloud tabs were inspected in a browser; the default Secure tab alone does not reveal Community pricing. Neither these public cards nor the following sample guarantees stock. Vast.ai: observed offers, not a fixed tariff ¶ At 01:16 UTC on November 13 , a public, read-only offer search returned 64 records. The query requested one RTX 5090 or RTX 4090, verified/rentable hosts, on-demand access, reported reliability at least 0.99, CUDA capability at least 13.0, at least 16,000 in the API's host-RAM field, 80 GB available disk and one direct port. Returned records were checked against those filters. GPU Offers in this response Compute-only range Sample median RTX 5090 33 $0.4533–$1.0533/h $0.72/h RTX 4090 31 $0.40–$0.80/h $0.5333/h These are the response's dph_base values, rounded to four decimals, not market-wide minima or an exhaustive regional inventory. The endpoint reported truncated: false , but the figures describe only this response. The cheapest 5090 record was offer 50556739 in Sichuan, China; the cheapest 4090 was 58010958 in Türkiye. Location suitability depends on your connection. The saved sample and query preserve the observed rates and relevant host metadata. Public IP addresses are omitted. dph_total includes the endpoint's default disk allowance, not our proposed 80 GB build disk , so it is not used as an all-in quote. Available disk capacity is not included storage. Transfer, selected storage and tax can increase cost. Vast's pricing documentation explains host-set pricing and separate compute, storage and bandwidth charges. Host verification and a reliability score do not establish Nsight permission or guarantee uptime. Requery with your region and full resources before renting. AutoDL and Lambda: why no current rate is quoted ¶ AutoDL is worth checking if its network locations and CNY payment options suit you. Its market was not verified to a specific current offer in this survey. Compare the exact GPU name, CPU/RAM, disk and driver; treat 4090D/5090D labels as distinct configurations rather than substituting them into a 4090/5090 result. Keep CNY and USD quotes separate until you have a dated exchange rate and payment fees. Lambda offered GPU instances, but its public table explicitly takes effect November 16, 2026 at 00:00 UTC , three days after this survey. It directs users to their account for the current price. The table also defaults to eight-GPU instances while quoting per GPU-hour : multiply by the actual GPU count. Those future-effective rates were excluded from the November 13 comparison; this article has not rechecked them since. Provider fit and tradeoffs ¶ Start by comparing a RunPod Community RTX 5090 with a suitable verified Vast.ai host . Match the complete resources, region, driver and profiling permissions; the lowest advertised compute rate may not be the lowest usable session cost. TensorDock is a 4090 VM option, but its advertised floor is not a live cart. AutoDL is a regional candidate if access and CNY payment suit the user; no current offer or counter permission was verified here. Jarvislabs' 96 GB card and Lambda's data-center GPUs become relevant when measured capacity or performance justifies them. Check the actual product/GPU count before comparing. This is a shortlist for one small CUDA session, not a global price ranking. Enterprise clouds need separate matched region, quota and instance quotes. The cost beyond GPU-hours ¶ Use one currency and the whole instance rate: \\[ C_{\\mathrm{session}} = r_{\\mathrm{instance}}t_{\\mathrm{billable}} + C_{\\mathrm{disk}} + C_{\\mathrm{transfer}} + C_{\\mathrm{tax/fees}}. \\] In the formula, r is the hourly price of the whole machine and t is the billable time. You pay while setting up software and building dependencies as well as while running kernels. Add any separate CPU, RAM, disk, transfer and tax charges. Money added to an account is prepaid credit; the experiment may use only part of it. Check the region, minimum deposit and billing unit (per second, minute or hour) on the final quote. Billing issue What the official sources establish RunPod Pods Per-second billing ; no ingress/egress fee; one hour's configuration credit needed to deploy on-demand RunPod retained volume $0.10/GB/month running and $0.20/GB/month stopped; terminating the Pod removes its attached volume disk RunPod network volume Separately retained storage: $0.07/GB/month below 1 TB; remove it separately when finished Vast.ai Compute, disk and bandwidth are separately priced; stopping halts compute but keeps disk charges TensorDock Stopping and unreserving the GPU changes an on-demand server to storage-only pricing; verify reserved resources AutoDL On-demand billing is per second with a CNY 0.01 minimum; paid disk expansion can remain billable while powered off Jarvislabs Paused instances retain storage charges; deleting compute does not necessarily remove separately created storage Export reports before deleting resources. Check the billing/resource view after cleanup, including independent volumes, snapshots and reserved IPs where used. Small-session budget examples ¶ The following is our arithmetic , using the observed public rates and exactly four or ten billable hours. It excludes all storage, transfer, tax and separately billed resources; it is not a quotation or a promise that the work finishes in that time. Configuration 4 hours of compute 10 hours of compute RunPod Community RTX 5090 $2.76 $6.90 RunPod Secure RTX 5090 $5.56 $13.90 RunPod Community RTX 4090 $1.36 $3.40 Vast sample's lowest RTX 5090 compute rate About $1.81 About $4.53 Jarvislabs RTX PRO 6000, 1 GPU $8.76 $21.90 For a first attempt, a $10–$15 spending allowance is a reasonable planning choice for one short 5090 session plus modest extras. It is neither a provider minimum deposit nor an upper bound: a long build, retained disks, transfer or a different quote can exceed it. Record actual costs and choose the next allowance from observed session time. At the RunPod Community card rates, 5090 compute costs about 2.03× the 4090. The 5090 must finish a fixed job over 2.03× faster to win on compute cost alone. That speedup is unmeasured; development value and extra VRAM are separate reasons to choose it. Rank later providers by dollars per verified workload, including transfers, rather than dollars per advertised GPU-hour. Profiling access can decide the provider ¶ A machine can run CUDA correctly and still reject Nsight Compute's hardware counters with ERR_NVGPUCTRPERM . NVIDIA's permission guide explains the host/driver permissions and container capabilities involved. SSH access or a root shell inside a container does not itself establish those permissions. Counter access is unverified for every specific offer in this survey. Ask whether Nsight Compute hardware-counter collection is permitted on the chosen GPU and product, and whether the provider enables the required host permissions. A full VM is worth considering when container restrictions block the experiment, but it also needs verification. A successful nvidia-smi is not that verification. Record three separate outcomes: whether the answers are correct, whether sanitizer tools found memory/threading errors, and whether profiling succeeded. A machine without hardware-counter access can still run correctness tests. Use another host for counter collection, and keep that task unfinished until its data exist. Recommended first rental ¶ Finish preparation on CPU. Use the pinned source/dependencies and the offline handoff . Check image compatibility before copying binaries; rebuilding OpenFHE can consume paid time. Compare two live carts. Select one full on-demand RTX 5090 on RunPod Community and a suitable verified Vast.ai host. Match region, driver, CPU/RAM, an explicit disk allocation and the complete hourly total. Resolve profiling access. Obtain a clear answer for the chosen product before a counter-dependent session. Confirm SSH and development tools too. Run the prepared collection path. Follow the handoff's gpu_session.py command with the actual CUDA and OpenFHE paths. Capture environment, native exactness, sanitizer and benchmark reports. The runner is not the complete later profiling/failure-test suite. Export evidence and end billing. Save logs and cost records, then stop or delete according to the provider's resource rules. Perform analysis offline. The next primary-device evidence is a reproducible RTX 5090 session on the current source. The later Ada run does not close that gate. Keep D11 open until its required evidence exists. Revisit 96 GB cards or H100/H200/B200 after measuring residency and complete-workload performance. Sources and freshness ¶ Pricing pages, linked billing documentation and NVIDIA's permission guide were checked on November 13, 2026. The Vast JSON is a timestamped observation, with the exact read-only query and selected public fields. RunPod's two cloud tabs were read in Chromium. AutoDL's billing rules were available through official indexed documentation; a current GPU offer was not established. No checkout, paid instance, network-speed measurement or provider support confirmation was performed. Recheck price, inventory and permissions before spending. Next: Before renting a GPU →"}, {"title": "Integration roadmap", "summary": "Read the sequence of work and the checks required to finish each phase.", "group": "Plans", "url": "openfhe-integration-plan.html", "text": "Integration roadmap Build the NTT first, then prime-basis conversion, Hybrid key switching and OpenFHE integration. D labels a working milestone, not a calendar date. D11/P2 remains open. The current Ada batch passed, while RTX 5090 acceptance, driver/context failure handling and hardware counters remain pending. Read full notes Roadmap to OpenFHE integration ¶ Build in this order: NTT → base conversion → Hybrid key switching → OpenFHE . The Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) helps multiply polynomials by turning that task into separate products at special evaluation points. Base conversion changes which primes represent each coefficient. Hybrid key switching combines these operations with evaluation-key arrays to change a ciphertext’s key representation without decrypting it. The estimate is 95 working days plus 15 reserve days . D labels a working-day milestone and P labels a phase; neither is a calendar date or a continuous rental period. Preparation may happen early, but a phase closes only after its acceptance checks pass. Target OpenFHE 1.6.1, N=65536, RTX 5090 primary and RTX 4090 control. Baseline benchmarks also span N=4096–65536 and 30/50/55/60-bit primes in uint64_t . Last reviewed: 2026-11-21 (Asia/Shanghai). Live tracker ¶ Field Current value Working day D11 Active phase P2 — first RTX GPU baseline (Ada diagnostic passed) Active task Run the prepared native ModUp/ModDown and conversion timing checks in the next combined RTX 5090 session Next gate P2: compare CUDA and OpenFHE answers on real hardware, then record the GPU and baseline measurements (D11–D15) Overall state In progress; the temporary Ada GPU was tested, while primary-device and other required P2 checks remain unfinished The paired Ada analysis revalidates the local November 19 archive. It favors group twiddles in device-event measurements; resident host-call differences are smaller. Conversion-chain timing is now prepared for the next native run. P2/D11 remains open. See the next GPU rental queue for the offline work and the order of the bundled native checks. Hardware: RTX 5090 primary, RTX 4090 control. Use larger-memory hardware only when measurements justify it; see the GPU comparison . Read the tracker as follows: DONE means its acceptance checks passed; IN PROGRESS means some work or evidence is missing; NOT STARTED means implementation has not begun; BLOCKED means a dependency prevents progress. An exit gate is the set of checks required to finish a phase. An oracle is a separate reference calculation used to check answers. Phase Working days Status Exit artifact P0 — pre-plan correctness baseline Before D1 DONE CPU oracle, schedule simulator, CUDA source, exact OpenFHE NTT gate P1 — contracts and architecture D1–D10 DONE Accepted ADR , frozen operation graph and implementation backlog P2 — first RTX GPU baseline D11–D15 IN PROGRESS Native Ada diagnostic passed; RTX 5090 evidence, failure injection and full profiling pending P3 — production GPU NTT D16–D40 NOT STARTED Tuned, device-resident NTT dispatch P4 — exact base-conversion model D41–D49 NOT STARTED OpenFHE-exact CPU Q↔QP oracle and fixtures P5 — GPU base conversion D50–D61 NOT STARTED Exact and tuned ModUp/ModDown kernels P6 — Hybrid key-switch contract D62–D68 NOT STARTED Frozen decomposition/QP/MAC/ModDown graph P7 — GPU Hybrid key switching D69–D85 NOT STARTED Exact relinearization and rotation backend P8 — OpenFHE integration D86–D95 NOT STARTED Integrated backend with fallback and full tests P9 — risk reserve D96–D110 NOT STARTED Used only for integration and correctness risks Definition of exact compatibility ¶ Use the same parameters, input arrays, ciphertext components and evaluation keys in both programs. The GPU must then produce exactly the same values in every prime tower as the selected OpenFHE version. CKKS decrypts to approximate numbers, so similar decrypted answers are a useful second check but cannot prove exact array compatibility. Generate a key once and use those same key arrays for both paths; independently generated random keys are different inputs. The concrete upstream path is NativePoly::SwitchFormat for transforms and KeySwitchCore → EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt → ApproxModDown for Hybrid contributions. Every production kernel must pass, in order: arithmetic primitive versus the __uint128_t oracle; operation versus this repository's CPU reference; operation versus pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1; full pipeline versus OpenFHE using fixed inputs and keys; benchmark/profiler gate on named hardware. P0 — pre-plan baseline (DONE) ¶ Negacyclic CPU NTT and Python big-integer oracle. Shared CPU/CUDA stage bodies and portable schedule simulator. Correctness-first two-pass CUDA source. Exact public OpenFHE 1.6.1 transform compatibility for all target N . RTX 4090/5090 build targets and rented-GPU setup script. Benchmark and tuning methodology. Primary-device native acceptance; tracked separately in P2. P1 — contract and architecture freeze (D1–D10) ¶ Status: DONE. ADR 0001 accepts the contracts and implementation backlog; native runtime gates remain in later phases. Day Work Required output D1 Read forward column kernel and launch geometry Index and memory-access map for N=65536 D2 Derive forward column butterfly indices Worked p,g,j,u,v,r,d example and twiddle proof D3 Read inverse column kernel and DIT arithmetic Forward/inverse correspondence D4 Read contiguous tile kernels Proof that every global stage appears exactly once D5 Read constructor, buffers, and public API Host/device dataflow and allocation map D6 Read modular arithmetic and exact test gates Arithmetic bounds and adversarial-test list D7 Trace OpenFHE NTT/DCRT representation Natural/bit-reversed and coefficient/evaluation contract D8 Trace OpenFHE Hybrid implementation Exact Q -> QP -> MAC -> Q operation graph D9 Design buffer views and backend seam Device buffer/stream/cache API proposal D10 Architecture review Accepted ADR and implementation backlog Completed evidence is documented in the linked lessons and dated journal: D1–D4: block/thread mapping and stage proofs , with exhaustive CPU probes of shared CUDA stage helpers. D5–D6: buffer/copy map and integer bounds , including bigint tests. D7: OpenFHE roots, formats and tower packing . D8: Hybrid graph , with 18 CPU cases and 117,571,584 residue comparisons. D9–D10: checked backend metadata , the accepted ADR and implementation tasks . These complete P1's design work. They do not replace native CUDA lifetime, failure, exactness or performance checks. Historical test counts and intermediate tracker states remain in the journal . P1 exit gate: NTT/DCRT (D7) and Hybrid (D8) representation, ordering, and active-tower transitions are explicit. The exact pinned CKKS approximate-CRT and reduction order is identified (D8); broader production fixtures remain P4/P6 work. Buffer views record ring size, Q/P towers, level, representation, ordering, device, stream, and event ownership (D9 proposal and host checks; runtime implementation pending). The first integration seam and CPU fallback behavior are frozen by ADR 0001 . Architecture review accepted and implementation work has dependencies, observable acceptance criteria and explicit remaining runtime gates. P2 — first real GPU baseline (D11–D15) ¶ The November 14 temporary Ada batch passed 29 native CTests, eight sanitizer runs and 280 GPU timing jobs. A separate M1 Pro baseline covered 140 CPU jobs. The reported GPU was RTX 4080 SUPER/compute 8.9, not the primary RTX 5090 or planned RTX 4090 control. Hardware counters were blocked by the provider. Later offline preparation and the resident-chain prototype pass host tests and sm_89/sm_120 compilation. The November 19 batch then passed the new resident, Shoup and conversion checks on reported compute 8.9. Both schedules passed their native gates and 40 sanitizer stages across the pair. All 48 paired measurement jobs, four resident repeat runs and a CUDA timeline were exported and verified locally. Primary-device acceptance, actual driver/context failure handling and hardware-counter profiling remain open. November 20 preparation adds resident ModUp/ModDown composition. CPU/OpenFHE checks, failure tests and both CUDA schedule builds pass locally. The new conversion fixture is part of the existing native resident gate and its sanitizer smoke. That later code still needs a real GPU run. The November 19 performance analysis also awaits its raw archive in this workspace. Next session: run the current native checks, resident sanitizer scope and per-pass profiling on an actual RTX 5090. Use the split collector for GPU-only measurements and collect the CPU baseline locally. Day Work Required output D11 Provision the selected GPU and CUDA toolkit Reproducible environment record D12 Build native architecture, resolve baseline safety/build blockers and run smoke tests Native build, constructor/failure cleanup and validated smoke tests (B02) D13 Run exact gates over all N and modulus widths Correctness report D14 Profile with CUDA events and Nsight Kernel, L2, DRAM, occupancy, and register data D15 Analyze and publish baseline Raw samples, summary table, first bottleneck decision B01–B04 define the acceptance criteria. Native ownership/failure tests and exact arrays must pass before accepting timings. GPU-event timings and a timeline show how long work takes and when it runs. We still need counters showing how much memory is fetched, how the L2 cache and GPU memory are used, and how many warps can remain active (occupancy). Minimum cases: zero, one, q-1 , alternating bounds, deterministic random, forward, inverse, round-trip, evaluation-domain inverse, and polynomial multiplication. Record 50-, 55-, and 59/60-bit OpenFHE-compatible primes. P2 exit gate: OpenFHE == CPU reference == CUDA for every target size. Kernel-only and transfer-inclusive timings are separated. Actual sectors/requested byte and L2/DRAM traffic are recorded for the strided pass. P3 — production GPU NTT (D16–D40) ¶ D16–D25 — arithmetic and device API ¶ Days Work D16–D17 Introduce an arithmetic-policy interface while retaining the oracle path D18–D20 Implement Shoup multiplication and parallel twiddle quotient tables D21 Add adversarial Shoup tests at maximum supported modulus width D22–D23 Implement and test Barrett variable multiplication D24 Add device-buffer, asynchronous stream, and event API D25 Run complete exact regression and benchmark D26–D40 — layout, radix, and selection ¶ Days Work D26–D29 Prototype coalesced transpose/Four-Step replacement for the column pass D30–D32 Implement radix-4 register staging D33–D35 Implement radix-8 register staging and padded shared memory D36 Compare warp shuffle and shared-memory exchange for small stages D37 Add compile-time specialization for N=65536 D38 Run latency/throughput autotuning over real tower and batch counts D39 Freeze the per-device/per-size dispatch table D40 Production NTT gate and documentation update P3 exit gate: Exact transform for all target sizes and moduli. No optimized kernel silently uses device-side integer remainder. Device-resident forward, inverse, and pointwise operations compose without host synchronization. A selected latency and throughput configuration exists for every target size on the primary GPU. P4 — exact base-conversion model (D41–D49) ¶ Day Work D41 Locate and trace OpenFHE ModUp ( Q -> QP ) D42 Locate and trace OpenFHE ApproxModDown ( QP -> Q ) D43 Record precomputed constants, reductions, rounding, and table layouts D44 Extract fixed OpenFHE intermediate fixtures D45 Implement standalone CPU Q -> QP oracle D46 Implement standalone CPU QP -> Q oracle D47 Test every target level, tower count, and adversarial coefficient D48 Design base-conversion buffer and batched APIs D49 Review and freeze the exact contract P4 exit gate: the standalone CPU base-conversion arrays equal pinned OpenFHE at every intermediate boundary, not only after a complete key switch. P5 — GPU base conversion (D50–D61) ¶ Days Work D50–D52 Implement batched GPU Q-to-P basis extension D53 Exact Q-to-P tests and profiler pass D54–D56 Implement GPU QP-to-Q reduction D57 Exact QP-to-Q tests and profiler pass D58 Add dynamic CKKS level and tower-count support D59 Tune coefficient tile, output-tower mapping, accumulation, and reduction D60 Run the complete size/tower/batch benchmark matrix D61 Production base-conversion gate P5 exit gate: Exact Q-to-QP and QP-to-Q arrays. Dynamic active Q/P tower counts. NTT and base conversion share device buffers without conversion copies. Selected latency and throughput configurations are recorded. P6 — Hybrid key-switch contract (D62–D68) ¶ Day Work D62 Trace evaluation-key generation and storage D63 Trace digit decomposition and transform placement D64 Trace ModUp and evaluation-key multiplication D65 Trace ModDown and final ciphertext folding D66 Extract fixed intermediate fixtures from OpenFHE D67 Design GPU evaluation-key layout, cache, and lifetime rules D68 Freeze the full Hybrid operation graph P6 exit gate: all decomposition, basis, ordering, scaling, rounding, and final accumulation boundaries have exact OpenFHE fixtures. P7 — GPU Hybrid key switching (D69–D85) ¶ Days Work D69–D71 Implement device-resident digit decomposition D72–D73 Connect inverse NTT and Q-to-QP ModUp D74–D76 Implement evaluation-key multiply-accumulate in QP D77–D78 Connect QP-to-Q ModDown D79 Implement final c0/c1 accumulation D80 Validate relinearization D81 Validate automorphism/rotation D82 Exercise all target CKKS levels and digit counts D83 Run exact intermediate and final-array tests D84 Profile key cache, L2 behavior, memory footprint, and launch gaps D85 Production Hybrid key-switch gate P7 exit gate: Fixed inputs and evaluation keys produce exact OpenFHE tower arrays. Relinearization and rotations pass exact and decryption tests. Evaluation keys and constants upload once and remain cached. Full key-switch latency and batched throughput are reported. P8 — OpenFHE integration (D86–D95) ¶ Day Work D86 Implement the OpenFHE GPU backend adapter D87 Add CMake CUDA discovery and opt-in configuration D88 Map DCRTPoly towers to versioned GPU buffer views D89 Add parameter, twiddle, basis-table, and scratch caches D90 Add evaluation-key cache D91 Dispatch NTT, base conversion, and Hybrid key switching D92 Implement explicit unsupported-case CPU fallback D93 Finish stream/event lifetime, synchronization, and error handling D94 Run OpenFHE CKKS examples and relevant upstream tests D95 Integration release gate, documentation, and benchmark report Dispatch policy: invalid input or inconsistent format -> error valid same-format request -> validated no-op available + supported + verified -> CUDA backend unavailable/unsupported/unverified -> CPU only if allowed before submission busy/budget/allocation failure -> visible resource error submission attempted, then any failure -> visible error; no automatic CPU retry The opt-in adapter defaults to RequireDevice . See ADR 0001 for the accepted transaction, ownership, cache-budget and admission rules. Production integration remains a P8 gate. P8 exit gate: OpenFHE CKKS runs with Hybrid key switching through the GPU backend. Original CPU tests and fallback remain green. NTT, base conversion, relinearization, and rotation meet exact gates. Constants and evaluation keys remain resident across operations. All results identify hardware, clocks/power mode, driver, toolkit, compiler, commit, parameters, timing boundary, and correctness status. P9 — risk reserve (D96–D110) ¶ Use only for work that blocks a preceding exit gate: OpenFHE rounding/reduction-order differences; natural/bit-reversed ordering mistakes; CKKS level/tower corner cases; register spilling, shared-memory pressure, or poor occupancy; evaluation-key memory pressure and cache eviction; CUDA stream/event lifetime defects; primary-GPU compiler or architecture problems; cross-GPU dispatch regressions. Do not schedule new features in the reserve. Running an encrypted inference model begins only after P8’s checks pass. That needs a separate plan for model operations, scale changes, rotations, execution order, numerical accuracy and the time for a complete request. Progress log ¶ Dated milestones and their original evidence Date Day Change Evidence/commit 2026-11-06 D1 Created tracked integration plan; began line-by-line CUDA forward-column review; completed primary-source GPU selection survey dbf06cd 2026-11-07 D1 Added the N=8 VS Code debug example and native LLDB DAP setup; clarified CPU butterfly variable names; expanded the direct-sum and negacyclic derivations with locally bundled math rendering. CPU learning prerequisites are covered; the D1 CUDA index/memory map remains the next deliverable. Debug example and CTest gates; 57 formulas verified in the browser 2026-11-08 D1 Organized the documentation into an HTML engineering notebook with a daily journal, shared navigation, search, and a Pages workflow. Historical entries cite commits and are marked reconstructed. Implementation gates remain unchanged. Daily entry ; documentation build and regression checks 2026-11-09 D1 → D2 Completed both endpoint index/memory maps (4096 and 65536), with an interactive size comparison and portable schedule regression at N=65536. Next: column butterfly indices and twiddle proof. P1 exit gate remains open. Work-distribution map ; daily entry ; CPU schedule and explorer checks 2026-11-09 D2 → D3 Completed the column-pair/twiddle proof and six worked examples; exhaustive CPU execution of the real stage helper checked 270,336 pairs using 540,672 probes. Next: inverse-column correspondence. P1 exit gate remains open. Column proof ; column_mapping_check ; device_schedule_check 2026-11-10 D3 → D4 Completed inverse-column correspondence, factor-2/factor-N checks and normalization, plus inverse-mode explorer and worked examples. Next: contiguous kernels and exact stage coverage. P1 exit gate remains open. Inverse proof ; inverse_column_check ; daily entry 2026-11-10 D4 → D5 Completed row-kernel pair/twiddle equivalence and exact stage coverage. Added exhaustive CPU execution of both row-stage helpers plus all-size stage-list and endpoint checks. Next: constructor/buffer/API dataflow. P1 exit gate remains open. Row proof ; tile_mapping_check ; all 11 CTests passed 2026-11-10 D5 → D6 Completed constructor/allocation/transfer/API map and six static source-contract checks. Recorded constructor exception cleanup and device/stream ownership gaps. Next: modular arithmetic bounds and adversarial tests. P1 exit gate remains open. D5 map ; tests/docs/test_cuda_dataflow.py ; daily entry 2026-11-10 D6 → D7 Completed arithmetic bounds, canonical-range proof and adversarial-test inventory; added independent bigint and full-size 50/55/60-bit CPU gates. Next: OpenFHE representation/tower contract. P1 remains open. D6 contract ; arithmetic_bigint_check ; arithmetic_transform_check ; daily entry 2026-11-11 D7 → D8 Frozen the pinned OpenFHE NTT/DCRT array contract; full-size formula, format API, root, and three-tower checks pass. P1 remains open. D7 contract ; daily entry ; openfhe_representation_contract 2026-11-11 D8 → D9 Completed the pinned Hybrid graph and full-size intermediate-array gate; P1 remained open for ownership and architecture. D8 graph ; daily entry 2026-11-12 D9 → D10 Completed checked metadata and the backend API proposal; runtime implementation remained pending. D9 contract ; 78 host checks and 16 CPU/OpenFHE CTests 2026-11-12 D10 → D11 Accepted ADR 0001 and the implementation backlog; P1 is DONE. P2 awaits native GPU provisioning. ADR ; backlog ; daily review 2026-11-12 D11 preparation Completed stock CUDA sm_89/sm_120 compilation, baseline ownership/preflight fixes, native fixture compilation and tested failure-aware session collection. P2 preparation is in progress; D11 hardware acceptance and native gates remain open. Preparation guide ; 19 host/OpenFHE CTests; daily entry 2026-11-14 D11 diagnostic Supplied RTX 4080 SUPER reports compute 8.9; clean native run passed 23 CTests, 169279488 CUDA-gate comparisons and four sanitizer smoke tools. Exported reports and initial 46-bit latency sample. Primary RTX 5090 and remaining P2 gates stay open. Native session ; local report.json : GPU_CHECKS_PASSED Next: Implementation backlog →"}, {"title": "Architecture decision", "summary": "Understand the accepted rules for ownership, errors and OpenFHE integration.", "group": "Plans", "url": "architecture-decision.html", "text": "Architecture decision Keep inputs unchanged and return new results. Queued work must retain every buffer it could still access. The planned OpenFHE adapter replaces the caller’s object only after success. A permitted CPU path must be chosen before any GPU work is submitted. Read full notes ADR 0001: the accepted backend design ¶ Accepted November 12, 2026 · D10/P1 design decision An architecture decision record (ADR) explains a design choice and its constraints. This decision keeps polynomial arrays on the GPU and gives callers handles to them. Inputs stay unchanged; operations allocate new results. Each handle records the primes, array format, owner and dependencies. Queued GPU work keeps its required resources alive until it finishes. The first OpenFHE adapter will convert a DCRT polynomial, which stores one array per prime, between coefficients and evaluations. Callers must choose to use it. The adapter builds a separate CPU result and replaces the original only when every step succeeds. If permitted, it may choose the CPU before submitting GPU work. It must not automatically retry on CPU after GPU work may already have started. This accepts the D7–D9 contracts and backlog . It accepts a design, not native safety or speed. See the roadmap for current progress and the resident prototype for the implemented subset. 1. Review scope and evidence ¶ The reviewed source baseline is f349e72 . OpenFHE remains pinned to 1.6.1, commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f , using NativeInteger 64. The production target is N=65536 with canonical 50–60-bit RNS primes. Small teaching examples and schedule boundary tests remain useful evidence; they do not expand production support. P1 question Accepted evidence Remaining runtime gate Does every butterfly/stage have the correct addresses and factors? D1–D4 proofs , exhaustive shared-helper probes and CPU schedule checks Native compilation, launch geometry, barriers and races Are arithmetic bounds and reductions explicit? D6 contract , independent bigint checks and full-size CPU patterns Native 50/55/60-bit adversarial matrix Are OpenFHE formats, roots and tower arrays exact? D7 contract and real NativePoly/DCRTPoly checks Same fixtures through CUDA and the adapter Is the Hybrid graph exact? D8 graph , 18 full-size cases and intermediate-array comparisons Broader parameter/key coverage and GPU composition Are buffer identity and ownership explicit? D9 contract , 78 metadata checks and a real OpenFHE metadata bridge Private registry, actual events, allocation and failure tests The existing CUDA wrapper remains a synchronous correctness baseline. The proposed interfaces in backend_api.hpp have no runtime implementation or factory. ntt::proposal stays in place until implementation tests justify promotion. This record fixes the required behavior. It does not promise that the public C++ types or binary interface will remain unchanged. Implementation update 2026-11-16: a separate bounded resident-chain prototype exercises these ownership and operation rules with a deferred host transport and a compiled CUDA adapter. It does not implement the complete abstract Backend , enable the OpenFHE adapter, or establish native CUDA/runtime acceptance. 2. Freeze the representation and operation boundary ¶ These rules define what later implementations must preserve. A basis is an ordered list of primes; Q holds ciphertext primes and P holds auxiliary primes. Canonical residues are the integers 0 through q−1. The domain says whether an array contains coefficients or evaluations. A deep immutable context carries N, the full ordered Q/P moduli and exact primitive roots, and the full-context digit width α. Each polynomial carries its ordered tower references, active Q count L, domain, order and encoding. Coefficients use natural order; evaluations use the D7 bit-reversed order. Residues are canonical uint64_t values. Forward, inverse and pointwise operations validate their input layouts. Inverse includes N-inverse normalization. Public inputs are immutable; results have fresh storage. Private scratch/output may use in-place kernels. Views alias only complete contiguous towers and retain allocation/readiness leases. A shortened full-QP span cannot stand in for reduced QlP. Reduced-level P key indices retain their full-context offsets. D8's digit/complement transform placement, unreduced approximate CRT, B-before-A output order, accumulation before ModDown, and delayed-QP scaling remain exact requirements. A decryption result alone cannot replace them. Semantic validity and device capability remain separate. Mathematical validity of an alternative primitive root does not establish OpenFHE cache compatibility. The first adapter accepts only the pinned context's canonical minimum roots and rejects known conflicting registrations before using the shared FTT cache. The process must not populate that upstream cache with conflicting custom roots; its entries do not retain enough root identity to detect such external usage after the fact (see GetTables ). Broader custom-root support requires a new reviewed contract and fixtures. Returning a separate output buffer is called an out-of-place operation. With the current kernels it may require an extra copy inside GPU memory. Include that copy in memory and timing reports. Public in-place writes, partial-tower strides, peer transfers and cross-device handles are excluded from the initial API. 3. Accept the first OpenFHE seam and fallback policy ¶ The first adapter is an explicit opt-in DCRT operation with SetFormat -equivalent behavior. It packs towers through ConvertToInt() , performs a backend transform, imports a private candidate, and commits the candidate only after complete success. It does not globally replace DCRTPoly::SwitchFormat or patch NativePoly storage. Although GPU work may be queued internally, this first adapter waits before returning to its caller. One such complete call is called a transaction. Validate the DCRT label, each tower's label, dimension, ordered q/ψ pairs, length and canonical values, including on a same-format request. Verify both tower labels and the outer DCRT label when importing; metadata overrides alone do not convert data. Access to the same host object must be serialized by its caller. Condition Accepted behavior Valid object already in the requested format Return without conversion Available, supported, verified device operation Use the device route Valid but unsupported layout, unavailable GPU, or unverified operation CPU only under AllowCpuBeforeSubmit ; otherwise error Invalid object/metadata/residues Error under either policy Admission busy, budget exhausted or allocation failure Report the resource error; do not silently change routes First enqueue-capable call attempted, then any failure Propagate failure; no automatic CPU retry RequireDevice is the explicit default for a caller opting into this adapter. Applications choosing fallback must select AllowCpuBeforeSubmit . The existing OpenFHE CPU APIs keep their existing behavior. Commit the completed candidate through the pinned DCRT move assignment, which is declared noexcept in the reviewed source . All validation and import work precedes that commit. Recoverable failures leave the original host object unchanged. This promise does not cover process termination: some upstream constructors/copy operations are declared noexcept while allocating. Adapter fault tests must distinguish recoverable injected failures from upstream fatal allocation paths; no universal out-of-memory rollback claim is accepted. 4. Close the partial-submission lifetime gap ¶ A lease keeps a resource alive; a pin is a lease held by queued work. D9 requires them until completion. D10 adds an important error rule: create these ownership references before any call that could queue GPU work . That call may fail after partially submitting work, before the caller receives a completion handle. Stage Required ownership and error behavior Preflight Validate handles, choose the route, reserve checked resource credits and create an operation record Submitting Mark the transaction as submitted before attempting the first async copy/kernel/wait; the operation record already owns all leases Event recorded Publish an immutable completion tied to that event/generation; retain all input/output/plan/staging/scratch pins Submission or event-recording failure Keep the record privately, mark it failed, drain the owning stream; do not release memory merely because no handle was returned Completed successfully Make readiness visible and release operation pins; external handles retain their own leases Failed, quiescence not established Poison the backend session, reject further submission and quarantine affected resources until safe teardown A stream executes work in submission order (first in, first out). A consumer on another stream must wait for the exact producer event. Dependencies may refer only to earlier operations, preventing circular waits. IDs and generation numbers must never wrap around and accidentally identify old resources. If a producer fails, its consumers fail too. An error does not prove that the GPU stopped accessing memory, and discarding a completion does not cancel work. The registry validates all allocation, stream and event identities against its own session; user-supplied structs are not authority. Constructor unwinding uses RAII, each runtime call uses the owning device guard, and close() drains and reports errors. Destructors do not throw. The D5 raw-Impl cleanup gap is a release blocker tracked as B02, not a fix delivered by this ADR. Subsequent offline preparation repairs baseline ownership in source and compiles it; B02's native fault verification stays open. Do not automatically call cudaDeviceReset to recover a session in a shared process: it can invalidate allocations owned by other users of the device. An unrecoverable session reports failure and retains quarantine until safe owner-controlled teardown, potentially at the worker-process boundary. 5. Choose initial cache and admission limits ¶ Use these conservative, configurable defaults for the first backend/adapter implementation. They are engineering limits, not measured optimal settings. MiB means 2²⁰ bytes. Setting Initial decision Device cache budget 256 MiB per backend session Device operand/output/scratch budget Separate 256 MiB per backend session Owned host staging budget Separate 128 MiB per backend session Initial executor One explicit nonblocking CUDA stream per backend session Initial adapter concurrency One host adapter transaction at a time per session Admission when limits cannot be met Fail immediately with a specific error; no hidden waiting or automatic budget growth These numbers are maximum budgets. Creating a session does not immediately allocate all that memory. A second initial stream or concurrent adapter transaction reports a resource-limit/busy error. The accepted dependency model supports later multi-stream execution, but that capability remains disabled until its race/failure tests pass. Pure metadata helpers remain portable. Charge the allocator's requested allocation extents, including explicit padding, before allocating. These budgets bound accounted allocations, not CUDA's internal physical-memory reservation or driver overhead; measure those separately. Pending cache uploads count against the cache budget; an entry's pinned bytes are counted once. Operand/output/scratch allocations remain charged while any operation or external polynomial handle owns them. Host staging includes owned upload snapshots and retained download results. Register table-build/host-object memory separately in measurements; it is not covered by the staging cap. The initial allocator uses individually owned allocations, so trim_cache() reports actual released device bytes. A later pooling allocator must distinguish reusable suballocations from memory returned to CUDA and revise accounting before claiming the same statistic. Before the adapter's first submission, reserve credits for its complete checked transaction plan. Individual asynchronous backend operations reserve their own requirements before enqueue; a later operation can still fail after an earlier operation has submitted, which forbids automatic fallback for that transaction. Preserve the original host object in either case. A cached table or key can be reused only when all of D9’s identifying fields match. A lock protects one pending entry per upload so concurrent requests do not duplicate it. To free space, remove the oldest unused eligible entries. An entry is eligible only when no caller or queued operation retains it and no GPU work can still access it. If eviction cannot admit a request, return CacheBudgetExceeded ; if output/staging credits are unavailable, report the corresponding resource limit. Budget reduction is atomic: if the new limit cannot be met, retain the previous limit. The D8 full-context key payload is 16 MiB and one eight-tower forward/inverse plan has approximately 8 MiB of payload. Those explain why a 256 MiB cache is a reasonable initial ceiling; they do not bound a production key set. No peak memory or throughput claim follows from these defaults. 6. Alternatives reviewed ¶ Alternative Review result Raw pointer plus length Rejected: omits basis, format, generation and producer ownership Add global format hooks immediately Deferred: broadens the first seam before packing and rollback are tested Expose public in-place writes first Deferred: adds aliasing and concurrent-reader hazards before a native baseline Keep the current host-vector wrapper as the production backend Rejected: forces transfers and hides stream/device ownership Retry CPU after a CUDA failure Rejected: submission can be partial and dependencies may still be live Unlimited caching or automatic cache growth Rejected: defeats predictable admission and live-reader safety Replace approximate CRT with canonical CRT Rejected: changes pinned OpenFHE intermediate arrays Tune layouts/arithmetic before measuring the baseline Deferred: native correctness and named-hardware evidence come first The existing wrapper is retained as the baseline comparison during migration. No algorithm rewrite or runtime adapter is introduced by D10. 7. Accept P1 and track the implementation ¶ P1 exit requirement D10 disposition NTT/DCRT and Hybrid representation, ordering and active towers are explicit Accepted: D7/D8 source and CPU exactness gates Pinned approximate-CRT and reduction order are identified Accepted: D8 independent intermediate-array checks Buffer/device/stream/event ownership is specified Accepted: D9 metadata model plus D10 submission-failure rules First integration seam and CPU fallback are frozen Accepted: explicit DCRT adapter and the policy in section 3 The backlog gives each follow-up a phase, dependency and observable acceptance criterion. Native build/safety and exactness block benchmark claims. Runtime lifecycle and cache gates block promotion from ntt::proposal . The transactional adapter and application tests block production OpenFHE integration. D11–D95 retain their phase order; completing D10 does not mark any later implementation gate complete. Review validation reruns the 16-test CPU/OpenFHE Release suite, documentation unit tests, interactive mathematics, route/fragment checks, and desktop/mobile browser checks. Exact observed results are appended to the daily journal . At D10 acceptance, no CUDA compiler or GPU was available in the review environment. Later offline preparation added compiler checks, and the Ada batch supplied native baseline evidence. Primary-device and current resident-runtime acceptance remain open. Any later change to residue encoding, root conventions, public aliasing, submission/fallback boundaries, ownership, or resource-accounting semantics requires a superseding ADR and exact regression evidence. Budget values may be explicitly configured within the accepted admission rules; benchmark-based default changes must record the hardware and workload that justify them. Next: Implementation backlog →"}, {"title": "Implementation backlog", "summary": "Choose an implementation task and read the checks that will prove it complete.", "group": "Plans", "url": "implementation-backlog.html", "text": "Implementation backlog Validate the GPU baseline, then implement resident operations, basis conversion, key switching and the OpenFHE adapter in dependency order. Each task lists its required evidence. CPU preparation and successful compilation cannot replace a test that must run on an actual GPU. Read full notes Implementation tasks and acceptance checks ¶ The design has been accepted, but the production GPU implementation is still being built. Each task below lists work to do and the checks required to call it complete. Those checks are its acceptance criteria. The roadmap gives phase order; ADR 0001 defines the behavior each task must preserve. Current state: D11/P2 remains open. The temporary Ada baseline is measured; RTX 5090 acceptance and remaining native failure/profiling checks are pending. Tasks B01–B04 have local preparation and some results from real GPU runs. B06 has a limited prototype verified on reported compute 8.9. B05 and B09 also have native arithmetic/conversion checks from the November 19 batch. Production work on B07–B12 remains open. Preparing part of a later task does not complete its phase. B labels a backlog task, P a phase, and D a working-day milestone; these are not calendar dates. 1. Deliverables and dependencies ¶ Select a task ID for its acceptance criteria. The comparison scrolls horizontally. ID Planned phase/days Deliverable Depends on B01 P2 / D11 Named GPU environment and reproducibility record Accepted ADR B02 P2 / D12 Native build, constructor safety and validated baseline B01 B03 P2 / D13 Exact native CUDA/OpenFHE baseline matrix B02 B04 P2 / D14–D15 Reproducible timing, profiler evidence and first bottleneck B03 B05 P3 / D16–D23 Checked arithmetic policies and optimized reductions B03; B04 informs selection B06 P3 / D24 Resident buffers, explicit stream and owned completions B02, B03 B07 P3 / D24–D25 Plan cache, budget admission and lifecycle regression B06 B08 P3 / D26–D40 Measured NTT layout/radix selection and production NTT gate B04–B07 B09 P4–P5 / D41–D61 Broader exact basis-conversion model and GPU implementation B08; D8 graph B10 P6–P7 / D62–D85 Production Hybrid fixtures, keys, hoisting and GPU graph B09; D8/D9 contracts B11 P8 / D86–D93 Transactional OpenFHE adapter and integrated dispatch B08–B10 B12 P8 / D94–D95 Application, fallback and integration release evidence B11 B02's safety fixes are prerequisites for a trustworthy native baseline. They are scheduled explicitly inside P2 rather than silently assuming the current wrapper is failure-safe. B06/B07 implement lifecycle rules before production resident execution. B11 is the first OpenFHE host adapter; library tests against OpenFHE in earlier phases do not themselves integrate that adapter. 2. P2: establish the native baseline ¶ B01 — environment record ¶ Record the actual GPU model, memory and compute capability; driver; CUDA compiler/runtime; host compiler; OS; CMake flags; source commit; OpenFHE commit, NativeInteger size and build options. Record clocks/power mode and profiler versions before performance measurements. Start with RTX 5090; use RTX 4090 as the control. If only a control GPU is available, label that run and keep the primary-device gate open. Use native architecture compilation on the selected device; sm_120 requires an appropriate toolkit (the current configured target starts at CUDA 12.8). Acceptance: checked-in or attached environment record plus successful device, compiler and driver queries. A CPU machine, toolkit version string alone, or PTX fallback without a native build does not complete B01/B02. This task does not provision or rent hardware as part of D10. Evidence so far: the November 14 Ada diagnostic recorded native execution on a machine reporting RTX 4080 SUPER. It is neither the primary RTX 5090 nor the planned RTX 4090 control. B02 — native build and baseline safety ¶ Work in cuda_ntt.hpp and ntt_kernels.cu . Give allocated resources owners that release them automatically, including when construction fails (RAII). Remember which GPU owns them and select it for each runtime call with a device guard. Before queuing work, check array sizes for overflow and require every input residue to be in 0…q−1. Resolve compiler/architecture blockers exposed by the first native build. Keep the baseline arithmetic and transform convention intact. Evidence so far: unique ownership, device guards and input validation are implemented. Host tests cover six allocations and four uploads; the older Ada batch passed the native pre-call injection probe. That does not establish actual driver OOM, partial-transfer or failed-context cleanup safety. The tooling guide separates these scopes. Acceptance: native build and smoke run; injected failures at each allocation and constant upload unwind all prior allocations; invalid inputs submit no work; normal/destructor cleanup uses the owning device and does not throw. Update the D5 source-contract tests and buffer map to match the actual fix. Static source checks alone cannot complete this item. B03 — exact CUDA matrix ¶ Evidence so far: the older Ada batch passed the direct OpenFHE matrix, including 169,279,488 original-gate comparisons, the expanded benchmark matrix and bounded sanitizer smoke. The report records the tested workload and limitations. Repeat the current source on the primary device; older results do not validate the new resident runtime. Extend the native harness to import the exact OpenFHE fixture moduli, roots, input arrays and expected arrays. At N=65536 cover 50/55/60-bit primes, active Q levels and representative tower counts; include D7's deliberately unsorted 50/55/60-bit packing case and D8's actual context parameters where applicable. Exercise zero, one, q−1, alternating bounds, monomials, deterministic dense residues, independent evaluation input, forward, inverse, round-trip, pointwise and negacyclic product. Compare complete arrays against the CPU oracle and pinned OpenFHE using the same fixtures. Run CUDA Compute Sanitizer memory, race, initialization and synchronization checks on the affected kernels. Acceptance: every selected case passes exactly with a recorded native build and no relevant sanitizer errors. The current CLI's generated baseline uses 46-bit parameters; its success alone does not cover this matrix. Keep the existing CPU/OpenFHE and small schedule-boundary gates green. B04 — timing and bottleneck report ¶ Evidence so far: the full-matrix report compares measured public-call and resident-event timings. Raw samples, validated reports and a selected CUDA timeline are retained. Hardware counters were unavailable, so there is no accepted first bottleneck decision. The collector now separates GPU-only and local CPU collection, with hash-checked resume. Host control-flow checks and a real Linux CPU run validate that split. Current GPU-only collector and resident-runtime execution still need native verification. Use the first-run analysis only for its earlier 46-bit sample. Separate construction/table upload, device-resident CUDA-event timings, transfer-inclusive wall time, and explicit synchronization. Add raw samples, warm-up, median/p5/p95, workload metadata and ptxas resource reports. Profile the strided column pass's sectors/requested byte, L2/DRAM traffic, bank conflicts, occupancy, registers/spills and launch gaps. The session collector , called by the AutoDL helper , now requires CUDA/OpenFHE coverage, retains logs and propagates failed stages, including benchmarks. Host control-flow tests exercise those failure classifications. Expanded batch tests cover partial completion, strict input/scope matching, and hashed restart records. Optional bounded Nsight captures and compiler resource output are prepared, not executed here. The old 46-bit wall-clock row does not complete B04. Acceptance: reproducible raw results, exact-gate status and one evidence-backed first bottleneck decision on named hardware. Follow the benchmark plan . Do not label the current wrapper's transfer-inclusive wall time as kernel-only time. 3. P3: implement resident NTT safely ¶ B05 — arithmetic policies ¶ Offline preparation on November 19 adds canonical Shoup multiplication, independent bigint checks and a native CUDA intrinsic probe. That probe passed on reported compute 8.9 in the November 19 batch. The current NTT dispatch keeps its reference arithmetic while the candidate is validated. See conversion preparation . Keep the simple exact implementation for checking answers. Add Shoup multiplication for precomputed constant multipliers and Barrett reduction for two changing operands. Prove their valid input ranges and generate the required tables before selecting either optimization. Run independent bigint, maximum-width adversarial, complete-transform and native OpenFHE comparisons before selecting optimized dispatch. Acceptance: no hidden integer-remainder path in selected optimized kernels; range/overflow tests and hardware-specific exactness evidence accompany each policy. Alternative encodings need a superseding contract, not a changed label. B06 — resident runtime and registry ¶ The November 19 batch passed the resident exactness and selected sanitizer checks on reported compute 8.9. Driver/context failure handling and primary RTX 5090 acceptance remain open. Offline preparation 2026-11-16: the resident-chain prototype implements private handles, one-stream dependencies, immutable outputs, bounded reservations, owned staging and failed-work quarantine. Its deferred host transport passes OpenFHE comparisons and lifetime/error tests; the real CUDA adapter compiles for sm_89/sm_120. Native CUDA execution, actual failed-context cleanup, broader concurrency/cache behavior and production API acceptance are still open. This is not a completed B06 or a P3 phase transition. Implement the accepted metadata through a private registry, move-only allocation owners, one explicit initial stream, immutable views/results, owned upload and download staging, and completions. Pin all resources before attempting submission. Retain failed operation records even when event recording or submission throws before a completion is returned. Poison/drain or quarantine a failed session. Acceptance: test wrong device/session, stale generations, alias rejection, input immutability, chained resident forward/pointwise/inverse, completion abandonment, close-with-live-handles, constructor unwinding, submission failure, event-recording failure, and failure before quiescence. Confirm a host download cannot expose data before success. Multi-stream capability stays disabled until exact cross-stream waits, event-generation handling and race tests pass. B07 — cache and memory admission ¶ A plan contains reusable tables for an operation. Identify it with every parameter that affects its values. Allow only one pending upload per plan, and make later readers wait until it is ready. Keep it alive while callers or queued work use it. Remove least-recently-used (LRU) entries only after those users finish. Apply ADR 0001's separate 256 MiB cache, 256 MiB device workspace and 128 MiB host staging defaults, with checked allocation-size charging and configurable limits. The initial allocator owns individual allocations. Acceptance: tests cover concurrent same-key lookup, changed roots/basis/device, live-reader eviction attempts, failed uploads, pending entries, budget exhaustion, actual released bytes, all-or-nothing budget reduction and retained output/host result credits. Busy/budget failures are explicit, bounded and cannot deadlock waiting for the caller's own lease. Do not enable multi-stream dispatch merely because the cache lookup mutex is tested. B08 — production NTT selection ¶ Evaluate coalesced transpose/Four-Step, radix-4/8, shared-memory padding and warp exchange only after the baseline is measured. Preserve exact ordering, roots, normalization and the required out-of-place copy cost. Acceptance: device-resident composition without intermediate host waits; exact gates for every supported workload; recorded launch/resource parameters; latency and throughput selections for N=65536 on the primary GPU, with labeled control-device evidence. P3 closes only with the complete regression and dispatch record, not one faster microbenchmark. 4. P4–P7: preserve exact conversion and Hybrid behavior ¶ B09 — base conversion ¶ Offline preparation on November 19 adds a reusable coefficient-domain switch, all-dimension CPU/OpenFHE fixtures through 40 towers and a prepared native CUDA probe. The native conversion probe passed its full OpenFHE matrix on reported compute 8.9 on November 19. Hybrid tests exercise the candidate's digit lifts and P-to-Q correction against independent BigInteger answers. Resident GPU composition and primary-device acceptance remain open. November 20 local preparation connects coefficient basis switching, ModUp and ModDown to the immutable resident session. Per-operation parameter tables are budgeted and pinned until completion, and no intermediate polynomial download is required. The shared host/native fixture compares both ModUp input formats and independent ModDown against OpenFHE; the new CUDA path requires its own native run. Full Hybrid execution, conversion-plan caching and production acceptance remain open. Upstream entry points: ApproxSwitchCRTBasis and ApproxModDown . Broaden D8's model into independent Q→QP and QP→Q CPU fixtures across the intended levels, tower counts and boundary coefficients. Freeze exact constants, integer representatives, reduction order, and separate digit-extension/ModDown plan kinds. Then implement conversion for batches of polynomials using views of arrays already stored on the GPU, so intermediate results need not return to the CPU. Acceptance: every intermediate array matches pinned OpenFHE; shortened digits and dynamic active prefixes work; NTT and conversion compose without host copies. Canonical CRT substitution must remain a detectable negative case. Record native sanitizer and performance evidence before the P5 production gate. B10 — Hybrid runtime, keys and hoists ¶ Trace EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore → EvalFastKeySwitchCoreExt → EvalFastKeySwitchCore . The full wrappers are RelinearizeInPlace and EvalFastRotationExt . Broaden the production parameter, digit, level and key-family fixtures before GPU composition. Snapshot immutable A/B evaluation-key material with versioned identity; cache full-context metadata; preserve reduced-level P offsets, B/A accumulation, delayed QP scaling and one final ModDown per accumulated result. Store the reusable digit decomposition as an explicit read-only result (a hoist). Record which input allocation and version produced it, so it cannot be reused after the input changes. Acceptance: complete intermediate/final arrays match for fixed input/key material; altered key snapshots cannot reuse stale cache entries; stale hoists are rejected. Actual rotation automorphisms and higher-degree relinearization need full exact and CKKS decryption tests; D8's source trace alone is insufficient. Record key upload reuse, peak live memory and full-operation timing. 5. P8: integrate and release ¶ B11 — transactional adapter ¶ Upstream boundaries: SetFormat , DCRTPoly::SwitchFormat , NativePoly::SetValues and DCRT move assignment . These CPU APIs motivate the proposed adapter; they are not GPU adapter implementations. Implement the explicit opt-in SetFormat-equivalent DCRT seam before integrating broader operations. Validate every tower and outer format, use per-element packing/import, reserve the complete transaction's resource credits, and commit through a verified nonthrowing move only after successful download/import. Use RequireDevice by default; permit CPU only before submission under the explicit fallback policy. Add later conversion/Hybrid dispatch behind their gates. Acceptance: same-format requests are validated no-ops; CPU-only builds keep working; unsupported/unavailable/unverified cases obey policy. Recoverable packing, allocation, upload, launch, event, download and import failures leave the original host object unchanged. No post-submission automatic fallback occurs. Test partial submission without a returned completion, concurrent host-object access rules and upstream noexcept limitations explicitly. Application input, keys and CKKS metadata must survive both device and permitted CPU paths. B12 — integration release ¶ Run relevant upstream CKKS examples/tests and the complete exact pipeline, including all target levels, relinearization, rotations and explicit fallback. Keep constants/keys resident across repeated operations and publish cold/warm, end-to-end and kernel-only measurements with raw evidence and source revisions. Acceptance: all P8 exit gates are green; runtime failure and teardown tests pass; exact arrays and secondary decryption checks agree; per-device dispatch, limits, configuration and reproducibility instructions are published. Production promotion removes the proposal label only after these relevant implementation gates, with a reviewed API migration. 6. D11 handoff and evidence record ¶ D11 begins on the provided GPU machine. Record its environment before installing or changing the selected toolkit. Configure a separate native build directory; reuse the pinned OpenFHE installation produced by scripts/openfhe_setup.sh . For example, after setting the install prefix for that machine: cmake -S . -B build/native-gpu -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \\ -DNTT_REQUIRE_CUDA=ON -DCMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES=native \\ -DNTT_ENABLE_OPENFHE_COMPAT=ON \\ -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=\"$NTT_OPENFHE_PREFIX\" cmake --build build/native-gpu -j2 ctest --test-dir build/native-gpu --output-on-failure CMake auto-detection can produce a CPU-only build. B02/B03 require an actual CUDA compiler, the CUDA targets and their registered CTests, a real GPU, and successful native execution. The commands above are a handoff, not evidence of execution on that GPU. Compiler-only builds are documented separately in the preparation guide. For each completed item, save the source revision, test inputs and parameters, machine and compiler details, exact commands, original logs, any failure explanation, and the result of each required check. Update this backlog, the roadmap and that calendar day's journal together. Leave unexecuted items visibly open. Next: Before renting a GPU →"}, {"title": "Before renting a GPU", "summary": "Finish builds and CPU checks before paying for GPU time.", "group": "Plans", "url": "offline-gpu-preparation.html", "text": "Before renting a GPU Use a CPU machine to install dependencies, compile code and prepare tests. Rent a GPU to run device checks and collect measurements. Earlier Ada results apply to their recorded source revision. Later changes, including the resident chain, need new hardware checks. Read full notes Before renting a GPU ¶ Do the builds, host tests and documentation on a CPU machine. Rent a GPU when ready to run native CUDA checks and collect measurements. The main target is RTX 5090 at N=65536; RTX 4090 is the planned control. A November 14 Ada run passed its selected native tests. The current source includes later changes, especially the resident runtime, that still need native verification. D11/P2 remains open on the roadmap ; working days are milestones, not rental days. 1. What is ready ¶ The following results were observed using the November 16 source version on Linux, without a GPU driver. They describe that version; they do not claim that later changes have run on a GPU: Check Result CPU/OpenFHE suite 24 CTests pass with CUDA disabled CUDA-linked host suite 26 CTests pass; GPU-labelled tests excluded CUDA compilation Stock nvcc 13.0.88 builds sm_89 and sm_120 Resident prototype Host ownership/failure tests and exact OpenFHE comparisons pass Native execution of this increment Pending; compilation is not GPU execution The original offline collector record, bench-results-offline-final/report.json , reported OFFLINE_READY and gpu_execution: NOT_RUN . Its older test counts are preserved in the November 12 journal . Toolchains, binaries and generated reports remain in ignored directories. Use the rental survey for dated provider evidence, then recheck the actual offer and profiling permissions. 2. Reproduce the offline preparation ¶ Use Linux x86_64, Python 3.12+, CMake 3.24+ and a compatible C++20 host compiler. The installer downloads specific versions of NVIDIA’s compiler, runtime libraries and headers into build/ . It checks each download’s SHA-256 hash (a file fingerprint) and keeps the license notices. It does not install a GPU driver or change system packages or shell settings. From the repository root, with cmake / ctest on PATH: bash scripts/openfhe_setup.sh python3 scripts/install_cuda_toolkit.py python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode offline \\ --cuda-root build/toolchains/cuda-13.0.2 \\ --openfhe-prefix build/openfhe-compat/install The OpenFHE helper pins commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f , NativeInteger 64 and reduced-noise off, and deliberately builds CPU reference tests with CUDA disabled. Its parallelism defaults to two jobs; set NTT_BUILD_JOBS if the machine supports more. The session runner selects project-local CMake/CTest from .venv/bin when present. The toolkit lock pins NVIDIA components and SHA-256 values. CUDA 13.0.2 contains compiler version 13.0.88. Matching installs are reused; incomplete installs or checksum mismatches fail. Compiler and dependency portability notes On this host, CUDA 12.8.93 conflicts with glibc 2.41's sinpi / cospi declarations. Stock CUDA 13 compiles without vendor-header patches. A rental may use another supported OS/toolkit combination; match its driver, toolkit and host compiler. Offline compilation does not establish driver compatibility. Reuse OpenFHE binaries only across compatible OS, glibc, architecture and compiler settings. Otherwise build the pinned dependency on a matching image before the session, or rebuild it on the GPU machine. 3. Fixes made before rental ¶ The synchronous wrapper now gives every allocated buffer an owner that releases it automatically when destroyed. This C++ technique is called RAII, and it also handles failures partway through setup. The wrapper selects the device that owns the buffers, checks N, primes and roots, and rejects arrays with the wrong length or values outside 0…q−1 before copying them to the GPU. The first nvcc build also repaired a namespace closure and private-type launcher issue; transform arithmetic and stage order were retained. Host tests and CUDA-linked invalid-constructor tests pass without allocating device memory. Native pre-call setup-failure checks passed in the older Ada batch. Real GPU tests must still cover out-of-memory errors, interrupted copies, restoring the caller’s selected device, and cleanup after the GPU context fails. A nonthrowing destructor cannot report cleanup errors to its caller. These fixes do not implement the full backend proposal , cache or transactional OpenFHE adapter. The separate resident prototype has its own scope and limits. 4. Prepared native exactness matrix ¶ The CUDA/OpenFHE test source is registered as cuda_openfhe_exact . It compares the CUDA wrapper with the independent CPU reference and actual OpenFHE NativePoly transforms /products at N=65536. Its prepared cases include: deliberately unsorted 55/50/60-bit primes selected by LastPrime and RootOfUnity ; the exact D8 Q/P moduli and roots, with Q and QlP bases at L=5,4,3,1; zero, one, q−1, alternating bounds, x¹⁷, xᴺ⁻¹ and deterministic dense residues; forward, inverse from independent evaluation arrays, round trip, pointwise multiplication followed by inverse, and the complete negacyclic product; exact complete-array comparisons and unchanged host inputs. The --smoke option runs a small subset: the three prime widths with zero and dense inputs, under each of the four Compute Sanitizer tools. A smoke test is a quick check for obvious failures. It does not replace checks for every supported parameter and error case. All GPU cases are compiled but unrun in this workspace. The current collector requires seven GPU-labelled gates: cuda_verify , cuda_matches_ref , cuda_oracle_crosscheck , cuda_openfhe_exact , cuda_setup_fault_injection , cuda_benchmark_matrix_exact and resident_cuda_exact . The last two cover the later benchmark matrix and resident operation-chain prototype . The wrapper constructor and resident factory preflights are separate host tests safe without a driver. The older Ada batch exercised the benchmark matrix and setup probe; it predates the new resident path and cannot validate it. 5. First session on real hardware ¶ Choose an RTX 5090 image for the primary target. An RTX 4090 can establish the control run; label it accordingly and leave primary-device evidence open. Use an image with a matching NVIDIA driver, CUDA compiler, Compute Sanitizer, and Nsight tools for later profiling. Native architecture mode needs CMake 3.24+. Use the intended clean source checkout and the pinned OpenFHE install. First check the image, then run the collector: nvidia-smi nvcc --version compute-sanitizer --version NTT_CUDA_ROOT=/usr/local/cuda \\ NTT_OPENFHE_PREFIX=\"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" \\ bash scripts/autodl_setup.sh The helper uses the source and packages already present; it does not pull new commits or install dependencies. First it records the source revision, GPU, driver, clock speeds, power settings and build tools. Then it builds CUDA, verifies that the expected OpenFHE/GPU tests exist, checks the device, and runs the exact-answer tests. It runs four sanitizer tools on the legacy, benchmark-matrix and resident-chain smoke cases. Finally it measures 30/50/55/60-bit primes, N=4096–65536 and K=1/3/8/16/24/32/40 towers. Each of the 140 workloads has two GPU timing scopes, giving 280 jobs. The helper now defaults to --benchmark-scope gpu ; request --benchmark-scope all for the previous three-scope, 420-job combined collection. GPU mode rejects dirty source unless explicitly marked exploratory with --allow-dirty . For full control, call the Python collector directly: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode gpu \\ --cuda-root /usr/local/cuda \\ --openfhe-prefix \"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" \\ --architectures native --benchmark --benchmark-scope gpu Collect the matching OpenFHE baseline on a local CPU machine, without CUDA: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode cpu \\ --openfhe-prefix \"$PWD/build/openfhe-compat/install\" \\ --benchmark --output-dir bench-results-local-cpu CPU mode records CPU identity, configures CUDA off, runs host/OpenFHE gates and collects 140 CPU jobs. It reports GPU execution as NOT_RUN . Keep CPU and GPU records separate, with matching workload parameters and repetition counts. Both modes support resume within the same clean source/machine/scope context; changing the selection requires a new output directory. --skip-sanitizers is available for an explicitly limited diagnostic run; its report says EXPLICITLY_SKIPPED . Such a run cannot satisfy sanitizer gates. Missing tools, missing GPU/OpenFHE tests, build/test/sanitizer/benchmark failures all produce a nonzero exit and a failed report. Existing directories are rejected unless explicitly using --resume --output-dir <previous-record> ; resume archives the prior report, reruns gates and preserves logs. Open report.json and the named stage logs after every run. The legacy bench command still uses 46-bit primes and public-call wall time. The collector now separates bench-jsonl public calls, events-jsonl resident work/pinned copies and ntt_openfhe_bench CPU arithmetic. The current CUDA paths compile offline; the new resident path still needs native execution. The CPU comparator is locally verified. Optional --profile needs working nsys and ncu plus counter permissions; it prepares a bounded capture, not a completed D14 bottleneck report. See the protocol and limits . 6. When to stop the rental ¶ Use the first GPU session to establish D11 hardware facts, execute D12 smoke and B02 failure-path work, run D13 exactness, and collect the D14 measurements that are ready. Export all raw records before stopping the machine. Analyze and publish results offline for D15. A report of GPU_CHECKS_PASSED means the selected checks passed. Work still remains: deliberately trigger real GPU failures, cover production parameters, review the individual GPU-event timing samples, and analyze the Nsight profiles. Completing this offline checklist does not automatically close D11, B02, B03, D14 or P2. The backlog controls those acceptance criteria. Next: Offline test tooling →"}, {"title": "Benchmarks & tuning", "summary": "Design fair performance comparisons before changing the GPU code.", "group": "Plans", "url": "benchmark-tuning-plan.html", "text": "Benchmarks & tuning Use the same inputs, prime widths, tower counts and hardware. Report setup, complete-call and GPU-only times separately. Change one major cost at a time. Keep an optimization only after its answers match and the relevant complete workload improves. Read full notes GPU NTT benchmark and tuning plan ¶ Primary target: NVIDIA RTX 5090 ( sm_120 ) Cross-architecture control: NVIDIA RTX 4090 ( sm_89 ) Workload target: OpenFHE 1.6.1 CKKS with Hybrid key switching Primary ring dimension: 65536 ; regression/benchmark sweep: 4096 – 65536 A kernel is a function executed by many GPU threads. Choose between kernel versions by checking their answers and measuring them at N=65536. Latency means time for one operation; throughput means operations completed per second. Record the GPU, encryption parameters, number of polynomials and whether CPU–GPU transfers are included. The full-matrix report records the temporary Ada baseline. The earlier diagnostic analysis explains why its single averaged row was insufficient. Neither supplies an RTX 5090 result or an accepted hardware-counter bottleneck diagnosis. 1. Implementations to compare ¶ Every version must produce the same array values in the same order and pass the same exact-answer tests. A “gate” below means a check that must pass before the next step. Some proposed optimizations are named here before they are implemented: Current radix-2 two-pass implementation using reference modular arithmetic. Two-pass implementation with Shoup twiddle multiplication and Barrett or Montgomery variable multiplication. Radix-4 register-staged implementation. Radix-8 register-staged implementation with padded shared memory. Coalesced Four-Step/transpose implementation. Fused variants used by base conversion and Hybrid key switching. Compare with the GPU-NTT and FIDESlib libraries only when they compute the same transform, accept the same prime sizes, process the same number of prime towers, and include the same work in their timings. 2. Workload matrix ¶ The minimum matrix is: Axis Values Ring dimension 4096 , 8192 , 16384 , 32768 , 65536 ; optimize primarily for 65536 Modulus width OpenFHE-compatible 30, 50, 55, 60-bit primes; retain actual production 59-bit fixtures too Active RNS towers representative shallow, medium, and deep CKKS levels Polynomial count one component, full ciphertext, key-switch digits Batch size 1 , 2 , 4 , 8 , then L2-capacity-limited sweep Direction forward, inverse, forward–pointwise–inverse Objective single-operation latency and steady-state throughput Transfer policy device-resident, pinned end-to-end, pageable end-to-end GPU RTX 5090 primary; RTX 4090 control Take tower and Hybrid digit counts from the pinned OpenFHE parameter sets: PrecomputeCRTTables defines the full-context partitions, and EvalKeySwitchPrecomputeCore derives the active-level count. Three-limb synthetic fixtures alone are insufficient. The next rental's bounded baseline batch uses all five dimensions, all four prime widths, and K=1/3/8/16/24/32/40: 140 workloads , with separate public GPU, resident event/pinned-copy, and OpenFHE CPU jobs ( 420 combined jobs ). The rental helper defaults to 280 GPU-only jobs; collect the 140 CPU jobs locally with the split collector. Select any K from 1 to 40; K counts primes per polynomial, not ciphertexts. Defaults are five warmups and 100 samples. This baseline does not sweep production CKKS parameter sets, ciphertext batches or alternative implementations. Thirty-bit primes use the same uint64_t storage/arithmetic; there is no specialized uint32_t kernel or four-byte transfer path yet. See the batch protocol for restart and timing limits. 3. GPU and kernel parameters to tune ¶ Group experiments by the cost they change: Area Variables to test or record Thread work Block size, butterflies/values per thread, radix, register staging and spills Memory Split/transpose shape, shared-memory padding, bank conflicts, warp exchange and coalescing Arithmetic Shoup/Barrett policy, lazy range, reduction frequency and twiddle representation Workload RNS tower layout, ciphertext/digit batch layout and active blocks/warps Runtime Streams, allocation/scratch reuse, asynchronous copies and CUDA Graph replay Change one major factor at a time before combining winners. Save ptxas -v compiler reports and the Nsight occupancy measurement. Occupancy describes how many warps can remain active on a GPU processor. A lower register count alone does not prove a faster program; measure the complete operation. 4. Measurements ¶ Timing ¶ CUDA-event kernel-only latency for every kernel and complete transform; transfer-inclusive latency, separating pinned and pageable host memory; transforms per second and coefficients/towers processed per second; full polynomial multiplication latency; Hybrid key switch, relinearization, rotation, and hoisted-rotation latency; complete encrypted-inference latency and peak device memory; cold setup, warm steady state, and CUDA Graph replay separately. Run warmups before collecting samples. Report the median (middle sample), p5 (the fifth percentile) and p95 (the ninety-fifth percentile) to show both typical time and variation. Save the individual samples so summaries can be recomputed without another rental. Hardware counters ¶ achieved DRAM and L2 bandwidth; global load/store transaction efficiency; L2 hit rate and read/write sectors; shared-memory bank conflicts; registers per thread, spills, occupancy, and eligible warps; SM and memory-pipeline utilization; kernel-launch count and synchronization gaps. In the current column pass, neighboring threads request coefficients 2,048 bytes apart. The GPU may fetch much more memory than the useful values. Record the number of memory sectors fetched for each requested byte to measure that overhead. 5. Reproducibility record ¶ Every result row must include: GPU name, compute capability, clocks, power mode, and memory size; driver, CUDA toolkit/runtime, compiler, CMake flags, and git commit; implementation/variant ID and all tuned launch parameters; N , modulus bits, actual moduli or parameter-set ID, tower count, digit count, batch shape, transform ordering, and transfer policy; correctness-gate result and profiler command/version; whether setup, allocation, transfers, and synchronization are included. Without these details, a result cannot support a reproducible comparison. Keep it as an exploratory observation rather than putting it in the published comparison tables. 6. Tuning procedure ¶ Run exact arithmetic and transform gates for the candidate variant. Reject variants that spill unexpectedly, exceed resource limits, or violate lazy-reduction bounds. Sweep launch and staging parameters at N=65536. Profile the best latency and throughput candidates with Nsight Compute. Repeat using realistic RNS tower and Hybrid digit batches. Validate finalists on RTX 4090 to identify architecture-specific choices. Record the winner in a dispatch table keyed by GPU capability, ring dimension, tower/batch range, and latency/throughput mode. Re-run the complete Hybrid key-switch and inference benchmarks. A locally faster NTT variant is accepted only if it improves the target pipeline or supplies a clearly justified fallback. 7. Acceptance gates ¶ Exact forward/inverse agreement for every supported N and OpenFHE modulus. No silent fallback to device-side integer remainder in optimized kernels. No benchmark mixes kernel-only and end-to-end timing boundaries. A selected latency and throughput implementation exists for N=65536 on RTX 5090. RTX 4090 results either validate the same dispatch or document a separate one. Full Hybrid key-switch and inference results accompany NTT microbenchmarks. The HTML explainer identifies which schedule is the correctness baseline and which implementation the tuner selected for each workload class. Compare the two butterfly schedules ¶ Build the default group_twiddle_ct and a separate -DNTT_CUDA_LEGACY_BUTTERFLY=ON build on the same GPU. Run exact checks and sanitizers first, then run events-jsonl on matched N, roots, primes and operands. Compare resident forward and inverse medians before comparing public calls; keep both builds' raw data and transform_schedule labels. The pass split and reduction are unchanged, but arithmetic and twiddle access differ. Prior Ada timings used the old schedule; no speedup is assumed. Next: Offline test tooling →"}, {"title": "First GPU run: analysis", "summary": "Understand what the first GPU run established and what its timing cannot show.", "group": "Research", "url": "first-native-analysis.html", "text": "First GPU run: analysis The first Ada run passed its selected device tests and saved one average timing row using 46-bit primes. That average cannot separate kernels from copies or show variation. The later matrix report adds individual samples and a separate OpenFHE CPU comparison. Read full notes First native run: analysis and next experiments ¶ 2026-11-14 · offline analysis · source 19cc285 · N=65536 The supplied GPU uses NVIDIA’s Ada architecture. It passed the prepared correctness tests for the cases that were run. This gives us a starting CUDA result on that machine; it does not validate a production backend or measure an RTX 5090. Prepare better timing and error-handling tests locally before paying for another session. This is the historical analysis of source 19cc285 , using exported logs. The later full-matrix report adds raw samples and a separate OpenFHE CPU baseline. Read that report for the newer measurements. No reconnection or new GPU run was made for this analysis. See the session record for environment, commands, archive checksum and local report paths. 1. Exactness: what the comparison count means ¶ The archive’s file fingerprint matches the supplied checksum. Its report records 17 successful collection stages, unmodified source, a CUDA build and GPU_CHECKS_PASSED . The full log shows that all 23 CTests passed. Only four of those tests execute GPU work; the others run on the CPU. The strongest native check is cuda_openfhe_tests.cpp : Scope Verified from saved output Basis groups Nine: unsorted 55/50/60-bit basis, then Q and QlP at L=5,4,3,1 Patterns per group Seven: zero, one, q−1, alternating bounds, x^17, x^(N−1), dense seeded residues Pattern/basis combinations 63 Sum of tower counts across groups 41 Residue positions across patterns 65536 × 41 × 7 = 18,808,832 Equality comparisons Nine checks per position: 169,279,488 The nine checks are CPU/OpenFHE forward, GPU forward, roundtrip, CPU/OpenFHE independent inverse, GPU independent inverse, CPU/OpenFHE square, GPU pointwise/inverse, GPU complete square and host-source immutability. The total is not 169 million independent random cases . Some inverse tests start with independently chosen evaluation arrays rather than output from our forward transform. This prevents matching forward and inverse mistakes from hiding each other. Comparison with OpenFHE also checks array order, root choice and final scaling. The separate cuda_verify also uses distinct left/right operands against the CPU reference; the direct OpenFHE product matrix primarily tests squares. No mismatch was observed. This supports real native correctness for this schedule/arithmetic on this machine, not every prime, key or concurrent use. GPU operations over D8's Q/P moduli test NTT/products on those bases; they do not implement the Hybrid graph. That graph's gate here executes on CPU. Sanitizer coverage ¶ Each of memcheck, racecheck, initcheck and synccheck ran zero/dense patterns at N=65536 with three towers: two pattern/basis rows and 3,538,944 comparisons per tool. Saved summaries report zero errors; racecheck also reports zero warnings and hazards. This is kernel smoke coverage, not all 63 combinations, allocation failure, failed-context cleanup or asynchronous ownership verification. 2. Latency: useful baseline, limited comparison ¶ One session, three synthetic 46-bit towers, ten timed repetitions per operation. The saved output contains only the arithmetic mean for each operation, not the ten individual times. Operation Printed mean Timing scope CPU reference forward 47.4496 ms Correctness-first scalar code, serial tower loop GPU forward 0.786293 ms Public host-vector call GPU inverse 0.806584 ms Public host-vector call GPU complete multiply 1.49319 ms Two forwards, pointwise, inverse and public-call costs Source: run_bench and synchronous wrapper . Device allocations and table construction/upload precede timing. Validation, device guards, launches, operand transfers and blocking download are included. 47.4496 / 0.786293 ≈ 60.35 : the recorded public GPU forward is about 60× faster than this scalar reference in this experiment . This is not an OpenFHE, Hybrid or inference speedup. No optimized OpenFHE CPU timing or application timing exists here. CPU twiddle generation also differs from the device's precomputed tables. CTest's 14.96-second native-gate duration includes many operations, CPU/OpenFHE expected values and comparisons. It is not kernel latency. The 36.64-second suite duration is not inference time either. Benchmark caveats found in source ¶ Finding Consequence Proposed correction Only means saved Cannot recover median/p5/p95, variance or outliers offline Save each sample and workload/timing metadata Inverse lacks dedicated warm-up and input reset It follows warm forward calls, then repeatedly inverts its previous result Prepare fixed evaluation data; warm inverse and reset outside timing CPU forward mutates its previous output; timed GPU forward resets Repetition input/cache policies differ Use matched fixed-input/reset protocols Multiply receives left operand by value Host copy/allocation and discarded-result destruction are inside call timing Keep honest public-call timing; add prepared-buffer/device timing separately No event/per-kernel measurements Copies, launch gaps, arithmetic and memory stalls cannot be separated Add resident CUDA-event and explicit transfer/call boundaries Performance only at 46 bits/K=3/B=1 50/55/60-bit correctness is not performance evidence there Time actual OpenFHE parameters, levels and batches Hardware snapshot captured idle state P8/210 MHz does not establish clocks during kernels Record active clocks, power/thermal state and repeated runs There are two warm-up forwards and multiplies, but no explicit inverse warm-up. Inverse does follow forward work, so this is not a claim that the device was cold. Forward/inverse means differ by about 2.58%; without samples, do not call this statistically meaningful or attribute it to inverse's extra launch. 3. Fused multiplication and transfer accounting ¶ One operand has S=8×65536×3=1,572,864 B (1.5 MiB). From the D5 transfer map : Pipeline Operand transfer payload Kernel launches Public forward(A), forward(B), pointwise, inverse 9S = 13.5 MiB 8 Public multiply(A,B) 3S = 4.5 MiB 8 Using one fused public call avoids six CPU–GPU operand copies. It still launches the same eight GPU kernels. It also reduces public validation/guard paths, although multiply has the host-copy cost above. Thus 1.49319 ms for multiply is plausible even though two separately timed forward means sum to 1.572586 ms. It is not evidence that the individual timings fail. Two forward means plus inverse total 2.37917 ms without the unmeasured standalone pointwise call. Their ratio to multiply is about 1.59, but this is not a measured fusion speedup : it combines unmatched measurements and omits a stage. Benchmark the complete unfused and fused chains under one protocol. When adding basis conversion and Hybrid operations, keep intermediate arrays on the GPU where possible. Measure the benefit for the complete chain. The ratio against this simple CPU reference does not predict the speedup of encrypted inference. 4. Bottleneck hypotheses, not a measured winner ¶ At N=65536 the code fuses eight far stages into the column pass and eight near stages into the row pass. There are 524,288 butterflies per tower. Source-derived property Candidate issue Missing evidence Two full-width modular multiplies per device butterfly Reduction instruction cost PTX/SASS, ptxas resources, per-kernel timing and a proven alternative Adjacent column-gather/scatter threads access coefficients 2,048 B apart Inefficient memory transactions Sectors/requested byte, L2/DRAM counters, equivalent coalesced variant A block loads 256 values but has 128 butterfly pairs per stage Some launched threads do not execute butterflies Register/shared-memory/active-warp data and measured staging variants; not an occupancy percentage Public calls transfer and synchronously download Host/copy/launch overhead Separate copy events and a resident-chain measurement For three towers, source-level modular-multiply call counts, excluding setup: forward 3,145,728; inverse 3,342,336 including normalization; complete product 9,830,400 including pointwise and normalization. These are not hardware instruction counts. Coefficient-array global reads/writes requested by the source total 4S for forward, 6S for inverse and 17S for multiplication. They omit twiddle/constants, shared memory and host/device copies. These are not measured DRAM traffic or bandwidth. Do not divide them by full public-call latency to infer utilization. The source calls integer remainder slow, but this session contains no instruction profile or alternative-reduction comparison. The saved evidence cannot tell us whether arithmetic, widely spaced memory accesses, transfers or kernel launches cost the most time. Profile first, then change one major factor at a time so an improvement has an identifiable cause. 5. Hardware and reproducibility limits ¶ Driver/runtime identify compute capability 8.9, name RTX 4080 SUPER, and 33,796,456,448 device bytes / 32,760 MiB. This is an observed rental identity, not independent confirmation of retail board specifications. Ask the provider before normalizing results against a supposedly identical card. Neither the RTX 5090 primary nor RTX 4090 control has been measured here. The snapshot reports a 320 W limit and idle clocks, not a continuous active trace. Driver API 13.2 does not mean the compiler is 13.2: saved nvcc is 13.0.88 and runtime API is 13.0. Preserve this distinction in comparison tables. Dependency downloads failed twice before a clean pinned source bundle resolved setup. Prepare compatible source/dependency caches before the next rental. Do not copy macOS binaries onto Linux. The successful collector ran for about 72 seconds after setup ; this excludes installation, dependency compilation and troubleshooting and is not total rental duration or cost. 6. Next experiments: prepare offline first ¶ No implementation changes are included in this analysis. Proposed order: Measurement harness: raw samples and actual q/root metadata, fixed-input reset/warm-up policy, matched OpenFHE CPU baseline, setup/public-call/resident timing scopes. Test statistics/report validation without a GPU. Failure-path harness: deterministic injection at six allocations and four constant uploads. Host doubles can test bookkeeping; real device cleanup and context failures still need native execution. B02 stays open. Broader cases: direct OpenFHE products with distinct operands, missing L=2 coverage, exact 50/55/60-bit performance workloads, opt-in full sanitizer coverage. Keep the current passing baseline for regression comparison. Profiling preflight: verify provider hardware identity, Nsight tools and counter permissions before booking. Prepare bounded commands/output paths. Next GPU session: obtain primary-device evidence, repeat baseline, execute fault tests, matched timing and per-pass profiling; export before stopping. Do not pay for a repeat of this limited row alone. Then optimize one factor: choose reduction, coalesced staging or residency/launch work from evidence, and retain an exact same-device A/B test. This report completes part of tasks B04 and D15. It does not yet identify the dominant performance cost. Remaining work includes tests on the primary GPU, real failure handling, production parameters, and GPU base conversion, Hybrid key switching and inference. Next: Full GPU matrix: analysis →"}, {"title": "Paired Ada NTT results", "summary": "Compare device-event and host-call results from the November 19 Ada batch.", "group": "Research", "url": "paired-ntt-native-analysis.html", "text": "Paired Ada NTT results Group twiddles improved all eight device-event NTT workloads, with median legacy/group ratios around 1.5. Resident-interface repeats show much smaller differences. Measure allocation, parameter and transfer costs before selecting the next optimization. Read full notes Analyze the paired Ada NTT measurements ¶ The November 19 Ada sample favors group twiddles for device-event NTT timings. The median per-workload legacy/group ratios were 1.52 for forward, 1.50 for inverse and 1.49 for fused multiplication. Host-call gains were smaller and sensitive to variability. Keep the group-twiddle default while measuring the resident interface's allocation, parameter and transfer costs. What was measured ¶ The server reported RTX 4080 SUPER, compute 8.9, driver 595.71.05 and CUDA 12.8. The clean source snapshot was 0f2df64, based on the local work after 587598a. Both schedules passed native exactness and the selected sanitizers. The run record describes the export and checks. Each schedule ran eight workloads, combining N=4,096/65,536, K=3/40 and 30/60-bit primes, with two warmups and ten samples per operation. Both used the same coefficients, primes, roots and fixed seed. The default schedule ran before the legacy schedule. The ratio below is the legacy median time divided by the group-twiddle median time for one matched workload. A ratio above one favors group twiddles. Each table entry summarizes those eight ratios, with equal weight per workload. Tables scroll horizontally on a phone. Timing scope and operation Median ratio Range Group faster Device event, forward 1.52 1.20–1.61 8/8 Device event, inverse 1.50 1.18–1.59 8/8 Device event, fused multiplication 1.49 1.26–1.53 8/8 Device event, pointwise control 1.01 0.68–1.21 4/8 Host public call, forward 1.01 0.89–1.38 4/8 Host public call, inverse 1.12 1.00–1.23 8/8 Host public call, fused multiplication 1.05 0.93–1.21 5/8 Resident interface, device chain 1.16 0.85–1.47 7/8 Resident interface, host boundary 1.03 0.66–1.28 4/8 Resident interface, host roundtrip 1.03 0.87–1.33 5/8 Device-event intervals exclude input/output transfers and session setup. Host public calls include their validation and transfers. The resident device chain includes allocation, copies, events and host orchestration, with inputs already uploaded. Its output check downloads after the timer. The two resident host controls include input uploads and the final download; the roundtrip control additionally copies intermediates through the host. The pointwise and transfer controls vary despite having equivalent arithmetic or copy work between schedules. Treat the ten-sample sweep as an initial comparison. Clock or host scheduling drift can affect the fixed run order. The order-balanced resident repeat ¶ A follow-up used N=65,536, K=3, 60-bit primes, five warmups and fifty samples per operation. The order was legacy, group, group, legacy. These are median host-wall milliseconds from each independent run. Order Schedule Device chain Host boundary Host roundtrip 1 Legacy 0.7401 3.2347 9.1446 2 Group 0.7172 3.3319 7.6012 3 Group 0.7264 3.2802 7.5232 4 Legacy 0.7298 3.0951 7.6587 Pairing runs 1/2 and 4/3 gives device-chain ratios of 1.032 and 1.005. The corresponding host-boundary ratios are 0.971 and 0.944, and host-roundtrip ratios are 1.203 and 1.018. The larger device-event advantage does not carry through consistently to these interface measurements. Pooling the four runs would hide this variation. Read the timeline with its setup boundary ¶ The captured resident experiment contains three sessions, warmups and checks. It records 71 cudaMalloc calls, 71 cudaFree calls and three stream creations. The stream-creation calls consumed 240.246 ms in the whole trace, including a 240.218 ms first call. Session creation occurs outside the benchmark timers. Consequently, the trace's 84.8% stream-creation share describes the whole profiled process. It cannot identify the dominant cost inside the measured resident calls. Inspect allocation and host orchestration within explicitly marked operation ranges in the next profile. Hardware counters were blocked by ERR_NVGPUCTRPERM, leaving occupancy and memory-traffic conclusions open. Reproduce the analysis on the M1 Pro ¶ Verify the saved archive checksum and analyze the extracted record: cd build/gpu-session-20261119 shasum -a 256 -c session-20261119.tar.gz.sha256 cd ../.. python3 scripts/analyze_gpu_batch.py \\ build/gpu-session-20261119/session-20261119 \\ --output-dir build/paired-analysis-review Use a fresh output directory. The analyzer checks native/sanitizer status, the complete selected matrix, log hashes, raw summaries, binary identities, paired inputs and environment settings. It also validates the four balanced repeat records. It writes 112 matched operation rows to CSV, twelve repeat rows to a separate CSV, and a JSON summary. Hash validation checks record consistency; the native tests supply the arithmetic evidence. The next work is to measure the resident ModUp/ModDown chain , then evaluate conversion-plan caching and allocation reuse. The November 19 source predates that composition. RTX 5090 acceptance and complete CKKS application timing remain future measurements. Next: Prepare basis conversion →"}, {"title": "Full GPU matrix: analysis", "summary": "Read the measured GPU results together with their limits.", "group": "Research", "url": "batch40-native-analysis.html", "text": "Full GPU matrix: analysis Keeping intermediate arrays on the GPU made the combined public multiplication faster in all 140 workloads, with a median unfused/fused time ratio of 2.36. GPU-event times exclude input and output copies. The M1 CPU and temporary Ada GPU results do not measure RTX 5090 or a complete CKKS application. Read full notes GPU results: what we learned ¶ Measured on November 14, 2026 · temporary sm_89 GPU · M1 Pro CPU baseline The NTT version used in this run passed the tests that were executed. Multiplication was faster when intermediate arrays stayed on the GPU. The newer resident API, which lets callers chain operations without copying those arrays back to the CPU, still needs its own GPU tests. Measure its complete cost before adding base conversion or key switching. The rental reports RTX 4080 SUPER, compute capability 8.9 . These are not RTX 5090 results . Correctness and coverage ¶ All 29 native tests and eight sanitizer runs passed. All 280 GPU measurement jobs and 140 M1 CPU jobs passed. The matrix covers N=4096–65536, 1–40 RNS prime towers, and 30/50/55/60-bit primes. A tower stores the polynomial modulo one prime. K counts those towers, not independent ciphertexts. Exact comparisons use the pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1 path. GPU base conversion, Hybrid key switching and encrypted inference are not implemented or validated by this run . Largest workload ¶ N=65536, K=40, 60-bit primes. All times below are medians in milliseconds . Operation GPU public call GPU resident experiment M1 OpenFHE CPU Forward NTT 8.079 0.552 21.071 Inverse NTT 8.045 0.598 21.827 Pointwise multiply 11.858 0.048 3.115 Complete polynomial multiply 12.431 1.723 65.687 A public call starts with CPU arrays, checks them, transfers them to the GPU, and returns a result to the CPU. The resident experiment times work with data already on the GPU; it excludes input upload, result download and result validation. It therefore measures a smaller interval. Subtracting the two times does not isolate the cost of the PCIe connection. M1-versus-GPU numbers compare different platforms, not same-host acceleration or the best possible parallel CPU implementation. Three findings ¶ 1. Keep data on the GPU. A fused call performs both forward transforms, pointwise multiplication and the inverse before returning the result. The unfused chain makes a separate public call for each operation. The directly measured fused call was faster in all 140 workloads: 2.36× median benefit , ranging from 1.77× to 3.03×. At the largest 60-bit workload, it reduces 33.412 ms to 12.431 ms. It removes repeated host roundtrips, not NTT kernel launches. Preserve this residency when adding base conversion and key switching. 2. The public-call cost matters. At the largest workload, resident multiplication takes 1.723 ms versus 12.431 ms through the public interface. Transfers, host array scans, staging, allocations and launch overhead need separate measurements. Small jobs can favor CPU: at N=4096/K=1, the M1 CPU product beats the public GPU call for both 30-bit and 60-bit primes. 3. Thirty-bit arithmetic still needs its own implementation. All widths currently use u64 storage and the same modular arithmetic. In this run the 60-bit resident cases are about 12% faster than their paired 30-bit cases. The reason is unproven; prime/input values and fixed run order differ. This does not establish an intrinsic advantage for wider primes. What to do next ¶ Use the split collector : GPU-only measurement jobs on the rental and the OpenFHE CPU baseline locally. Validate the prepared resident operation chain on named GPU hardware, including its lifetime and failure behavior. Test arithmetic and memory-layout candidates separately. Repeat a frozen batch on the actual RTX 5090, with controlled ordering and profiling permissions checked early. All preparation and analysis can proceed locally; no rental needs to stay running. Follow the roadmap and daily journal for the evidence and remaining gates. Measurement notes ¶ Protocol, uncertainty and evidence Each operation uses five warmups and 100 samples with matched N/q/roots/seed. The reported 32 GiB GPU capacity is unusual for that model; its physical configuration is unverified. The GPU batch contains 154,000 timed samples; the separate CPU batch contains 56,000. Raw JSONL summaries and manifest hashes were revalidated locally. The original mixed batch was cancelled after 366 completed jobs. The fresh 280-job GPU batch is separate; the cancelled run is not labelled a full pass. The archived benchmark-only patch changes timing selection, not device kernels. The M1 baseline runs OpenFHE’s NativeVector arithmetic and CRT-FTT transform one tower at a time. OpenMP parallel execution and native compiler optimizations are OFF. It does not measure a tuned, parallel multi-tower implementation or a complete CKKS application. Only one ordered run per workload was collected. At the largest 60-bit workload, public product p95 is 20.732 ms versus a 12.431 ms median; close differences and non-monotonic public timings need repeated runs. These percentiles describe the collected samples; they do not quantify how accurately one run estimates the performance of future runs. Large pinned-copy experiments reach approximately 13.0 GB/s H2D and 13.5 GB/s D2H payload rates. They do not decompose the pageable public-call measurements. A selected Nsight Systems trace was captured. Nsight Compute hardware counters were blocked by provider permissions, so the dominant resident-kernel bottleneck is still unproven. Sanitizers cover bounded smoke inputs, not every input or concurrent lifecycle. See the November 14 record for archive identity, exactness scope, source provenance and local evidence locations. Next: Resident operation chain →"}, {"title": "Offline test tooling", "summary": "Run the measurement scripts and check the records they save.", "group": "Plans", "url": "offline-test-tooling.html", "text": "Offline test tooling Collect GPU measurements on the rental and matching OpenFHE CPU measurements locally. Keep each machine’s report separate. The collector saves individual times, parameters and failures. Resume reuses only matching records after checking their contents and file fingerprints. Read full notes Collect GPU and CPU measurements separately ¶ Run GPU tests and timings on the rented machine. Measure OpenFHE on your local CPU to provide a baseline for comparison. Both runs use the same polynomial parameters and random-number seed, so they generate the same inputs. Each report identifies its own machine and build. The November 14 batch exercised an earlier CUDA baseline. The November 16 collector and resident runtime pass host tests and CUDA compilation; their remaining native checks are still open. The preparation guide covers dependencies and hardware setup. Collector and validation ¶ A collector is a script that runs checks and saves measurements and logs. Before starting, use the intended source revision with no uncommitted changes, and install the required OpenFHE version. Then run this on the GPU machine: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode gpu \\ --cuda-root /path/to/cuda --openfhe-prefix /path/to/openfhe \\ --architectures native --benchmark --benchmark-scope gpu \\ --output-dir bench-results-gpu \\ --benchmark-warmups 5 --benchmark-samples 100 On the local Linux or macOS CPU machine, use the same source revision: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode cpu \\ --openfhe-prefix /path/to/local/openfhe \\ --benchmark --output-dir bench-results-cpu CPU mode configures CUDA off and never calls nvcc, a CUDA executable or a GPU profiler. Its successful report says CPU_CHECKS_PASSED and gpu_execution: NOT_RUN . This path is verified on Linux; the newer macOS collector path has control-flow tests but still needs a native M1 run. Choose the measurement scopes ¶ A workload fixes N, the number of prime towers K, and the prime width. K counts primes in one polynomial, not ciphertexts. Defaults cover five ring sizes (4096–65536), four widths (30/50/55/60 bits), and K=1/3/8/16/24/32/40: 140 workloads . Session / selection Jobs Timing scopes --mode gpu --benchmark-scope gpu 280 Five-operation GPU public calls, resident events/pinned copies --mode cpu 140 Four-operation OpenFHE CPU baseline --mode gpu --benchmark-scope all 420 Legacy public calls including scalar CPU forward, events, OpenFHE CPU baseline Add --benchmark to collect timings. Direct GPU collector commands default to all ; the rental helper scripts/autodl_setup.sh defaults to gpu . Set --benchmark-scope all or NTT_BENCHMARK_SCOPE=all for a combined rental run. The GPU-only selection runs the tests labelled for real GPU execution, including their OpenFHE reference calculations and test inputs. It then runs the requested memory and threading error checks. Run standalone CPU checks and CPU timing on the local host. Use --benchmark-dimensions 65536 --benchmark-towers 3 40 for a bounded batch. Any K from 1 through 40 is selectable; the default samples seven counts. Match dimensions, tower counts, widths, warmups and samples across machines. Different platforms are not a same-host acceleration comparison. Use --benchmark-prime-bits 30 60 to limit widths. For the matched default versus legacy GPU experiment, use --compare-ntt and the fresh-directory commands in the rental queue . At 100 samples per operation, the default GPU selection records 154,000 timed samples; CPU records 56,000; combined records 224,000. These repeat fixed operands, not that many independent random inputs. Thirty-bit primes still use uint64_t buffers and arithmetic. Parameter files contain zero placeholders; the executables generate the seeded dense operands used for timings. Validate and retain records ¶ The collector keeps parameter files, raw JSONL, logs, hashes and report.json . The report validator rejects missing/truncated samples, duplicate indices, wrong parameters/scopes, nonfinite or negative times, and summaries inconsistent with the raw samples. A malformed record fails even if the executable exits zero. To recompute a saved summary: python3 scripts/benchmark_report.py /path/to/benchmark-n65536-k3-b30-public.log The report validator checks that the saved records are complete and consistent. Separate exact-answer tests check the arithmetic. A hash can show that a file changed or was corrupted; it cannot prove that a measurement was honestly collected. Keep the source and hardware details too. Restart and preserve partial results ¶ Rerun the same command with --resume --output-dir <record-directory> . Environment probes, build, exactness and requested sanitizers rerun. Successful measurement jobs can be reused only when clean source, selected scope, binary and OpenFHE hashes, hardware identity, parameters and repetitions match. Their raw logs must pass hash and content validation again. Changing CPU/GPU mode or scope requires a fresh directory. CPU resume ignores unused CUDA binaries, GPU visibility and architecture settings. Clock readings are not CPU identity keys. Old reports are archived; new stage logs have unique attempt names. Reused jobs retain their original attempt identity. Independent measurement failures preserve completed jobs, but the overall run fails. Setup, exactness or sanitizer failure stops before timing. The default per-command timeout is 1,800 seconds. Do not run concurrent collectors in one directory. Export the entire record directory and verify its archive hash before ending the rental. Optional selected profiling ¶ Add --profile only when both nsys and ncu work and the host permits GPU counter access. The bounded capture uses N=65536/K=3/60-bit, one warmup and two samples. Nsight Systems records a public-call timeline; Nsight Compute's basic set records the first eight launches of the event experiment. Attempt-specific prefixes preserve earlier artifacts. Requested profiling fails if tools, permissions or capture fail; omit the flag to leave profiling pending. Profiler timings are separate from benchmark samples. Native build logs retain ptxas -v resources and line mappings. Full per-pass counters, active telemetry, production CKKS batches and an evidence-backed bottleneck decision remain later work. Raw measurement protocol ¶ The command-line programs read N, primes and roots from a parameter file. They use a fixed random seed to generate repeatable inputs. Before timing each warmup or sample, they restore and pack the input arrays. This also applies to inverse transforms. Output includes individual samples, summaries and an explicit completion marker. Percentiles use linear interpolation at (count−1)*fraction . Command Operations and timing boundary ntt_cuda_cli gpu-bench-jsonl parameters.txt 5 100 GPU forward, inverse, pointwise, unfused product and fused product; host-wall public-call time ntt_cuda_cli bench-jsonl parameters.txt 5 100 The same GPU operations plus scalar reference forward; legacy combined public protocol ntt_cuda_cli events-jsonl parameters.txt 5 100 Resident forward, inverse, pointwise and product; separate pinned H2D/D2H copies ntt_resident_bench parameters.txt 2 10 Host-wall time for the device-resident chain, public host boundary, and explicit host-roundtrip control ntt_openfhe_bench parameters.txt 5 100 Four OpenFHE NativeVector/CRT-FTT CPU operations Select --benchmark-scope gpu-conversion to add six ModUp/ModDown timing operations to the GPU and resident NTT scopes. The active Q count is the parameter file's tower count, with three disjoint 60-bit P primes recorded alongside it. The four resident operations exclude the final verification download. The host controls include input/output copies and optionally the intermediate QP roundtrip. Session setup is excluded, while conversion parameter setup inside each operation is included. Select --benchmark-scope gpu-chain to add the resident prototype timings to the GPU public and CUDA-event records. For the device chain, inputs are uploaded once and the timer ends when the coefficient output is ready on the device. The exact coefficient check downloads it after the timer. The host boundary times both input uploads and the final download. The roundtrip control also downloads and re-uploads each intermediate. All three scopes include prototype allocations, copies and synchronization inside their measured interval. Each checks every result and excludes one-time plan setup. Keep warmups and samples at 200 combined or fewer. Public GPU calls include validation, guards, transfers and the multiply interface's by-value input allocation/copy. Result retention/destruction is outside the repeated timer. Last results are checked against the reference outside timing; the full exactness suite remains a separate check. gpu-bench-jsonl uses host_wall_gpu_public_call ; legacy bench-jsonl retains its distinct protocol. The old bench command is a 46-bit legacy path and is no longer used by the collector. The measurement helper has fake-clock tests for reset order, warmups, summaries and JSON escaping. They test control logic, not performance. Resident events and CPU baseline ¶ The event experiment times operations while their data are already on the GPU. It excludes setup, memory allocation, input upload/reset, result download and answer checks. After restoring the input, each iteration waits for earlier work on the default stream to finish. It places start/stop events around the selected work and waits for the stop event. A multi-kernel interval can include gaps between submissions, so it is not a sum of isolated kernel instruction times. H2D means host-to-device (CPU-to-GPU); D2H is the reverse. The copy experiment uses preallocated pinned CPU memory, which the operating system keeps in place for transfers, and cudaMemcpyAsync . The public call uses ordinary pageable memory. These separate experiments cannot be added or subtracted to obtain an exact breakdown of the public call. The public wrapper remains synchronous. There is no measured copy/compute overlap or multi-stream throughput here. See NVIDIA's event API and timing guidance . The OpenFHE comparator uses sequential per-tower preconditioned CRT-FTT and NativeVector arithmetic. Tables, packing, reset and reference checks are outside timing; internal library allocations remain included. It records compiler/OpenFHE flags and OpenMP/native-optimization settings. This is not a best-tuned DCRT/OpenMP/HEXL or complete CKKS baseline, and its multiplication allocation boundary differs from the GPU public API. Exactness and sanitizer scopes ¶ cuda_benchmark_matrix_exact checks the 140 default parameter cells against OpenFHE with seven patterns, independent inverse, roundtrip and product. Custom K values also receive the benchmark's reference checks, but do not extend that fixed direct-OpenFHE gate. Each of memcheck, racecheck, initcheck and synccheck runs three scopes: Legacy --smoke : N=65536, three widths, zero/dense patterns. --matrix-smoke : N=4096/65536, 30/60-bit, K=3/40, zero/dense. ntt_resident_cuda_tests --smoke : dense N=65536, unsorted 55/50/60-bit basis. That is twelve sanitizer runs in the current collector. The older Ada batch passed the first two scopes; it predates the resident scope. Host gates now include resident_runtime_host and resident_openfhe_host ; CUDA builds add resident_cuda_preflight and GPU-labelled resident_cuda_exact . Host/compiler success does not close these native gates. Shared allocation ownership and injected failures ¶ SetupResources registers each of the wrapper constructor's six allocations immediately and releases them in reverse order. Its four constant uploads use the same adapter boundary. Host tests exercise this actual ownership code with a host-memory adapter. setup_ownership_host injects before and during each of ten adapter operations: 20 failure cases, plus normal and cleanup-reporting paths. The GPU-labelled cuda_setup_fault_injection uses the real constructor at N=65536 with three 50-bit towers and verifies successful CUDA frees after pre-call injection. That probe passed in the older Ada batch. Throwing a test exception does not reproduce a real GPU out-of-memory error, a partially completed memory transfer, incorrect device restoration or a destroyed GPU context. Those cases still need hardware tests. Ordinary destructor cleanup is nonthrowing and cannot report errors to its caller; a diagnostic trace is not a production recovery interface. Verify without renting ¶ cmake -S . -B build -DNTT_ENABLE_CUDA=OFF cmake --build build -j2 ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure .venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests/tooling -v These run host arithmetic, ownership, timing and collector/report checks. Use the preparation guide for compiler-only CUDA checks. Native validation must identify the exact source revision and machine; older results cannot establish correctness of later runtime changes. Next: Full GPU matrix: analysis →"}, {"title": "Writing & publishing", "summary": "Edit notebook sources, preview them and publish the generated website.", "group": "Contribute", "url": "contributing-docs.html", "text": "Writing & publishing Explain the purpose first, define unfamiliar words and place commands beside their expected results. Keep detailed evidence available in labelled notes. Build and check the notebook, inspect desktop and phone views, then export and push the website. Pushing only the source repository does not update the personal site. Read full notes Write and publish the notebook ¶ Edit Markdown or the three interactive HTML lessons in this repository. Build and check the notebook, then export it to the personal website. The HTML in the website repository is generated from these files. Make content changes here so the next export preserves them. Preview locally ¶ python3 -m venv .venv .venv/bin/python -m pip install -r docs/requirements.txt .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check python3 -m http.server 8000 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory build/docs-site Open http://127.0.0.1:8000/ . Rebuild after each edit. The interactive source HTML also works as a standalone page. Read the site ¶ Use the left menu to change pages and search with Ctrl+K / Cmd+K . Each page starts with its main idea. Expand the notes for explanations, commands, evidence or an interactive lesson. A link to a specific section opens any collapsed notes automatically. Learn explains concepts; Research reports evidence; Plans lists remaining work. The journal records what happened on each calendar date. Its dates are separate from the roadmap's working-day milestones. Add a daily entry ¶ python3 scripts/new_journal.py --date 2026-11-17 --title \"Describe the outcome\" The helper creates a draft and never overwrites an existing entry. Append new work to the existing day. Keep the metadata fields title , date , summary , phase and status , and complete these six sections: Section What to write Outcome The result Reasoning Why the change was needed Changes What changed, with source links Verification Checks actually run, their environment and limits Open questions What is unresolved Next step The next deliverable Set status: published when ready. Drafts stay off the website. Label reconstructed history and cite commits. Update the roadmap only when a milestone's acceptance checks pass. Add or edit an article ¶ Add an entry to docs/site.json for each new article. Include its source file, web address, title, group, summary, one to three takeaways, and the address of the next page. Keep existing page addresses and section bookmarks stable so old links continue to work. The builder turns relative links into notebook pages or GitHub links to the exact source revision. Lead with the page's purpose and main result. Define unfamiliar terms before using abbreviations. Put commands beside their expected outcome; keep long source excerpts and historical evidence in labelled expandable sections: <details markdown=\"1\"> <summary>Source and verification details</summary> Reference material goes here. </details> Use ## for main headings and ### for subsections. Write inline math as \\(...\\) and display math as \\[...\\] ; KaTeX is bundled locally. Search includes collapsed text. Keep wide tables scrollable inside the page. For OpenFHE behavior, link the function name to the actual implementation at the pinned commit, with GitHub line anchors. Verify the cited lines and retain the BSD license for literal excerpts. Distinguish existing upstream code from our proposed GPU interfaces. Preserve the Sunlit Paper theme: warm paper, orange/green accents, Anton headings and Space Grotesk body text. Its source is docs/site-assets/sunlit-paper.css ; layout and lesson contrast rules stay in their existing stylesheets. Validate before publishing ¶ .venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests/docs -v .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check node --test tests/docs/*.test.cjs The build checks that every article is listed, required description fields exist, and local page and section links point to real targets. The Node tests execute the lesson scripts and require Node.js 22+. Inspect changed pages in a browser at desktop and phone widths, including expanded notes, math, controls, search and long tables. Documentation checks do not establish CUDA correctness. For implementation checks, use the relevant guide: Area Instructions and scope Stage indices and twiddles Work-distribution proofs : CPU execution of shared stage helpers Modular arithmetic Arithmetic contract : bigint and full-size CPU checks OpenFHE arrays and formats Representation contract : pinned core-library comparisons Hybrid key switching Operation graph : intermediate CPU arrays and actual keys Runtime ownership Resident-chain prototype : host lifetime/failure tests; native CUDA pending Native CUDA session Preparation guide : actual GPU exactness, sanitizers and measurements Publish with GitHub Pages ¶ From this repository: .venv/bin/python scripts/export_website.py --website-root /path/to/minnow54426.github.io Review, commit and push the generated systems/ntt-gpu/ files in the website repository. Its Pages workflow publishes the notebook . The exporter refreshes only its managed files and retains the return link to the personal website. Ordinary pushes to ntt-gpu create a documentation preview artifact; they do not publish the personal website. For a separate project Pages site, choose Settings → Pages → Source → GitHub Actions , then run the Documentation workflow with deploy_standalone enabled. That optional site lives at minnow54426.github.io/ntt-gpu . Next: Project & setup →"}, {"title": "Analyze Ada timings and prepare conversion measurements", "summary": "Revalidated the November 19 records, compared schedule timings and prepared six resident ModUp/ModDown timing cases for the next GPU batch.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-21.html", "text": "2026-11-21 Offline analysis and rental preparation / P2 D11 ← All entries Analyze Ada timings and prepare conversion measurements Revalidated the November 19 records, compared schedule timings and prepared six resident ModUp/ModDown timing cases for the next GPU batch. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Analyze Ada timings and prepare conversion measurements ¶ Outcome ¶ The raw November 19 archive is available on the M1 Pro and its checksum matches. The earlier cloud workspace's missing-input blocker is resolved here. Revalidated the paired measurements and balanced repeats, then wrote the analysis . Group twiddles improved all eight device-event NTT workloads. Median legacy/group ratios were 1.515 for forward, 1.503 for inverse and 1.493 for fused multiplication. Resident-interface repeats showed substantially smaller differences. P2/D11 remains open. Reasoning ¶ The source of a speedup matters when selecting the next optimization. Host public calls, the resident interface and device events measure different work. Whole-profile stream creation includes setup excluded by the benchmark. These distinctions make conversion parameter setup, allocation and transfer costs useful targets for the next measurements. Changes ¶ Added a reusable paired-record analyzer with manifest, hash, native gate, environment, workload, binary and raw-summary validation. Generated 112 matched operation rows and twelve balanced-repeat rows. Prepared six conversion timing operations covering both ModUp input formats, independent ModDown, resident composition, the host boundary and an intermediate host-roundtrip control. Reused pinned OpenFHE fixture helpers for exact answers outside timing. Every warmup and sample checks its output. Transfer counters check the intended boundaries. Added the GPU conversion selection to the collector, with a bounded conversion-benchmark smoke under all four sanitizer tools before timing. Kept one-time NTT session setup outside timing and explicitly recorded per-operation conversion parameter setup inside timing. Updated the roadmap, analysis and next-rental commands. Verification ¶ All 30 local CPU/OpenFHE CTests and 57 tooling tests passed. The host protocol covers six scopes, special-prime metadata and sample admission. The focused legacy protocol and schedule checks also passed. The analyzer's synthetic checks distinguish known ratios from damaged logs, incomplete matrices, changed hardware, identical executables, missing sanitizer evidence, mismatched summaries and escaped log paths. They verify record handling. The performance findings come from the original native archive. The largest host-driver cases use N=65,536, 40 Q towers and three P towers with 30- and 60-bit Q primes. Their output is marked as simulation and is rejected by the native collector. This preparation ran locally. Open questions ¶ The new conversion timing executable needs compiler-only CI and native GPU execution. The resident ModUp/ModDown implementation added after November 19 requires its expanded native and sanitizer gates. Hardware counters and primary RTX 5090 acceptance remain open. Next step ¶ Compile both schedules for sm_89/sm_120 without renting. Then include the conversion exactness, sanitizer probes and timings in the next combined RTX 5090 session. The repeated resident-interface differences motivate measuring parameter and allocation costs before selecting a production optimization. Follow-up compiler and publication checks ¶ Commit edd868f passed compiler-only CI in both schedules for sm_89 and sm_120, including the new native conversion benchmark. Both jobs also passed their host checks. Native GPU measurements remain queued for the next rental. The documentation build validates 45 published notebook pages with seventeen journal entries. Desktop and phone browser checks cover all 64 tracked website pages, with a transient local photo-page connection retried successfully. The new analysis tables use contained horizontal scrolling so labels and numbers stay readable on a phone. The report's final expanded view passed both widths. Existing local research drafts were preserved separately. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Connect resident NTT and basis conversion", "summary": "Added resident ModUp/ModDown composition with exact CPU comparisons, failure checks and both CUDA builds. Raw-timing analysis still awaits the archive.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-20.html", "text": "2026-11-20 Resident conversion preparation / P2 D11 unchanged ← All entries Connect resident NTT and basis conversion Added resident ModUp/ModDown composition with exact CPU comparisons, failure checks and both CUDA builds. Raw-timing analysis still awaits the archive. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Connect resident NTT and basis conversion ¶ Outcome ¶ The source and website repositories were pulled to 52b87ac and 40e004d . The November 19 journal records the completed Ada run, but its raw archive is not present in this cloud workspace. No connected Mac document session or GitHub release containing the archive was found. Requested the archive from the user's M1 Pro before calculating or publishing performance findings. Reasoning ¶ The recorded 48 measurement jobs and four order-balanced resident repeats need their original samples. A test-pass count cannot establish the relative speed of the schedules. The Nsight timeline includes session setup, so its whole-trace totals cannot be substituted for steady-state timings. Changes ¶ Prepared analyze_batch.py and its control tests under the local directory /workspace/scratch/ntt-gpu-analysis/ . The analyzer uses the repository's existing JSONL validator, checks each log against its manifest hash, checks the complete selected workload matrix, and requires matching source, hardware, parameters and measurement settings across schedules. It emits one CSV row per matched operation and timing scope, with median, fifth/ninety-fifth percentile and legacy/group median ratio. It keeps public, event and resident-interface timings separate. The balanced repeats and setup-aware timeline interpretation remain separate analysis steps once the original files are available. These local helpers are preparation rather than a new production collector interface. Verification ¶ Eight synthetic control tests pass: known statistics and ratios, modified log hash, incomplete sanitizer checks, different hardware, identical binary identities, inconsistent report summaries, a wrong matrix cell, and a path outside the record directory. These tests establish parser behavior only; none is GPU execution or an observed performance result. Open questions ¶ The November 19 .tar.gz archive from the M1 Pro's build/gpu-session-20261119/ directory is required. Its archive checksum, balanced-repeat records and timeline exports must be inspected after receipt. No native milestone or performance conclusion changes during preparation. Next step ¶ Receive and verify the archive, analyze the paired measurements and balanced repeats, inspect the CUDA timeline with setup excluded where appropriate, then publish the supported findings and their limitations to the notebook. The timing report remains blocked on that input. The independent implementation work completed later on this date is recorded below. Follow-up: compose resident ModUp and ModDown ¶ Outcome ¶ Added coefficient basis switching and CKKS ModUp/ModDown to the resident session. Polynomial intermediates remain on the device transport. The previously tested standalone converter is now composed with NTT operations, whole-tower views, Q/P packing and the final P-inverse correction. This is local preparation; the November 19 GPU results apply to the earlier source. Reasoning ¶ The missing raw archive blocks timing interpretation but not exact operation composition. ModUp must preserve the original Q evaluations and transform only the data needed to create P. ModDown must inverse-transform P, form and transform the Q correction, then subtract and scale. A chain cannot safely borrow stack-local conversion tables while GPU work is pending. Changes ¶ Added explicit-layout upload, coefficient switch_basis , mod_up and mod_down to the public resident prototype. Added packed parameter tables, range copies, conversion and correction arithmetic to both the deferred CPU transport and CUDA adapter. Charged per-operation parameter storage against device/host budgets and retained it with queued work before submission. A failed unproven drain keeps tables, staging and operands alive. Added Stats::parameter_uploads separately from polynomial transfers. Upload → ModUp → ModDown → download has one polynomial upload, three small parameter uploads and one final polynomial download, excluding fixed session setup tables. Extended the existing host/native OpenFHE fixture and resident controls. The collector already requires that native test and runs its expanded smoke case under all four sanitizer tools. Updated the conversion guide , resident guide, backlog, roadmap and rental queue. P2/D11 stays open. Verification ¶ The full CPU/OpenFHE build passes all 29 CTests. The expanded resident comparison covers N=64/4096/65536, five active Q levels, mixed 30/50/55/60-bit primes, four patterns and an additional 40-Q/three-P dense case. It compares both ModUp input formats, independent ModDown, composition, input immutability and offset tower views with actual OpenFHE. The latest host control suite passes 450 checks, including every injected transport failure and every failure after enqueue in the conversion chain. Parameter device/host budget rejections and retention during unproven drain also pass. The suite passes AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and leak detection. Stock CUDA 13.0.88 builds the new adapter and native fixture for sm_89 and sm_120 in both default and legacy schedules. Three default-build driver-free preflight tests and the legacy resident preflight pass. No new GPU kernel, GPU sanitizer or performance run was executed in this workspace. All 27 documentation tests and 14 Node math/interaction tests pass. The build validates 43 notebook pages with 16 journal entries. Chromium passes 248 page/width checks across all 62 website pages at 1440/1024/375/320 pixels, including 164 expanded article/journal checks. Math, contrast, English text, unique IDs, theme, navigation, search and existing interactive lessons pass without script/HTTP errors or page-wide overflow. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. Source CI and public deployment follow the two repository pushes. Open questions ¶ The new conversion chain requires its own native exactness and sanitizer run. Conversion-plan caching, allocator reuse, GPU Hybrid key switching and production integration remain future work. Real driver/context failures and primary RTX 5090 acceptance are still open. The raw November 19 measurement archive remains unavailable here. Next step ¶ Publish the source and revised guides, then run the expanded resident gate on the next RTX 5090 session. Analyze the existing Ada measurements when the verified archive is transferred from the M1 Pro. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Validate the expanded GPU batch on Ada", "summary": "Both schedules passed native Ada checks, 40 sanitizer stages and 48 measurement jobs, with a verified local export and CUDA timeline.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-19.html", "text": "2026-11-19 Native Ada diagnostic / P2 D11 ← All entries Validate the expanded GPU batch on Ada Both schedules passed native Ada checks, 40 sanitizer stages and 48 measurement jobs, with a verified local export and CUDA timeline. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Prepare more work before renting ¶ Outcome ¶ Added Shoup constant multiplication and a reusable coefficient-domain approximate basis-conversion plan. The plan preserves the unreduced CRT sum, prime order and canonical residues required by pinned OpenFHE. The new native probes join the NTT and resident-chain checks in the next rental batch . At preparation time, native execution was pending. The completed Ada run is recorded below. The roadmap stays at P2/D11. Reasoning ¶ The M1 Pro can establish the arithmetic, parameter tables and exact expected arrays before provisioning. Shoup multiplication prepares each fixed multiplier once. Each conversion target coefficient is independent, allowing a simple first CUDA kernel with one output per thread. The target-product reduction retains the reference 128-bit remainder path. Native profiling will guide later optimization. Full resident ModUp/ModDown composition remains a later deliverable. Changes ¶ Added canonical Shoup multiplication with a documented quotient bound and host/device high-product implementations. Added immutable basis plans for disjoint prime sets with up to 40 towers, source-prefix-specific constants and input validation. Added an independent Python CRT oracle and a broad OpenFHE comparison. Checked the converter inside existing Hybrid digit and ModDown-correction fixtures against independent BigInteger answers. Prepared CUDA Shoup and basis-conversion tests and added both to the collector's mandatory tests and four sanitizer tools. Extended compiler-only CI to link the pinned OpenFHE fixture for both CUDA schedules. Updated the roadmap, backlog and rental queue. The conversion guide explains the formulas and test coverage. Verification ¶ On the M1 Pro, all 29 CTests passed, including the broader conversion test's 107,675,648 residue comparisons against pinned OpenFHE. Its matrix covers all five ring dimensions from 4,096 through 65,536, all four benchmark prime widths, source counts 1/3/40, four patterns and both conversion directions. The independent Python conversion oracle passed 334 cases, including 1,057 coefficients that distinguish approximate conversion from canonical CRT. The arithmetic oracle checked 19,170 results including Shoup products. The Hybrid graph and standalone ModUp/ModDown tests passed with the new converter checked at each lift. AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer also passed the host arithmetic and conversion probes. The native test callers passed host syntax checks. All 52 tooling tests passed, including candidate sanitizer failure stopping collection before timing. Open questions ¶ Native execution must establish agreement for CUDA's high-product intrinsic and the prepared conversion kernel. Compile these probes for sm_120 before renting. Performance and production resident composition remain open. Next step ¶ Run the updated driverless CUDA build checks, then collect native exactness, sanitizer results, matched NTT timings and the resident profile in one RTX 5090 rental. Native GPU batch completed ¶ Used the supplied rental server for the prepared CUDA work. The device reported RTX 4080 SUPER, compute 8.9, 32,760 MiB and driver 595.71.05. CUDA was 12.8. CMake 3.22 could not configure CUDA C++20; installing CMake 3.31.6 resolved that configuration failure. Pinned OpenFHE was built as a dependency. Standalone CPU tests and CPU performance measurements stayed on the M1 Pro. The native source was an isolated clean snapshot, 0f2df64 , of the current workspace based on 587598a . The record contains a full source bundle, the original workspace archive, exact dependency revision and their provenance. Both the group-twiddle and legacy builds passed all 11 selected CTests, including two fixture generators per build. Each build passed 20 sanitizer stages across memcheck, racecheck, initcheck and synccheck. The new Shoup, standalone conversion and resident-path checks passed. The conversion fixture compared 107,675,648 residues against OpenFHE in each build. Both schedules also compiled the CUDA targets and native probes for sm_120. The paired sweep saved 48 validated measurement jobs across N=4,096/65,536, K=3/40 and 30/60-bit primes. The three recorded scopes were GPU public calls, CUDA events and the resident interface. Each used two warmups and ten samples. After observing host-call variability, recorded an order-balanced resident repeat at N=65,536/K=3/60-bit, with five warmups and 50 samples per operation in each of four runs. Detailed performance interpretation remains local work. Nsight Systems captured the resident timeline and exported its statistics. Nsight Compute returned ERR_NVGPUCTRPERM ; hardware counters remain unavailable on this provider. The timeline includes session setup, so whole-trace totals need care when interpreting timings that exclude setup. Downloaded the complete 3.7 MiB archive into build/gpu-session-20261119/ . Verified its SHA-256, extracted it locally, then rechecked all measurement hashes and records, sanitizer statuses, repeat hashes and the timeline. The local verification record is verified-results.json . Told the user that the rental can stop after this verification. Further analysis and notebook checks run on the M1 Pro. P2/D11 remains open for primary RTX 5090 evidence, actual driver/context failure handling and hardware-counter profiling. Full resident ModUp/ModDown composition and GPU Hybrid key switching remain later deliverables. Publication checks ¶ Updated the public summaries to reflect the completed native Ada run. All 61 tracked website pages passed desktop and phone browser checks. The conversion guide's equations were split into shorter lines for its phone view. Documentation tests and generated links passed locally. The existing Chinese research drafts are preserved locally while the English engineering notebook is published. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Explain NTT concepts in plain English", "summary": "Explained NTT formulas, prime-tower data and butterfly merges, then reviewed all 40 notebook pages and rewrote unclear passages for beginners.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-18.html", "text": "2026-11-18 Documentation maintenance / P2 unchanged ← All entries Explain NTT concepts in plain English Explained NTT formulas, prime-tower data and butterfly merges, then reviewed all 40 notebook pages and rewrote unclear passages for beginners. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Read the NTT evaluation and inverse formulas ¶ Outcome ¶ Expanded exact evaluation and inverse formulas for beginners. The section now explains the symbols, distinguishes coefficient indices from stored evaluation positions, and works through a complete forward and inverse example. The transform contract and implementation are unchanged. Reasoning ¶ The compact formulas assumed that readers already knew polynomial evaluation, fixed-width bit reversal and modular inverses. Explain each operation before combining them. A four-point example makes the stored ordering visible without requiring a reader to follow a 65536-element calculation. Keep the production formulas at N=65536 and label the small example explicitly. Put the cancellation proof in optional notes, so readers can follow the calculation before studying why it works. Changes ¶ Added a symbol table for coefficients, memory indices, root indices, roots, bit reversal and N-inverse. Explained why odd powers are roots of x^N+1 and mapped the first five memory positions to their natural point indices and root exponents. Read forward as polynomial substitution and inverse as a weighted sum with modular normalization. Distinguished negative powers from array indices and decimal reciprocals. Added N=4, q=17, psi=2 with coefficients [1,2,3,4] , stored evaluations [15,11,13,16] , inverse roots [9,8,15,2] and recovered coefficients. Added an optional geometric-sum explanation and retained the existing diagnostic probes, upstream source citations and section anchor. Verification ¶ Direct modular evaluation gives the displayed forward values. A separate calculation solves the evaluation equations using Gaussian elimination with arithmetic modulo 17. It recovers the original coefficients. The inverse weighted sums modulo 17 are [4,8,12,16] ; scaling by 4-inverse, 13, returns [1,2,3,4] . A temporary C++20 executable using the actual NegacyclicNtt header independently checks the forward result and inverse of the literal displayed evaluation array. Both pass with compiler warnings treated as errors. All 27 documentation tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests pass before the final publication build. These are arithmetic, CPU and documentation checks, not a new GPU run. The 40-page build, including 14 journal entries, validates local links and fragments. Chromium passes four focused checks at 1440/1024/375/320px for the symbol guide, original section bookmark, worked arrays, expanded inverse proof, formula rendering, contrast and contained table scrolling. All 59 website pages also pass desktop and phone layout/math checks (118 page/width checks), without script or HTTP errors. Desktop/phone screenshots were inspected; main equations use multiple lines and the worked table has compact, explained symbols. Publication and live checks follow the source commit. Open questions ¶ No transform or roadmap decision changed. The current native RTX 5090 and resident-runtime validation work remains tracked separately in the roadmap and next-session guide . Next step ¶ Publish the explanation in both repositories and verify readable formulas, wide-table scrolling, source links and the section bookmark on desktop and phone layouts. Follow-up: explain the three-tower data and byte counts ¶ Expanded the big picture from a size summary into a data walkthrough. It now distinguishes one RNS polynomial from ciphertext components and decoded slots, shows coefficient reduction and tower-major packing, and derives word indices, byte offsets, 512 KiB per tower and 1.5 MiB per polynomial. The teaching example uses N=4, q=[17,41,73], roots=[2,3,10] and coefficients [1,−2,20,100]. Reducing each coefficient under each prime and storing one whole tower after another gives [1,15,3,15, 1,39,20,18, 1,71,20,27] . The first tower transforms to [10,16,3,9] without changing its four-word payload. The section labels this as a small example and keeps N=65536 as the project workload. Added actual NTT multipliers and modular inverse-length values. Explained why each tower’s multiplier table reserves N+1 words, and distinguished the two polynomial buffers from the four arrays of fixed constants. The six-buffer total is 6,291,552 bytes; only 3,145,824 constant bytes are uploaded at construction. Corrected the later table-index explanation to distinguish the default bit-reversed exponent from the legacy exponent. A temporary C++20 program using the actual CPU NTT and transform_twiddles passes the residue/packing, transform/inverse, twiddle, normalization and payload checks with warnings treated as errors. Independent modular evaluation and CRT reconstruction confirm the example modulo Q=50,881. All 27 documentation tests and 14 Node tests pass. These are CPU/source/documentation checks; no GPU run or roadmap gate is added. The three-tower page passed browser checks at 1440, 1024, 375 and 320 pixels. Its tables have keyboard-focusable horizontal scrolling, preserving whole numbers and labels on narrow screens. Follow-up: derive the butterfly invariant from the negacyclic merge ¶ Expanded the butterfly invariant with the factorization of x^(2d)−β into x^d−α and x^d+α, where α²=β. The child remainders U±αV explain the default group-twiddle butterfly, its per-stage polynomial meaning, its final bit-reversed evaluations and its canonical stored range. Reversing a merge gives 2u and 2v, which explains the single final N-inverse normalization. Added a four-word example using N=4, q=17 and ψ=2, including both forward and unnormalized inverse stage arrays. A separate disclosure derives the CPU/legacy implicit twist and explains why its intermediate arrays differ. Corrected the adjacent arithmetic-work paragraph: the default helper uses one modular multiply per butterfly, the legacy helper two, and the CPU reference three including twiddle advancement. Temporary C++20 probes compiled with warnings as errors in both default and legacy configurations confirm the published stage arrays, final CPU agreement, roundtrip, and all 867 canonical pair merges for three example twiddles. Independent Python polynomial evaluation and factor remainders agree. These are CPU checks of shared helpers, not CUDA execution. Documentation tests pass after both article updates. Both revised articles pass browser checks at 1440, 1024, 375 and 320 pixels, including rendered equations, contrast, bookmarks, table scrolling and the CPU-reference disclosure. Screenshot review confirmed readable desktop/phone text and numeric tables. All 59 website pages also pass desktop and phone checks (118 page/width combinations), with no page-wide overflow, math errors, HTTP failures or script errors. The existing Sunlit Paper theme is preserved. Follow-up: review every notebook page for beginners ¶ Reviewed all 40 pages: 24 topic guides, 14 dated entries, the overview and the journal index. Rewrote unclear passages throughout the article bodies and journal explanations, as well as all topic summaries and opening takeaways. The review retains useful technical terms but explains them where they first matter. Examples include canonical residues, prime bases, streams, ownership pins, test fixtures, independent reference calculations and acceptance gates. The overview now includes a small optional glossary. The interactive lessons explain coefficients, modular arithmetic, threads and blocks before using them. The research pages explain the mechanisms behind library techniques; the design pages connect ownership rules to the errors they prevent. Journal prose is clearer while dates, commands, observed counts, source revisions, follow-up order and historical limitations remain intact. The journal index and entry notice explain how dated status differs from the current roadmap. Source comparison confirms that existing Markdown equations, fenced code, headings and source URLs are preserved. Inline scripts, styles, math and IDs in the three HTML lessons are unchanged. The external 16-point and Hybrid explorer scripts have wording changes only; their arithmetic is unchanged. All 38 article/journal source files have editorial changes. No C++ code, GPU execution, design theme or roadmap completion state changes in this review. All 27 documentation tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests pass. The 40-page build validates local routes and section links. Chromium passes 236 page/width checks across all 59 website pages at 1440, 1024, 375 and 320 pixels. This includes 152 expanded article/journal checks, readable contrast, formula rendering, English text, unique IDs and the original paper palette and fonts. Search, section bookmarks, keyboard disclosures, next-page links, arithmetic widgets, the 16-point example, and the existing portfolio photo/painting controls work. No script or HTTP errors or page-wide overflow were observed. Another 28 checks cover the final overview/setup wording, introductory lessons and all three standalone lessons at the same four widths. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. Source CI and live deployment are verified separately after pushing. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Make the notebook easier to read", "summary": "Reviewed every notebook page, simplified explanations and distinguished current instructions from results recorded on earlier dates.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-17.html", "text": "2026-11-17 Documentation maintenance / P2 D11 unchanged ← All entries Make the notebook easier to read Reviewed every notebook page, simplified explanations and distinguished current instructions from results recorded on earlier dates. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Make the notebook easier to read ¶ Outcome ¶ Reviewed all 37 existing notebook pages: the overview, 23 topic guides, 12 dated entries and the journal index. The guides now define unfamiliar terms earlier, use shorter explanations and point to a useful next page. The Sunlit Paper theme, interactive examples and exact engineering results are retained. The review also corrects stale execution notes. The temporary Ada baseline has measured results; later resident-runtime changes still need native GPU verification. D11/P2 remains open. Reasoning ¶ A short summary helps readers start, but the full article must be clear too. Explain its purpose and required background first. Remove repeated progress updates and place commands separately from detailed measurement notes. Keep the short default view and full notes for readers who need the proof or reproduction record. Dated journal bodies remain historical evidence. Their shorter editorial titles and summaries describe the day's overall outcome without changing recorded commands, counts, source revisions or follow-up chronology. Each entry links to the current roadmap so an old next step is not mistaken for today's task. Changes ¶ Reworked all 23 topic guides, including the three interactive lessons. Added plain-language definitions of NTT, tower, format, canonical residue, buffer layout, completion and resource ownership where they are first needed. Shortened repeated survey recommendations, completed-phase descriptions and collector history. Put current CPU/GPU commands and scope choices first. Corrected the GPU lesson's overstatement about CPU simulation and clarified that code snippets use abbreviated names. Preserved the executable scripts, formulas, controls and upstream source excerpts. Added a short beginner reading path to the overview and a next-guide link to each topic. Retained full notes, source links, search and deep-link reveal. Reviewed all 12 journal entries, simplified their titles/summaries and retained their original dated bodies. Clarified calendar dates versus milestones. Page-by-page review Page Reading change Overview Added definitions and a three-step beginner path; retained the checkpoint and complete directory Project & setup CPU build first; compact OpenFHE checks and accurate current native status NTT & butterflies Shorter root/evaluation explanations; clarified fixed-ring multiplication From CPU to CUDA Replaced repeated metaphors with thread/block explanations; separated simulation from native verification 65536 work distribution Defined towers and shortened ownership/dependency prose; retained the index proofs CUDA buffers Shortened setup/cleanup and distinguished the synchronous wrapper from resident handles Arithmetic Added a canonical-residue example and distinguished raw CPU assumptions from wrapper validation OpenFHE representation Defined NativePoly, DCRTPoly and format before the exact array rules Hybrid graph Explained key switching, Q/P and towers before the source-level graph Backend contract Explained layouts/completions; separated the full proposal from the implemented subset Resident chain Lead with the chain and limits; simplified failure wording while preserving guarantees Run & debug Added a terminal path, fewer initial watch variables and an optional recorded workaround GPU NTT survey One actionable recommendation per library; removed duplicated measurement lists GPU selection Defined the resident working set and kept the decision tied to measurements Rental market Shorter provider comparison; retained dated prices, billing and permissions evidence Roadmap Explained D/P labels, compressed completed work and corrected current P2 context Architecture decision Shorter accepted-design opening; linked current implementation separately Backlog Consolidated evidence for B01–B04; retained every task's acceptance criteria Before renting Current host/compiler scope first; older counts remain in dated history Benchmark plan Grouped tuning variables and clarified GPU-only versus combined job counts First GPU analysis Labelled the historical source and linked the later full-matrix evidence Full GPU matrix Defined towers and led with the measured result and next implementation boundary Collection tooling Commands and scope selection first; removed superseded split-runner instructions Writing & publishing One editing/check/export workflow; linked specialist checks instead of repeating them Journal index Explained historical status and linked the live tracker November 5 Shorter outcome summary; preserved reconstructed evidence November 6 Plain-language roadmap summary; preserved the original gate state November 7 Clear debugger/derivation summary; preserved recorded CPU/formula checks November 8 Short publishing summary; retained deployment history November 9 Summary covers final column-proof outcome, not only the early 4096 example November 10 Summary covers D3–D6; retained all intermediate updates and counts November 11 Summary includes both D7 and D8 plus teaching follow-ups November 12 Clear design/offline-build outcome; preserved the review and citation records November 13 Plain-language rental/counter summary; retained dated research November 14 Summary includes the completed matrix as well as the first diagnostic November 15 Short benchmark/UI outcome; retained host-only scope November 16 Summary covers the theme, collector split and resident preparation Verification ¶ All 27 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests pass. The three lesson script blocks are byte-for-byte unchanged, as are the original bodies of all twelve earlier journal entries. Existing heading anchors are preserved, including aliases for renamed headings. The build validates routes, links and fragments for 38 pages, including this new entry and 13 journal dates. Chromium passes 228 page/width checks across all 57 website pages at 1440/1024/375/320px, including compact and expanded notes. After refining the new next-link theme and reference-title hierarchy, all 38 notebook pages pass another 152 page/width checks. Each pass includes 144 expanded source-page checks. English text, math, contrast, unique IDs and page width pass without script or HTTP errors. The three standalone lessons pass nine additional desktop/phone checks. Keyboard disclosure, the beginner reading path, next-guide/current-roadmap links, existing deep links, search, twiddle calculator, 16-point input validation and stage controls, and the original arithmetic/network widgets all pass. Portfolio photo/painting interactions remain intact. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. This change does not run CUDA or advance a hardware milestone; source CI and live deployment are checked after pushing. Open questions ¶ The remaining engineering questions are unchanged: actual RTX 5090 execution, current resident failure/lifetime checks on hardware, and complete profiling. Dated provider rates and historical test counts are not current availability or new execution evidence. Next step ¶ Publish the reviewed source and website, verify the live pages and preserve the short reading path in future updates. Continue engineering with the current native acceptance checks rather than another documentation-driven phase change. Follow-up: remove the two pass-example cards ¶ Removed the “Pass 1: column 3, thread 5” and “Pass 2: row 3, thread 5” cards from the work-distribution page's resources section at the user's request. The resource table now leads directly to the memory-access explanation. The proofs, interactive explorer and NTT implementation are unchanged. All 27 documentation tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests pass; the 38-page build validates links and fragments. Chromium passes 114 page/width checks across all 57 website pages at desktop and phone widths. Focused checks confirm both cards are absent, the memory section follows the resource table, and forward/inverse explorer controls still work. Screenshots were inspected. Follow-up: visualize the memory-access map ¶ Added an accessible diagram under “Read the memory access map.” It compares the first four loading threads: column reads at indices 0/256/512/768 versus row reads at 0/1/2/3, both writing consecutive shared slots. Labels distinguish the 2,048-byte column stride from the 8-byte row stride. A shared lifecycle shows load once, eight local stages and store back to the original addresses. The diagram uses the existing theme and stacks on phones. It is a schematic of logical addresses, not measured GPU traffic. All 27 documentation tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests pass. The build validates all 38 pages. Chromium passes eight focused checks of the generated and standalone lesson at 1440/1024/375/320px: labels, SVG bounds, contrast, responsive layout, deep-link opening and forward/inverse controls. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected; phone labels were enlarged before the final focused pass. All 57 website pages also pass desktop/phone layout and math checks (114 page/width checks), without script or HTTP errors. Follow-up: one group twiddle per CUDA butterfly ¶ The older DIF butterfly uses two multiplications and remains correct. Its second multiplier comes from that particular schedule; the negacyclic transform can also be computed with a different schedule. The default CUDA path now uses OpenFHE-style group twiddles: forward adds and subtracts after one multiply; inverse multiplies the difference by the reciprocal, then normalizes once after all stages. The column/row split, reduction, final array layout and launch order are unchanged. The CPU reference remains independent. Configure a separate build with -DNTT_CUDA_LEGACY_BUTTERFLY=ON for the old arithmetic. GPU timing records identify the selected schedule. The work-distribution, butterfly, GPU code, buffer and OpenFHE representation pages distinguish the new default from the legacy derivation. The explorer shows the global group, table index, and root exponent for each direction. All 24 OpenFHE-enabled host CTests and the separate legacy simulator gate passed. A supplemental 28-case simulator matrix used three towers per cell, N=256/512/4096/8192/16384/32768/65536 and 30/50/55/60-bit primes. Forward and independent inverse arrays matched pinned OpenFHE directly; products matched the CPU reference. Desktop and phone browser checks confirmed the 65536 lesson renders and the inverse selector shows the reciprocal table entry. The new CUDA source was not compiled with nvcc or run on a device in this session. Existing Ada timings are for the legacy schedule; no P2 gate closed. Next: compile both CUDA builds, run native exactness and sanitizer gates, then compare resident forward/inverse timings on the same RTX 5090 inputs. Follow-up: explain the cyclic DIT equations step by step ¶ Separated the displayed equations into stage width, array addresses, and butterfly writes. Added an N=8 example modulo 17 and explained why stepping through the global root powers by N/M gives a root of exactly order M. Cross-linked the two explanations and distinguished cyclic DIT's offset twiddle from the current CUDA negacyclic group's twiddle. No algorithm or interactive control changed. Existing documentation and interaction tests, local link checks, and desktop/phone formula checks cover the presentation. Follow-up: publish the one-twiddle work-distribution chapter ¶ The local implementation and lesson had not been deployed, so the live chapter still showed the old two-multiplier formula. The revised chapter explains the current one-twiddle schedule using the two substitutions X^d = ±ζ. The old formula stays in the separately linked legacy derivation, not in the current CUDA walkthrough. No arithmetic or acceptance gate changes in this documentation follow-up. Added comments immediately below the cyclic DIT equations: what each index counts, why the partner is d slots away, why both writes read the old upper value, and how the three forms of the twiddle describe the same power. The N=8 root-stride example lists the four exponents explicitly. Reviewed the equations throughout the 65,536-point work-distribution lesson. Defined tower length, pair distance, slot addresses, group counts, table indices, exponents, and inverse factors beside their equations. Added a worked lookup at distance 256 and renamed the row and loading-thread indices so they cannot be confused with butterfly values. No implementation or roadmap gate changed. Follow-up: queue one combined RTX 5090 session ¶ Recorded the next-rental queue so offline work can continue before provision. Prepare matching NTT builds, resident-chain timings, and the standalone OpenFHE CPU basis-conversion oracle first. The paid queue prioritizes native exactness and sanitizer checks, matched timings, and profiling. Optional candidates must be ready before the freeze; do not pay to finish implementation on a rental. No device was rented and no working-day or acceptance gate advanced. A/B collector prepared ¶ Added a paired collector mode: default group twiddles and legacy DIF compile and record separately, with identical benchmark settings. It checks the CMake cache, executable schedule, workload labels, hardware and binary identity, and the actual paired inputs. Prime widths can be selected without shrinking the native exactness gate. The combined comparison fails if either version’s run fails or their actual inputs differ. The first sweep runs one schedule then the other; controlled repeat order remains separate from that sweep. Local orchestration tests cover matched runs, failure preservation, wrong or missing labels, changed roots or device, and identical binaries. No GPU was rented. Next: compile both builds on a driverless Linux CUDA host and prepare resident-chain timing. The standalone CPU conversion oracle remains queued for local work. Resident benchmark and standalone basis-conversion checks ¶ Added host-wall timing for the resident device chain, the normal host boundary, and an explicit roundtrip through host memory after each intermediate. Every warmup and timed sample is checked against an exact reference product. The collector now pairs the three scopes with the public and CUDA-event records and rejects the host driver's simulated timings. GPU-only mode selects the native CTests and their fixtures, leaving standalone CPU checks on the M1 Pro. Source review found that the resident CUDA adapter stored root powers in ordinary exponent order, while the new default kernels expected them in bit-reversed exponent order. The adapter now uses the same forward and inverse table builder as the public CUDA path. The CUDA build and native exactness for this revision are pending. Added a standalone CPU comparison against pinned OpenFHE ApproxModUp and ApproxModDown. At N=65,536, all five Q prefixes with three P towers passed zero, boundary, monomial, and dense fixtures. The test checked 39,321,600 residues. It found 303 examples where keeping the approximate CRT sum and first reducing it to a canonical source value lead to different answers. Both ModUp input formats and the unchanged ModDown source were checked. This is an oracle for the next base-conversion phase. On the M1 Pro, all 26 CTests and 51 tooling tests passed. The focused legacy host check passed, and the deferred host benchmark produced complete, validated simulation records for 40 primes at both 30 and 60 bits. A local archive of those records passed SHA-256 and listing checks. The next step is to run the new two-schedule compiler-only CI matrix, then execute the bundled RTX 5090 checks. No native GPU gate or roadmap day advanced. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Shared theme and resident NTT preparation", "summary": "Restored Sunlit Paper, separated CPU/GPU measurements and prepared the resident chain. CPU tests and compilation passed; the new runtime had not run on a GPU.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-16.html", "text": "2026-11-16 Website maintenance / P2 D11 tooling ← All entries Shared theme and resident NTT preparation Restored Sunlit Paper, separated CPU/GPU measurements and prepared the resident chain. CPU tests and compilation passed; the new runtime had not run on a GPU. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Restore the website's earlier visual design ¶ Outcome ¶ Restored the earlier website design from Git history at the user's request. The portfolio uses its Sunlit Paper palette, Anton/Space Grotesk typography, photo-filled lettering and full-width photo grids. The research notebook returns to its earlier green and paper theme with an expanded page directory. Current articles, journals, native GPU results and roadmap state are preserved. Later the same day, the user asked to apply the portfolio theme to the NTT-GPU pages. The notebook now shares Sunlit Paper backgrounds, burnt-orange and forest-green accents, Anton headings and Space Grotesk body text. The earlier restoration remains recorded above; the original design is also backed up on the website repository's backup/old-website-design branch. A later change on this date separates measurement collection. Rentals can run GPU-only jobs, local machines can run CPU-only jobs, and an explicit combined mode preserves the earlier workflow. No GPU was rented and D11/P2 remains open. The next change adds a limited prototype for keeping a chain on the GPU: upload two inputs, transform, multiply, inverse-transform and download. A CPU driver that delays operations passes exact comparisons with OpenFHE. The CUDA adapter and corresponding GPU test compile for sm_89 and sm_120. No native execution or speedup is claimed, and D11/P2 remains open. Reasoning ¶ The November 11 shared blue/white redesign changed the visual identity beyond what the user wanted. Restore the presentation from the preceding revisions without reverting the repository or losing later engineering work. Keep the English text, readable lesson panels, keyboard navigation and current controls. The follow-up explicitly requests a common theme. Keep the portfolio's visual identity while tuning type sizes, code surfaces, tables and controls for long technical articles. Load the theme from source so exports retain it. The next rental should spend time on native checks and GPU measurements, not repeat the local CPU timing sweep. Each mode records its workload and stable machine identity, so resuming cannot accidentally reuse a measurement from a different selection or host. CPU reference preparation and exact-result checks remain part of GPU validation. Keep this prototype separate from the complete backend proposal. It leaves inputs unchanged, creates new output buffers and uses one ordered GPU queue. These choices make buffer lifetimes and dependencies easier to test locally. If submission fails and work may still be running, retain its resources. Production recovery, cache limits and the OpenFHE adapter remain later work. Preserve the website's shared theme when publishing the new article. Changes ¶ Recovered portfolio presentation from website revision 0f213f4 , the parent of redesign commit 499594c , and notebook CSS from the parent of 69ed734 . Restored the portfolio's masked display lettering, warm palette, original gallery grid and painting-card presentation. Serve the restored fonts locally with their SIL Open Font License files. Kept the native photo dialog, English descriptions, painting disclosure buttons, accessible media controls and responsive navigation. Restored the notebook's green/paper CSS and expanded directory, retaining current page destinations, GPU status, source citations and interactive math. Retained the later lesson contrast repairs with the historical colors. Follow-up theme alignment: Added a shared sunlit-paper.css theme and locally hosted Anton/Space Grotesk fonts with their original OFL licenses in the notebook source assets. Load the theme on every generated page and in standalone lesson previews. Matched sidebar, section navigation, search, journal, table, code and interactive-lesson colors and typography to the restored portfolio. Updated website design guidance to preserve the shared theme during export. Preserved research content, formula markup, source citations and algorithms. Benchmark collection split: Added --mode cpu without a CUDA toolkit requirement; its build explicitly disables CUDA and requires the OpenFHE/host gates. Added --benchmark-scope gpu|cpu|all , with 280 GPU-only, 140 CPU-only or 420 legacy combined jobs in the default workload matrix. Rental GPU selection uses the existing five-operation gpu-bench-jsonl protocol and event/copy measurements, retaining exactness/sanitizer gates. The rental helper defaults to GPU-only; direct GPU commands keep their combined default. CPU identity supports Linux and macOS with stable fields. Resume records only selected executable hashes and relevant hardware policy; mode/scope changes fail before replacing the original report. CPU collection ignores unused GPU binaries and visibility. Updated the handoff, tooling guide, README, results next steps and active task. Roadmap gates and arithmetic/kernel code are unchanged. Resident-chain increment: Added resident.hpp , an internal transport interface and the host runtime: private handle provenance, owned upload/download staging, whole-tower views, implicit/explicit dependencies, per-session budgets and a bounded journal. Added a CUDA transport reusing the original kernels on one explicit stream; existing synchronous launcher callers retain their default behavior. Added completion retirement, poisoned admission, explicit close and retained resource quarantine when quiescence cannot be proved. Final destructor errors and actual failed-context behavior remain limitations needing native review. Added a deferred CPU transport with injection before/after submission and asynchronous failures, a shared full-size OpenFHE fixture, CUDA preflights, required collector gates and a dense resident scope for all four sanitizers. Published the implementation and limits , linked real OpenFHE source, and updated the backlog and next native task. No roadmap phase or hardware gate is closed by this preparation. Verification ¶ All 24 documentation unit tests and 14 Node math/interaction tests passed. The build validated local links and fragments for 36 notebook pages, including 12 journals. Chromium passed 220 page/width checks across all 55 website pages at 1440/1024/375/320px, with English content, rendered math and no page-wide overflow, script errors or failed HTTP responses. Browser checks verified the historical background and loaded display fonts, photo dialog navigation/Escape/focus restoration, 27 painting videos and folder controls, expanded notebook directory, the N=65536 twiddle calculator, section navigation and search. Focused text-contrast checks passed for both introductory lessons, the rental survey, the GPU matrix report and this entry. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. An old Music-page grid overflow at 320px was corrected without changing its restored visual design. Existing engineering articles, lesson source, roadmap, C++ implementation, photo image/link inventories and all media files are unchanged. This is a presentation change; no new GPU execution or roadmap gate is claimed. Source commit, website export and deployment follow these local checks. Follow-up verification: all 24 documentation and 14 Node tests passed again. The shared theme passed 220 page/width checks across the complete website at 1440/1024/375/320px, including warm background and Anton/Space Grotesk assertions on all 36 notebook pages. Every notebook page passed computed text-contrast checks at all four widths after darkening small orange lesson labels. The three standalone lesson previews passed nine checks at 1440/375/320px for the shared fonts/paper, math, contrast and layout. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. No page-wide overflow, script errors or failed HTTP responses were observed. Portfolio styles/content are unchanged; notebook font/license files match the portfolio assets byte for byte. Benchmark-split verification so far: 47 tooling tests pass, including all three full synthetic job selections, CPU-only no-CUDA behavior, native-gate retention, mode/scope resume rejection, CPU retry history, stable CPU identity and malformed record/failure propagation. These synthetic controller tests are not GPU runs. A real Linux development run using --mode cpu --allow-dirty built the pinned OpenFHE configuration and passed all 22 CPU/OpenFHE CTests in 67.17 seconds, including the 140-workload CPU protocol gate. Its bounded N=4096/K=1 batch collected four native CPU jobs covering 30/50/55/60 bits and reported CPU_CHECKS_PASSED , gpu_execution: NOT_RUN . No CUDA tool or executable was invoked. This exploratory report records dirty source explicitly; clean-source collection/resume verification follows the implementation commit. Clean-source verification at implementation commit b7334fc then passed on Linux/AMD EPYC 9V74. All 22 CPU/OpenFHE CTests passed in 71.63 seconds, and eight N=65536/K=1,40 jobs covered all four prime widths. A real resume reran the 22 gates successfully in 67.29 seconds, reused all eight hash-validated jobs from attempt 1 without executing another benchmark, and retained the prior report and original logs. Changing CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES for the retry did not invalidate the CPU-only context. Both reports record clean source and gpu_execution: NOT_RUN . One warm-up and two samples were used only to verify the collector, not to establish a performance comparison. The generated record remains in ignored bench-results-cpu-split-clean/ . All 24 documentation tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The 36-page build validates links/fragments; the seven updated pages passed 28 browser checks at 1440/1024/375/320px for theme, English text, math, layout, text contrast, section navigation and search, with no script or HTTP errors. Desktop/phone screenshots were inspected. The new selection table also passed keyboard horizontal-scrolling checks on phones. The existing website theme and all C++ arithmetic/kernel implementations are unchanged. Resident-chain verification: all 24 CPU/OpenFHE CTests passed in 69.45 seconds. The CUDA-linked build passed all 26 host CTests in 69.76 seconds and compiled the native resident fixture for sm_89 and sm_120 using stock CUDA 13.0.88. All seven GPU-labelled tests were excluded and remain unrun in this workspace. The control suite passes 293 checks. It compares small polynomial products with an independent calculation and checks unchanged inputs, ownership of temporary transfer buffers, nested views, dependencies and memory limits. It also tests handles from closed or different sessions, failures before and after queuing work, errors reported later, discarded outputs and safely reclaiming retained resources. AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer and leak detection also pass. The deferred host transport matches pinned OpenFHE on 4,456,448 residues at N=65536 with unsorted 55/50/60-bit primes across zero, bounds, monomial and dense patterns, forward, independent inverse, roundtrip, product and tower views. Five CUDA-linked factory preflights reject invalid input without a driver. All 47 tooling tests pass, including the added resident sanitizer selection. These are host, simulation and compiler observations, not CUDA arithmetic results. Publication checks: all 24 documentation tests pass; the build validates local links and fragments for 37 notebook pages, including 12 journals. Chromium passes 224 page/width checks across all 56 website pages at 1440/1024/375/320px for English text, rendered math and layout, with no script/HTTP errors. All notebook pages pass computed text-contrast checks and retain the shared paper palette and fonts. Photo/painting controls, the twiddle calculator, section navigation and search also pass. New article desktop/phone screenshots were inspected. Publication will pin these pages to the source commit. Open questions ¶ None for the visual work. Future content publishing should retain the established design unless the user explicitly requests a redesign. For the collector, native M1 and new GPU-only session execution remain unverified; primary RTX 5090, native failure handling and full profiling gates stay open. For the resident runtime, actual device execution, cleanup under a failed CUDA context and performance costs remain unverified. Permanent quarantine retains resources conservatively; automatic recovery, pooling, cross-stream scheduling, thread safety and production adapter/cache behavior are outside this prototype. Next step ¶ Publish both repositories and verify the restored website on desktop and phone layouts. Continue research from the existing roadmap and measured GPU evidence. Following the subsequent theme alignment, publish the shared styling and verify the live portfolio/notebook match before continuing engineering work. For engineering, prepare the resident operation-chain prototype and complete remaining native safety/primary RTX 5090 gates on a later paid session. That prototype is now prepared. Next, run the current seven native gates, resident smoke under all four sanitizers, failure checks and profiling on an actual RTX 5090. Publish evidence with the source and hardware identity before closing D11/P2 or making performance claims. A shorter default view across the notebook ¶ Added a plain-language opening to every topic page and kept each daily entry's summary visible. Full notes, evidence, and interactive lessons stay behind clearly labelled controls. Existing section links open any collapsed notes automatically. The homepage directory now shows compact page links by category, and the journal index goes straight to entries. The source notes, page routes, interactive examples, and engineering gates are unchanged. All 25 documentation tests and 14 interactive tests passed, and the build validated links and fragments across 37 pages. Browser checks at 1440 and 390 pixels confirmed the directory, expanded butterfly lesson and roadmap deep link without page-level overflow. No new CUDA work was run. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Clearer benchmark modes and GPU results", "summary": "Made benchmark modes and the results page clearer. The arithmetic and the work included in each timing stayed the same.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-15.html", "text": "2026-11-15 P2 / D11 ← All entries Clearer benchmark modes and GPU results Made benchmark modes and the results page clearer. The arithmetic and the work included in each timing stayed the same. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Simplify benchmark modes and the website ¶ Outcome ¶ Reviewed the CPU NTT, shared stage helpers, CUDA wrapper/benchmark, measurement helpers and website builder. Simplified benchmark selection without changing NTT arithmetic, kernel launch schedules, exactness checks or timing scopes. Shortened the main GPU report and reduced homepage clutter. No working-day gate advances and no new GPU timing results are claimed. Reasoning ¶ Two true/false settings represented three measurement modes, which allowed redundant combinations. Replacing them with named modes makes the choice clearer. One table now keeps each command, timing label and operation count together. Existing command-line calls and saved JSONL records still work. The homepage repeated long URL strings and a roadmap sequence already available elsewhere. Its GPU status also incorrectly implied that no native validation had run. Readers need the current result and next action first; hardware limitations and evidence must remain available without dominating the page. Changes ¶ Benchmark modes : one shared protocol table and a named mode instead of two independent booleans. CUDA interface and command handling : select the mode once; the benchmark implementation reads its metadata/counts from the table. CMake applies the same host-target options in one list. Website builder : preserve every page link, group the directory into native disclosures, remove duplicate URL text and the redundant implementation sequence, and correct the GPU status. GPU report : one key-results table, three findings and the next steps; secondary measurement notes are collapsed. Raw evidence and the November 14 journal remain intact. Verification ¶ On the M1 Pro, all 18 standalone and 22 OpenFHE CPU CTests pass, including the measurement/JSONL and exact OpenFHE checks. The CUDA command translation unit passes host syntax checking with warnings treated as errors. New host tests cover all three command/mode mappings, timing labels, operation counts, unknown commands and invalid mode values. All 24 documentation tests and 14 interactive Node tests pass; the 35-page build validates every page/journal route and fragment. Browser checks at 1440×960 and 390×844 verify disclosure expansion, phone navigation, the report and a scroll-contained results table, with no page-level horizontal overflow. The report shrinks from 257 to 106 source lines. This is host/UI verification, not a native GPU rerun; primary RTX 5090 validation remains open. CUDA compiler verification is delegated to the existing driverless CI workflow, not a rental. Open questions ¶ The collector still needs a choice between GPU-only rental work and local CPU measurements when starting or resuming a session. The next performance tasks are keeping operation chains on the GPU and testing arithmetic and memory-layout changes. Simplification does not establish a new performance improvement. Next step ¶ Publish the checked pages and keep preparing a bounded next GPU batch locally. Do not rent a GPU for documentation or host-only refactoring. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Native GPU checks and full-matrix timings", "summary": "The temporary Ada GPU passed its selected checks. Saved 280 GPU and 140 M1 CPU jobs; RTX 5090 tests and hardware-counter collection remained unfinished.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-14.html", "text": "2026-11-14 P2 / D11 diagnostic ← All entries Native GPU checks and full-matrix timings The temporary Ada GPU passed its selected checks. Saved 280 GPU and 140 M1 CPU jobs; RTX 5090 tests and hardware-counter collection remained unfinished. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry First native GPU diagnostic session ¶ Outcome ¶ Connected to the user-provided rental machine. It reports NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4080 SUPER , compute capability 8.9, and 32,760 MiB memory. This is the reported identity, not an independently verified board specification. It is neither the primary RTX 5090 nor the planned RTX 4090 control; record the run as an additional Ada diagnostic. No primary-device gate advances. The completed collector reports GPU_CHECKS_PASSED . All 23 CTests passed, including all four registered native GPU gates; all four sanitizer smoke tools passed. Raw logs and the report have been copied back to the local workspace. Reasoning ¶ Use the supplied machine to compile and run the actual CUDA code, then compare its answers. Keep its driver unchanged. Store builds, dependencies and reports on the rental’s data disk. A successful compile is recorded separately from a successful GPU run. Changes ¶ The remote checkout is clean at 19cc2854def743ebfa69f3429fe44ac7fe50ae58 . No project implementation changes have been made. Verification ¶ Initial discovery: Ubuntu 22.04.1, GCC 11.3, driver 595.71.05, bundled CUDA 11.8.89, Python 3.10.8 and CMake 3.22.1. Project-local preparation installs CMake 3.31.6, managed Python 3.12.16, the locked CUDA 13.0.2 redistributables (nvcc 13.0.88), and matching Compute Sanitizer 2025.3.1.0. NVIDIA downloads are checked against their SHA-256 values. Downloading OpenFHE with git failed twice: first with an HTTP/2 error, then with a TLS error after retrying that command using HTTP/1.1. Instead, transferred a git bundle from the unmodified local checkout. Checked that its SHA-256 file fingerprint matched on both machines and verified commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f . Its four submodules match the local checkout. No vendor source/header changes were required. Final native evidence ¶ The collector ran from 07:02:25 to 07:03:36 UTC (15:02–15:03 Asia/Shanghai) after dependency preparation, with a clean source checkout. The report contains 17 successful stages. OpenFHE uses NativeInteger 64, reduced-noise off, OpenMP off, native optimization off, Release; GCC is 11.3 and glibc is 2.35. Native preflight: 19/19 tests passed , including three real GPU tests. Pinned dependency setup: 18/18 CPU/OpenFHE/tooling tests passed . Full native collector: 23/23 CTests passed in 36.64 seconds , including cuda_oracle_crosscheck , cuda_matches_ref , cuda_verify , and cuda_openfhe_exact . The direct CUDA/CPU/OpenFHE gate checked 169,279,488 residues across N=65536, unsorted 55/50/60-bit towers, D8 Q and QlP bases at L=5,4,3,1, and seven input patterns. It checks complete forward/independent inverse arrays, roundtrip, pointwise/inverse, ring square and source immutability. This is GPU NTT/product evidence using the Hybrid fixture's tower sets, not a GPU implementation of the Hybrid graph . Compute Sanitizer memcheck, racecheck, initcheck and synccheck each passed the prepared zero/dense three-tower smoke scope, with 3,538,944 comparisons per tool . Memory/init/sync summaries report zero errors; racecheck reports zero errors/warnings/hazards. This is not the full production sanitizer matrix. Native compilation uses -arch=native on the observed compute-8.9 device. Device queries report runtime API 13.0 and driver API 13.2. The pre-session hardware snapshot reports a 320 W power limit and idle clocks; clocks were not locked and are not a continuous benchmark trace. Initial latency sample ¶ This sample uses N=65536 and three 46-bit primes. Timings cover a complete synchronous call: CPU input checks, selecting the owning device, CPU–GPU copies and execution. Creating the transform object and its tables happens before timing. The implementation uses warmups and averaged repetitions; this is one session's summary, not a raw timing distribution or Nsight profile. Operation Mean latency CPU reference forward 47.4496 ms GPU forward 0.786293 ms GPU inverse 0.806584 ms GPU complete ring multiply 1.49319 ms The CPU number is this repository's correctness-first scalar reference, not an optimized OpenFHE throughput benchmark. Do not extrapolate these results to 50–60-bit performance, a standard retail board, or the RTX 5090. Evidence and reproduction ¶ Remote workspace: /root/autodl-tmp/ntt-gpu . Run directory: bench-results-gpu-20261114-ada . Reproduce with the clean pinned checkout, the local CUDA 13 toolchain and matching Compute Sanitizer directory on PATH: python3 scripts/gpu_session.py --mode gpu \\ --cuda-root build/toolchains/cuda-13.0.2 \\ --openfhe-prefix build/openfhe-compat/install \\ --architectures native --jobs 8 --benchmark Downloaded archive: build/gpu-session-evidence-20261114.tar.gz , SHA-256 cccf41b6231ac1ac3b62d1540c8d2e7d8c9012216a29a7c23f51148dd7625db4 . The digest matches on both machines. Extracted local report: build/gpu-session-evidence-20261114/bench-results-gpu-20261114-ada/report.json . The archive also includes every collector log, CTest's complete output, native compiler flags, preflight/setup logs, toolchain lock and submodule list. These ignored artifacts are local evidence, not a published remote download. No SSH address, login secret or authentication transcript is included. After documenting the result locally, all 23 documentation tests and 14 Node lesson/explorer tests passed. The 31-page notebook build validated local links, fragments and assets. No layout or interaction changes were made. Open questions ¶ Native exactness and sanitizer smoke results are established for this machine. Nsight tools were not found on the initial PATH; profiling was not performed. Reported model/memory identity needs provider confirmation before hardware comparisons. The session does not verify native allocation/upload failure unwinding, failed-context cleanup, full production cases or RTX 5090 behavior. Next step ¶ Evidence is safely exported. Stop the rental through the provider when no further work is needed; disconnecting SSH does not itself establish that billing has stopped. No machine shutdown/deletion or provider billing action was taken. Next obtain primary-device evidence and complete failure injection and full profiling, tracked separately in the backlog . Follow-up: analyze the exported native evidence offline ¶ The user reports the rental is stopped. No reconnection, billing action or new GPU execution was performed during this analysis. Outcome and reasoning ¶ Added the first-run analysis . Correctness evidence is strong for the tested arrays, but performance evidence is one averaged 46-bit/K=3 row. There is no measured bottleneck, optimized OpenFHE CPU timing, kernel-only breakdown or production inference speedup. P2 and B04 remain open. Audited the 169,279,488-comparison count: 63 pattern/basis combinations, 41 towers summed across nine basis groups, seven patterns and nine equality checks per residue position. It is not 169 million independent random cases. Each sanitizer covers two patterns/three towers, not the full basis matrix. The benchmark's approximately 60.35× forward ratio is against this repository's scalar reference only. Source inspection found missing dedicated inverse warm-up/reset, different CPU/GPU forward repetition input policies, host-copy costs inside multiply timing, and no saved individual samples. These are measurement limitations to fix in a separate implementation task, not evidence that the executed exactness tests or means are fabricated. Let S be the bytes in one input polynomial. Separate public calls copy 9S bytes between CPU and GPU; the combined multiply call copies 3S. Both launch eight kernels. This motivates keeping data on the GPU. To measure the speedup, however, time both complete routes with one consistent protocol instead of adding means from separate experiments. Arithmetic cost, 2,048-byte column-thread strides, partial butterfly-thread participation and host copies are profiling hypotheses only. Changes and verification ¶ Added the analysis article to the generated notebook's Research directory; linked it from the benchmark plan and B04 backlog. Rechecked the saved archive SHA-256, 17 successful report stages, clean source identity, complete 23-test output, all 63 native pattern rows, the comparison-count formula and four zero-error sanitizer summaries. Recomputed scalar ratio, transfer payloads, source-level multiply counts and coefficient-array traffic without contacting the rental machine. No kernel, arithmetic, benchmark implementation or runtime changed. All 23 documentation tests and 14 Node tests passed; the 32-page build validated local links, fragments and assets. git diff --check passed. Open questions and next deliverable ¶ Next prepare the raw-sample/fixed-input measurement harness and deterministic allocation/upload failure tests offline. Confirm profiling access and hardware identity before the next paid session. That session should collect missing primary-device, failure-path and per-pass evidence rather than only repeat the existing averaged row. Do not advance the working-day tracker from this partial B04/D15 analysis alone. Follow-up: implement offline measurement and setup-failure preparation ¶ Outcome ¶ Implemented the offline tooling increment : raw public-call timing protocol, strict JSONL validation, a shared setup allocation owner, host fault tests and a prepared native constructor probe. No connection to the stopped rental was made. The new CUDA code is uncompiled/unrun here ; the successful native record above applies only to 19cc285, not these changes. No working-day or native gate is marked complete. Reasoning and changes ¶ The CUDA constructor and CPU test adapter use the same resource owner. This lets CPU failure tests exercise the cleanup logic used by production code, instead of a separate test-only cleanup algorithm. SetupResources tracks the actual six allocations and four constant uploads; host tests cover failures before adapter calls and adapter exceptions, reverse cleanup, and cleanup-failure accounting. The GPU-labelled cuda_setup_fault_injection constructs the real wrapper and checks real CUDA frees after synthetic pre-call failures. It is prepared, not executed. bench-jsonl uses parameter files for exact N/q/root identity and deterministic seeded operands. Each operation has fixed-input reset and explicit warm-up; reset/packing is outside timing. It measures scalar reference forward, public GPU forward, inverse, pointwise, unfused product and fused product. It saves all samples and linear-interpolated percentile summaries. Fused output is now retained/released outside timing; its by-value input allocation/copy remains inside the public call. Legacy bench stays available for reproducing old rows. The collector now requires the new native setup probe and new host gates; benchmark mode generates 50/55/60-bit parameters and retains three JSONL logs. It recomputes summaries and rejects truncated, malformed, wrongly scoped, incomplete or explicitly synthetic data even after a zero executable exit. Tooling mocks remain labelled synthetic/control-flow evidence, not GPU results. The parameter generator now accepts an optional bit-width argument. Updated the D5 ownership map/static checks, collector instructions and backlog. The mathematical NTT kernels and modular arithmetic algorithms are unchanged. Verification ¶ All 18 standalone CTests and 21 OpenFHE-enabled CPU CTests passed. All 20 Python tooling tests passed, including malformed/synthetic output rejection, raw percentile recomputation and three-width collection control flow. measurement_json_interop compiled the C++ JSON writer and validated its explicitly synthetic six-operation output with the Python parser. Host ownership and measurement tests passed with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. No native CUDA sanitizer results are claimed. Host CLI syntax passed Clang C++20 with warnings treated as errors. Generated small parameter fixtures at 50/55/60 bits; existing vector/oracle and full-size arithmetic gates remain green. All 23 documentation tests , 14 Node tests and the 33-page build/link/fragment checks passed. No shared layout or interactions changed. Remaining work ¶ Native compile/execution must validate the refactor and probe. Synthetic pre-call failures are not actual driver OOM, partially failed transfers, wrong-device restoration or context destruction. Cleanup remains nonthrowing and is not a production error-reporting lifecycle interface. Kernel-only CUDA events, optimized OpenFHE CPU timing, active telemetry and Nsight profiling still need implementation/measurement. Primary RTX 5090 evidence stays open. These limits are recorded in the guide; the offline tests do not close B02/B04. Follow-up: prepare the next rental as a batch, including 30-bit primes ¶ Outcome ¶ Prepared a bounded 30/50/55/60-bit batch at N=4096/8192/16384/32768/65536, K=1/3. The default is 40 workloads and 120 independently recorded measurement jobs, with five warm-ups and 100 samples per operation. No GPU was rented or reconnected. D11/P2 remain in progress; current CUDA changes remain uncompiled and unrun on native hardware. Reasoning and changes ¶ The user requested 30-bit primes and approved offline batch preparation. Keep public-call wall time separate from standalone resident CUDA-event and pinned-transfer experiments. A shared input generator uses the same N, primes, roots and random seed for GPU and OpenFHE CPU jobs, so their operands match. Thirty-bit values still occupy uint64_t buffers; no specialized uint32_t kernel or bandwidth reduction has been implemented. Added event timing of resident forward/inverse/pointwise/fused product and pinned H2D/D2H copies, with reset and verification outside the event interval. The public wrapper's transfer behavior and mathematical kernels are unchanged. A GPU-event interval around several launches may include gaps while the CPU submits work. These times and the separate copy experiments cannot simply be added to reconstruct a public call using ordinary CPU memory. Added a real pinned-OpenFHE NativeVector CRT-FTT comparator for forward, inverse, pointwise and ring product. It uses preconditioned upstream arithmetic and sequential per-tower calls, not our scalar CPU algorithm. Metadata records library/compiler settings. This is not a fastest-possible CPU/DCRT/HEXL or full CKKS comparison, and multiplication API allocation boundaries differ. The collector now requires a prepared direct GPU/CPU/OpenFHE exactness gate over all 40 cells and runs each sanitizer on legacy smoke plus a bounded 30/60-bit boundary-dimension matrix smoke. Added optional selected nsys/ncu captures and compiler resource/line-info output. Captures do not replace a full D14 counter analysis and are not accepted as benchmark samples. Batch checkpoints retain successful jobs after independent measurement failures; the overall exit remains nonzero. Resume reruns environment/build/correctness/ sanitizer gates, verifies matching clean source, device/driver, binary/library hashes and matrix settings, and revalidates raw log hashes/parameters/scopes. Reports are archived, logs use unique retry names, and origin attempts remain visible. Command timeouts/interrupts terminate their own process group on POSIX; this is not permission to stop unrelated processes. Concurrent use of one record directory is unsupported. Verification ¶ All 18 standalone CTests and 22 OpenFHE-enabled CPU CTests passed. The new OpenFHE protocol gate ran 40 actual CPU workloads , covering every default dimension/width/tower cell and exact final outputs for four operations. These correctness/protocol runs are not published performance baselines. All 31 Python tooling tests passed, including the full 120-job synthetic matrix, 30-bit coverage, partial failure/resume, tampered logs, changed binary/ dependency/device rejection, profiler command bounds and process-group timeout cleanup control flow. Mocked subprocess results are not GPU evidence. All 23 documentation tests and 14 Node tests passed. CLI host syntax passes Clang C++20 with warnings treated as errors. Open questions and next deliverable ¶ Native compilation must validate event APIs, fault ownership and new exactness fixtures before accepting timings. Run the batch on the next compatible rental, copy/hash-verify the complete evidence directory, then analyze it offline. Primary RTX 5090 evidence, actual driver/context failures, per-pass timing and full profiler/production CKKS coverage stay open. No new measured GPU speedup or bottleneck claim is made. Follow-up: extend prime batches through 40 towers ¶ Outcome and reasoning ¶ The user requested prime batches up to 40. Interpreted this as RNS primes per polynomial, not ciphertext batch size. The default representative sweep is now K=1/3/8/16/24/32/40 across five dimensions and four prime widths: 140 workloads, 420 timing jobs and 224,000 timed operation samples at the default 100 samples. Arbitrary integer counts from 1 through 40 can be selected; the direct native matrix gate covers the representative default set. Changes ¶ Expanded both the OpenFHE CPU protocol gate and prepared native exactness gate to the default tower counts. Matrix sanitizer smoke includes K=3 and K=40 at the boundary dimensions and 30/60-bit widths. These CUDA changes have not been compiled or executed on a GPU. Wrapper layouts and arithmetic are unchanged. Benchmark-only parameter files now use explicitly labelled zero coefficient placeholders, avoiding large, unused CRT integers at 40 towers. The benchmark executables still create their original seeded dense operands. Ordinary correctness fixture generation remains unchanged unless the new optional flag is explicitly selected. Parameter tests check all 40 distinct primes, exact bit widths, 2N divisibility, primitive-root identities and compact files at all four widths, including N=65536. Verification and next deliverable ¶ The real OpenFHE CPU protocol gate passed all 140 workload cells , checking four operation outputs against the reference. Its local 48.07-second test runtime is not a GPU-rental runtime estimate or a performance baseline. All 33 tooling tests passed, including a synthetic 420-job matrix, acceptance of custom counts through 40, and rejection of 0/41. All 23 documentation tests and the 33-page link/fragment build passed. The updated native exactness test's host C++ syntax passed with warnings treated as errors; this is not a CUDA compile or execution result. Next: compile and execute the enlarged native batch on the next rental, retain all evidence, and analyze timings offline. P2/D11 and primary-device gates remain open. Previous entries above describe their earlier, smaller verified scopes. Freeze checkpoint: complete local regression before rental ¶ Outcome and verification ¶ At the user's request, rebuilt and reran the complete offline regression suite after the 40-tower expansion: 18/18 standalone CTests and 22/22 OpenFHE-enabled CPU CTests passed. The latter includes the full 140-cell OpenFHE benchmark protocol gate. The tooling suite now has 33 passing tests . Documentation checks passed 23 Python tests , 14 Node tests , and the 33-page HTML/link/fragment build. Measurement and setup-ownership host tests also passed AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. Host CLI and updated CUDA/OpenFHE fixture C++ syntax checks passed with warnings treated as errors. git diff --check is clean. Reasoning, publication and next gate ¶ Freeze this batch before adding more kernels or timing features. Commit/push the reviewed source, use the existing no-GPU CI compiler job to check sm_89 and sm_120, and export the revision-pinned notebook to the personal website. CI compiler success, if observed, will be recorded separately; it cannot establish GPU correctness, timings or primary-device acceptance. No rental is started by this checkpoint. The next paid session begins with native compilation and exact gates before collecting the batch; download/hash-verify evidence before stopping. P2/D11 remain in progress. Freeze follow-up: compiler-only CI passed; request the next rental ¶ The batch implementation is pushed as f1ecff1 . Its Correctness and CUDA compilation run completed successfully: Linux CPU checks pass, stock pinned CUDA builds both sm_89 and sm_120, and the CUDA-linked host-only checks pass without a driver. The documentation CI also passes. These are observed CI results, not a real GPU execution record. Offline freeze is complete. The next meaningful gate now needs a rental: probe actual hardware/driver and profiler access, build the full CUDA/OpenFHE configuration, run exactness/fault/sanitizer gates, then collect the 420 timing jobs and selected profiler captures. Copy and hash-verify the evidence before stopping the instance. Notify the user that a GPU is now needed; no instance has been started or reconnected automatically. GPU timing/correctness and primary RTX 5090 acceptance remain pending. Website export follows the latest committed revision; deployment success is verified separately after pushing. Temporary native batch: setup and execution ¶ Scope and reasoning ¶ The user authorized running the frozen batch on the supplied machine as a temporary validation step before the final RTX 5090 run. The device reports RTX 4080 SUPER , compute capability 8.9 , 32,760 MiB memory, driver 595.71.05. Preserve that reported identity; it is not a 5090 measurement or independent verification of the retail board specification. Setup ¶ Transferred a clean project git bundle at bbfa6db and the clean pinned OpenFHE source/submodules from the local checkout; archive SHA-256 values match on both hosts. The remote source checkout remains clean. OpenFHE is b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f with all four recorded submodule IDs. Build/install stays on the instance's data disk; no driver changes are made. The image supplies Ubuntu 22.04.5, GCC 11.4, Python 3.12.3, CUDA 12.8.93, and Compute Sanitizer 2025.1.0.0. Added project-local CMake 3.31.6. The full native CUDA/OpenFHE configuration builds with -arch=native ; runtime API reports 12.8, driver API 13.2. The OpenFHE-only setup passed all 22 CPU tests, including the expanded 140-cell benchmark protocol gate. Profiling access and execution status ¶ Nsight Compute 2025.1.1 preflight fails with ERR_NVGPUCTRPERM . Root inside the container cannot access the provider's GPU counters. No host/module/sysctl permissions are changed. Installed Nsight Systems 2025.1.3 locally from an official package after checking its published SHA-256. Its CUDA-only trace probe succeeds with CPU sampling/context-switch tracing disabled; CPU perf access is unavailable in the container. Profiler-perturbed samples are not benchmark evidence. The detached runner starts the frozen collector's exactness/fault gates, legacy and matrix sanitizer smoke, then the 420-job timing batch. --profile is deliberately omitted after the counter-access failure; a separate selected Nsight Systems capture follows only after the collector succeeds. A separate session-context artifact records this limitation. Native batch results are still pending at this checkpoint; no timing or correctness gate is advanced. Next deliverable ¶ Monitor completion, retain failures as well as successes, copy the complete evidence archive locally and verify its hash before telling the user it is safe to stop. Primary RTX 5090 and hardware-counter gates remain open regardless of the temporary batch outcome. Native gate progress ¶ The frozen CUDA 12.8 build passes all 29 native CTests in 442.61 seconds, including all six GPU-labelled gates and the real setup-fault cleanup probe. Saved CTest output records 169,279,488 original-matrix and 3,967,746,048 expanded-matrix exact residue comparisons. The expanded scope is 140 parameter cells × seven patterns, not independent random trials for every comparison. Setup/build logs and complete CTest output are copied locally. Both legacy and expanded memcheck smoke pass with zero reported errors. The expanded smoke records 107,790,336 exact comparisons at N=4096/65536, 30/60-bit widths and K=3/40; legacy smoke records 3,538,944. Other sanitizers and the benchmark batch remain pending at this progress checkpoint. Subsequently, all eight sanitizer executions pass : memcheck, racecheck, initcheck and synccheck each on legacy plus expanded matrix smoke. Downloaded logs show zero errors; both racecheck summaries also show zero warnings/hazards. This is the bounded zero/dense smoke scope, not every production parameter or every possible input. The 420-job raw timing batch then starts without changing the frozen implementation. Compile-time ptxas output is preserved and reports zero spill loads/stores for all six kernels; achieved occupancy and hardware counters remain unavailable on this provider. Separate rental GPU and local M1 CPU collection ¶ At the user's request, the original mixed collector was terminated (exit 143) before completion. Its partial manifest/raw logs remain intact; its RUNNING report is not a successful full-batch result. Repeated CPU timings are no longer scheduled on the paid machine. Added gpu-bench-jsonl , which times only the five GPU public operations, retaining the untimed exact reference preparation required to validate their outputs. Its timing scope is explicitly host_wall_gpu_public_call ; legacy six-operation records remain supported. No device kernels or arithmetic were changed. A separate fresh GPU-only collection uses the 140-cell matrix, two timing scopes, five warmups and 100 samples. It records the base commit, dirty source patch/hash, binary hashes, actual device identity and validated per-log hashes; it does not reuse the old collector's binary identity. In parallel, the native OpenFHE CPU baseline runs on the local M1 Pro, with separate platform evidence. These timings support cross-platform comparisons, not same-host speedup claims. Completion and safe rental shutdown remain pending until the GPU collection, selected CUDA timeline and local archive verification finish. Collection complete — rental can stop ¶ The separate GPU-only collection passes 280/280 jobs , covering all 140 parameter cells with five public GPU operations and six event/copy experiments: 154,000 raw timed samples . All 140/140 local M1 Pro OpenFHE jobs also pass, with matching exact q/roots and the same seeded-input protocol. A selected N=65536, K=3, 60-bit Nsight Systems CUDA timeline and kernel/memory summary are captured successfully. Hardware counters remain blocked by the provider. The original mixed collection retains 366 completed jobs ; it was cancelled at user direction and must not be labelled a full successful 420-job run. Its original report, logs and hashes are preserved separately from the fresh GPU-only manifest. The latter records the benchmark-only dirty source patch and rebuilt binary hash. Subsequent local comment/indentation and documentation edits are not claimed as the precise remote source snapshot. Downloaded build/ntt-evidence-20261114-ada-batch40.tar.gz and extracted it under build/ntt-evidence-20261114-ada-batch40/ . Archive SHA-256 matches the remote value: d7cdbe24ea9e87270c248011408d983f8760ef451dc72abba2be87ccdca5a1b6 Independent local verification passes 1,087 per-file checksums , retained legacy job hashes, all fresh GPU JSONL counts/summaries/parameters, selected timeline contents, and the M1 baseline's matching parameter lists. CPU evidence is in build/cpu-m1-pro-20261114/ . The user was explicitly told the rental can now stop; no cloud shutdown was performed on their behalf. Local verification also passes 34 tooling tests, 23 documentation tests, the 33-page documentation link/fragment build, and whitespace checks. Next: analyze these temporary sm_89 results offline and integrate platform/scope selection into the regular resumable collector. Primary RTX 5090/sm_120 native execution and hardware-counter gates remain open; M1-versus-GPU comparisons must not be presented as same-host speedups. Offline analysis of the full matrix ¶ Analyzed all 280 fresh GPU and 140 M1 CPU jobs locally, rechecking log hashes, recomputing JSONL summaries and matching q/roots/seed/counts across scopes. Registered the full-matrix report in the site catalog. No remote execution, arithmetic changes or kernel tuning was done. The directly measured unfused/fused public ratio exceeds one in all 140 cells: range 1.77–3.03, median 2.36. At N=65536/K=40/60 bits, public fused product is 12.431 ms versus 1.723 ms in the separately timed resident experiment and 65.687 ms on the M1 CPU. These are distinct timing scopes/platforms, not an application acceleration claim. Large pinned copies achieve approximately 13.0 GB/s H2D and 13.5 GB/s D2H payload rates; they are not an additive public timing breakdown. Source inspection confirms public validation scans remain inside timing, so the scope gap cannot be assigned solely to PCIe. Thirty-bit primes still take the u64 path. All 35 paired resident forward and product comparisons put 60-bit execution below 30-bit in this run (median 60/30 ratios 0.882 and 0.881). The cause is unknown. Prime and input values differ, remainder arithmetic may behave differently, and versions run in a fixed order. Slow outlying public-call samples and irregular workload times need repeat measurements before we select different implementations for different cases. Priority remains split-runner integration and resident-pipeline preparation, then individually validated arithmetic/layout candidates. Counters and actual RTX 5090 execution remain pending. No roadmap gate advances from this analysis. Publish the analysis to the personal website ¶ The user requested publication of the new report and current session notes. Publication preparation passes 23 documentation tests, 34 tooling tests, 14 interactive Node tests, the 34-page link/fragment build and whitespace checks. Export uses the existing managed systems/ntt-gpu topic and relative navigation; unrelated website files are preserved. Source is committed before export so generated code links point to the matching repository revision. Remote deployment and live-page availability are checked separately from a successful Git push. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Compare rental costs and profiling access", "summary": "Compared dated provider prices, saved an offer search and calculated example session costs. This survey did not rent a GPU.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-13.html", "text": "2026-11-13 P2 D11 preparation ← All entries Compare rental costs and profiling access Compared dated provider prices, saved an offer search and calculated example session costs. This survey did not rent a GPU. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Compare GPU rentals for the first native session ¶ Outcome ¶ Completed a GPU rental market survey for this project's first native CUDA session. Compare RunPod Community and verified Vast.ai RTX 5090 offers; retain RTX 4090 as the control. No GPU was rented or tested. D11 remains open and no roadmap gate advances. Reasoning ¶ The lowest advertised GPU price may not give the cheapest complete session. The machine needs compatible drivers and compilers, enough CPU/RAM/disk, and permission to collect Nsight performance counters. Setup time and network charges also affect the bill. Offline preparation makes a short on-demand session more practical than continuous rental or an early reservation. Changes ¶ Added an English comparison of RunPod, Vast.ai, TensorDock, AutoDL, Jarvislabs and Lambda, with clickable official sources and quote limitations. Inspected RunPod's Community and Secure pricing tabs in Chromium and distinguished Lambda's November 16 future-effective prices from current rates. Saved a timestamped, read-only Vast offer sample with its query, compute prices and selected public metadata; omitted public host IP addresses. Added worked compute budgets, stopped-storage billing rules, profiling requirements and a first-session handoff; linked the survey from hardware selection and offline preparation and registered it in the Research sidebar. Verification ¶ Official pricing, billing and NVIDIA permission sources were reviewed on November 13, 2026. The Vast query returned 64 records: 33 RTX 5090 and 31 RTX 4090 offers. Its response reported no truncation; the survey describes a sample, not an exhaustive inventory or guaranteed booking. AutoDL's current GPU offer and all specific hosts' hardware-counter permissions remain unverified. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The build validated local links and fragments for 30 HTML pages, including nine journal entries. Chromium passed 120 page/width checks at 1440/1024/375/320px: English content, rendered mathematics, no page-wide overflow and no script or HTTP errors. The new article and journal passed computed text-contrast checks; section navigation and search worked. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected, and wide price tables were adjusted for horizontal scrolling on phones. All 64 saved offer records were checked against every stated query filter; the article's counts, minima, maxima and medians match the snapshot, and each record's compute plus default storage equals its reported total. This research establishes no GPU correctness or performance result. Website export, deployment and live verification follow the source commit. Open questions ¶ Which suitable host is available at checkout? Does its complete configuration provide the required driver, toolkit, build resources and Nsight permissions? What are the actual setup time, verified workload time and final invoice? Next step ¶ Select a live full-GPU on-demand RTX 5090 offer, resolve profiler permissions, then run the prepared session and save evidence. Mark D11 complete only after recording the actual GPU environment and running its required checks. Download the results and analyze them locally between paid sessions. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Accept the backend design and prepare CUDA offline", "summary": "Accepted the backend design, compiled CUDA without a GPU, and linked explanations to exact OpenFHE source lines. Hardware tests remained unfinished.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-12.html", "text": "2026-11-12 P1 complete / P2 D11 preparation ← All entries Accept the backend design and prepare CUDA offline Accepted the backend design, compiled CUDA without a GPU, and linked explanations to exact OpenFHE source lines. Hardware tests remained unfinished. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Define and accept the device backend architecture ¶ Outcome ¶ Completed the D9 backend proposal . The roadmap advances to D10's architecture decision and implementation backlog. P1 remains in progress until that review accepts the proposal. This change implements host metadata checks and compile-checked abstract interfaces; a CUDA runtime backend and OpenFHE adapter remain proposed. Reasoning ¶ D7 specifies the array representation; D8 specifies the exact Hybrid operation order. A pointer and length still cannot say which Q primes are active, where P towers sit in the key, which roots apply, or whether earlier GPU work has finished. The design keeps those facts in read-only parameter copies and checked views that select complete prime towers. The first connection to OpenFHE will be an adapter that callers explicitly choose to convert a multi-prime polynomial between coefficients and evaluations. It builds a separate CPU result and replaces the original only after success. It may choose a permitted CPU path during initial checks, before GPU submission. Invalid inputs and later runtime failures report errors. Keeping inputs unchanged and allocating separate outputs makes shared-memory ownership simpler, but any extra GPU copies must be measured. Changes ¶ Added backend_contract.hpp : immutable parameters, canonical layouts, checked extents, allocation generations, operation layouts, dependencies, cache identities, retirement and hoist rules. Added backend_api.hpp : proposed stream, completion, immutable polynomial, owned download, cache-budget and backend APIs. There is no runtime implementation or factory. Added backend_contract_tests.cpp as the normal-build backend_contract_check CTest. Extended the Hybrid gate to import real OpenFHE context parameters and compare active QP metadata/key offsets with actual Hybrid output and key towers at L=5,4,3,1. Documented staging ownership, event waits, single-upload cache entries, resource pins, failure quarantine, out-of-place costs and Hybrid lifetimes. The existing raw-Impl CUDA constructor cleanup gap is still open. Registered the English article, linked D5/D8 to it, updated build guidance, and prepared the website export with the existing readable theme and sidebar. Verification ¶ The portable contract executable passes 78 checks on Linux x86_64/GCC 14.2. The same test passes AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer with no reported issues. Tests include invalid roots, canonical values, overflow, incorrect reduced-QP spans, stale generations, overlap, missing cross-stream waits, failed-resource retirement, changed cache identities and forbidden post-submission CPU fallback. Final local verification: All 16 OpenFHE-enabled Release CTests passed in 11.31 seconds, including the 78-check metadata gate and the existing 117,571,584-residue Hybrid gate. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The documentation build generated 25 HTML pages, including eight journal entries, and validated local links, fragments, assets and catalog coverage. Chromium passed 100 page/width checks across all 25 notebook pages at 1440/1024/375/320px: English content, rendered formulas, no page-wide overflow, and no script or failed HTTP responses. D9's section links and search worked; its article, this entry, the roadmap and both introductory lessons passed computed text-contrast checks. The tracker displays D10. Desktop and phone screenshots of the new article were inspected. Publication exports the source article and journal to the existing personal website, preserving section navigation and the shared design. Deployment and live verification follow the website push. These are CPU/metadata and browser gates; no CUDA compiler or real GPU is available in this environment, and no GPU correctness or performance result is claimed. Open questions ¶ D10 must accept the first seam, immutable API, copy cost, registry provenance, cache budget/backpressure, failure quarantine and ordered implementation backlog. Runtime tests still need allocation/upload fault injection, cache races, asynchronous error/drain handling, completion abandonment and host rollback. Production Hybrid conversion/key-family coverage remains later work. Next step ¶ Produce the D10 architecture decision record and actionable backlog, accepting or amending the D7–D9 contracts. Keep P1 open until that review is complete; then proceed to P2's named-hardware native CUDA exactness and profiling gates. D10: accept architecture and close P1 ¶ Outcome ¶ Accepted ADR 0001 and the implementation backlog . The roadmap now marks P1 DONE and points to D11. P2 remains NOT STARTED pending native GPU provisioning. This acceptance freezes behavioral contracts and implementation priorities; it does not implement or validate the CUDA runtime backend or OpenFHE adapter. Reasoning ¶ The D1–D9 evidence establishes stage/index arithmetic, exact representation, the pinned Hybrid graph and checked metadata. Review identified two design gaps that needed explicit decisions: resource admission limits, and resource ownership when work is partially submitted but event recording or handle publication fails. The decision requires ownership references before any call that might queue work. If submission fails before returning a handle, the runtime still keeps the operation record and its buffers. It waits for work to stop, or retains the resources separately, instead of immediately reusing them. It sets separate configurable limits: 256 MiB for cached tables/keys, 256 MiB for GPU working buffers and 128 MiB for temporary CPU transfer buffers. Initially each session has one ordered GPU queue and one adapter call at a time. A request that cannot fit reports an error immediately. These are conservative design limits rather than measured optimal settings. The DCRT adapter remains the first integration seam. RequireDevice is its explicit default; invalid data and resource errors cannot silently fall back. Only valid unsupported/unavailable/unverified operations may use an explicitly permitted CPU route before submission. Candidate import precedes the pinned nonthrowing DCRT move commit. Upstream allocating noexcept operations and the shared FTT cache's missing root identity remain explicit limitations. Changes ¶ Added the accepted ADR with decisions, source/evidence traceability, alternatives, partial-submission/error semantics and P1 exit dispositions. Added twelve dependency-ordered backlog items, B01–B12, each with phase, deliverable and observable acceptance criteria through P8. Made constructor RAII/device/input validation a P2 prerequisite, and recorded the setup helper's missing benchmark-error propagation and default-build coverage as baseline-reporting work. No CUDA fix is claimed in D10. Updated D5/D8/D9 cross-links, build guidance, catalog and roadmap. Historical D9 results above remain intact; the new ADR supplies the current acceptance. Prepared both new English pages for the existing website, section sidebar, search and shared readable theme. Runtime code and kernels are unchanged. Verification ¶ Observed on Linux x86_64/GCC 14.2: All 16 OpenFHE-enabled Release CTests passed in 11.41 seconds, retaining the 78 metadata checks and 117,571,584 Hybrid residue-array comparisons. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The site builds 27 HTML pages with eight journal entries and validates routes, fragments, assets and catalog coverage. Chromium passed 108 page/width checks at 1440/1024/375/320px: English text, rendered formulas, no page-wide overflow and no script/HTTP errors. ADR and backlog section links/search worked. Focused contrast checks covered the new pages, current journal, roadmap and both introductory lessons. The homepage and tracker show D11, P1 DONE and P2 NOT STARTED. Visual review found the four-column task table cramped on phones. A contained, keyboard-scrollable table preserves column widths; twelve task IDs link to their acceptance criteria. Four focused width checks passed after the change, including keyboard scrolling and task navigation. Desktop and phone screenshots of the ADR/backlog were inspected. Both repositories are prepared for publication from this source revision. Deployment and public-file verification follow the website push. The review environment has no usable native CUDA/GPU execution path, so none of the later native implementation gates is marked done. Open questions ¶ B01 must establish an actual primary/control GPU environment. B02/B03 must resolve native compilation and lifecycle failures before correctness or benchmark claims. Runtime budget defaults need measured memory-pressure evidence; broad parameter/key coverage, multi-stream capability, adapter rollback and process failure handling remain implementation acceptance gates. Next step ¶ D11 / B01: record the provided GPU, driver, toolkit, compiler, source revisions, OpenFHE build and native architecture configuration. Then run B02's native build and baseline-safety work before B03 exactness and B04 profiling. P2 is ready to start, not completed by the D10 review. Offline preparation before GPU rental ¶ Outcome ¶ Completed the offline preparation for the first native session. Real nvcc builds and links both sm_89 and sm_120 targets, including a new direct CUDA/OpenFHE exactness program. The collector returns OFFLINE_READY after 19 host/OpenFHE tests and explicitly leaves four GPU tests unrun. D11 remains the current working day: its actual hardware/driver record cannot be completed in this driverless workspace. P2 preparation is now IN PROGRESS. Reasoning ¶ The user asked to finish work that can be done before renting. Compiling without a GPU finds build and setup problems before paid rental time starts. CUDA 12.8.93 failed compiler identification on this host's glibc 2.41 because of math-header exception-specification conflicts. A stock CUDA 13.0.88 compiler from the 13.0.2 redistributable release works here; vendor headers were not patched. The first nvcc build exposed the existing CUDA file's missing namespace closure. Free launch helpers now deduce the private implementation type without exposing it publicly. The original raw-Impl construction path also needed unique ownership, while default CMake detection and the old benchmark pipeline could misclassify incomplete/failed work as success. The changes address those offline blockers without claiming native execution or performance. Changes ¶ Added a SHA-256-locked local NVIDIA compiler/runtime/header installer, preserving licenses and leaving drivers/system packages unchanged. Added explicit CMake CUDA enable/require controls, a shared CUDA backend library, GPU labels, host validation/constructor targets, and compiler-only CI. Changed the synchronous wrapper to unique Impl ownership and owning-device guards. Added checked dimensions/counts, prime/root bounds and canonical input rejection; preserved the six-buffer dataflow and arithmetic stage bodies. Added the N=65536 direct CUDA/CPU/OpenFHE comparison program: exact-width 55/50/60-bit primes, D8 Q/QlP bases at L=5,4,3,1, seven coefficient patterns, independent inverse input, round trip, pointwise/inverse and ring product. It is compiled but not run on a GPU. Replaced the AutoDL helper's implicit pull/install and fragile verdict with explicit session collection, immutable report directories and stage logs. Missing coverage or failed build/test/sanitizer/benchmark stages return nonzero. The pinned OpenFHE helper now explicitly selects its 64-bit/no-reduced-noise CPU build and bounded parallelism, so offline preparation cannot accidentally execute newly detected GPU tests. Updated the D5 map, roadmap/backlog, source guidance and website catalog. Verification ¶ Observed on Linux x86_64, glibc 2.41, GCC 14.2 and stock CUDA compiler 13.0.88: Both architecture targets and all CUDA executables compile and link. The offline collector passed 19 host/OpenFHE CTests. These include 17 standalone host-preflight assertions and 20 assertions in the CUDA-linked constructor executable; the latter rejects three invalid constructors before device access. The separate CUDA-disabled build passed all 15 CTests. Eleven mocked-process reporting tests passed, including benchmark/sanitizer failure propagation, missing GPU labels/tests and preservation of prior records. They test collection control flow, not GPU arithmetic or CUDA allocation failure. Actual ntt_cuda_cli info returned a visible insufficient-driver error here. An explicit CUDA-required/CUDA-disabled configuration also failed as intended. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The build generates 28 pages, including eight journal entries, with valid routes, fragments and assets. Chromium passed 112 page/width checks at 1440/1024/375/320px: English content, rendered math, no page-wide overflow or script/HTTP errors. The preparation guide's section links/search and current P1/D11/P2 state passed; focused text contrast and desktop/phone screenshots were reviewed. Source CI and public deployment are checked after publication. Reports remain under ignored bench-results-* ; local development reports record that the tested source working tree was dirty before its publication commit. No GPU was rented, no driver was installed, and no native kernel, sanitizer, performance or peak-device-memory result is claimed. Open questions ¶ D11 still needs an actual GPU/driver environment record. B02 requires native allocation/upload fault injection, wrong-device and cleanup-failure evidence. The collector's sanitizer scope is a smoke subset; full production cases and kernel-only/raw/Nsight profiling remain later gates. CUDA 13's successful offline compilation does not establish compatibility with a rental machine's driver. Next step ¶ Use the prepared clean checkout on the chosen compatible GPU image, record the hardware, and run the native collection command. Export raw records before stopping rental; analyze them offline. Keep D11/B02/B03/P2 open until their specific native acceptance criteria pass. Link OpenFHE explanations to the real implementation ¶ Outcome ¶ The D7 representation page and D8 Hybrid graph now link function names where their behavior is discussed and show seven expandable literal excerpts. Related backend, architecture, roadmap, preparation, survey and introductory pages also link their OpenFHE-specific claims to exact upstream code. D11's hardware gate and implementation status are unchanged. For example, the format discussion opens the actual SetFormat condition , and the Hybrid explanation opens the actual key-offset/B-A MAC loop . Reasoning ¶ A bibliography alone makes readers search for the code behind an explanation. Placing a source link beside the claim lets them inspect the relevant implementation immediately. Inline code links and short attributed excerpts make format dispatch, cache conditions, digit conversion, key offsets and reduction order directly inspectable. Commit-pinned line links remain stable as the upstream default branch evolves. Public NativePoly/DCRTPoly aliases are linked to their template definitions so readers recognize the implementation names after opening GitHub. Changes ¶ Added clickable code names and exact file/line ranges beside technical claims, including the main Hybrid flow steps and wrapper/API tables. Added seven expandable source excerpts: NativePoly dispatch, conditional SetFormat, FTT cache hit, key-switch call chain, digit format/conversion, reduced-level B/A MAC, and the final ModDown correction. Extended source citations to the proposed adapter/cache design, accepted ADR, implementation backlog, offline guide, arithmetic review, roadmap, benchmark plan, surveys, project overview and introductory HTML lessons. Retained OpenFHE copyright attribution and the full BSD 2-Clause license with the redistributed excerpts. Added source-citation guidance for future authors. Kept proposed GPU APIs labeled separately from existing upstream CPU code. No kernels, fixtures or runtime behavior changed. Verification ¶ Verified 46 distinct upstream file/line links across 15 generated pages. All 17 referenced implementation files match the pinned raw GitHub files byte for byte; all seven displayed excerpts match their cited lines in the clean checkout. All 23 documentation unit tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The site builds 28 pages with valid routes, fragments and assets. Chromium passed 112 page/width checks at 1440/1024/375/320px, including all seven expanded code blocks, source links, formula rendering, focused text contrast, section navigation and search. Both introductory lessons retain readable text. Actual clicks from the SetFormat and Hybrid MAC excerpts navigated to GitHub with HTTP 200 and the expected pinned file/line fragments. Visual review caught one nested Markdown link in the Hybrid paragraph; it was flattened and rechecked at all four widths. Desktop and phone screenshots of the excerpts were inspected. Source CI and live publication follow the two repository pushes. No runtime or working-day gate changed. Open questions ¶ A future OpenFHE version upgrade must recheck source ranges and excerpt text, not simply change the revision in each URL. Native GPU execution remains pending and is not established by source citations. Next step ¶ Publish these source-backed explanations in both repositories and retain this citation style as implementation and GPU validation notes are added. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Freeze OpenFHE arrays and the Hybrid graph", "summary": "Specified OpenFHE array formats and Hybrid operation order using exact CPU comparisons. Also recorded teaching and website changes.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-11.html", "text": "2026-11-11 P1 / D7 → D8 ← All entries Freeze OpenFHE arrays and the Hybrid graph Specified OpenFHE array formats and Hybrid operation order using exact CPU comparisons. Also recorded teaching and website changes. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Freeze the OpenFHE NTT and DCRT representation contract ¶ Outcome ¶ D7's NTT/DCRT boundary is documented against pinned OpenFHE 1.6.1. The tracker advances to D8, the Hybrid operation graph; P1's architecture gate stays open. The website export includes the previously unpublished D3–D6 work and this D7 contract, with the shared section navigation retained. Reasoning ¶ A forward transform and its inverse can share an ordering or scaling mistake and still recover the input. D7 therefore checks every output against polynomials with directly calculable answers and inverse inputs with one nonzero position. It also uses a real three-prime OpenFHE polynomial to check tower order and packing. OpenFHE’s towers are allocated separately, so packing must not assume one flat array. The pinned source distinguishes SetFormat (conditional conversion), SwitchFormat (always conversion), SetValues (data plus label), and OverrideFormat (label only). DCRT OverrideFormat changes only the outer label. RootOfUnity selects the minimum primitive root, but the FTT cache lookup does not compare roots on a cache hit; consistent imported roots are part of this contract. Changes ¶ Added the D7 representation contract , including exact formulas, parameter/root requirements, format APIs, ordered tower packing, active-level behavior, and pinned source references. Added openfhe_representation_tests.cpp and the opt-in openfhe_representation_contract CTest gate. At N=65536, checked four monomials and four independent inverse slots at each of three exact-width primes (50/55/60 bits), minimum roots, canonical values, and format API behavior against actual OpenFHE NativePoly operations. Checked a deliberately unsorted 55/50/60-bit tower list, BigInteger coefficients Q-1 and 2^64+17, CRT reconstruction, flat evaluation-array import, pointwise multiplication by x with negative wrap, and tower dropping. Clarified that FirstPrime(50, m) searches above 2^50: the original gate uses a 51-bit prime there. D7 uses LastPrime for exact-width fixtures. Corrected reference-header comments that conflated mathematical and stored evaluation order or described individual ladder twiddles as odd powers. The NTT algorithm is unchanged. Reused stage-coverage storage across stages in the tile test. This removes the observed GCC 14 Release vector-teardown build failure without suppressing warnings or removing coverage. Registered the article, updated build/test instructions, and advanced the roadmap to D8. Verification ¶ Built OpenFHE from commit b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f locally on Linux x86_64 with GCC 14.2, static libraries, NativeInteger 64, and OpenMP off. The initial OpenFHE-enabled Release build passed all 14 CTests, including the original exact compatibility gate and the new representation gate. The tile coverage target also compiles in the normal GCC Release configuration. Final verification: All 14 OpenFHE-enabled Release CTests passed after the complete test changes. All 23 documentation unit tests and all 4 explorer tests passed. The build generated 22 pages with seven journal entries and validated links, fragments, assets, and catalog coverage. Chromium followed every section link on all 22 pages at 1440, 1280, 1024, and 375px: 88 page/width checks passed without JavaScript errors or page-wide horizontal overflow. Search, menu, sections, and the forward explorer worked. Focused desktop/mobile checks confirmed D7 math without KaTeX errors, the D7→D8 progress row inside its table, and inverse explorer stage order, reciprocal twiddles, and pass labels. Screenshots of the D5–D7 articles were inspected at 1440 and 375px. Publication exports all current source pages, including D3/D4 proofs, D5/D6 articles, and this D7 entry, to the existing personal website repository. Live verification follows the website deployment. These are CPU/OpenFHE and browser checks; no CUDA compiler or real GPU was available, and no GPU performance result is implied. Open questions ¶ D8 must trace the order of Hybrid digit splitting, changes in prime basis, key multiplication and accumulation, rounding and final reduction. D9 must specify who owns buffers and GPU work queues, how setup cleans up after a failure, and where the new backend connects to OpenFHE. Arbitrary custom-root contexts sharing a modulus are outside the D7 test matrix because of the pinned OpenFHE cache behavior. Next step ¶ Trace OpenFHE 1.6.1 Hybrid key switching and freeze the exact operation graph with intermediate-state fixtures. Preserve bit equality rather than replacing it with approximate CKKS decryption agreement. Manuscript supplement: recursion and stage twiddles ¶ The user's handwritten derivation was added to the NTT and butterfly lesson : Split the original sum into even and odd inputs and derive the shared multiplication used by the two outputs. Recurse into the a₄ᵣ and a₄ᵣ₊₂ subsequences and explain why roots square. Expand all four outputs and draw an accessible P/R/Q/S-to-b₀/b₂/b₁/b₃ SVG. Specify zero-based cyclic DIT stages, group lengths, and local offsets: w(s,j) = omega_N^(j*N/2^(s+1)) . Interpret 8; 4,12; 2,10,6,14 as one four-bit reversed root-table convention, rather than a universal list of cyclic DIT factors. Map this to the repository's reverse-stage negacyclic DIF, including c=psi^d and w=omega_N^(N*J/(2d))=psi^(N*J/d) . Add an N=4/8/16/65536 exponent calculator with indices, distances, both root conventions, and stage direction. Preserve the existing numerical examples. New Node tests execute the actual calculator script and compare cyclic DIT and negacyclic DIF with independent direct sums. They check target-size stage boundaries, indices, and control limits. Documentation CI runs both groups of interactive mathematical checks. This documentation supplement did not advance D8 or P1 and did not add a GPU result. At publication, 23 documentation tests and eight interactive tests passed; all 22 generated pages passed local link and fragment validation. Standalone and generated lessons passed browser checks at 1440/375/320px: no KaTeX errors or page overflow; the target-size calculator, section links, search, four-point verification, and N=8 controls worked. Desktop/mobile network and calculator screenshots were inspected. The original record included Chinese navigation, which is translated in the English review below. Teaching revisions from the second manuscript ¶ The page review addressed each handwritten item without advancing D8 or P1: Merge direct-evaluation cost and savings, retaining the slider, chart, and old s8 anchor. Distinguish multiplication counts from butterfly counts and correct the doubling ratio and log–log curve description. State the cyclic DIT stage lengths, distances, and dependencies. Derive the negacyclic odd-root split and prove the folded factors using the remaining local diagonal twist. Every stage has c=ψᵈ; the final stage still has c=ψ. Share one editable 16-point example between the NTT and CPU-to-CUDA pages: q=97, ψ=19, all four stages, 32 pairs, factors and intermediate values. Column 3's first two stages and row 3's last two stages read the full trace. Add a weighted-butterfly diagram and final-stage explanation to the 65536 schedule page; typeset the column, row, and inverse proofs. Use N=65536/K=3 in the D5 host/GPU flow diagram: 1.5 MiB operands, six buffers, constant uploads, eight launches, and 4.5 MiB operand transfers. Typeset D7's ring, evaluation positions, probes, root tables, tower packing, and negative wrap. Refine D6 ranges and invariants and fix narrow-screen table overflow in the standalone HTML. At publication, 23 Python tests and 12 Node tests passed. All 16 basis vectors, boundary and signed inputs matched independent odd-root evaluation. Both pair groupings matched the full transform; every intermediate stage satisfied the diagonal relation with pre-twisted cyclic DIF. All 22 pages built with valid links/fragments. Five revised pages passed 1440/375/320px interaction, math, and overflow checks; three standalone lessons also passed. Desktop and phone screenshots were inspected. Site-wide navigation and formulas were checked at 1440/1280/1024/375px; the checker was corrected to decode Chinese URL fragments before comparison. Production NTT algorithms were unchanged. No GPU execution result was claimed. English edition and website design review ¶ Applied the public frontend-design skill to the personal portfolio and notebook. The design uses porcelain, blue, and white surfaces, locally hosted Newsreader headings and Source Sans 3 body text, clear reading widths, and less decorative chrome. The portfolio leads with an existing photograph; the notebook keeps its page outline, search, and exact interactive examples. Translated the manuscript derivation, calculator, 16-point example, and prior journal additions into English while retaining their technical observations. The old Chinese fragment remains an invisible compatibility anchor. Photo collection titles and labels are also English. Published search text and accessible labels are checked for remaining Chinese characters. The review covers all 41 HTML pages: four portfolio hubs, 15 photo collections, and 22 notebook pages. The personal site now has visible phone navigation, a keyboard-accessible native photo viewer with arrow keys and focus restoration, and real painting disclosure buttons with inert collapsed content. Existing image and video files are retained. Local font files include their OFL licenses. Observed browser verification: 164 page/width checks passed at 1440/1024/375/320px, with no JavaScript errors, failed HTTP responses, formula errors, duplicate IDs, or page-wide overflow. The first pass found one 320px notebook-heading overflow; allowing the heading row to wrap resolved it. Local links, fragments, and referenced assets were validated across all 41 pages. Desktop and phone interaction checks passed for photo browsing, Escape and focus return, painting playback/pause/speed/folder controls, the 65536 calculator, 16-point input validation, original arithmetic widgets, and search. Screenshots of each page were captured; representative portfolio, collection, notebook, and phone layouts were inspected. Documentation and mathematical checks remain the separate CPU/browser gates; this design work does not advance D8 or imply a GPU result. Restore contrast in the two introductory lessons ¶ The user reported faint content in the NTT/butterfly and CPU-to-CUDA pages. The design refresh had made definition panels white while retaining pale foregrounds from the previous dark panels. Their equations reached only 1.04:1 contrast. Recap headings were dark on dark, and selected ghost buttons used dark text on a dark blue background. The shared standalone lesson theme now pairs light panels with dark text, readable accent colors, and explicit selected/hover button colors. Recaps use a light blue surface. Success badges define their own contrast-safe variables, so recalculation's inline variable references retain readable results. Small SVG labels and the 16-point network connections are darker; all network stages retain full-opacity lines, with thicker highlighted pairs. Color crossfades on stage controls and the standalone table of contents are removed to avoid low contrast during state changes. The source lessons and exported pages share these rules. Verification: 23 documentation unit tests and 12 interactive mathematical tests passed. Sixteen browser checks covered both lessons, standalone and generated, at 1440/1024/375/320px. With optional sections expanded, visible HTML text passed computed contrast checks (4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text; excluding disabled controls and SVG). Checks included selected/hovered stage buttons, recalculated badges, arithmetic examples, math rendering, and page overflow. SVG labels and panels were inspected in screenshots; network lines retain full opacity and a stroke width of at least one pixel. The arithmetic and working-day roadmap are unchanged. D8: freeze the pinned Hybrid operation graph ¶ Completed the D8 graph against the clean OpenFHE 1.6.1 checkout at b59e1f291965f14fbee5172cd83a20f5b856204f . The tracker advances to D9. P1 remains open for buffer/device/stream/cache ownership and the final architecture decision. The source trace records how adjacent Q towers form digits, including a shorter last digit. It identifies which arrays change between coefficients and evaluations, which missing towers are computed, and which key positions are used after dropping a level. It also records the B-before-A output order, final CKKS reduction and updates to two- or three-component ciphertexts. It also records extended ciphertext scaling, delayed QP accumulation, and the source ordering of extended rotation/relinearization. Approximate CRT keeps its integer sum before reducing under a destination prime. Reducing that sum under the source basis first would change the arrays. Reducing each digit separately instead of reducing the accumulated result also gives different answers. Added openfhe_hybrid_graph , an opt-in PKE-linked CTest using N=65536 throughout. The deterministic context has M=5, alpha=3, K=3 and two full-context digits. The executable pins all eight moduli/roots. Its scaling-size request is 50, but two selected Q primes have 51 bits; arithmetic uses the exact primes. Levels L=5,4,3,1 exercise full/partial last digits and a partial first digit. Four coefficient patterns (zero, q−1, x^17, deterministic dense residues) use repeatable diagnostic key arrays. Two further cases use an actual key generated from two real secret keys; that key is fixed within each comparison, not across separate random key-generation runs. An independent arbitrary-precision unreduced-CRT sum and scalar arithmetic check the complete digit INTT, complement conversion/NTT, assembled digit, two MAC outputs, P INTT, Q correction conversion/NTT, and final P-inverse scale. The independent integer calculation uses arbitrary-precision BigInteger values. It does not read OpenFHE’s conversion tables to obtain its expected answer, so it can expose mistakes in those table-driven calculations. Transform expectations use the already-checked repository NTT with the exact imported roots. Public-core composition, source immutability, in-place add/replace behavior, both extended addFirst settings, delayed reduction, and first-element-only reduction are checked separately. Observed results on Linux x86_64/GCC 14.2, Release, NativeInteger 64, OpenMP off: All 15 OpenFHE-enabled CTests passed (11.44 seconds in this run). The new gate passed 18 cases and 117,571,584 complete-array residue comparisons. Fixtures detected canonical-CRT substitution and wrong reduced-level P offsets. For the diagnostic L=4 dense fixture, 524,288 residues distinguish the incorrect per-digit reduction schedule from accumulation followed by reduction. All 23 documentation tests and 14 Node interaction/math tests passed. The site builds 23 pages and validates local routes, fragments, and assets. D8's 18 math expressions, five-level explorer, all section links, search, English content, text contrast, and overflow checks passed at 1440/1024/375/320px. The roadmap shows D9, and both introductory lessons retain their contrast fix. Desktop and phone article/explorer screenshots were inspected. This is a CPU/source contract gate. It does not claim native CUDA results, end-to-end CKKS encryption/decryption correctness, complete rotation/key-family coverage, or production performance. P4/P6 broaden those fixtures; D9 next specifies the buffer/stream/cache contract and integration seam. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Inverse proofs and exact arithmetic", "summary": "Explained how inverse recovers inputs, checked that both passes cover all stages, mapped GPU buffers and tested exact CPU arithmetic.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-10.html", "text": "2026-11-10 P1 / D3 → D7 ← All entries Inverse proofs and exact arithmetic Explained how inverse recovers inputs, checked that both passes cover all stages, mapped GPU buffers and tested exact CPU arithmetic. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Prove inverse correspondence and complete stage coverage ¶ Outcome ¶ D3 is complete. The tracker now points to D4: contiguous tile kernels and exact global-stage coverage. No GPU arithmetic or launch behavior changed; two source comments were clarified. The P1 architecture gate remains open. Follow-up: D4 is also complete. The tracker now points to D5 constructor, buffer and public-API dataflow. D3 and D4 are being committed together at the user's request; no push or website publication was requested this turn. D5 follow-up: completed the constructor/buffer/API map and advanced the tracker to D6. The user requested committing and pushing this follow-up; the earlier D3/D4 commit is 404e88a. Website export is a separate action. Reasoning ¶ For the CPU/legacy schedule studied here, inverse uses the same paired positions as forward but the inverse multipliers. First undo the multiplier on the difference output. Then add and subtract, and undo the lower input’s root factor. The result is twice the original pair. Each reversed stage adds another factor of two; after log₂N stages, the factor is N. One final multiplication by the modular inverse of N removes it. The inverse must execute near row stages first, far column stages second, and scaling last. This is three launches at both endpoint sizes, not two. The bit-reversed evaluation output of forward is the inverse's direct input; neither direction needs a separate bit-reversal pass. Changes ¶ Added the inverse proof and examples , including both endpoint sizes and a numeric N=8/q=17 butterfly. Extended the explorer with forward/inverse direction selection, ascending inverse distances, reciprocal twiddle display, pass order, and scale note. The panel DOM order follows execution order for keyboard navigation too. Extended column mapping checks with --inverse , registered as inverse_column_check in CTest. Tagged-table probes cover all 270,336 inverse column pairs (540,672 active probes), and inactive threads, at N=4096 and N=65536. Valid-root checks for two 46-bit primes per size verify each column stage composition, then the full inverse's unnormalized and normalized outputs against the original and independent CPU reference. Updated the roadmap . Calendar dates do not advance working days; completed deliverables do. D4: added the row-kernel and complete-stage proof . Since 2d divides the tile length, row-local groups are aligned CPU groups and J=j; E=(N/d)j still uses the global transform length. Far and near distances are disjoint and concatenate to the full CPU distance sequence. Added tile_mapping_tests.cpp and tile_mapping_check . The tests use distinguishable multiplier-table entries to run the actual contiguous-stage helpers in both directions. They inspect the helper’s output rather than checking only a second copy of its index formulas. Separate stage-list and global endpoint coverage checks exercise all five target sizes and N=2,64,256,512 boundaries. D5: added CUDA buffers and host/device dataflow , registered in the HTML notebook. Mapped six persistent buffers, constructor uploads, normal cleanup, host-vector API contracts, flat versus limb-grid launches, transfer payloads, and fused multiplication. Clarified two public header comments without changing runtime behavior. Added six static source-contract checks in test_cuda_dataflow.py . These pin allocation sizes/frees, constructor copies, public length guards and copies, fused launch ordering, getters/interface shape, and endpoint byte arithmetic. Recorded source-inspected constructor exception leaks and missing device/stream/representation ownership contracts. No RAII fix, failure injection, or native CUDA compile was performed as part of D5. Verification ¶ Configured CMake and built ntt_column_mapping_tests and ntt_device_sim . Targeted CTest run: column_mapping_check , inverse_column_check , device_schedule_check , and four fixture generators all passed (7/7). Four Node explorer tests passed, now covering both directions, sizes, all stages, boundary owners, twiddle exponents, and inverse panel order. All 15 documentation unit tests passed; the site build validated local links and fragments. Requirements were confirmed in the isolated .venv . These checks execute on CPU. No native CUDA compilation, actual GPU synchronization check, hardware timing, or GPU occupancy measurement is claimed. Full CMake build and all 10 CTests passed, including unit tests, the Python oracle crosscheck, forward/inverse column gates, and the device-schedule simulator. Desktop and 375px browser checks confirmed proof reflow and inverse controls. At N=65536, inverse stage 9/distance 256 shows column 3/thread 5, J=3, inverse ladder exponent 256, inverse twiddle exponent 768. D4: full CMake build and all 11 CTests passed, including the new tile gate. It checked 278,528 row pairs per direction (1,114,112 active probes across both directions), plus idle threads. Stage lists in both directions and complete per-stage endpoint coverage passed for all nine tested sizes. All four explorer tests, 15 documentation tests and the 18-page build/link check also passed. These are CPU-side checks, not real GPU evidence. D4 browser review confirmed readable proof/formula reflow at desktop and a 375px embedded viewport. The existing forward/inverse explorer remains intact; no GPU kernels were changed by D4. D5: all 21 documentation/source-contract tests, four explorer tests and 11 CTests passed. The documentation build passed local link and fragment validation. Allocation/transfer byte counts are derived from source, not measured VRAM use or transfer throughput. D5 browser review checked the generated article at desktop and 375px widths, including table-of-contents navigation and allocation tables. Open questions ¶ CUDA launch and barrier behavior still need real-device checks in P2. Performance impact of strided column loads is still unmeasured. Constructor exceptions can leak Impl and any completed device allocations; an RAII implementation and fault-injection tests remain required. Device, stream/event and representation ownership are not yet frozen. Next step ¶ D6: review modular-arithmetic preconditions and reduction bounds; inventory existing exact gates and specify missing adversarial tests. Parallel workload and navigation changes ¶ The following parallel work was recorded on origin/main before this merge. It is preserved as history; the current tracker is D6 and the current workload is N=65536. Earlier multi-size D3/D4 results above describe checks run before the N=65536-only changes were integrated. Integration result: merged origin/main's a578e7a and 8aa94c3 without discarding the D3–D5 work. The current lesson is ntt-65536-work-distribution.html , with no size selector; inverse direction and all proof sections remain. D3/D4 current gates now target N=65536 (small boundary coverage remains). The post-merge build and all 10 CTests, 4 explorer tests, 23 documentation/source tests, and the 19-page build/link check passed. The old 4096 fixture is not reintroduced. Current row probes total 1,048,576 across both directions; each column direction has 262,144 pairs and 524,288 active probes. Fresh desktop/mobile previews preserve the responsive navigation and D5 layout. Outcome ¶ The project workload is now N=65536. The work-distribution lesson has one fixed size, with 256 rows and columns, 16 stages, 524,288 butterflies, and 512 KiB per limb. Both passes launch 256 blocks per limb and use 2 KiB of shared memory per block. Requested value-array traffic is 2 MiB per forward. Reasoning ¶ The user requested N=65536 throughout the main lessons and implementation examples. Small teaching examples and N=64/1024 test inputs remain useful. They check changes in the schedule and keep the independent Python direct sum and basic coefficient-by-coefficient product fast enough to run. The general transform APIs still accept power-of-two lengths. Historical notebook observations and cited research ranges remain historical. Changes ¶ Renamed the work-distribution lesson , updated links and navigation, removed the size selector and comparison column, and corrected the memory, launch, and column-proof counts. Aligned the butterfly and GPU explainers, site shell, current project scope, benchmark plan, and integration plan to N=65536. Removed the 4096 CTest fixture. CUDA comparisons and verification now use the 65536 fixture. The column mapping and optional OpenFHE compatibility gates target 65536. Vector generation defaults to 65536; CMake explicitly requests 1024 for the quadratic oracle. CUDA CLI and AutoDL benchmarks default to exponent 16 only. Explicit benchmark arguments can still explore other lengths. Verification ¶ Release C++ build with GCC 14.2 and warnings as errors: all 8 portable CTest checks passed, including the N=65536 schedule and column-mapping checks. Documentation: all 15 Python unit tests passed; build_docs.py --check built 18 pages and validated local links and fragments. Explorer: all 3 Node tests passed, covering all 16 stages, boundary selections, inactive threads, twiddle exponents, and exhaustive per-stage index coverage. Default vector generation produced N=65536, byte-identical to the explicitly sized deterministic fixture. AutoDL shell syntax validation passed. Chromium/Playwright checked all three interactive HTML lessons at 1440px and 375px. No horizontal page overflow or JavaScript errors occurred. The explorer selected 1283/34051 initially, 773/901 at distance 128, and 65534/65535 at the final row/stage. The cost widget initialized at 65,536 and 4,294,967,296 direct products. Desktop/mobile explorer screenshots were visually inspected. No CUDA compiler or OpenFHE installation was present. Those native gates were not run; all implementation execution reported above was on CPU. Open questions ¶ Native CUDA and OpenFHE checks depend on their respective toolkit and library. CPU simulation does not establish GPU launch or synchronization correctness. Next step ¶ Continue the inverse-column correspondence proof at N=65536; no roadmap gate advances solely because the workload scope changed. Notebook reading review ¶ Reviewed all 18 published pages individually. The site now has one section sidebar generated from each page's main headings, including interactive HTML. Desktop sections stay beside the article; smaller screens expose the same links through Sections in the top bar. The current section updates while scrolling, and keyboard users can open, close, and follow section links. Page-by-page changes ¶ Page Reading change Overview Removed repeated collections and recent-entry cards; retained one complete directory and a shorter implementation sequence Project & setup Cut repeated status/design prose; put build commands and exactness gates first NTT & butterflies Shorter introduction and recap; preserved derivations, formulas, and interactive controls CPU to CUDA Shorter vocabulary and SIMT explanation; corrected wave, occupancy, and memory-traffic claims 65536 work distribution Shorter ownership explanation; preserved the index proof and explorer Run & debug Removed assumptions about installed tools; compressed recorded debugger troubleshooting GPU NTT implementations Decision tables and shorter headings; library evidence and citations in expandable sections Choosing the GPU Compact specifications and decision tables; historical pricing and full citations remain expandable Integration roadmap Shorter introduction; preserved day tables and gates; corrected the stale five-size specialization task Benchmarks & tuning Shorter policy prose; corrected the stale five-dimension acceptance requirement to N=65536 Writing & publishing One concise editing, validation, and export workflow; documented page and section navigation Journal index Clearer date-versus-deliverable explanation and a single-column entry list Journal 2026-11-05 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved reconstructed evidence Journal 2026-11-06 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved historical roadmap state Journal 2026-11-07 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved recorded test results Journal 2026-11-08 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved publishing history Journal 2026-11-09 Added summary and six-section navigation; preserved the original mapping work and follow-ups Journal 2026-11-10 Kept the workload change above and appended this review Article text, tables, code, and section links use larger type and consistent spacing. Wide tables scroll within the article. Existing heading IDs and bookmarked research-section aliases are retained. Search still includes collapsed references. No roadmap gate or CUDA implementation changed. Review verification ¶ All 17 documentation unit tests passed, including heading-ID collisions, inline-script preservation, and Markdown/math inside expandable references. The documentation build validated all 18 pages, local links, and fragments. All 3 explorer tests passed. Chromium checked every section link on every page at 1440, 1280, 1024, and 375px: 72 page/width checks passed with no JavaScript errors or page-wide horizontal overflow. Menu, section navigation, search, and Escape behavior were exercised at each width. Desktop and mobile screenshots were inspected. The review found and fixed Escape handling in search. No GPU execution or performance measurement is implied by these browser checks. Final narrow-screen checks passed on all 18 pages at both 320 and 375px. The 320px pass exposed an unwrapped GPU vocabulary table; shared scrolling wrappers now also cover lesson tables without nesting existing matrix wrappers. Focused browser checks confirmed sticky desktop sections, reading-position updates, final-section selection, both butterfly coefficient widgets, network step/reset, GPU pair-stage controls, expandable citations, and rendered math without KaTeX errors at desktop/mobile widths. Mobile section-menu screenshots were also inspected. D6 follow-up: arithmetic bounds and adversarial tests ¶ Outcome and reasoning ¶ D6 is complete; the live tracker advances to D7, not past the P1 architecture gate. The arithmetic contract records required inputs, why temporary integers are wide enough, why each stage keeps values in 0…q−1, independent answer checks, and difficult cases that still need testing. No reduction algorithm, kernel geometry or public API changed. The implementation uses canonical residues after every operation, not lazy reduction. A 60-bit modulus still needs a product wider than uint64_t. The arithmetic-header comment incorrectly counted CPU butterfly multiplies: the CPU loop uses three including twiddle advance; shared device helpers use two with table lookup. Corrected the comment and made q>0 explicit. Changes ¶ Added a compiled arithmetic probe and full-size tests . Added Python arbitrary-precision comparisons for 14,956 primitive results, including boundary moduli/operands, full-width products, large exponents and valid/zero-input inverse behavior. Added N=65536 two-sided roundtrips and canonical-range checks for six patterns at 50/55/60-bit primes, plus the closed-form negative wrap product x^(N-1) * x = -1 . Registered both gates in CMake/CTest. Registered the D6 article in the generated HTML notebook and linked the dataflow article, validation instructions and roadmap to it. Verification ¶ All 12 standalone CTests passed , including both new arithmetic gates. Rebuilt the opt-in OpenFHE configuration and ran openfhe_exact_compat : passed against locally installed OpenFHE 1.6.1 at N=65536. Compiled the new C++ test with Clang AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; both the 14,956-result Python comparison and all full-size patterns/products passed with no sanitizer diagnostics. Invalid preconditions such as q=0 were deliberately not executed. All 23 documentation tests , 4 explorer tests , and the 20-page documentation build/link/fragment checks passed. No shared layout or interaction changed; this update adds article content. This is macOS host execution. No nvcc build, actual GPU run, race check or GPU performance measurement was performed or implied. Open questions and next step ¶ The constructor assumes primality, the public API assumes canonical residues, and parameter-search helpers are unsafe for some invalid dimensions/bit counts. The article records these as input-contract/hardening work, not tests that already pass. Native CUDA checks of the new 50/55/60-bit matrix remain P2/P3 work. D7 next traces OpenFHE coefficient/evaluation order, root selection and DCRT tower representation. Personal website export/publication is separate from this repository commit and was not requested for D6. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Map columns, threads and twiddle factors", "summary": "Explained which values each block and thread processes, then checked every forward-column pair using the actual helper on CPU.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-09.html", "text": "2026-11-09 P1 / D1 → D3 ← All entries Map columns, threads and twiddle factors Explained which values each block and thread processes, then checked every forward-column pair using the actual helper on CPU. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Make the 4096-point CUDA schedule tangible ¶ Outcome ¶ Added a standalone HTML learning page explaining the current forward CUDA schedule using a concrete 4096-coefficient polynomial and one RNS limb. No CUDA implementation or roadmap gate changed. Later follow-up: completed the D1 index/memory map for both target extremes and advanced the working-day tracker to D2; the P1 architecture exit gate is still open. Subsequent D2 work completed the column index/twiddle proof and exhaustive stage-helper checks; the tracker now points to D3 inverse-column work. Reasoning ¶ Reading individual source lines made it hard to see the whole schedule. The explanation starts with which data each block loads: columns for four far-distance stages, then rows for eight near-distance stages in the original 4096-point example. Readers can select a stage, block and thread to see the changing pairs and threads with no butterfly to compute. Changes ¶ 4096-point NTT work distribution . Registered the learning page in the site catalog. Exported the existing interactive explanation with a standalone runtime and added context about synchronization, shared memory, and limb-major storage. Formatting follow-up: replaced the exported iframe/runtime with a small, self-contained lesson. Added consistent notebook typography, numbered sections, jump links, side-by-side pass descriptions, and an integrated light-themed explorer. The page now scrolls as one document; controls and pass descriptions stack at narrow widths. Mathematical content and thread mapping are retained. Added N=65536 to the same explorer, preserving the existing page URL and N=4096 example. Sparse row rendering includes selected operands and labels all omitted rows instead of drawing 256 rows. Documented the 256×256 layout, 8+8 stages, 512 KiB per-limb storage, 2 KiB shared memory per block, active thread ranges, stride-2048-byte column accesses, contiguous row accesses, and logical value-array traffic. Added a three-limb N=65536 fixture to the portable CTest schedule gate and dependency-free Node tests of the actual explorer script. D2: added the CPU-offset identity J = 256j + r , the pair-ownership bijection, both multiplier exponents, bit-operation equivalence, and table bounds to the HTML page, with six first/intermediate/last-stage examples. The explorer now displays CPU offset J, ladder exponent d, and difference exponent E for active threads, and no twiddle read for inactive threads. Added tests/column_mapping_tests.cpp and the column_mapping_check CTest target. Two test inputs isolate the contribution of each side of a pair. They run the actual helper with distinguishable table entries, then check every output position and table choice using independently derived indices. Verification ¶ Installed/confirmed docs/requirements.txt in the isolated .venv . .venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests/docs -v : all 15 tests passed. .venv/bin/python scripts/build_docs.py --check : built 17 pages and validated local links and fragments. Browser inspection at desktop and 375px embedded viewport widths: controls render and reflow; selecting distance 128 gives positions 773/901 for block 3, thread 5, and distance 1 gives 778/779. This documentation work does not constitute a real GPU run or benchmark. Formatting follow-up verification: all 15 documentation tests and the 17-page link/fragment build passed again. Browser inspection confirmed the desktop layout and 375px reflow, and the distance-128 control still displays global positions 773/901 for block 3, thread 5. N=65536 follow-up: node --test tests/docs/work_distribution.test.cjs passed 3 tests, including all-stage selected-pair checks and complete index coverage for both sizes. ctest --test-dir build -R '^device_schedule_check$' --output-on-failure passed the schedule gate plus its 4 fixture generators, including N=65536 with three primes. The simulator compares forward arrays, round trips, and multiplication against the CPU reference. These are CPU results, not real CUDA results. Browser checks of the extended page at desktop and a 375px viewport passed: N=65536 selects 16 stage controls and displays 1283/34051 for the first stage at block 3/thread 5. Comparison and explorer tables remain separate; selected operand rows stay visible in the sparse matrix. D2 checks: ctest --test-dir build -R 'column_mapping_check|device_schedule_check' --output-on-failure passed both gates and four fixture generators (6/6). The column gate checks 8,192 + 262,144 pairs, using 540,672 active-pair probes, plus every inactive thread at every column stage. Every global index and active thread owner is covered once per stage. All 3 explorer tests (now including displayed twiddle exponents), all 15 documentation tests, and the documentation build/link checks also passed. These remain CPU-only checks. D2 browser review: proof text and formula blocks reflow at desktop and 375px widths; the live N=65536 example displays J=1283, c=ψ^32768, and w=ψ^2566 for column 3/thread 5 in the first stage. Open questions ¶ Only part of each block computes butterflies in the first pass, and neighboring threads read widely separated global addresses. We need real GPU checks and measurements before deciding how to improve that schedule. Next step ¶ D3: derive how the inverse-column DIT butterfly undoes each forward butterfly, including the final N-inverse normalization and reversed stage order. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Publish the engineering notebook", "summary": "Published the notebook with page navigation, search, interactive lessons and a dated record of results.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-08.html", "text": "2026-11-08 P1 / D1 ← All entries Publish the engineering notebook Published the notebook with page navigation, search, interactive lessons and a dated record of results. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Open the engineering notebook ¶ Outcome ¶ The documentation now builds into one website. It has shared navigation, search, a homepage showing current progress, and a daily journal. Readers can still use the interactive lessons. Reasoning ¶ Developers need stable explanations and the history behind decisions. Research stays in reference articles; each journal entry records changes, evidence, decisions, and unresolved questions. Edit the Markdown source and generate the HTML website from it, so there are no separate text copies to maintain. Existing interactive HTML keeps its controls and equations. Entries written later from old records say that they are reconstructed. Journal dates are calendar dates; roadmap D labels are working milestones. Changes ¶ Site catalog . Writing and publishing guide . Site builder . Daily-entry helper . Integration tracker . Follow-up: added a complete main-page directory with a direct link and visible URL for every article, plan, and published daily entry. Displayed URLs resolve to the current host and project subpath, so the same page works locally and after deployment. Website integration: added NTT-GPU · CKKS From Scratch beside the Rust blockchain topic in the personal website's Systems from Scratch section. The notebook is exported to /systems/ntt-gpu/ , with a return link to the website. A repeatable export helper keeps the website copy derived from these source documents. Corrected the integration to use the user's existing website checkout. The topic now opens the explicit HTML page systems/ntt-gpu/index.html rather than a directory URL. Bundled search data also supports opening HTML files directly, alongside local navigation and math assets. Publishing follow-up: directory labels and canonical URLs now use the public https://minnow54426.github.io/systems/ntt-gpu/ address, including in local previews. Relative navigation continues to support local testing. The source notebook and personal website are being published together. Verification ¶ The site builds 15 HTML pages with four published daily entries. All 11 documentation regression checks passed, including route rewriting, code links, math preservation, draft exclusion, and protection against overwriting entries. The build checks that every document is listed and that local pages, images, scripts and section bookmarks exist. Browser checks confirmed full-text search from both root and nested journal pages, all 57 rendered formulas, and interactive NTT agreement after changing a coefficient. At a phone-sized viewport, the menu, journal, roadmap tables, and butterfly page fit without page-wide horizontal scrolling. Documentation changes do not add a GPU execution result. The main-page directory follow-up adds a coverage check for all 14 destination URLs, bringing the documentation suite to 12 passing tests. Desktop and phone-sized browser checks confirmed visible URLs and no page-wide overflow. Personal-website integration adds three export regression checks, bringing the suite to 15 passing tests. All 15 exported HTML pages returned HTTP 200 under /systems/ntt-gpu/ ; local assets, links, and fragments resolve. Browser checks confirmed the homepage topic, all 14 destination URLs, 57 rendered formulas, interactive butterfly agreement, and the nested-journal return link. The website and notebook fit a phone-sized viewport without horizontal overflow. Public release verification: source commit f91945b and website commit fd1a73e5 were pushed. Both website Pages workflows completed successfully; the deployment run is the release evidence. All 15 public HTML pages returned HTTP 200. Live browser checks confirmed the homepage topic, public directory URLs, search, all 57 rendered formulas, and interactive butterfly agreement. Open questions ¶ The notebook is now public at the personal website . Future entries should attach named-device measurements when they exist; real GPU execution and the D1 kernel mapping remain outstanding. The standalone project Pages workflow is optional. Next step ¶ Resume D1: map forward_strided_group_kernel blocks, threads, butterfly pairs, twiddles, and memory accesses for N=4096 and N=65536. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Debug one butterfly at a time", "summary": "Linked the NTT formulas to CPU loops and added an eight-value debugging example. CPU and formula-rendering checks passed.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-07.html", "text": "2026-11-07 P1 / D1 ← All entries Debug one butterfly at a time Linked the NTT formulas to CPU loops and added an eight-value debugging example. CPU and formula-rendering checks passed. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Make the butterfly implementation inspectable ¶ Reconstructed on 2026-11-08 from commit 751e723 and verification recorded during that work. Outcome ¶ Connected the formula for evaluating a polynomial to the small paired operations called butterflies and to the CPU loops that execute them. Added an eight-value VS Code example so readers can pause and inspect each step. Reasoning ¶ Splitting the input into even and odd positions gives two smaller transforms. Two outputs reuse their results with opposite signs, producing a butterfly. For multiplication modulo x^N+1, evaluate at odd powers of a primitive 2N-th root. Rendering those formulas makes the derivation easier to read. The site includes KaTeX, its formula renderer, so equations do not require a separate network download. For debugging, Xcode’s LLDB DAP connection replaced the CodeLLDB adapter that crashed in the recorded setup. Changes ¶ Implementation and explainer commit . Butterfly derivation . Debugging guide . N=8 example . Verification ¶ Recorded on 2026-11-07: 7/7 CPU CTest checks and 8/8 OpenFHE configuration checks passed. All 57 converted formulas passed strict parsing and browser rendering. The interactive example still matched the direct definition after changing a coefficient. These are not RTX CUDA runtime results. Open questions ¶ How do CUDA blocks and threads select butterfly indices and memory addresses? Next step ¶ Complete D1's forward-kernel index and memory-access map. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Plan the path to OpenFHE", "summary": "Planned the order of integration work and compared GPUs. Each phase has separate answer and performance checks.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-06.html", "text": "2026-11-06 P1 / D1 ← All entries Plan the path to OpenFHE Planned the order of integration work and compared GPUs. Each phase has separate answer and performance checks. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry A roadmap with explicit gates ¶ Reconstructed on 2026-11-08 from the linked commits and reports. Outcome ¶ Added the 95-working-day integration plan, GPU selection survey, and clearer implementation naming and formatting. Reasoning ¶ The sequence is NTT, base conversion, Hybrid key switching, then OpenFHE. Each step needs its own exact-answer and performance checks before it can be called complete. Running an encrypted inference model comes after the OpenFHE integration. RTX 5090 is the provisional primary target and RTX 4090 the control; the hardware report requires workload-specific benchmarks before claiming a winner. Changes ¶ Roadmap and hardware survey . Readability changes . Tracked plan . Verification ¶ The plan separates the CPU/OpenFHE baseline from the first real GPU gate. No new GPU timing is recorded here. Open questions ¶ The design still needs to specify what each stored number means, its array position, who owns each buffer, and the order of OpenFHE operations. Next step ¶ Map the forward column kernel's indices and memory accesses for N=4096 and N=65536. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Exact NTT results before GPU tuning", "summary": "Added comparisons with OpenFHE and surveyed GPU techniques. The checks ran on CPU; no actual GPU result was recorded.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal/2026-11-05.html", "text": "2026-11-05 P0 baseline ← All entries Exact NTT results before GPU tuning Added comparisons with OpenFHE and surveyed GPU techniques. The checks ran on CPU; no actual GPU result was recorded. This is a dated record of work on its calendar date. Its test counts, design and next steps describe that time; use the current roadmap for today’s status. Read full entry Exactness before GPU speed ¶ Reconstructed on 2026-11-08 from the linked commits and reports. This records documented outcomes, not a contemporaneous account of experiments. Outcome ¶ Added tests that compare every transform value with a specific OpenFHE 1.6.1 revision. Also added a survey of GPU NTT techniques and a plan for repeatable performance measurements. Reasoning ¶ CKKS decryption gives approximate numbers. Similar decrypted answers can hide different polynomial arrays, so the required test compares every transform value at the same array position. The survey identifies modular division, strided access, and host transfers as optimization candidates, not measured speedups. Changes ¶ OpenFHE gate . Survey and tuning plan . Compatibility test . Benchmark methodology . Verification ¶ The gate covers N=4096 through 65536, 50- and 59-bit moduli, and deterministic input patterns. The CPU schedule simulator is a separate correctness check. Real GPU execution remained outstanding. Open questions ¶ How much time does the GPU spend reducing integer products modulo a prime? How much does it waste fetching widely spaced column values? Answering these questions requires profiling on an identified GPU. Next step ¶ Define the implementation sequence and architecture contracts. Current roadmap →"}, {"title": "Building the GPU path for CKKS", "summary": "The open engineering notebook for ntt-gpu: progress, reasoning, and exactness.", "group": "Start here", "url": "index.html", "text": "An open engineering notebook GPU NTT for CKKS Learn how the NTT helps multiply polynomials, see the GPU test results, and follow the work toward OpenFHE integration. Read the GPU results Browse all pages ↓ CKKS · Hybrid N = 4,096–65,536 Target: RTX 5090 Working day D11 ⌁ Current checkpoint P2 — first RTX GPU baseline (Ada diagnostic passed) Run the prepared native ModUp/ModDown and conversion timing checks in the next combined RTX 5090 session. Explore the working-day plan Verified Temporary GPU baseline passed Recorded answer checks, memory/threading checks and timings. Still pending RTX 5090 validation Base conversion and key switching come later. Current record 2026-11-21 Latest published journal entry. New to the project? CKKS is an encryption scheme that allows calculations on encrypted data. It represents data using polynomials. OpenFHE is the cryptography library we aim to integrate with. The Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) changes how a polynomial is represented so multiplication becomes easier. This project first aims to run that transform efficiently on a GPU. Learn the NTT and butterflies with small numerical examples. See how the GPU divides the work , then trace one thread in the 65536-point explorer . Read the measured results or build the project . Useful words before you start Modulo q: keep the remainder after division by q. For example, 20 modulo 17 is 3. Coefficient: a number multiplying a power of x in a polynomial. An evaluation is the value obtained by substituting a point for x. Tower: one polynomial represented under one prime. Several towers represent its coefficients under several primes. Host / device: the CPU and its memory / the GPU and its memory. An upload copies host data to the device; a download copies it back. Kernel / stream: a function executed by GPU threads / an ordered queue of GPU work. Fixture / oracle: repeatable test inputs / a separate calculation used to check the answers. Native check: a test that actually runs on the target hardware. Compiling CUDA alone does not run a GPU test. Gate: a required check before work can be called complete. D labels a working milestone and P labels a phase. All pages Choose a lesson, reference, plan, or dated journal entry. Start here Project & setup Learn NTT & butterflies From CPU to CUDA 65536: work distribution CUDA buffers & dataflow Arithmetic bounds & tests OpenFHE representation Hybrid operation graph Device backend contract Resident operation chain Run & debug Prepare basis conversion Research GPU NTT implementations Choosing the GPU GPU rental market First GPU run: analysis Paired Ada NTT results Full GPU matrix: analysis Plans Next GPU rental queue Integration roadmap Architecture decision Implementation backlog Before renting a GPU Benchmarks & tuning Offline test tooling Daily journal All journal entries 2026-11-21 · Analyze Ada timings and prepare conversion measurements 2026-11-20 · Connect resident NTT and basis conversion 2026-11-19 · Validate the expanded GPU batch on Ada 2026-11-18 · Explain NTT concepts in plain English 2026-11-17 · Make the notebook easier to read 2026-11-16 · Shared theme and resident NTT preparation 2026-11-15 · Clearer benchmark modes and GPU results 2026-11-14 · Native GPU checks and full-matrix timings 2026-11-13 · Compare rental costs and profiling access 2026-11-12 · Accept the backend design and prepare CUDA offline 2026-11-11 · Freeze OpenFHE arrays and the Hybrid graph 2026-11-10 · Inverse proofs and exact arithmetic 2026-11-09 · Map columns, threads and twiddle factors 2026-11-08 · Publish the engineering notebook 2026-11-07 · Debug one butterfly at a time 2026-11-06 · Plan the path to OpenFHE 2026-11-05 · Exact NTT results before GPU tuning Contribute Writing & publishing"}, {"title": "Daily engineering journal", "summary": "Dated outcomes, reasoning, verification, and next steps.", "group": "Daily journal", "url": "journal.html", "text": "Daily journal Read what changed, why it changed and which checks ran on each calendar date. An older entry may describe work that was unfinished then. Its results remain tied to the recorded source version and machine. Use the current roadmap to find today’s milestone; a roadmap working day is not a calendar date. Entries 21 Nov 2026 Offline analysis and rental preparation / P2 D11 Analyze Ada timings and prepare conversion measurements Revalidated the November 19 records, compared schedule timings and prepared six resident ModUp/ModDown timing cases for the next GPU batch. Read the entry 20 Nov 2026 Resident conversion preparation / P2 D11 unchanged Connect resident NTT and basis conversion Added resident ModUp/ModDown composition with exact CPU comparisons, failure checks and both CUDA builds. Raw-timing analysis still awaits the archive. Read the entry 19 Nov 2026 Native Ada diagnostic / P2 D11 Validate the expanded GPU batch on Ada Both schedules passed native Ada checks, 40 sanitizer stages and 48 measurement jobs, with a verified local export and CUDA timeline. Read the entry 18 Nov 2026 Documentation maintenance / P2 unchanged Explain NTT concepts in plain English Explained NTT formulas, prime-tower data and butterfly merges, then reviewed all 40 notebook pages and rewrote unclear passages for beginners. Read the entry 17 Nov 2026 Documentation maintenance / P2 D11 unchanged Make the notebook easier to read Reviewed every notebook page, simplified explanations and distinguished current instructions from results recorded on earlier dates. Read the entry 16 Nov 2026 Website maintenance / P2 D11 tooling Shared theme and resident NTT preparation Restored Sunlit Paper, separated CPU/GPU measurements and prepared the resident chain. CPU tests and compilation passed; the new runtime had not run on a GPU. Read the entry 15 Nov 2026 P2 / D11 Clearer benchmark modes and GPU results Made benchmark modes and the results page clearer. The arithmetic and the work included in each timing stayed the same. Read the entry 14 Nov 2026 P2 / D11 diagnostic Native GPU checks and full-matrix timings The temporary Ada GPU passed its selected checks. Saved 280 GPU and 140 M1 CPU jobs; RTX 5090 tests and hardware-counter collection remained unfinished. Read the entry 13 Nov 2026 P2 D11 preparation Compare rental costs and profiling access Compared dated provider prices, saved an offer search and calculated example session costs. This survey did not rent a GPU. Read the entry 12 Nov 2026 P1 complete / P2 D11 preparation Accept the backend design and prepare CUDA offline Accepted the backend design, compiled CUDA without a GPU, and linked explanations to exact OpenFHE source lines. Hardware tests remained unfinished. Read the entry 11 Nov 2026 P1 / D7 → D8 Freeze OpenFHE arrays and the Hybrid graph Specified OpenFHE array formats and Hybrid operation order using exact CPU comparisons. Also recorded teaching and website changes. Read the entry 10 Nov 2026 P1 / D3 → D7 Inverse proofs and exact arithmetic Explained how inverse recovers inputs, checked that both passes cover all stages, mapped GPU buffers and tested exact CPU arithmetic. Read the entry 09 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 → D3 Map columns, threads and twiddle factors Explained which values each block and thread processes, then checked every forward-column pair using the actual helper on CPU. Read the entry 08 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 Publish the engineering notebook Published the notebook with page navigation, search, interactive lessons and a dated record of results. Read the entry 07 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 Debug one butterfly at a time Linked the NTT formulas to CPU loops and added an eight-value debugging example. CPU and formula-rendering checks passed. Read the entry 06 Nov 2026 P1 / D1 Plan the path to OpenFHE Planned the order of integration work and compared GPUs. Each phase has separate answer and performance checks. Read the entry 05 Nov 2026 P0 baseline Exact NTT results before GPU tuning Added comparisons with OpenFHE and surveyed GPU techniques. The checks ran on CPU; no actual GPU result was recorded. Read the entry"}];
